{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.550859+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_prompt", "agent": "pi", "prompt": "# DataSQRL Requirements Implementation Task\n\n## Context\nYou are implementing a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Implement User Requirements\n\nYour primary goal is to implement the following user requirements:\n---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\nA new DataSQRL pipeline in `loan-payments/` that reads the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan\nsources, builds a per-loan payment summary and a per-payment table, and serves them through a\nJWT-authorized GraphQL/REST/MCP API. It reuses the catalog connectors, its test data and the\nproject layout of the sibling `lending/` project, and adds no data of its own.\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\nThe specification lives in `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`: goal and scope, `### Sources`,\n`### Time semantics`, `### Entities and keys`, `### Transformations and aggregations`,\n`### API surface`, `### Test data and acceptance criteria`, `### Non-functional requirements`,\n`### Hard constraints`, and `### Open Questions`. Build the project to satisfy `FR-1`\u2013`FR-13`\nand the acceptance criteria there; the payload shapes and expected test values are quoted there\nand are not repeated in this plan.\n\nThe sibling `lending/` project (`lending/lending360.sqrl`, `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`,\n`lending/lending360-test-package.json`, `lending/lending360-prod-package.json`, and its\n`lending/lending360-api/` folder) is the layout and API-configuration reference. The shared\n`data-catalog` module (sibling directory, read-only) supplies the source tables `Personal_Loan`\nand `Loan_Payment` and their test data; the project imports them and never copies or edits them.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\nLegend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n- **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n  payments to `Personal_Loan`: a `PERSONAL` payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish\n  from `LoanPayments` and undercount its summary. The catalog already guarantees this cannot\n  happen (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture table is warranted. FR-8 is handled\n  by construction (latest-version dedup feeding an unwindowed re-aggregation), not by monitoring.\n- **Skip** \u2014 do not repeat the catalog's referential-integrity, enum, or numeric-sanity assertions;\n  do not add ingestion counters for the two sources, because the independent-sender failure mode\n  does not apply and the catalog's root snapshot queries already surface content changes; do not\n  monitor the intentional auto-loan exclusion, which is a required filter, not an anomaly.\n- **Reuse** \u2014 the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology snapshot queries, and the\n  `lending/` project's convention of carrying no pipeline-local DQ tables.\n\n## Technical Decisions\n- A custom GraphQL schema plus operations (not an inferred schema) is used, because `customer_id`\n  must be injected from the JWT rather than accepted from the caller and the exposed type names are\n  fixed; the API is configured like `lending/lending360-api/`.\n- The engine set is Flink + Postgres + Vert.x. No `kafka` engine is enabled: the API has no mutation\n  or subscription, and the catalog's Kafka sources are external Flink connectors that do not need\n  the DataSQRL-managed Kafka engine.\n- The catalog is included through one of its top-level package files with the batch mode disabled,\n  exactly like the sibling `lending/` base config; the consumer's per-environment configuration\n  selects which catalog connector file it imports, so the included module's own environment setting\n  never reaches the running pipeline.\n- CDC streams are reduced to the latest version per key ordered by the source update timestamp, and\n  the summary is an unwindowed re-aggregation, so a late or changed payment updates the API within\n  the streaming freshness target instead of waiting for a window boundary.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary` is anchored on the latest personal loans and left-joins the applied-payment\n  aggregates. Grouping only the payments would omit the loans that have none, which the accepted\n  Open-Question default requires to appear with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Authorization is implemented as claim-injected table functions and relationships over an internal\n  state table, with a per-environment Vert.x auth provider; a required claim argument produces the\n  required authorization error when the claim is absent.\n\n## Assumptions\n**FR-8 coverage**: Assumed FR-8 is verified by construction \u2014 the latest-version `DISTINCT` feeds an\nunwindowed re-aggregation \u2014 and documented in the README instead of by a snapshot. Rationale: no\ncatalog payment changes status, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data of our own.\n[impact: low]\n\n**Catalog include config**: Assumed the catalog is included with one of its top-level package files\nand `is_batch` false (as `lending/` does) while the consumer's `environment` selects the connector\nfile. Rationale: the consumer imports only the `-{{environment}}` connector, so the include's own\nenvironment setting is inert. [impact: medium]\n\n**Freshness**: Assumed new or changed payments appear within one minute through the streaming Flink\npipeline with no window. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`, and the\ncatalog watermarks (10 s test, 30 s prod) are used as-is. [impact: high]\n\n**JWT validation**: Assumed HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test` and a key from `${JWT_SECRET}`\nin `prod`, with issuer and audience supplied as environment variables. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault in `### Open Questions`. [impact: high]\n\n**Production workload**: Assumed up to 1,000,000 personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and a\n50 requests/second peak. Rationale: the recommended default; it only affects deployment sizing.\n[impact: medium]\n\n**Dev/local environment**: Assumed only `test` and `prod` overlays, because the scope names exactly\nthose two and follows `lending/`. [impact: low]\n\n**Loans without payments**: Assumed `LoanPaymentSummary` returns a row for every personal loan, with\nzero counts/sums and a null `last_payment_date` when no applied payment exists. Rationale: the\nrecommended default and the acceptance criterion for `PL-011`. [impact: medium]\n\n**Loan status**: Assumed the API exposes loans in every status, not only `ACTIVE`. Rationale: the\nrecommended default, and the scope keeps all statuses. [impact: medium]\n\n**Last payment date**: Assumed the latest `applied_date` among applied payments. Rationale: the\nrecommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Total amount paid**: Assumed `total_paid_cents` is the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied\npayments. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Customer-query loan details**: Assumed the signed-in customer's loans also show `loan_status`,\n`original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n`next_payment_due_date`. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**API protocols**: Assumed GraphQL, REST and MCP, matching `lending/`. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault and `### API surface`. [impact: low]\n\n**Budget**: Assumed the smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment size. Rationale: the recommended default;\nno budget was given. [impact: low]\n\n\n---\n\n## Pipeline Implementation\n\n### Self-Improvement Loop\n\nYou MUST follow this iterative development process:\n\n1. **Implement**: Write or modify the project code and configuration to meet the requirements.\n   Before any file change, work through this checklist:\n   a. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n   b. Match your action against the triggers below and invoke every skill that matches:\n      - Invoke the `/init-sqrl` skill when initializing a new project.\n      - Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill when writing or editing a `.sqrl` file.\n      - Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill when writing any file in `connectors/` or `data_catalog/`. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, even when following an existing pattern.\n      - Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill when editing a `*-package.json`.\n      - Invoke the `/design-api` skill when adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE statement, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` file. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/from-api` skill when the requirements themselves specify the API to expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, MCP tools). **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill when writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, or running tests for the first time on a project. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally.\n      - Invoke the `/monitor-pipeline` skill when writing any data-quality capture or observability stats table. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally. Treat catalog assertions as upstream guarantees unless explicitly documented otherwise. Update the plan when monitoring should be added, narrowed, removed, or reused. \n      - Invoke the `/data-observation` skill when given source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling.\n      - Invoke the `/handle-large-data-files` skill when a large data file is present, or a run reported `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`.\n   c. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n   d. For MUST skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n2. **Compile**: Run the DataSQRL compiler for every generated or affected (sub-)project with the help of the `/configure-sqrl` skill (How to Compile/Test).\n3. **Test**: Run the tests of every generated or affected (sub-)project using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests).\n4. **Review**: Analyze compiler output and test results\n5. **Iterate**: If there are errors or test failures, fix them and repeat from step 2\n\n**Continue this loop until**:\n- The project compiles without errors\n- All tests pass successfully\n- The implementation meets all user requirements\n- Code follows DataSQRL best practices\n- Test snapshots are validated\n- Documentation is updated\n\nYour last action should be running the tests successfully.\n\n### Critical Rules\n\n- Always review relevant reference projects in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md` before implementing new features.\n- Always create test cases for features you implement and make sure all tests pass. Validate new snapshots.\n- Always ensure all package.json files for the project compile successfully.\n- The project must have a runnable `run-tests.sh` at its root whose suite lines cover every test.\n- If the project consumes a shared SQRL module (e.g. a data catalog mounted as a read-only sibling), never modify the module; invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill, and compile/test using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests, Project inside a repository).\n- Always review and create/update README.md with the `/write-documentation` skill.\n- Clean up at the end of the implementation: Delete copied files that are no longer needed.\n\n### Response\n\nYour last action must be a successful run of `./run-tests.sh`, scoped with the names of every sub-project you generated or edited plus the ones affected by an edit to shared code or configuration, use the script with no arguments when run all (sub-)projects test or the project is new. Output that exact invocation as your final line, no markdown, no explanation, just the bare command. \n\nExample: \n```\n./run-tests.sh [affected-sub-project-names] # only the affected sub-project(s) \n```\nand if change affect every sub-project, or completely new project:\n```\n./run-tests.sh\n```\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```", "session_id": null}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.923966+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "session", "version": 3, "id": "codeagentf6720a64027e44ff9c4a4380dc6ba4c5", "cwd": "/workspace/loan-payments", "source_timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.889Z"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.925597+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_start"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.926077+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_start"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.927820+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "system", "content": "", "sections": {"preamble": "You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.", "tools": "<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- grep: Search file contents for patterns (respects .gitignore)\n- find: Find files by glob pattern (respects .gitignore)\n- ls: List directory contents\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>", "rules": "<rules>\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>", "docs": "<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>", "addendum": "<addendum>\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Starting an implementation run, resuming after a compaction, or choosing the next plan step                           | `/execute-plan`            | **MUST** \u2014 the loop that drives every step below (see \"Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\") |\n| Initializing a new project                                                                                            | `/init-sqrl`               | If applicable                                      |\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n## Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\n\nYour implementation plan is in `adr/plan_<ts>.md` (the exact path is in your \"## Requirements &\nplan checklist\" context). Its `## Implementation Checklist` is a list of markdown checkboxes and is\nyour **durable, on-disk progress state**. Invoke the `/execute-plan` skill at the start of the run and\nagain after any compaction \u2014 it is the loop for the rules below.\n\n- Resume at the in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several); otherwise take the first\n  `- [ ]` box whose `[deps: ...]` are all done.\n- Mark a box `- [*]` when you start its step; before the step, read its `\u2192 skill: /name` (and any\n  MUST skills for the files you touch).\n- Mark a box `- [x]` **only after** that step's work is done. Edit the plan file in place; mark one box at a time.\n- Never redo a `- [x]` step. Mark a `[cond: ...]` step that does not apply as `- [~]` with a short\n  reason (`- [ ]` = todo, `- [*]` = in progress, `- [x]` = done, `- [~]` = skipped).\n- After any compaction, re-read the checklist and resume at the first `- [*]` (or, if none, `- [ ]`).\n- Keep the checklist current: if the plan changes, add/re-scope `- [ ]` steps (never delete or\n  renumber existing steps \u2014 `[deps: N]` references them). Change only checkbox glyphs; never disturb the frontmatter or `## Assumptions` section.\n\nInvoke the `/execute-plan` skill for the full loop.\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nAn implementation run changes only what its plan checklist asks for. Code that already exists and works is **out of scope**, especially if the change is not necessary.\n\n- Touch a file only if a checklist step names it, or it must change for a named step to compile or pass its tests.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when necessary and a plan step requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n\n# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /execute-plan \u2014 Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\nThis repo contains other SQRL projects/modules. `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists them (paths, READMEs, SQRL files, package summaries) \u2014 read it before deciding how to build. They are mounted READ-ONLY: never edit them, never copy their files into this project (include shared modules via `script.include` in `*-package.json` files instead), and never read their `build/` directories.\n\n## Requirements & plan checklist\nYour requirements and implementation plan are in `adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` (authoritative). Read it before implementing and re-read it after any compaction. Its `## Implementation Checklist` is your durable progress state: resume at an in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several), else the first unchecked `- [ ]`; mark a step `- [*]` when you start it and `- [x]` only once it compiles/passes; never redo a `- [x]` step. Follow the `execute-plan` skill.\nSkill form takes precedence over step wording: where a step and its skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form and correct the step text. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n</addendum>", "project_context": "<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/workspace/loan-payments/AGENTS.md\">\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Starting an implementation run, resuming after a compaction, or choosing the next plan step                           | `/execute-plan`            | **MUST** \u2014 the loop that drives every step below (see \"Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\") |\n| Initializing a new project                                                                                            | `/init-sqrl`               | If applicable                                      |\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n## Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\n\nYour implementation plan is in `adr/plan_<ts>.md` (the exact path is in your \"## Requirements &\nplan checklist\" context). Its `## Implementation Checklist` is a list of markdown checkboxes and is\nyour **durable, on-disk progress state**. Invoke the `/execute-plan` skill at the start of the run and\nagain after any compaction \u2014 it is the loop for the rules below.\n\n- Resume at the in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several); otherwise take the first\n  `- [ ]` box whose `[deps: ...]` are all done.\n- Mark a box `- [*]` when you start its step; before the step, read its `\u2192 skill: /name` (and any\n  MUST skills for the files you touch).\n- Mark a box `- [x]` **only after** that step's work is done. Edit the plan file in place; mark one box at a time.\n- Never redo a `- [x]` step. Mark a `[cond: ...]` step that does not apply as `- [~]` with a short\n  reason (`- [ ]` = todo, `- [*]` = in progress, `- [x]` = done, `- [~]` = skipped).\n- After any compaction, re-read the checklist and resume at the first `- [*]` (or, if none, `- [ ]`).\n- Keep the checklist current: if the plan changes, add/re-scope `- [ ]` steps (never delete or\n  renumber existing steps \u2014 `[deps: N]` references them). Change only checkbox glyphs; never disturb the frontmatter or `## Assumptions` section.\n\nInvoke the `/execute-plan` skill for the full loop.\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nAn implementation run changes only what its plan checklist asks for. Code that already exists and works is **out of scope**, especially if the change is not necessary.\n\n- Touch a file only if a checklist step names it, or it must change for a named step to compile or pass its tests.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when necessary and a plan step requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>", "skills": "<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>build-catalog</name>\n    <description>Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/build-catalog/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>configure-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when configuring a DataSQRL project&apos;s `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>data-observation</name>\n    <description>Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/data-observation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-api</name>\n    <description>Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/design-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>execute-plan</name>\n    <description>Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a &quot;your conversation was compacted&quot; reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_&lt;ts&gt;.md.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/execute-plan/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>from-api</name>\n    <description>Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/from-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>handle-large-data-files</name>\n    <description>Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/handle-large-data-files/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-udf</name>\n    <description>Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-udf/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-ui</name>\n    <description>Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>init-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/init-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>manage-connector</name>\n    <description>Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/manage-connector/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>monitor-pipeline</name>\n    <description>Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline&apos;s monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/monitor-pipeline/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize-deployment</name>\n    <description>Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.&lt;flink|postgres|vertx&gt;.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/optimize-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>troubleshoot-test</name>\n    <description>Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/troubleshoot-test/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>write-documentation</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/write-documentation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>", "cwd": "<cwd>\n/workspace/loan-payments\n</cwd>"}, "timestamp": 1795510933924, "toolsAdded": [{"name": "read", "description": "Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"}, "offset": {"type": "number", "description": "Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of lines to read"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "bash", "description": "Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["command"], "properties": {"command": {"type": "string", "description": "Shell command to execute"}, "timeout": {"type": "number", "description": "Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "edit", "description": "Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "edits"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"}, "edits": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["oldText", "newText"], "properties": {"oldText": {"type": "string", "description": "Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."}, "newText": {"type": "string", "description": "Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}}, "description": "One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "write", "description": "Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "content"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"}, "content": {"type": "string", "description": "Content to write to the file"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "grep", "description": "Search file contents for a pattern. Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 100 matches or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Long lines are truncated to 500 chars.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Search pattern (regex or literal string)"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory or file to search (default: current directory)"}, "glob": {"type": "string", "description": "Filter files by glob pattern, e.g. '*.ts' or '**/*.spec.ts'"}, "ignoreCase": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false)"}, "literal": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Treat pattern as literal string instead of regex (default: false)"}, "context": {"type": "number", "description": "Number of lines to show before and after each match (default: 0)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of matches to return (default: 100)"}}}}, {"name": "find", "description": "Search for files by glob pattern. Returns matching file paths relative to the search directory. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 1000 results or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Glob pattern to match files, e.g. '*.ts', '**/*.json', or 'src/**/*.spec.ts'"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to search in (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of results (default: 1000)"}}}}, {"name": "ls", "description": "List directory contents. Returns entries sorted alphabetically, with '/' suffix for directories. Includes dotfiles. Output is truncated to 500 entries or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to list (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of entries to return (default: 500)"}}}}]}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:13.929525+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# DataSQRL Requirements Implementation Task\n\n## Context\nYou are implementing a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Implement User Requirements\n\nYour primary goal is to implement the following user requirements:\n---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\nA new DataSQRL pipeline in `loan-payments/` that reads the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan\nsources, builds a per-loan payment summary and a per-payment table, and serves them through a\nJWT-authorized GraphQL/REST/MCP API. It reuses the catalog connectors, its test data and the\nproject layout of the sibling `lending/` project, and adds no data of its own.\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\nThe specification lives in `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`: goal and scope, `### Sources`,\n`### Time semantics`, `### Entities and keys`, `### Transformations and aggregations`,\n`### API surface`, `### Test data and acceptance criteria`, `### Non-functional requirements`,\n`### Hard constraints`, and `### Open Questions`. Build the project to satisfy `FR-1`\u2013`FR-13`\nand the acceptance criteria there; the payload shapes and expected test values are quoted there\nand are not repeated in this plan.\n\nThe sibling `lending/` project (`lending/lending360.sqrl`, `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`,\n`lending/lending360-test-package.json`, `lending/lending360-prod-package.json`, and its\n`lending/lending360-api/` folder) is the layout and API-configuration reference. The shared\n`data-catalog` module (sibling directory, read-only) supplies the source tables `Personal_Loan`\nand `Loan_Payment` and their test data; the project imports them and never copies or edits them.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\nLegend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n- **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n  payments to `Personal_Loan`: a `PERSONAL` payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish\n  from `LoanPayments` and undercount its summary. The catalog already guarantees this cannot\n  happen (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture table is warranted. FR-8 is handled\n  by construction (latest-version dedup feeding an unwindowed re-aggregation), not by monitoring.\n- **Skip** \u2014 do not repeat the catalog's referential-integrity, enum, or numeric-sanity assertions;\n  do not add ingestion counters for the two sources, because the independent-sender failure mode\n  does not apply and the catalog's root snapshot queries already surface content changes; do not\n  monitor the intentional auto-loan exclusion, which is a required filter, not an anomaly.\n- **Reuse** \u2014 the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology snapshot queries, and the\n  `lending/` project's convention of carrying no pipeline-local DQ tables.\n\n## Technical Decisions\n- A custom GraphQL schema plus operations (not an inferred schema) is used, because `customer_id`\n  must be injected from the JWT rather than accepted from the caller and the exposed type names are\n  fixed; the API is configured like `lending/lending360-api/`.\n- The engine set is Flink + Postgres + Vert.x. No `kafka` engine is enabled: the API has no mutation\n  or subscription, and the catalog's Kafka sources are external Flink connectors that do not need\n  the DataSQRL-managed Kafka engine.\n- The catalog is included through one of its top-level package files with the batch mode disabled,\n  exactly like the sibling `lending/` base config; the consumer's per-environment configuration\n  selects which catalog connector file it imports, so the included module's own environment setting\n  never reaches the running pipeline.\n- CDC streams are reduced to the latest version per key ordered by the source update timestamp, and\n  the summary is an unwindowed re-aggregation, so a late or changed payment updates the API within\n  the streaming freshness target instead of waiting for a window boundary.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary` is anchored on the latest personal loans and left-joins the applied-payment\n  aggregates. Grouping only the payments would omit the loans that have none, which the accepted\n  Open-Question default requires to appear with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Authorization is implemented as claim-injected table functions and relationships over an internal\n  state table, with a per-environment Vert.x auth provider; a required claim argument produces the\n  required authorization error when the claim is absent.\n\n## Assumptions\n**FR-8 coverage**: Assumed FR-8 is verified by construction \u2014 the latest-version `DISTINCT` feeds an\nunwindowed re-aggregation \u2014 and documented in the README instead of by a snapshot. Rationale: no\ncatalog payment changes status, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data of our own.\n[impact: low]\n\n**Catalog include config**: Assumed the catalog is included with one of its top-level package files\nand `is_batch` false (as `lending/` does) while the consumer's `environment` selects the connector\nfile. Rationale: the consumer imports only the `-{{environment}}` connector, so the include's own\nenvironment setting is inert. [impact: medium]\n\n**Freshness**: Assumed new or changed payments appear within one minute through the streaming Flink\npipeline with no window. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`, and the\ncatalog watermarks (10 s test, 30 s prod) are used as-is. [impact: high]\n\n**JWT validation**: Assumed HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test` and a key from `${JWT_SECRET}`\nin `prod`, with issuer and audience supplied as environment variables. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault in `### Open Questions`. [impact: high]\n\n**Production workload**: Assumed up to 1,000,000 personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and a\n50 requests/second peak. Rationale: the recommended default; it only affects deployment sizing.\n[impact: medium]\n\n**Dev/local environment**: Assumed only `test` and `prod` overlays, because the scope names exactly\nthose two and follows `lending/`. [impact: low]\n\n**Loans without payments**: Assumed `LoanPaymentSummary` returns a row for every personal loan, with\nzero counts/sums and a null `last_payment_date` when no applied payment exists. Rationale: the\nrecommended default and the acceptance criterion for `PL-011`. [impact: medium]\n\n**Loan status**: Assumed the API exposes loans in every status, not only `ACTIVE`. Rationale: the\nrecommended default, and the scope keeps all statuses. [impact: medium]\n\n**Last payment date**: Assumed the latest `applied_date` among applied payments. Rationale: the\nrecommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Total amount paid**: Assumed `total_paid_cents` is the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied\npayments. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Customer-query loan details**: Assumed the signed-in customer's loans also show `loan_status`,\n`original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n`next_payment_due_date`. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**API protocols**: Assumed GraphQL, REST and MCP, matching `lending/`. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault and `### API surface`. [impact: low]\n\n**Budget**: Assumed the smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment size. Rationale: the recommended default;\nno budget was given. [impact: low]\n\n\n---\n\n## Pipeline Implementation\n\n### Self-Improvement Loop\n\nYou MUST follow this iterative development process:\n\n1. **Implement**: Write or modify the project code and configuration to meet the requirements.\n   Before any file change, work through this checklist:\n   a. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n   b. Match your action against the triggers below and invoke every skill that matches:\n      - Invoke the `/init-sqrl` skill when initializing a new project.\n      - Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill when writing or editing a `.sqrl` file.\n      - Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill when writing any file in `connectors/` or `data_catalog/`. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, even when following an existing pattern.\n      - Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill when editing a `*-package.json`.\n      - Invoke the `/design-api` skill when adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE statement, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` file. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/from-api` skill when the requirements themselves specify the API to expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, MCP tools). **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill when writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, or running tests for the first time on a project. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally.\n      - Invoke the `/monitor-pipeline` skill when writing any data-quality capture or observability stats table. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally. Treat catalog assertions as upstream guarantees unless explicitly documented otherwise. Update the plan when monitoring should be added, narrowed, removed, or reused. \n      - Invoke the `/data-observation` skill when given source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling.\n      - Invoke the `/handle-large-data-files` skill when a large data file is present, or a run reported `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`.\n   c. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n   d. For MUST skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n2. **Compile**: Run the DataSQRL compiler for every generated or affected (sub-)project with the help of the `/configure-sqrl` skill (How to Compile/Test).\n3. **Test**: Run the tests of every generated or affected (sub-)project using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests).\n4. **Review**: Analyze compiler output and test results\n5. **Iterate**: If there are errors or test failures, fix them and repeat from step 2\n\n**Continue this loop until**:\n- The project compiles without errors\n- All tests pass successfully\n- The implementation meets all user requirements\n- Code follows DataSQRL best practices\n- Test snapshots are validated\n- Documentation is updated\n\nYour last action should be running the tests successfully.\n\n### Critical Rules\n\n- Always review relevant reference projects in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md` before implementing new features.\n- Always create test cases for features you implement and make sure all tests pass. Validate new snapshots.\n- Always ensure all package.json files for the project compile successfully.\n- The project must have a runnable `run-tests.sh` at its root whose suite lines cover every test.\n- If the project consumes a shared SQRL module (e.g. a data catalog mounted as a read-only sibling), never modify the module; invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill, and compile/test using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests, Project inside a repository).\n- Always review and create/update README.md with the `/write-documentation` skill.\n- Clean up at the end of the implementation: Delete copied files that are no longer needed.\n\n### Response\n\nYour last action must be a successful run of `./run-tests.sh`, scoped with the names of every sub-project you generated or edited plus the ones affected by an edit to shared code or configuration, use the script with no arguments when run all (sub-)projects test or the project is new. Output that exact invocation as your final line, no markdown, no explanation, just the bare command. \n\nExample: \n```\n./run-tests.sh [affected-sub-project-names] # only the affected sub-project(s) \n```\nand if change affect every sub-project, or completely new project:\n```\n./run-tests.sh\n```\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795510933924}}
