_analytics/connectors/test-data/transaction.jsonl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequestCustomer2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequestCustomer2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequest.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequests.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/PotentialRewards.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsByDay.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsWithSupportRole.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsWithSupportRole.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/test-customerId-2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/test-customerId-2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/TotalPotentialReward.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-local-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards.sqrl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-test-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/README.md\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/run-tests.sh\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CardRequestCustomer2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CardRequest.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CustomerRewardsTest.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/MyCardRequests.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/PotentialRewards.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/RewardsByDay.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/RewardsWithSupportRole.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/test-customerId-2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/TotalPotentialReward.snapshot\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510943503}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccabe8478d1bac53402", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# User Analytics for Financial Transactions\n\nThis project computes credit card rewards for each customer from card transactions.\nIt joins each transaction with the card assignment, the merchant and the merchant's reward program that were valid at the time of the transaction.\nIt then computes the rewards the customer earned with their card, and the rewards they could have earned with other card types.\nA card issuer can use the second result to recommend a card.\n\nA user-facing GraphQL API serves the results. JWT authentication makes sure that each customer sees only their own data.\n\nThe sub-project name is `creditcard_rewards`.\n\n## What this example shows\n\n- Deduplication of CDC streams to the latest version of each record (`DISTINCT`).\n- Temporal joins (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) with the card, merchant and reward state at transaction time.\n- `CROSS JOIN UNNEST` of an array of rewards, filtered by the validity period of each reward.\n- Daily tumbling windows.\n- Access control from JWT claims: table functions take `customerId` and `roles` from `METADATA FROM 'auth.*'`.\n- A GraphQL subscription for new rewards.\n- A mutation that takes the customer from a JWT claim: the `CardRequest` column `customerId` comes from `METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId'`.\n- Tests that use different JWT tokens.\n\n## Sources\n\nThe source schemas are in `connectors/sources.sqrl`.\nThe main script imports `connectors/sources-{{environment}}.sqrl`, so each environment picks its own connectors.\n\n| Table | One record is | Key | Test data (`connectors/test-data/`) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `CardAssignmentStream` | a card assigned to a customer, with its card type | `customerId`, `cardNo` | `card_assignment.jsonl`, 14 records for customers 1 to 11 |\n| `MerchantStream` | one version of a merchant profile | `merchantId` | `merchant.jsonl`, 101 records |\n| `MerchantRewardStream` | the reward program of a merchant: a reward percentage for each card type, with start and end times | `merchantId` | `merchant_reward.jsonl`, 468 records |\n| `Transaction` | one card payment | `transactionId` | `transaction.jsonl`, 5184 records |\n\n- **test** (`connectors/sources-test.sqrl`): reads the JSONL files with the filesystem connector.\n- **prod** (`connectors/sources-prod.sqrl`): reads the Kafka topics `cardassignment`, `merchant`, `merchantreward` and `transaction`. Needs `KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS`.\n\n## Processing\n\nThe main script is `creditcard_rewards.sqrl`.\n\n| Table | What it computes |\n|---|---|\n| `_CustomerTransactionRewardsByCard` | Each transaction with the customer, the merchant, and one row for each card type that the merchant rewards at that time. `reward = amount \u00d7 percentage / 100`. |\n| `CustomerRewards` | The rows where the reward card type is the customer's card type: the rewards the customer earned. |\n| `CustomerPotentialRewards` | The rows with other card types: the rewards the customer could have earned. |\n\nThe API functions read these two tables. Each function filters by the `customerId` claim of the caller.\n\n| Function | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `Rewards(fromTime, toTime)` | The rewards of the customer in a time range. A caller with the `support` role sees the rewards of all customers. |\n| `TotalReward` | The total reward of the customer and the time of the first reward. |\n| `RewardsByDay` | The daily reward totals of the customer. |\n| `PotentialRewards(cardType, fromTime, toTime)` | The rewards the customer could have earned with one card type. |\n| `TotalPotentialReward` | The total potential reward of the customer for each card type. |\n| `PotentialRewardsByDay` | The daily potential reward totals of the customer. |\n| `NewCustomerRewards` | A subscription: each new reward of the customer. |\n| `MyCardRequests` | The card requests of the customer, starting from the most recent one. |\n\nThe mutation `CardRequest(event: {cardType})` records a request for a new card type.\nThe client sends only `cardType`. The server sets `requestId`, the `customerId` from the token, and `requestTime`.\nThe table `CardRequest` has `no_query`, so a customer reads requests only through `MyCardRequests`.\n\n## Authorization\n\nThe API accepts only requests with a valid JWT.\nThe functions read two claims:\n\n- `customerId`: every function returns only the data of this customer, and `CardRequest` writes it into each request.\n- `roles`: `Rewards` also returns the data of all customers when `roles` contains `support`.\n\nFor an array claim like `roles`, check it with `array_contains` and cast the argument, for example `array_contains(cast(:roles as ARRAY<STRING>), 'support')`.\n\nThe JWT settings are in the `engines.vertx.config.auth` block of each environment config, not in the shared config.\nThe script reads claims, so each config that compiles it needs an `auth` block. Without one, the compile fails.\nEach `auth` block has one issuer with its audience and one `oct` key. The server accepts a token only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature match that issuer.\nThe `k` value of the key is the signing key in base64url without padding.\nThe test and local configs use the key `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` with issuer `dummy-issuer` and audience `dummy-audience`.\nTo make the `k` value, run `printf '%s' 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' | base64 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=\\n'`. Use `printf`, not `echo`, because `echo` adds a newline to the key.\n\nTo make a token for another customer (1 to 11 in the test data), run:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret \"dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string\" --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"dummy-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"dummy-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customerId\": 6, \"roles\": [\"user\"]}'\n```\n\nThe prod config uses the same dummy key with issuer `my-issuer` and audience `my-audience`. Before you deploy, replace the issuer entry with your identity provider: its issuer URL and your audiences, without `keys`. The server then gets the signing keys from the issuer.\n\n## Environments\n\n| Config | Environment | What it adds |\n|---|---|---|\n| `creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json` | base | The engines (Vert.x, Postgres, Kafka, Flink) and the main script. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-test-package.json` | `test` | The JWT settings, the test runner and the default test token. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-local-package.json` | `test` connectors | The JWT settings. Use it to run the pipeline on your machine with the test data. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json` | `prod` | The Kafka sources and the prod JWT settings. |\n\n## Tests\n\nThe API tests are in `creditcard_rewards-api/tests/`.\nThe test config sets a default token for customer 9 in `test-runner.headers`.\nA test with its own `.properties` file uses that file's header instead:\n\n- `test-customerId-2.graphql` runs as customer 2.\n- `RewardsWithSupportRole.graphql` runs with the `support` role and gets the rewards of several customers.\n- `CardRequest.graphql` and `CardRequestCustomer2.graphql` send one card request as customer 9 and one as customer 2.\n- `MyCardRequests.graphql` and `MyCardRequestsCustomer2.graphql` show that each customer gets only their own request.\n\nThe script also has one test table, `CustomerRewardsTest`.\nThe expected results are in `snapshots/`.\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from this project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root of the code-agent repository.\n\n```bash\ncd datasqrl-examples/user_analytics                         # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                                              # test every sub-project\n./run-tests.sh creditcard_rewards                           # test one sub-project\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env local creditcard_rewards     # compile with the local environment\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod creditcard_rewards      # compile with the prod environment\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands below make the same DataSQRL calls as the `./run-tests.sh` compile and test lines.\nThey work from any folder inside the git repository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-local-package.json -b creditcard_rewards\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-test-package.json -b creditcard_rewards\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, the same folder that `./run-tests.sh` uses. It is optional. Without it, the output goes to `build/`.\n\nThe `run` command starts the whole pipeline on your machine with the `local` environment.\nIt works from any folder inside the git repository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 \\\n  -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-local-package.json\n```\n\nThe pipeline loads the test data at start.\nEvery request needs a token. In GraphiQL, add this header to query as customer 9:\n\n```json\n{\"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6OSwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNDcsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.317UGknkS-dke26iQs3H6_Oj2WsoKO78H300hRsePrc\"}\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs:\n\n- The Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/.\n- The GraphQL API and GraphiQL are at http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/.\n- The Swagger UI for the REST API is at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui.\n- The MCP API is at `http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795510943503}]}
