{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:41.202474+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03517c809cfcb5ab", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"1 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/ontology/\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510961200}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:41.202795+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb037e7552b9ffa648", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Vert.x Engine Configuration\n\n| Key        | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                 |\n|------------|------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n\n## Server Configuration Overrides\n\nThe compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|-----|-------|\n| `httpServerOptions.port` | HTTP port (default `8888`). Keep `8888` for the `test` command, whose test runner connects to that port |\n| `corsHandlerOptions` | CORS policy. Defaults allow all origins; restrict `allowedOrigin`/`allowedOrigins` in production |\n| `poolOptions.maxSize` | PostgreSQL connection pool size |\n| `poolOptions.maxLifetime` | Seconds before a pooled PostgreSQL connection is closed and replaced (default `600`), so connections rebalance across replicas; `0` keeps them forever |\n| `pgConnectOptions.cachePreparedStatements` | Cache prepared statements per connection so each query is planned once (default `true`) |\n| `servletConfig` | Endpoint paths (`graphQLEndpoint`, `restEndpoint`, `mcpEndpoint`, `graphiQLEndpoint`), prefixed with the API version, e.g. `/v1/graphql` |\n| `publicGraphQLEndpointEnabled` | Set to `false` to disable the public GraphQL endpoint; REST and MCP endpoints keep working |\n| `onlyConfiguredGraphQLOperations` | Set to `true` to only accept the predefined API operations instead of arbitrary GraphQL queries |\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"corsHandlerOptions\": {\n          \"allowedOrigin\": \"https://app.example.com\",\n          \"allowCredentials\": true\n        },\n        \"poolOptions\": {\n          \"maxSize\": 16\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n\n## Slow-Query Logging\n\nEnable slow-query logging in every project that enables `vertx`. The server then logs each API request that takes longer than its threshold or that fails. A request's threshold is the lowest threshold among the fields it runs. A slow query then appears in the logs, not only as a client timeout.\n\nPut the block in the base config, so every deployed environment has it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the logging on |\n| `defaultThresholdMs` | `60000` | The threshold for fields without an entry in `thresholds` |\n| `thresholds` | `{}` | A threshold for requests that run this field, keyed `Type.field`, e.g. `{\"Query.OrdersByCustomer\": 5000}` or `{\"Customers.orders\": 5000}`. A request uses the lowest threshold among the fields it runs |\n| `alwaysLogErrors` | `true` | Also logs failed requests, such as a timeout while waiting for a database connection |\n| `maxLoggedTracesPerMinute` | `60` | Limits the log volume during an incident |\n| `maxFieldTraces` | `50` | Maximum number of query timings in one log line |\n\n### Choosing the threshold\n\nMake an educated guess for each threshold from how the API is used. The values in the example are a starting point, not a default to copy.\n\n- If the requirements state a latency target for the API, use that target. Otherwise, start from the consumer of the API: 1000 to 2000 ms for user interfaces and dashboards, 2000 to 5000 ms for service-to-service calls.\n- Look for heavy queries, such as a large aggregate. If their latency is much higher than that of the other queries, give each one its own entry in `thresholds`, keyed by its field (`Query.<Field>` for a table or table function, `<Type>.<field>` for a relationship). As a request uses the lowest threshold among its fields, the entry applies only when a request runs that field alone. Any other field in the same request, including a nested relationship field without its own entry, brings the lower threshold back.\n\n- Keep every threshold well below the client request timeout. The server writes the log line after a request ends, and it does not stop the request. A threshold below the timeout logs the requests that are slow but still succeed.\n\n## Large-Result Logging\n\nThe server logs a warning for each database query that returns many rows, before it converts the rows to JSON. Converting a very large result blocks the server, so the warning is written even when the pod is killed afterwards. The warning names the GraphQL field path, the row count, the elapsed time and the SQL (cut after 2000 characters), and never the parameter values. The elapsed time counts from issuing the query to receiving its rows, including any wait for a pool connection, so it is not the database time. Also when this warning is off, the server logs an error for a failed query, with its field path and SQL, and for rows it cannot convert to JSON, with the field path.\n\nThis warning is off by default and only covers large results. Slow requests are logged by [Slow-Query Logging](#slow-query-logging), which has per-field thresholds.\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the warning on |\n| `rowCountThreshold` | `10000` | Warns when a query returns at least this many rows. `0` or less turns the warning off |\n\n## Authentication\n\nConfigure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\ntoken is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n            },\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key                       | Required | Default                          | Meaning                                                                          |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n| `resource`                | No       | derived from the request         | Public MCP endpoint URL for RFC 9728 metadata                                    |\n| `scopesSupported`         | No       | `[\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"]` | Scopes advertised to MCP clients                                                 |\n| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n\nWith neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\nbe fetched, and must be restarted after a key rotation. Each key verifies tokens signed with its own `alg`, or with the\ndefault for its type (`RS256` for `RSA`, `ES256` for `EC`, `EdDSA` for `OKP`, `HS256` for `oct`); tokens signed with\nany other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\na key whose `alg` is not listed is ignored, and a key without `alg` is used with each listed algorithm of its type (for\nexample `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\ndoes not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\nshapes are compile errors.\n\nThe MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\nwhen no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n\nFor a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n\nString values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\nserver starts.\n\nPackage layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\nlayer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n\n## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"jwtAuth\": {\n          \"pubSecKeys\": [{\n            \"algorithm\": \"HS256\",\n            \"buffer\": \"<base64-encoded signer secret>\"\n          }],\n          \"jwtOptions\": {\n            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n            \"expiresInSeconds\": 3600,\n            \"leeway\": 30\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor asymmetric algorithms such as `ES256`, `buffer` must contain the appropriately formatted public key (normally PEM).\n\n## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"oauthConfig\": {\n          \"oauth2Options\": {\n            \"site\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n            \"clientId\": \"my-client-id\"\n          },\n          \"authorizationServerUrl\": \"${AUTH_EXTERNAL_URL}\",\n          \"scopesSupported\": [\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`oauth2Options.site` is required. `clientId` defaults to `datasqrl-mcp`; `authorizationServerUrl`, `scopesSupported`, and `resource` are optional. Both deprecated blocks can be set together, but then a token that either one accepts is accepted; use one `auth` block that lists every issuer with its audiences instead.\n\n`resource` is the public URL of the MCP endpoint. Without it, the server derives the discovery `resource` and the `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"...\"` URL of `401` responses from each request (`X-Forwarded-Proto` or the request scheme, plus `Host`). With it, the discovery metadata returns it unchanged, and `resource_metadata` points to `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` on its origin. Set it behind a proxy that terminates TLS, so MCP clients always receive `https` URLs.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961200}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:41.203184+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03b27070a20f5cc7", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Flink Engine Configuration\n\n| Key          | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                                                              |\n|--------------|------------|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------| \n| `config`     | **object** | see below | Copied verbatim into the generated Flink SQL job (e.g. `\"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"5 s\"`). |\n\n```json5\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\", //or \"BATCH\" for batch pipelines\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 sec\" // so pipeline can advance when some sources are idle\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn STREAMING mode, DataSQRL sets `execution.checkpointing.interval: 30 s`, `execution.checkpointing.min-pause: 20 s`, and `table.exec.source.idle-timeout: 1 s` unless the package configuration sets them explicitly.\n\nFrequently configured options:\n* `execution.runtime-mode`: `BATCH` or `STREAMING` which controls how the pipeline is executed.\n* `taskmanager.memory.network.max`: How much memory to assign to the network buffers.\n\n\n## Common Flink Table Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure                                                                                              |\n|---|---|---|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enables mini-batch optimization to reduce state access overhead in aggregations and more efficient processing |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.allow-latency` | `0 ms` | `1s` \u2013 `5s` | Max wait time to accumulate a mini-batch; trade-off between latency and throughput. >0 when enabled.          |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.size` | `-1` | `1000` \u2013 `10000` | Max number of records per mini-batch bundle. >0 when enabled                                                  |\n| `table.exec.state.ttl` | `0 ms` (no expiry) | `1d`, `7d`, `24h` | Caps state size for unbounded joins and aggregations to prevent OOM                                           |\n| `table.optimizer.agg-phase-strategy` | `AUTO` | `TWO_PHASE`, `ONE_PHASE` | Forces two-phase (partial + final) aggregation for better parallelism                                         |\n| `table.optimizer.distinct-agg.split.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Splits `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` into two phases to avoid data skew                                               |\n| `table.optimizer.join-reorder-enabled` | `false` | `true` | Lets the optimizer reorder joins based on table statistics for better plans                                   |\n| `table.exec.source.idle-timeout` | `1 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL); `0 s` (disabled) for `test` | `30s`, `60s` | Marks idle sources so watermarks can advance when some partitions go quiet                                    |\n| `table.exec.sink.not-null-enforcer` | `ERROR` | `DROP` | Controls whether null-constraint violations throw errors or silently drop rows                                |\n| `table.exec.sink.upsert-materialize` | `AUTO` | `NONE`, `FORCE` | Controls materialization of upsert streams before writing to non-upsert sinks                                 |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-sub-plan-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabling can help when shared sub-plans cause unexpected state sharing                                       |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-source-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls whether identical source scans are shared across the plan                                            |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.buffer-capacity` | `100` | `10` \u2013 `1000` | Max number of in-flight async lookup requests for async lookup joins                                          |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.timeout` | `3 min` | `30s`, `1min` | Timeout for each async lookup request before it fails                                                         |\n| `table.exec.resource.default-parallelism` | `-1` (inherit) | `4`, `8`, `16` | Sets operator-level default parallelism for table/SQL jobs                                                    |\n| `table.dynamic-table-options.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Allows per-query connector option overrides via SQL hints (`/*+ OPTIONS(...) */`)                             |\n| `table.optimizer.multiple-input-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls chaining of multiple operators into a single task for reduced overhead                               |\n| `table.exec.rank.topn-cache-size` | `10000` | `1000` \u2013 `100000` | Cache size for TopN operator; larger cache reduces state reads but uses more heap                             |\n| `table.optimizer.bushy-join-reorder-threshold` | `12` | `4` \u2013 `20` | Max number of joins considered for bushy tree reordering                                                      |\n| `table.exec.window-agg.buffer-size-limit` | `100000` | `10000` \u2013 `500000` | Row buffer limit for window aggregation before spilling; affects memory vs. speed                             |\n\n## Common Flink Execution Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `execution.runtime-mode` | `STREAMING` | `BATCH` | Switched for bounded DataStream batch jobs |\n| `execution.checkpointing.interval` | `30 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `1min`, `5min` | Checkpoint frequency; trades recovery time and end-to-end latency of transactional sinks against checkpoint overhead |\n| `execution.checkpointing.mode` | `EXACTLY_ONCE` | `AT_LEAST_ONCE` | Relaxed for higher throughput when exactly-once isn't required |\n| `execution.checkpointing.timeout` | `10min` | `2min` \u2013 `30min` | Increased when checkpoints are slow due to large state or slow storage |\n| `execution.checkpointing.unaligned.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to speed up checkpointing under heavy backpressure |\n| `execution.checkpointing.min-pause` | `20 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `10s` \u2013 `1min` | Prevents checkpoint storms by enforcing a gap between checkpoints |\n| `execution.checkpointing.max-concurrent-checkpoints` | `1` | `1` | Rarely increased; relevant when unaligned checkpoints are disabled |\n| `execution.checkpointing.tolerable-failed-checkpoints` | `0` | `3` \u2013 `5` | Prevents job failure from transient checkpoint issues |\n| `taskmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `2gb` \u2013 `16gb` | Primary memory sizing knob for containerized deployments |\n| `taskmanager.memory.managed.fraction` | `0.4` | `0.3` \u2013 `0.6` | Adjusted when RocksDB needs more or less managed memory |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.fraction` | `0.1` | `0.05` \u2013 `0.2` | Increased for jobs with high shuffle/network traffic |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.max` | `infinite`; `800m` for `run` | `1gb`, `2gb`, `4gb` | Upper bound on network memory; set explicitly to cap buffer memory usage |\n| `jobmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `1gb` \u2013 `4gb` | Required sizing for containerized JobManager deployments |\n| `parallelism.default` | `1` | `4` \u2013 `256` | Sets job-wide default parallelism |\n| `pipeline.auto-watermark-interval` | `200ms` | `500ms`, `1s`, `5s` | Increased to reduce watermark overhead in high-throughput jobs |\n| `pipeline.max-parallelism` | `-1` | `128` \u2013 `4096` | Set explicitly to control key-group count and future rescaling |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.size` | `64mb` | `128mb` \u2013 `256mb` | Increased for write-heavy workloads to reduce flush frequency |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.count` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased alongside writebuffer.size for write throughput |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.thread.num` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased to parallelize RocksDB compaction and flush |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.checkpoint.transfer.thread.num` | `4` | `8` \u2013 `16` | Increased to speed up checkpoint upload/download |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.interval` | `100ms` | `0`, `200ms`, `500ms` | Lowered for latency, raised or disabled for throughput |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabled to maximize throughput at cost of latency |\n| `taskmanager.network.memory.buffer-debloat.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to automatically tune in-flight buffer sizes |\n| `heartbeat.timeout` | `50s` | `30s` \u2013 `120s` | Increased in flaky network or cloud environments |\n| `heartbeat.interval` | `10s` | `5s` \u2013 `30s` | Tuned alongside timeout for failure detection sensitivity |\n| `pekko.ask.timeout` | `10s` | `30s` \u2013 `60s` | Increased when RPC timeouts occur under load |\n| `pekko.framesize` | `10mb` | `50mb` \u2013 `200mb` | Increased when large serialized objects are passed via RPC |\n| `execution.checkpointing.aligned-checkpoint-timeout` | `0ms` | `30s` \u2013 `5min` | Set when using unaligned checkpoints with fallback to aligned |\n| `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state` | `false` | `true` | Set only for one restore from a savepoint whose plan had operators the new plan removed; drops their state (see below) |\n\n## Upgrading from a Savepoint\n\nFlink refuses to restore a savepoint that holds state for an operator the new `flink-compiled-plan.json` no longer has.\nCompare the compiled plans of both versions before upgrading. If stateful operators were removed, restore once with `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state: true` (`job.allowNonRestoredState: true` with the Flink Kubernetes Operator), then remove the option so it cannot hide an unexpected state mismatch later.\nThe state of the removed operators is dropped. For example, plans compiled by earlier DataSQRL versions write `STREAM` tables through `WatermarkTimestampAssigner` and `SinkMaterializer` operators that current plans no longer have; rows still held in their state are not written to the database after the restore.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961201}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:02:41.203593+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The configure-sqrl skill references vertx.md, flink.md, postgres.md for engine config, and says always read them before writing the config. Let me read vertx.md (for auth and slow-query logging) and flink.md. Also I need to know about the catalog include config: since catalog scripts contain `{{environment}}` and `{{#is_batch}}`, when we include via a package file, which one? lending uses `../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json` with config `is_batch: false`. But our inventory says declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`. Hmm the AGENTS addendum says top-level package. lending uses ontology-test-package.json. Let me check what ontology-test-package.json contains.\n\nLet me read the vertx.md, flink.md, and data-catalog ontology-test-package.json and ontology.sqrl (to see the consumer_loans import). Actually the main concern: the catalog's `consumer_loans-{{environment}}.sqrl` uses `{{environment}}` and that's the consumer's script.config, not the included module's. Let me check how lending imports: `IMPORT data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}}`. The `{{environment}}` is resolved from the consumer's script.config. Right. But the catalog scripts might have their own imports. Let me check ontology.sqrl and the consumer_loans.sqrl's imports (it had no imports, just CREATE TABLE). The `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` does `IMPORT consumer_loans.*;`. So the namespace resolution: `data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-test` \u2192 the file imports `consumer_loans.*` (relative within the catalog). That's fine.\n\nWait - but the included package determines the include root and the `config`. lending includes `ontology-test-package.json` with config `is_batch:false`. Let me look.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb03517c809cfcb5ab", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb037e7552b9ffa648", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/vertx.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb03b27070a20f5cc7", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/flink.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 6918, "output": 576, "cacheRead": 61568, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 444, "totalTokens": 69062, "cost": {"input": 0.0020754, "output": 0.0006912, "cacheRead": 0.000369408, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0031360079999999996}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510958849, "responseId": "dc1d9e432bff4a7d882b7ed2733a24d0-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2345, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03517c809cfcb5ab", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"1 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/ontology/\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510961200}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb037e7552b9ffa648", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Vert.x Engine Configuration\n\n| Key        | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                 |\n|------------|------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n\n## Server Configuration Overrides\n\nThe compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|-----|-------|\n| `httpServerOptions.port` | HTTP port (default `8888`). Keep `8888` for the `test` command, whose test runner connects to that port |\n| `corsHandlerOptions` | CORS policy. Defaults allow all origins; restrict `allowedOrigin`/`allowedOrigins` in production |\n| `poolOptions.maxSize` | PostgreSQL connection pool size |\n| `poolOptions.maxLifetime` | Seconds before a pooled PostgreSQL connection is closed and replaced (default `600`), so connections rebalance across replicas; `0` keeps them forever |\n| `pgConnectOptions.cachePreparedStatements` | Cache prepared statements per connection so each query is planned once (default `true`) |\n| `servletConfig` | Endpoint paths (`graphQLEndpoint`, `restEndpoint`, `mcpEndpoint`, `graphiQLEndpoint`), prefixed with the API version, e.g. `/v1/graphql` |\n| `publicGraphQLEndpointEnabled` | Set to `false` to disable the public GraphQL endpoint; REST and MCP endpoints keep working |\n| `onlyConfiguredGraphQLOperations` | Set to `true` to only accept the predefined API operations instead of arbitrary GraphQL queries |\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"corsHandlerOptions\": {\n          \"allowedOrigin\": \"https://app.example.com\",\n          \"allowCredentials\": true\n        },\n        \"poolOptions\": {\n          \"maxSize\": 16\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n\n## Slow-Query Logging\n\nEnable slow-query logging in every project that enables `vertx`. The server then logs each API request that takes longer than its threshold or that fails. A request's threshold is the lowest threshold among the fields it runs. A slow query then appears in the logs, not only as a client timeout.\n\nPut the block in the base config, so every deployed environment has it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the logging on |\n| `defaultThresholdMs` | `60000` | The threshold for fields without an entry in `thresholds` |\n| `thresholds` | `{}` | A threshold for requests that run this field, keyed `Type.field`, e.g. `{\"Query.OrdersByCustomer\": 5000}` or `{\"Customers.orders\": 5000}`. A request uses the lowest threshold among the fields it runs |\n| `alwaysLogErrors` | `true` | Also logs failed requests, such as a timeout while waiting for a database connection |\n| `maxLoggedTracesPerMinute` | `60` | Limits the log volume during an incident |\n| `maxFieldTraces` | `50` | Maximum number of query timings in one log line |\n\n### Choosing the threshold\n\nMake an educated guess for each threshold from how the API is used. The values in the example are a starting point, not a default to copy.\n\n- If the requirements state a latency target for the API, use that target. Otherwise, start from the consumer of the API: 1000 to 2000 ms for user interfaces and dashboards, 2000 to 5000 ms for service-to-service calls.\n- Look for heavy queries, such as a large aggregate. If their latency is much higher than that of the other queries, give each one its own entry in `thresholds`, keyed by its field (`Query.<Field>` for a table or table function, `<Type>.<field>` for a relationship). As a request uses the lowest threshold among its fields, the entry applies only when a request runs that field alone. Any other field in the same request, including a nested relationship field without its own entry, brings the lower threshold back.\n\n- Keep every threshold well below the client request timeout. The server writes the log line after a request ends, and it does not stop the request. A threshold below the timeout logs the requests that are slow but still succeed.\n\n## Large-Result Logging\n\nThe server logs a warning for each database query that returns many rows, before it converts the rows to JSON. Converting a very large result blocks the server, so the warning is written even when the pod is killed afterwards. The warning names the GraphQL field path, the row count, the elapsed time and the SQL (cut after 2000 characters), and never the parameter values. The elapsed time counts from issuing the query to receiving its rows, including any wait for a pool connection, so it is not the database time. Also when this warning is off, the server logs an error for a failed query, with its field path and SQL, and for rows it cannot convert to JSON, with the field path.\n\nThis warning is off by default and only covers large results. Slow requests are logged by [Slow-Query Logging](#slow-query-logging), which has per-field thresholds.\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the warning on |\n| `rowCountThreshold` | `10000` | Warns when a query returns at least this many rows. `0` or less turns the warning off |\n\n## Authentication\n\nConfigure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\ntoken is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n            },\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key                       | Required | Default                          | Meaning                                                                          |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n| `resource`                | No       | derived from the request         | Public MCP endpoint URL for RFC 9728 metadata                                    |\n| `scopesSupported`         | No       | `[\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"]` | Scopes advertised to MCP clients                                                 |\n| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n\nWith neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\nbe fetched, and must be restarted after a key rotation. Each key verifies tokens signed with its own `alg`, or with the\ndefault for its type (`RS256` for `RSA`, `ES256` for `EC`, `EdDSA` for `OKP`, `HS256` for `oct`); tokens signed with\nany other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\na key whose `alg` is not listed is ignored, and a key without `alg` is used with each listed algorithm of its type (for\nexample `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\ndoes not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\nshapes are compile errors.\n\nThe MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\nwhen no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n\nFor a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n\nString values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\nserver starts.\n\nPackage layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\nlayer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n\n## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"jwtAuth\": {\n          \"pubSecKeys\": [{\n            \"algorithm\": \"HS256\",\n            \"buffer\": \"<base64-encoded signer secret>\"\n          }],\n          \"jwtOptions\": {\n            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n            \"expiresInSeconds\": 3600,\n            \"leeway\": 30\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor asymmetric algorithms such as `ES256`, `buffer` must contain the appropriately formatted public key (normally PEM).\n\n## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"oauthConfig\": {\n          \"oauth2Options\": {\n            \"site\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n            \"clientId\": \"my-client-id\"\n          },\n          \"authorizationServerUrl\": \"${AUTH_EXTERNAL_URL}\",\n          \"scopesSupported\": [\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`oauth2Options.site` is required. `clientId` defaults to `datasqrl-mcp`; `authorizationServerUrl`, `scopesSupported`, and `resource` are optional. Both deprecated blocks can be set together, but then a token that either one accepts is accepted; use one `auth` block that lists every issuer with its audiences instead.\n\n`resource` is the public URL of the MCP endpoint. Without it, the server derives the discovery `resource` and the `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"...\"` URL of `401` responses from each request (`X-Forwarded-Proto` or the request scheme, plus `Host`). With it, the discovery metadata returns it unchanged, and `resource_metadata` points to `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` on its origin. Set it behind a proxy that terminates TLS, so MCP clients always receive `https` URLs.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961200}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03b27070a20f5cc7", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Flink Engine Configuration\n\n| Key          | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                                                              |\n|--------------|------------|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------| \n| `config`     | **object** | see below | Copied verbatim into the generated Flink SQL job (e.g. `\"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"5 s\"`). |\n\n```json5\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\", //or \"BATCH\" for batch pipelines\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 sec\" // so pipeline can advance when some sources are idle\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn STREAMING mode, DataSQRL sets `execution.checkpointing.interval: 30 s`, `execution.checkpointing.min-pause: 20 s`, and `table.exec.source.idle-timeout: 1 s` unless the package configuration sets them explicitly.\n\nFrequently configured options:\n* `execution.runtime-mode`: `BATCH` or `STREAMING` which controls how the pipeline is executed.\n* `taskmanager.memory.network.max`: How much memory to assign to the network buffers.\n\n\n## Common Flink Table Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure                                                                                              |\n|---|---|---|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enables mini-batch optimization to reduce state access overhead in aggregations and more efficient processing |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.allow-latency` | `0 ms` | `1s` \u2013 `5s` | Max wait time to accumulate a mini-batch; trade-off between latency and throughput. >0 when enabled.          |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.size` | `-1` | `1000` \u2013 `10000` | Max number of records per mini-batch bundle. >0 when enabled                                                  |\n| `table.exec.state.ttl` | `0 ms` (no expiry) | `1d`, `7d`, `24h` | Caps state size for unbounded joins and aggregations to prevent OOM                                           |\n| `table.optimizer.agg-phase-strategy` | `AUTO` | `TWO_PHASE`, `ONE_PHASE` | Forces two-phase (partial + final) aggregation for better parallelism                                         |\n| `table.optimizer.distinct-agg.split.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Splits `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` into two phases to avoid data skew                                               |\n| `table.optimizer.join-reorder-enabled` | `false` | `true` | Lets the optimizer reorder joins based on table statistics for better plans                                   |\n| `table.exec.source.idle-timeout` | `1 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL); `0 s` (disabled) for `test` | `30s`, `60s` | Marks idle sources so watermarks can advance when some partitions go quiet                                    |\n| `table.exec.sink.not-null-enforcer` | `ERROR` | `DROP` | Controls whether null-constraint violations throw errors or silently drop rows                                |\n| `table.exec.sink.upsert-materialize` | `AUTO` | `NONE`, `FORCE` | Controls materialization of upsert streams before writing to non-upsert sinks                                 |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-sub-plan-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabling can help when shared sub-plans cause unexpected state sharing                                       |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-source-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls whether identical source scans are shared across the plan                                            |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.buffer-capacity` | `100` | `10` \u2013 `1000` | Max number of in-flight async lookup requests for async lookup joins                                          |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.timeout` | `3 min` | `30s`, `1min` | Timeout for each async lookup request before it fails                                                         |\n| `table.exec.resource.default-parallelism` | `-1` (inherit) | `4`, `8`, `16` | Sets operator-level default parallelism for table/SQL jobs                                                    |\n| `table.dynamic-table-options.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Allows per-query connector option overrides via SQL hints (`/*+ OPTIONS(...) */`)                             |\n| `table.optimizer.multiple-input-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls chaining of multiple operators into a single task for reduced overhead                               |\n| `table.exec.rank.topn-cache-size` | `10000` | `1000` \u2013 `100000` | Cache size for TopN operator; larger cache reduces state reads but uses more heap                             |\n| `table.optimizer.bushy-join-reorder-threshold` | `12` | `4` \u2013 `20` | Max number of joins considered for bushy tree reordering                                                      |\n| `table.exec.window-agg.buffer-size-limit` | `100000` | `10000` \u2013 `500000` | Row buffer limit for window aggregation before spilling; affects memory vs. speed                             |\n\n## Common Flink Execution Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `execution.runtime-mode` | `STREAMING` | `BATCH` | Switched for bounded DataStream batch jobs |\n| `execution.checkpointing.interval` | `30 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `1min`, `5min` | Checkpoint frequency; trades recovery time and end-to-end latency of transactional sinks against checkpoint overhead |\n| `execution.checkpointing.mode` | `EXACTLY_ONCE` | `AT_LEAST_ONCE` | Relaxed for higher throughput when exactly-once isn't required |\n| `execution.checkpointing.timeout` | `10min` | `2min` \u2013 `30min` | Increased when checkpoints are slow due to large state or slow storage |\n| `execution.checkpointing.unaligned.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to speed up checkpointing under heavy backpressure |\n| `execution.checkpointing.min-pause` | `20 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `10s` \u2013 `1min` | Prevents checkpoint storms by enforcing a gap between checkpoints |\n| `execution.checkpointing.max-concurrent-checkpoints` | `1` | `1` | Rarely increased; relevant when unaligned checkpoints are disabled |\n| `execution.checkpointing.tolerable-failed-checkpoints` | `0` | `3` \u2013 `5` | Prevents job failure from transient checkpoint issues |\n| `taskmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `2gb` \u2013 `16gb` | Primary memory sizing knob for containerized deployments |\n| `taskmanager.memory.managed.fraction` | `0.4` | `0.3` \u2013 `0.6` | Adjusted when RocksDB needs more or less managed memory |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.fraction` | `0.1` | `0.05` \u2013 `0.2` | Increased for jobs with high shuffle/network traffic |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.max` | `infinite`; `800m` for `run` | `1gb`, `2gb`, `4gb` | Upper bound on network memory; set explicitly to cap buffer memory usage |\n| `jobmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `1gb` \u2013 `4gb` | Required sizing for containerized JobManager deployments |\n| `parallelism.default` | `1` | `4` \u2013 `256` | Sets job-wide default parallelism |\n| `pipeline.auto-watermark-interval` | `200ms` | `500ms`, `1s`, `5s` | Increased to reduce watermark overhead in high-throughput jobs |\n| `pipeline.max-parallelism` | `-1` | `128` \u2013 `4096` | Set explicitly to control key-group count and future rescaling |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.size` | `64mb` | `128mb` \u2013 `256mb` | Increased for write-heavy workloads to reduce flush frequency |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.count` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased alongside writebuffer.size for write throughput |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.thread.num` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased to parallelize RocksDB compaction and flush |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.checkpoint.transfer.thread.num` | `4` | `8` \u2013 `16` | Increased to speed up checkpoint upload/download |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.interval` | `100ms` | `0`, `200ms`, `500ms` | Lowered for latency, raised or disabled for throughput |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabled to maximize throughput at cost of latency |\n| `taskmanager.network.memory.buffer-debloat.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to automatically tune in-flight buffer sizes |\n| `heartbeat.timeout` | `50s` | `30s` \u2013 `120s` | Increased in flaky network or cloud environments |\n| `heartbeat.interval` | `10s` | `5s` \u2013 `30s` | Tuned alongside timeout for failure detection sensitivity |\n| `pekko.ask.timeout` | `10s` | `30s` \u2013 `60s` | Increased when RPC timeouts occur under load |\n| `pekko.framesize` | `10mb` | `50mb` \u2013 `200mb` | Increased when large serialized objects are passed via RPC |\n| `execution.checkpointing.aligned-checkpoint-timeout` | `0ms` | `30s` \u2013 `5min` | Set when using unaligned checkpoints with fallback to aligned |\n| `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state` | `false` | `true` | Set only for one restore from a savepoint whose plan had operators the new plan removed; drops their state (see below) |\n\n## Upgrading from a Savepoint\n\nFlink refuses to restore a savepoint that holds state for an operator the new `flink-compiled-plan.json` no longer has.\nCompare the compiled plans of both versions before upgrading. If stateful operators were removed, restore once with `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state: true` (`job.allowNonRestoredState: true` with the Flink Kubernetes Operator), then remove the option so it cannot hide an unexpected state mismatch later.\nThe state of the removed operators is dropped. For example, plans compiled by earlier DataSQRL versions write `STREAM` tables through `WatermarkTimestampAssigner` and `SinkMaterializer` operators that current plans no longer have; rows still held in their state are not written to the database after the restore.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961201}]}
