ons expose it with auth.\n\nBut naming: If the internal table is `LoanPayments` (no underscore, `/*+no_query*/`), then the function can't also be `LoanPayments`. So the function would be named differently, e.g. `LoanPaymentsByLoan` or `GetLoanPayments`. But acceptance criteria explicitly uses `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` as a query. Hmm.\n\nWait, acceptance says \"*(FR-5)* `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` includes payment `LPMT-046`\". This is describing the lookup. It might be fine to name the GraphQL query `LoanPayments`. So we want the function named `LoanPayments`.\n\nResolution: Make the underlying tables **internal with `_` prefix** (`_LoanPayments`, `_LoanPaymentSummary`), and define functions named `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. The plan's \"table\" language is loose. But the plan step 4 says \"keeping the exact table/type names\". The type names will be `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary` in the custom schema. This satisfies the API contract. The internal table names being underscored is fine per Scope (they're internal).\n\nBut the Data Quality plan / type... Also FR-2 acceptance: \"LoanPayments has one row per payment_id, and LoanPaymentSummary has one row per loan_id.\" These are tested via table tests? Step 10 says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria\". A table test (`/*+test*/`) must select from a table that's in the interface. If `_LoanPayments` is hidden, then the test table can be a separate public table. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Step 10 wants an inferred-schema test package with `/*+test*/` tables. The inferred schema exposes tables via the interface. But our custom schema is configured for the main API. The test runner with `use-inferred-schema: true` infers the schema from the SQRL script, exposing all non-hidden tables. So if `_LoanPayments` is hidden, the inferred schema won't expose it, and a `/*+test*/` table selecting from it... Actually `/*+test*/` tables are themselves table definitions; the test runner executes them. They can select from hidden tables. The `/*+test*/` table just needs to be defined; it becomes an endpoint in the inferred schema. So:\n\n```\n/*+test */\nLoanPaymentsIntegrity_Test := SELECT COUNT(*) AS ... FROM _LoanPayments ...\n```\n\nBut the main script has a custom schema configured. Does an inferred-schema test run still work? The test config: `use-inferred-schema: true` means tests run against the inferred schema, ignoring the custom schema, and includes test tables. This is what the plan step 10 wants: a *separate* inferred-schema table-test package. Hmm, \"package\" = another test package overlay? The plan says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package\". Maybe a second set of configs? Let me re-read step 10: \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria ... [deps: 9]\".\n\nHmm, \"package\" might mean a separate `*-test-package.json` that sets `use-inferred-schema: true`. But the main test package for API tests should set `use-inferred-schema: false`. Can one project have two test packages? Yes: `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` (custom schema) and `loan-payments-integrity-test-package.json` (inferred). Or one test package with `use-inferred-schema: true` would run both API tests AND table tests; but then the API tests would run against inferred schema. Is that a problem? The plan step 11 says \"Add API tests in the custom-schema test package\". So separate.\n\nActually, re-reading: DataSQRL test runner: `use-inferred-schema` default true. With true, the test runner uses the inferred schema for API tests (ignores custom), but the `/*+test*/` tables are also part of the inferred schema and executed. So a single test run with use-inferred-schema true could run API test queries too but against inferred schema. The plan wants API tests against the **custom schema** because customer_id is injected from JWT and the inferred schema would expose the function args? Actually the functions have the claim arg, and inferred schema would expose `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` without customer_id (metadata claim args excluded). Hmm, actually inferred schema automatically handles auth metadata. So maybe both can work with inferred. But the requirement says custom schema to fix type names and exclude customer_id. The plan explicitly separates.\n\nLet me follow the plan:\n- Main API test package: `loan-payments-test-package.json` with `use-inferred-schema: false`, tests folder `loan-payments-api/tests/`. This is the custom-schema test package.\n- Integrity test package: `loan-payments-integrity-test-package.json` with `use-inferred-schema: true` (default), tests folder maybe `loan-payments-api/integrity-tests/`? Actually `/*+test*/` table tests don't need a test folder; they're executed automatically. The `test-folder` is for GraphQL query files. So the integrity package can have no test-folder, just snapshots and the `/*+test*/` tables.\n\nWait, but there's only one snapshot folder per test package. The two packages can share snapshot folder or have separate. Let me use `snapshots/loan-payments/` for both? The `/*+test*/` snapshots go there. The API tests go there too. Filenames distinct. Should be fine. But to be safe use separate folders? The plan says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package\". I'll give it a distinct snapshot folder `snapshots/loan-payments-integrity/`.\n\nHmm, but the test runner's `test-folder` default is `./tests`. If integrity package has no test-folder, it defaults to `./tests` which doesn't exist \u2014 might error. Let me set `test-folder` to a directory with an empty/ignored file, or set `snapshot-folder`. Actually the `/*+test*/` tables are executed regardless of test-folder. Let me check test-sqrl skill for the separate package pattern (library_loans example). Let me read test-sqrl skill."}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:03:08.420895+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "source": "agent", "kind": "tool", "tool": "read", "target": "/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md", "message": "read /opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:03:08.422052+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb6daa7ba183adfe60", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: test-sqrl\ndescription: Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n---\n\nAlways implement test coverage for all major features or complex data transformations implemented in SQRL.\nWithout a custom GraphQL schema, prefer table tests. Use API tests for complex interaction patterns (mutation followed by query/subscription) or authorization.\nWith a custom GraphQL schema, follow [Projects with a Custom GraphQL Schema](#projects-with-a-custom-graphql-schema).\n\n# Writing Tests\n\n## Test Types\n\nDataSQRL supports two snapshot test types (run via `test` command):\n1. **Table tests**: SQRL tables annotated with `/*+test */` hint\n2. **API tests**: GraphQL operations (queries/mutations/subscriptions) in configured test folder.\n\nBoth write snapshots to the configured snapshot folder and pass when the new output is identical to the stored snapshot \u2014 see [Snapshot Lifecycle](#snapshot-lifecycle) under Running Tests.\n\n## Table Tests\n\nAdd `/*+test */` hint to any table definition:\n\n```sql\n-- Tests that the query returns an identical snapshot of data\n/*+test */\nCustomerRetrievalTest := SELECT customerid, name, email FROM Customer\n  WHERE name = 'John' ORDER BY customerid;\n\n-- Tests an invariant and returns failing records\n/*+test(no_rows) */\nInvalidCustomersTest := SELECT * FROM Customer\n  WHERE name = '' OR email IS NULL;\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n- Use `/*+test(no_rows) */` hint to test invariants\n- Snapshot results must have deterministic order (use `ORDER BY`)\n- Only select predictable columns and exclude non-deterministic ones (e.g. columns computed via `rand()` or `now()`)\n- Table tests do not include relationships\n- Every column in `ORDER BY` must also appear in `SELECT`. To sort by a column without including it in snapshot output, alias it with a `_` prefix: `event_time AS _event_time`. Omitting an ORDER BY column from SELECT causes: `All sort columns must be part of the SELECT clause for table definitions, missing: [col]`.\n\n## API Tests\n\nPlace `.graphql` files in the configured test folder, **one operation definition per file**. The file name (without extension) is the test name and the snapshot name: every `query`/`mutation`/`subscription` block in a file is registered under that name, so a second block in the same file is compared against the first one's snapshot and fails on every run. One block may still query several root fields at once (use aliases: `byOrg: alerts(...) {...}  byDeployment: alerts(...) {...}`); that is a single operation and a single snapshot.\n\n`GetAllCustomers.graphql`:\n```graphql\nquery GetAllCustomers {\n  Customer { customerid name email }\n}\n```\n\n`AddCustomer.graphql`:\n```graphql\nmutation AddCustomer {\n  Customer(event: {customerid: 123, email: \"test@example.com\", name: \"Test\"}) {\n    customerid\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Test Execution Order:**\n1. Subscribe to all subscriptions (must be triggered by mutation)\n2. Execute mutations sequentially (alphabetical filename order) with `mutation-delay-sec` between\n3. Wait for configured timeout, then drain the Flink job\n4. Execute queries\n5. Collect subscription results\n\n**Testing SUBSCRIBE tables:**\nIf your implementation adds or modifies a `SUBSCRIBE` statement, complete all three steps before compiling:\n1. Add `kafka` to `enabled-engines` in the test `*-package.json` \u2014 subscriptions are backed by Kafka topics even in test mode. Omitting kafka produces BUILD SUCCESS but the Subscription type will be absent from the generated schema.\n2. Create a `.graphql` API test file in the test folder containing the subscription query.\n3. Ensure test data includes records that trigger the subscription.\n\nIf no mutation exists to trigger the subscription, document why testing is not feasible and add a table test validating the subscription's data source instead.\n\n## Test Data Sources\n\n**Setup Pattern:**\nIf project does not currently follow recommended setup:\n1. Move the production `CREATE TABLE` statements to `connectors/sources-prod.sqrl`\n2. Import the connector file through a template variable: `IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources;`\n3. Set the variable per environment in the `<env>-package.json` overlays (invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill, Base Config and Environment Overlays).\n4. Create `connectors/sources-test.sqrl` with the same table names with static test data (invoke the `/manage-connector` skill, Connector Definitions)\n5. Create test config file (See /configure-sqrl` skill)\n\n**Test Source Definition:**\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE MyTable (\n    -- Same columns as prod, but METADATA \u2192 regular columns with static values\n    my_timestamp TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3),\n    WATERMARK FOR `my_timestamp` AS `my_timestamp` - INTERVAL '1' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'format' = 'flexible-json',\n    'flexible-json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/my_table.jsonl',\n    'connector' = 'filesystem'\n);\n```\n\nThe `timestamp-format.standard` option is required for ISO-8601 timestamps (`2024-01-01T10:00:00.000Z`) in the data file; the format's default is `SQL` (`2024-01-01 10:00:00.000`), and timestamps in the other notation fail to parse.\n\n**Test Data Guidelines:**\n- Use realistic data covering relationships and joins\n- Cover edge cases and failure scenarios\n- For any filter that excludes records (e.g. `enabled = false`, reading out of bounds), include at least one record that should be excluded and verify it is absent from the output snapshot. Tests containing only passing records cannot detect broken filter logic.\n- Use jsonl for data with `flexible-json` format in connector\n- For CSV sources, the header data only lives in the schema: the column names go into the `CREATE TABLE` statement, and the CSV file contains only data rows. Write CSV fixtures without a header row and read them with `'format' = 'csv'`. Flink's `csv` format reads every line as data, so a header row becomes a bad record, and the compiler warns `CSV file [...] seems to start with a header row`. If a provided CSV file starts with a header row, note the column names first, then remove the line: `tail -n +2 data.csv > data.csv.tmp && mv data.csv.tmp data.csv`, or for a compressed file `zcat data.csv.gz | tail -n +2 | gzip > data.csv.gz.tmp && mv data.csv.gz.tmp data.csv.gz`. Declare the columns in the `CREATE TABLE` statement in the order of the removed header. CSV files have no schema discovery, so `` LIKE `data.csv` `` fails at compile time (see the `/manage-connector` skill, `formats/csv.md`).\n- Set static event timestamps for determinism, make sure: a) timestamps are monotonically increasing and b) align across multiple test datasets\n- Convert METADATA columns to regular columns with static values\n- Convert non-deterministic columns (e.g., `NOW()`) to regular columns with static values\n- Use data files for streaming tests as well. A `datagen` source fills random fields and timestamps with new values on every run, so snapshot tests over them fail.\n- Reuse an existing test source or connector configuration when it already supplies the required data and semantics.\n\n**Critical Test Setup:**\n- Before taking snapshots, the `test` command drains the Flink job, which advances all watermarks to the end. Fixtures therefore need no dummy sentinel records to close windows or temporal joins.\n- Keep `table.exec.source.idle-timeout` at `\"0 s\"` in the test package, because the test runner disables idleness. If a base package sets it, override it with `\"0 s\"` in the test overlay. With idleness enabled, the drain can drop the last events behind chained temporal joins.\n- The `test` command runs in UTC, so snapshots show `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` values and time-zone-dependent results (e.g. casting a `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` to a string) in UTC. Write fixture timestamps with an explicit offset (e.g. `2025-10-13T12:11:26.575Z`) so they are independent of the time zone. When the logic depends on a different session time zone, set `table.local-time-zone` in the Flink configuration of the package (`/configure-sqrl` skill) instead of changing the container's `TZ`.\n\n## Authentication Testing\n\nConfigure custom headers per query using `.properties` file (same name as `.graphql` file):\n```text\nAuthorization: Bearer XYZ\n```\n\nA script that reads a claim (`METADATA FROM 'auth.<claim>'`) compiles only with an auth provider in `engines.vertx.config`, and the `test` command compiles the test configuration. Put the `auth` block in the `<project>-shared-package.json` base, or in the `<project>-test-package.json` overlay (invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill, Authentication in `vertx.md`). With the `auth` block only in the `<project>-prod-package.json` overlay, the `test` command fails with `The script reads JWT claims (...), but engines.vertx.config has no auth provider.` To generate a test token that matches the `auth` block, invoke the `/design-api` skill (Generating Tokens in `authorization.md`).\n\n## Projects with a Custom GraphQL Schema\n\nA project has a custom schema when its package configuration names a schema file under `script.api.<version>.schema` or `script.graphql`.\n`sqrl test` starts one API server with one schema. `test-runner.use-inferred-schema` in the test package chooses that schema:\n\n| `use-inferred-schema` | Schema of the test server | Table tests (`/*+test*/` tables in the `.sqrl` file) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `true` (default) | the inferred schema. The custom schema is not read | run |\n| `false` | the custom schema | do not run, and `sqrl test` reports no message |\n\nNotes:\n- API tests (the `.graphql` files in `test-runner.test-folder`) run with `use-inferred-schema` both values.\n- With `use-inferred-schema=true`, an API test gives a wrong result where the two schemas differ: a request with an enum value, a renamed argument or a custom type fails validation, and a field that the custom schema hides still returns data.\n\n### Choose the kind of test\n\nA custom schema holds changes that matter to the project, so the tests check the custom schema. Choose the tests by the condition that applies to the project:\n\n| Condition | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| **Default.** Every project with a custom schema, with or without mutations | Only API tests, in `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json`. No `/*+test*/` table in the `.sqrl` file. |\n| **Exception.** The requirements or the plan ask for a test that must not be in the API, for example a data integrity check or a validation query on data that users must not see | API tests in `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json`, and table tests for the requested checks in `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`. Follow [Table tests in a second test package](#table-tests-in-a-second-test-package). |\n\nIn both cases:\n\n- Write each test that reads data the API returns as an API test, also when the project already has a `/*+test*/` table for it.\n- Keep API tests and table tests in separate test packages, because each test package uses one schema (see the table above).\n- Add a field to the custom schema only when users need that field. A field that exists only for a test is part of the deployed API, so every user can read that data. When the requirements or the plan ask for a test on such data, follow the exception above.\n\n### Rules for every project with a custom schema\n\n1. Set `\"use-inferred-schema\": false` in the test package that runs the API tests, so that the API tests check the custom schema. Name it `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json`, or `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json` when the (sub-)project also requires separate table tests (`<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`).\n2. Write each API test with the fields, arguments, enum values and types of the custom schema. For example, write an enum value without quotes (`genre: fantasy`). The test server rejects a request that does not fit the custom schema, and `sqrl test` saves that error as the expected result and still reports success.\n3. Keep exactly one schema file. A copy of the schema for tests looks like a way to run table tests with the custom schema, but the tests then read only the copy. An error in the real schema then stays hidden from the tests, and every schema change must be made in two files.\n\n### Table tests in a second test package\n\nFollow these steps only for the exception in [Choose the kind of test](#choose-the-kind-of-test): the requirements or the plan ask for a test that must not be in the API. In the default case, the project has one test package and no table tests, so skip this section.\n\n1. Rename `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json` to `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json`, so that each name says which tests it runs. Update its suite line in `run-tests.sh` such that each test exists.\n2. Create `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json` with `\"use-inferred-schema\": true`, its own `snapshot-folder`, and a `test-folder` that holds no `.graphql` file. A folder that does not exist works.\n3. In `run-tests.sh`, add `<(sub-)project>_table_test` to `SUBPROJECTS`, and add the suite line `<(sub-)project>_table_test/test) echo \"<(sub-)project>-shared-package.json <(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json\" ;;`.\n4. If the project has no mutation table, stop here. The table tests read the same test data files as the API tests.\n5. If the project has a mutation table, the table tests need the test data without the mutations, because the mutation test files do not fit the inferred schema:\n   1. Set `\"script\": { \"config\": { \"environment\": \"table_test\" } }` in `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`.\n   2. Create `connectors/sources_table_test.sqrl`. Define each mutation table there as a filesystem table with the same name and columns.\n   3. Create a `.jsonl` file for each of these tables with the same records as the mutation test files.\n\nSee `/opt/datasqrl-examples/support_tickets/` for the default: a mutation table and API tests only.\nSee `/opt/datasqrl-examples/library_loans/` for a mutation table, API tests, and table tests in a second test package.\n\n## Test Configuration\n\n```json5\n{\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/myproject/\", // Snapshots output directory (default: \"./snapshots\")\n    \"test-folder\": \"api/tests/\",               // Directory containing test GraphQL queries (default: \"./tests\")\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,               // true: tests use the inferred schema and ignore a configured schema. false: tests use the schema under \"script.api.<version>.schema\" or \"script.graphql\" (default: true)\n    \"delay-sec\": 30,                           // Max wait in sec for the job to finish before taking snapshots; ends early when the job terminates. -1 = wait until all operators are idle and required-checkpoints completed (streaming) or until the job completes (batch) (default: 30)\n    \"mutation-delay-sec\": 0,                   // Pause(s) between mutation queries (default: 0)\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 0,                 // Minimum completed Flink checkpoints before taking snapshots (requires delay-sec = -1)\n    \"create-topics\": [\"topic1\", \"topic2\"],     // Kafka topics to create before tests start\n    \"headers\": {                               // Any HTTP headers to add during the test execution. For example, JWT auth header\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <token>\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n# Running Tests\n\n## Project Test Script\n\nEvery project root must hold a test runner script, **`run-tests.sh`**, that is the entry point for the project's tests: you as the code agent run it, CI runs it and developer can run it. It owns the whole test process (setup, every suite, teardown) and returns exit code 0 ONLY if all tests pass. It also *reports* what the project contains.\n\nExamples:\n```bash\n./run-tests.sh                                          # every suite, env=test default\n./run-tests.sh fraud_store                              # only the named sub-project(s)\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev                      # compiles the dev config\n./run-tests.sh --list-envs                              # Example output: test dev prod\n./run-tests.sh --list-subprojects                       # Example output: fraud_store fraud_training\n./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev   # print what would run, run nothing\n```\n\n### The script contract\n\n- **No arguments runs every suite**; sub-project names select suites.\n- **Sub-project names are single words**: letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` and nothing else. They are carried in a space-separated list and reported one per line as `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project-name> <verb> <config...> -b <build-dir>`, so a name containing a space is read as two names and the report cannot be parsed. `fraud_store` and `fraud-store` are fine; `fraud store` is not.\n- **Setup and teardown live in the script** and run on every invocation, e.g. test-data generation or download (data too large for git), fixture start, cleanup. This is the only place complex test setup can live. Guard them with `$LIST` so listing stays side-effect free.\n- Every selected suite runs even after one fails, and the script exits non-zero when any of them failed.\n- **Declare, do not detect.** `SUBPROJECTS`, `ENVS` and the `suite` table are written out; a name or environment the project does not have is an error (exit 2), never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n- **Declare every environment.** `ENVS` names every environment that has a `<sub-project>-<env>-package.json` overlay, and the `suite` table has a line for each of them. The verification compile and the README commands use only the environments that `ENVS` names.\n- **The `suite` table names configs literally**, in merge order \u2014 base first, environment config last, because a later configuration file overrides an earlier one.\n- **Every sub-project gets its own build folder.** Every invocation (compile or test) carries\n  `-b <sub-project>`, so one suite never overwrites a sibling's artifacts and after a single run\n  every sub-project's build is still on disk. The flag names a **subfolder of `build/`**: `-b fraud_store` writes to `<project-root>/build/fraud_store`.\n- **`-b` requires `-r`.** Pointing `-b` inside `build/` makes the compiler resolve `script.main`\n  against the wrong root unless the project root is given too: `-r .` for a project that is the\n  mount root, `-r <project-dir>` when it sits inside a repository. With `-r`, config paths are\n  relative to that root and carry no prefix.\n- **Keep it in sync**: a new sub-project means a new entry in `SUBPROJECTS` and a `suite` line per environment it supports.\n- `chmod +x run-tests.sh` after writing it.\n\n**Scope:** While iterating, pass the names of the sub-projects you are working on as arguments of `run-tests.sh` so only their suites run. Your run must cover everything that changed: every sub-project newly generated or edited, plus every sub-project affected by an edit to shared code or configuration. Before running the script, list the generated, edited and affected sub-projects, and pass exactly that list as the arguments.\n\n**DataSQRL CLI version.**  Currently used DataSQRL CLI version: 0.12.5. The code agent container runs specific DataSQRL version. The test shell scrip pins `SQRL_VERSION` to the same default so a CI or local run compiles and snapshots with the version the project was built against.\n\n### The script\n\nThis skill comes with a [run-tests.sh](run-tests.sh) template that follows the contract above. Copy it into the project root and make the copied file executable.\n\nThen edit only the three blocks marked `EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT`: the `ENVS` and `SUBPROJECTS` declarations, the `suite` table, and the optional setup and teardown hooks. Keep everything else unchanged.\n\nAfter completing the script, verify the script. First run `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations` and check that every sub-project appears with the configs you expect. Then run `./run-tests.sh` for only the sub-projects you changed (see **Scope** above).\n\nFor an example with two sub-projects (one of them has no prod config), see `/opt/datasqrl-examples/feature_store/run-tests.sh`.\n\n## One Invocation per Test Package\n\nA project folder may hold several **sub-projects** (deployments), each with its own\n`*-test-package.json`. The `suite` table in `run-tests.sh` carries one entry per\n(sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order:\n\n```bash\n    <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n```\n\nThe driver runs one `cmd.sh` invocation per selected sub-project per selected environment from those entries.\n\n- Multiple package arguments to one invocation always result in a merged configuration (last file overwrites). That is what makes base + overlay work.\n- Drop the `<project>-shared-package.json` argument only for a project that has no base config. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Base Config and Environment Overlays, Sub-projects) while setting the config files.\n- Listing two different sub-projects' test packages in one invocation merges them into one configuration and fails the build.\n- Keep the `-r <root> \u2026 -b <sub-project>` form on every direct invocation too, matching the suite's build folder, so a hand-run compile lands where the framework and the next suite run expect it.\n- Give each test package its own `test-runner.snapshot-folder`, set in its `-test-package.json` overlay (for example `snapshots/fraud_store/`, `snapshots/fraud_training/`); sharing one folder makes the runs clobber each other's snapshots.\n\n**Project inside a repository.** Package paths resolve at the compiler's mount root. When the project is a subdirectory of the repository (the compiler mounts the repository root), every package path carries the project directory as a prefix. The suite lines pick that prefix up from `SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX` (`${P}`), and the `sqrl()` function adds `-r <project-dir>` on the CI/local docker path. As a fallback, when you call `/opt/agent/cmd.sh` directly, qualify the paths yourself. The same form applies to `compile` and `run`:\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r {your-project-dir} {project}-shared-package.json {project}-test-package.json -b {sub-project}\n# equivalently\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . {project}-shared-package.json {project}-test-package.json -b {sub-project}   # project IS the mount root\n```\n\nThe `-b` value stays **un-prefixed**: it is resolved against the root given by `-r`, so `-r {your-project-dir} \u2026 -b x` writes `{your-project-dir}/build/x`.\n\nWhen the project *is* the repository root, the plain `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test {package.json}` form applies.\n\n**Direct CLI fallback.** When the script is broken, fix it. As a fallback, call `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r <root> <base config> <overlay config> -b <sub-project>`; `run-tests.sh` stays the command of record and the layering rules above apply to the direct call unchanged.\n\nExample: `/opt/datasqrl-examples/feature_store/` ships two sub-projects (deployments) over one shared feature computation (`fraud_features.sqrl`): a realtime feature store with an API (`fraud_store`) and a batch training run (`fraud_training`).\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud_store-shared-package.json fraud_store-test-package.json -b fraud_store\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud_training-shared-package.json fraud_training-test-package.json -b fraud_training\n```\n\n**Edge case: `-r` must match what is mounted at `/workspace`.**: \n* Wrong `-r`: If a compile fails with:\n\n```\nProject root does not exist or not a directory: /workspace/<path>\n```\n\nthe `-r` value points at the wrong place. `-r` is resolved against `/workspace`, and the mount at `/workspace` is fixed for the whole run, so the fix is to correct `-r`:\n\n- The project itself is mounted at `/workspace` (single-project run): use `-r .` and pass the package files without a prefix.\n- The repository is mounted at `/workspace` (the project sits in a subdirectory of the repo): use `-r <project-dir>` or prefix every package path with `<project-dir>/` too.\n\nThis error comes from a direct `cmd.sh` call. `run-tests.sh` script derives the root from `SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX` when it is provided.\n\n\n## Snapshot Lifecycle\n\n- **The first run of a new test always reports BUILD FAILURE** and prints `Snapshot created for test: <name> \u2014 Rerun to verify.` \u2014 this is expected, not an error. Re-run the same test immediately; the second run compares against the created snapshots and reports BUILD SUCCESS if the output is consistent.\n- After snapshot creation, read each snapshot file to verify the captured data is correct before accepting it.\n- Snapshots are generated compiled output, so never edit them by hand. When SQRL code, test data, or a `/*+test*/` table's name or output schema changes, delete the affected snapshot files and re-run the tests to regenerate them; then verify the new snapshots as above.\n\n# Test Automation (CI)\n\nAfter `run-tests.sh` works, you MUST read [github-test-automation.md](github-test-automation.md) and follow its steps to set up the CI workflow.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510988421}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:03:08.422815+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Key insight: The result type name for a function/table is the base table's generated result type name. For a function whose base table is `LoanPaymentSummary` (a `/*+no_query*/` table), the type name is `LoanPaymentSummary`. So we can have:\n\n- `/*+no_query */ LoanPaymentSummary := ...` (base table, owns type)\n- `LoanPaymentSummaryById(loan_id STRING NOT NULL, customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT * FROM LoanPaymentSummary WHERE loan_id = :loan_id AND customer_id = :customer_id;`\n\nBut then GraphQL query field must be `LoanPaymentSummaryById`, not `LoanPaymentSummary`. FR-9 just says query by loan_id. Could name the function `LoanSummary`? But the plan step 4 said \"keeping the exact table/type names\". Table names are fixed: `LoanPaymentSummary` and `LoanPayments`. The type names should be those. The query field names can differ. Hmm, but acceptance says `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: \"PL-115\")`. That strongly suggests the query endpoint is named `LoanPaymentSummary`. \n\nCan a table and a function share a name? Let's think: The GraphQL field `LoanPaymentSummary` matches \"the queryable SQRL table or SQRL function with the same name\". If there's both a table `LoanPaymentSummary` with `no_query` and a function `LoanPaymentSummary`, ambiguous. Probably compile error.\n\nAlternative approach: Make `LoanPaymentSummary` itself a **table function** named `LoanPaymentSummary` with the claim argument, and have its base table be an internal `_LoanPaymentSummary` table. Then:\n- The function is `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id..., customer_id claim...)`. GraphQL Query field `LoanPaymentSummary` matches it. \n- The result type: base table of the function is `_LoanPaymentSummary`. Hidden columns (`_` prefix) are not included in result type, but the base table's type name is... hmm the base table `_LoanPaymentSummary` would generate a type named `_LoanPaymentSummary`? Hidden columns are excluded but the type name would be `_LoanPaymentSummary`. The custom schema would then declare `type LoanPaymentSummary` but the function returns type `_LoanPaymentSummary`. The compiler checks field compatibility but not necessarily the type name (the type can have any name unless relationship fields). Since no relationships, the custom schema `type LoanPaymentSummary` would be accepted for the function result? Let me re-read: \"A field of the type that a table, function, or relationship returns matches the first of: 1. the SQL relationship ... 2. the column ... 3. the SQL relationship <base table>.<field>... So the type can have any name, unless a relationship field must resolve by the type name.\" That's about fields *within* the type. But how does the compiler connect `type LoanPaymentSummary` to the function's result? It just validates that all fields in the type are columns. It doesn't require the type name to equal the base table name. So `type LoanPaymentSummary` with the right column fields will work.\n\nHmm, but actually, does the compiler even care about the type name matching? For the query field, the schema `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: String!): [LoanPaymentSummary!]` \u2014 the compiler finds the function `LoanPaymentSummary`, validates the return fields against the function's row columns. The type name can be anything. Good.\n\nBut to be safe and clean, I can make the base table a public `/*+no_query*/` table named `LoanPaymentSummaryBase` and the function `LoanPaymentSummary`. Then the type name in schema: I'd declare `type LoanPaymentSummary`. The compiler validates fields against the function rows. Since no relationships, type name irrelevant. But the from-api rule says \"Name each object type that has relationship fields after the SQRL table that owns those relationships.\" No relationships here, so fine.\n\nHmm wait, but if no relationships, why not just skip custom schema and use inferred? Because customer_id must be injected from JWT and the exposed type names fixed. The custom schema is needed to control which functions are exposed and names. Actually with inferred schema, the query field would be `LoanPaymentSummary` if the function is named that. Let me reconsider.\n\nLet me design with functions named exactly as the endpoints:\n- `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id STRING NOT NULL, customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT * FROM _LoanPaymentSummary WHERE loan_id = :loan_id AND customer_id = :customer_id;`\n- `LoanPayments(loan_id STRING NOT NULL, customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT * FROM _LoanPayments WHERE loan_id = :loan_id AND customer_id = :customer_id ORDER BY due_date DESC, payment_id;`\n- `CustomerLoans(customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT ... FROM _LatestPersonalLoan l LEFT JOIN _LoanPaymentSummary s ON ... WHERE l.primary_borrower_customer_id = :customer_id ...;`\n\nWait but FR-4/3.3 says `LoanPayments` is a table. And FR-6 says `LoanPaymentSummary` is a table. The plan step 6/7 says \"Implement the `LoanPayments` table\" and \"Implement `LoanPaymentSummary`\". But then step 8 adds \"the three authorization-filtered lookups\". So the tables are internal (underscore) and the lookups are functions. But the requirement's \"Results\" section says LoanPayments and LoanPaymentSummary are API tables. Hmm.\n\nActually re-reading plan step 6: \"Implement the `LoanPayments` table ... FR-1\u2013FR-5\". Step 8: \"Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by loan_id, payments by loan_id, and the signed-in customer's loans\". So the table `LoanPayments` is the internal data table, and the lookup is a function. The table itself presumably has `no_query` (not directly exposed) and the functions expose it with auth.\n\nBut naming: If the internal table is `LoanPayments` (no underscore, `/*+no_query*/`), then the function can't also be `LoanPayments`. So the function would be named differently, e.g. `LoanPaymentsByLoan` or `GetLoanPayments`. But acceptance criteria explicitly uses `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` as a query. Hmm.\n\nWait, acceptance says \"*(FR-5)* `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` includes payment `LPMT-046`\". This is describing the lookup. It might be fine to name the GraphQL query `LoanPayments`. So we want the function named `LoanPayments`.\n\nResolution: Make the underlying tables **internal with `_` prefix** (`_LoanPayments`, `_LoanPaymentSummary`), and define functions named `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. The plan's \"table\" language is loose. But the plan step 4 says \"keeping the exact table/type names\". The type names will be `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary` in the custom schema. This satisfies the API contract. The internal table names being underscored is fine per Scope (they're internal).\n\nBut the Data Quality plan / type... Also FR-2 acceptance: \"LoanPayments has one row per payment_id, and LoanPaymentSummary has one row per loan_id.\" These are tested via table tests? Step 10 says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria\". A table test (`/*+test*/`) must select from a table that's in the interface. If `_LoanPayments` is hidden, then the test table can be a separate public table. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Step 10 wants an inferred-schema test package with `/*+test*/` tables. The inferred schema exposes tables via the interface. But our custom schema is configured for the main API. The test runner with `use-inferred-schema: true` infers the schema from the SQRL script, exposing all non-hidden tables. So if `_LoanPayments` is hidden, the inferred schema won't expose it, and a `/*+test*/` table selecting from it... Actually `/*+test*/` tables are themselves table definitions; the test runner executes them. They can select from hidden tables. The `/*+test*/` table just needs to be defined; it becomes an endpoint in the inferred schema. So:\n\n```\n/*+test */\nLoanPaymentsIntegrity_Test := SELECT COUNT(*) AS ... FROM _LoanPayments ...\n```\n\nBut the main script has a custom schema configured. Does an inferred-schema test run still work? The test config: `use-inferred-schema: true` means tests run against the inferred schema, ignoring the custom schema, and includes test tables. This is what the plan step 10 wants: a *separate* inferred-schema table-test package. Hmm, \"package\" = another test package overlay? The plan says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package\". Maybe a second set of configs? Let me re-read step 10: \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria ... [deps: 9]\".\n\nHmm, \"package\" might mean a separate `*-test-package.json` that sets `use-inferred-schema: true`. But the main test package for API tests should set `use-inferred-schema: false`. Can one project have two test packages? Yes: `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` (custom schema) and `loan-payments-integrity-test-package.json` (inferred). Or one test package with `use-inferred-schema: true` would run both API tests AND table tests; but then the API tests would run against inferred schema. Is that a problem? The plan step 11 says \"Add API tests in the custom-schema test package\". So separate.\n\nActually, re-reading: DataSQRL test runner: `use-inferred-schema` default true. With true, the test runner uses the inferred schema for API tests (ignores custom), but the `/*+test*/` tables are also part of the inferred schema and executed. So a single test run with use-inferred-schema true could run API test queries too but against inferred schema. The plan wants API tests against the **custom schema** because customer_id is injected from JWT and the inferred schema would expose the function args? Actually the functions have the claim arg, and inferred schema would expose `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` without customer_id (metadata claim args excluded). Hmm, actually inferred schema automatically handles auth metadata. So maybe both can work with inferred. But the requirement says custom schema to fix type names and exclude customer_id. The plan explicitly separates.\n\nLet me follow the plan:\n- Main API test package: `loan-payments-test-package.json` with `use-inferred-schema: false`, tests folder `loan-payments-api/tests/`. This is the custom-schema test package.\n- Integrity test package: `loan-payments-integrity-test-package.json` with `use-inferred-schema: true` (default), tests folder maybe `loan-payments-api/integrity-tests/`? Actually `/*+test*/` table tests don't need a test folder; they're executed automatically. The `test-folder` is for GraphQL query files. So the integrity package can have no test-folder, just snapshots and the `/*+test*/` tables.\n\nWait, but there's only one snapshot folder per test package. The two packages can share snapshot folder or have separate. Let me use `snapshots/loan-payments/` for both? The `/*+test*/` snapshots go there. The API tests go there too. Filenames distinct. Should be fine. But to be safe use separate folders? The plan says \"Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package\". I'll give it a distinct snapshot folder `snapshots/loan-payments-integrity/`.\n\nHmm, but the test runner's `test-folder` default is `./tests`. If integrity package has no test-folder, it defaults to `./tests` which doesn't exist \u2014 might error. Let me set `test-folder` to a directory with an empty/ignored file, or set `snapshot-folder`. Actually the `/*+test*/` tables are executed regardless of test-folder. Let me check test-sqrl skill for the separate package pattern (library_loans example). Let me read test-sqrl skill.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb6daa7ba183adfe60", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 5674, "output": 2796, "cacheRead": 102784, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 2744, "totalTokens": 111254, "cost": {"input": 0.0017021999999999999, "output": 0.0033552, "cacheRead": 0.000616704, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.005674104}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510980224, "responseId": "d18ec666f34e4e10ae5a81a678ed1fa1-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 8192, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb6daa7ba183adfe60", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: test-sqrl\ndescription: Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n---\n\nAlways implement test coverage for all major features or complex data transformations implemented in SQRL.\nWithout a custom GraphQL schema, prefer table tests. Use API tests for complex interaction patterns (mutation followed by query/subscription) or authorization.\nWith a custom GraphQL schema, follow [Projects with a Custom GraphQL Schema](#projects-with-a-custom-graphql-schema).\n\n# Writing Tests\n\n## Test Types\n\nDataSQRL supports two snapshot test types (run via `test` command):\n1. **Table tests**: SQRL tables annotated with `/*+test */` hint\n2. **API tests**: GraphQL operations (queries/mutations/subscriptions) in configured test folder.\n\nBoth write snapshots to the configured snapshot folder and pass when the new output is identical to the stored snapshot \u2014 see [Snapshot Lifecycle](#snapshot-lifecycle) under Running Tests.\n\n## Table Tests\n\nAdd `/*+test */` hint to any table definition:\n\n```sql\n-- Tests that the query returns an identical snapshot of data\n/*+test */\nCustomerRetrievalTest := SELECT customerid, name, email FROM Customer\n  WHERE name = 'John' ORDER BY customerid;\n\n-- Tests an invariant and returns failing records\n/*+test(no_rows) */\nInvalidCustomersTest := SELECT * FROM Customer\n  WHERE name = '' OR email IS NULL;\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n- Use `/*+test(no_rows) */` hint to test invariants\n- Snapshot results must have deterministic order (use `ORDER BY`)\n- Only select predictable columns and exclude non-deterministic ones (e.g. columns computed via `rand()` or `now()`)\n- Table tests do not include relationships\n- Every column in `ORDER BY` must also appear in `SELECT`. To sort by a column without including it in snapshot output, alias it with a `_` prefix: `event_time AS _event_time`. Omitting an ORDER BY column from SELECT causes: `All sort columns must be part of the SELECT clause for table definitions, missing: [col]`.\n\n## API Tests\n\nPlace `.graphql` files in the configured test folder, **one operation definition per file**. The file name (without extension) is the test name and the snapshot name: every `query`/`mutation`/`subscription` block in a file is registered under that name, so a second block in the same file is compared against the first one's snapshot and fails on every run. One block may still query several root fields at once (use aliases: `byOrg: alerts(...) {...}  byDeployment: alerts(...) {...}`); that is a single operation and a single snapshot.\n\n`GetAllCustomers.graphql`:\n```graphql\nquery GetAllCustomers {\n  Customer { customerid name email }\n}\n```\n\n`AddCustomer.graphql`:\n```graphql\nmutation AddCustomer {\n  Customer(event: {customerid: 123, email: \"test@example.com\", name: \"Test\"}) {\n    customerid\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Test Execution Order:**\n1. Subscribe to all subscriptions (must be triggered by mutation)\n2. Execute mutations sequentially (alphabetical filename order) with `mutation-delay-sec` between\n3. Wait for configured timeout, then drain the Flink job\n4. Execute queries\n5. Collect subscription results\n\n**Testing SUBSCRIBE tables:**\nIf your implementation adds or modifies a `SUBSCRIBE` statement, complete all three steps before compiling:\n1. Add `kafka` to `enabled-engines` in the test `*-package.json` \u2014 subscriptions are backed by Kafka topics even in test mode. Omitting kafka produces BUILD SUCCESS but the Subscription type will be absent from the generated schema.\n2. Create a `.graphql` API test file in the test folder containing the subscription query.\n3. Ensure test data includes records that trigger the subscription.\n\nIf no mutation exists to trigger the subscription, document why testing is not feasible and add a table test validating the subscription's data source instead.\n\n## Test Data Sources\n\n**Setup Pattern:**\nIf project does not currently follow recommended setup:\n1. Move the production `CREATE TABLE` statements to `connectors/sources-prod.sqrl`\n2. Import the connector file through a template variable: `IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources;`\n3. Set the variable per environment in the `<env>-package.json` overlays (invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill, Base Config and Environment Overlays).\n4. Create `connectors/sources-test.sqrl` with the same table names with static test data (invoke the `/manage-connector` skill, Connector Definitions)\n5. Create test config file (See /configure-sqrl` skill)\n\n**Test Source Definition:**\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE MyTable (\n    -- Same columns as prod, but METADATA \u2192 regular columns with static values\n    my_timestamp TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3),\n    WATERMARK FOR `my_timestamp` AS `my_timestamp` - INTERVAL '1' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'format' = 'flexible-json',\n    'flexible-json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/my_table.jsonl',\n    'connector' = 'filesystem'\n);\n```\n\nThe `timestamp-format.standard` option is required for ISO-8601 timestamps (`2024-01-01T10:00:00.000Z`) in the data file; the format's default is `SQL` (`2024-01-01 10:00:00.000`), and timestamps in the other notation fail to parse.\n\n**Test Data Guidelines:**\n- Use realistic data covering relationships and joins\n- Cover edge cases and failure scenarios\n- For any filter that excludes records (e.g. `enabled = false`, reading out of bounds), include at least one record that should be excluded and verify it is absent from the output snapshot. Tests containing only passing records cannot detect broken filter logic.\n- Use jsonl for data with `flexible-json` format in connector\n- For CSV sources, the header data only lives in the schema: the column names go into the `CREATE TABLE` statement, and the CSV file contains only data rows. Write CSV fixtures without a header row and read them with `'format' = 'csv'`. Flink's `csv` format reads every line as data, so a header row becomes a bad record, and the compiler warns `CSV file [...] seems to start with a header row`. If a provided CSV file starts with a header row, note the column names first, then remove the line: `tail -n +2 data.csv > data.csv.tmp && mv data.csv.tmp data.csv`, or for a compressed file `zcat data.csv.gz | tail -n +2 | gzip > data.csv.gz.tmp && mv data.csv.gz.tmp data.csv.gz`. Declare the columns in the `CREATE TABLE` statement in the order of the removed header. CSV files have no schema discovery, so `` LIKE `data.csv` `` fails at compile time (see the `/manage-connector` skill, `formats/csv.md`).\n- Set static event timestamps for determinism, make sure: a) timestamps are monotonically increasing and b) align across multiple test datasets\n- Convert METADATA columns to regular columns with static values\n- Convert non-deterministic columns (e.g., `NOW()`) to regular columns with static values\n- Use data files for streaming tests as well. A `datagen` source fills random fields and timestamps with new values on every run, so snapshot tests over them fail.\n- Reuse an existing test source or connector configuration when it already supplies the required data and semantics.\n\n**Critical Test Setup:**\n- Before taking snapshots, the `test` command drains the Flink job, which advances all watermarks to the end. Fixtures therefore need no dummy sentinel records to close windows or temporal joins.\n- Keep `table.exec.source.idle-timeout` at `\"0 s\"` in the test package, because the test runner disables idleness. If a base package sets it, override it with `\"0 s\"` in the test overlay. With idleness enabled, the drain can drop the last events behind chained temporal joins.\n- The `test` command runs in UTC, so snapshots show `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` values and time-zone-dependent results (e.g. casting a `TIMESTAMP_LTZ` to a string) in UTC. Write fixture timestamps with an explicit offset (e.g. `2025-10-13T12:11:26.575Z`) so they are independent of the time zone. When the logic depends on a different session time zone, set `table.local-time-zone` in the Flink configuration of the package (`/configure-sqrl` skill) instead of changing the container's `TZ`.\n\n## Authentication Testing\n\nConfigure custom headers per query using `.properties` file (same name as `.graphql` file):\n```text\nAuthorization: Bearer XYZ\n```\n\nA script that reads a claim (`METADATA FROM 'auth.<claim>'`) compiles only with an auth provider in `engines.vertx.config`, and the `test` command compiles the test configuration. Put the `auth` block in the `<project>-shared-package.json` base, or in the `<project>-test-package.json` overlay (invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill, Authentication in `vertx.md`). With the `auth` block only in the `<project>-prod-package.json` overlay, the `test` command fails with `The script reads JWT claims (...), but engines.vertx.config has no auth provider.` To generate a test token that matches the `auth` block, invoke the `/design-api` skill (Generating Tokens in `authorization.md`).\n\n## Projects with a Custom GraphQL Schema\n\nA project has a custom schema when its package configuration names a schema file under `script.api.<version>.schema` or `script.graphql`.\n`sqrl test` starts one API server with one schema. `test-runner.use-inferred-schema` in the test package chooses that schema:\n\n| `use-inferred-schema` | Schema of the test server | Table tests (`/*+test*/` tables in the `.sqrl` file) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `true` (default) | the inferred schema. The custom schema is not read | run |\n| `false` | the custom schema | do not run, and `sqrl test` reports no message |\n\nNotes:\n- API tests (the `.graphql` files in `test-runner.test-folder`) run with `use-inferred-schema` both values.\n- With `use-inferred-schema=true`, an API test gives a wrong result where the two schemas differ: a request with an enum value, a renamed argument or a custom type fails validation, and a field that the custom schema hides still returns data.\n\n### Choose the kind of test\n\nA custom schema holds changes that matter to the project, so the tests check the custom schema. Choose the tests by the condition that applies to the project:\n\n| Condition | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| **Default.** Every project with a custom schema, with or without mutations | Only API tests, in `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json`. No `/*+test*/` table in the `.sqrl` file. |\n| **Exception.** The requirements or the plan ask for a test that must not be in the API, for example a data integrity check or a validation query on data that users must not see | API tests in `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json`, and table tests for the requested checks in `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`. Follow [Table tests in a second test package](#table-tests-in-a-second-test-package). |\n\nIn both cases:\n\n- Write each test that reads data the API returns as an API test, also when the project already has a `/*+test*/` table for it.\n- Keep API tests and table tests in separate test packages, because each test package uses one schema (see the table above).\n- Add a field to the custom schema only when users need that field. A field that exists only for a test is part of the deployed API, so every user can read that data. When the requirements or the plan ask for a test on such data, follow the exception above.\n\n### Rules for every project with a custom schema\n\n1. Set `\"use-inferred-schema\": false` in the test package that runs the API tests, so that the API tests check the custom schema. Name it `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json`, or `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json` when the (sub-)project also requires separate table tests (`<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`).\n2. Write each API test with the fields, arguments, enum values and types of the custom schema. For example, write an enum value without quotes (`genre: fantasy`). The test server rejects a request that does not fit the custom schema, and `sqrl test` saves that error as the expected result and still reports success.\n3. Keep exactly one schema file. A copy of the schema for tests looks like a way to run table tests with the custom schema, but the tests then read only the copy. An error in the real schema then stays hidden from the tests, and every schema change must be made in two files.\n\n### Table tests in a second test package\n\nFollow these steps only for the exception in [Choose the kind of test](#choose-the-kind-of-test): the requirements or the plan ask for a test that must not be in the API. In the default case, the project has one test package and no table tests, so skip this section.\n\n1. Rename `<(sub-)project>-test-package.json` to `<(sub-)project>-api-test-package.json`, so that each name says which tests it runs. Update its suite line in `run-tests.sh` such that each test exists.\n2. Create `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json` with `\"use-inferred-schema\": true`, its own `snapshot-folder`, and a `test-folder` that holds no `.graphql` file. A folder that does not exist works.\n3. In `run-tests.sh`, add `<(sub-)project>_table_test` to `SUBPROJECTS`, and add the suite line `<(sub-)project>_table_test/test) echo \"<(sub-)project>-shared-package.json <(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json\" ;;`.\n4. If the project has no mutation table, stop here. The table tests read the same test data files as the API tests.\n5. If the project has a mutation table, the table tests need the test data without the mutations, because the mutation test files do not fit the inferred schema:\n   1. Set `\"script\": { \"config\": { \"environment\": \"table_test\" } }` in `<(sub-)project>-table-test-package.json`.\n   2. Create `connectors/sources_table_test.sqrl`. Define each mutation table there as a filesystem table with the same name and columns.\n   3. Create a `.jsonl` file for each of these tables with the same records as the mutation test files.\n\nSee `/opt/datasqrl-examples/support_tickets/` for the default: a mutation table and API tests only.\nSee `/opt/datasqrl-examples/library_loans/` for a mutation table, API tests, and table tests in a second test package.\n\n## Test Configuration\n\n```json5\n{\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/myproject/\", // Snapshots output directory (default: \"./snapshots\")\n    \"test-folder\": \"api/tests/\",               // Directory containing test GraphQL queries (default: \"./tests\")\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,               // true: tests use the inferred schema and ignore a configured schema. false: tests use the schema under \"script.api.<version>.schema\" or \"script.graphql\" (default: true)\n    \"delay-sec\": 30,                           // Max wait in sec for the job to finish before taking snapshots; ends early when the job terminates. -1 = wait until all operators are idle and required-checkpoints completed (streaming) or until the job completes (batch) (default: 30)\n    \"mutation-delay-sec\": 0,                   // Pause(s) between mutation queries (default: 0)\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 0,                 // Minimum completed Flink checkpoints before taking snapshots (requires delay-sec = -1)\n    \"create-topics\": [\"topic1\", \"topic2\"],     // Kafka topics to create before tests start\n    \"headers\": {                               // Any HTTP headers to add during the test execution. For example, JWT auth header\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <token>\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n# Running Tests\n\n## Project Test Script\n\nEvery project root must hold a test runner script, **`run-tests.sh`**, that is the entry point for the project's tests: you as the code agent run it, CI runs it and developer can run it. It owns the whole test process (setup, every suite, teardown) and returns exit code 0 ONLY if all tests pass. It also *reports* what the project contains.\n\nExamples:\n```bash\n./run-tests.sh                                          # every suite, env=test default\n./run-tests.sh fraud_store                              # only the named sub-project(s)\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev                      # compiles the dev config\n./run-tests.sh --list-envs                              # Example output: test dev prod\n./run-tests.sh --list-subprojects                       # Example output: fraud_store fraud_training\n./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev   # print what would run, run nothing\n```\n\n### The script contract\n\n- **No arguments runs every suite**; sub-project names select suites.\n- **Sub-project names are single words**: letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` and nothing else. They are carried in a space-separated list and reported one per line as `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project-name> <verb> <config...> -b <build-dir>`, so a name containing a space is read as two names and the report cannot be parsed. `fraud_store` and `fraud-store` are fine; `fraud store` is not.\n- **Setup and teardown live in the script** and run on every invocation, e.g. test-data generation or download (data too large for git), fixture start, cleanup. This is the only place complex test setup can live. Guard them with `$LIST` so listing stays side-effect free.\n- Every selected suite runs even after one fails, and the script exits non-zero when any of them failed.\n- **Declare, do not detect.** `SUBPROJECTS`, `ENVS` and the `suite` table are written out; a name or environment the project does not have is an error (exit 2), never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n- **Declare every environment.** `ENVS` names every environment that has a `<sub-project>-<env>-package.json` overlay, and the `suite` table has a line for each of them. The verification compile and the README commands use only the environments that `ENVS` names.\n- **The `suite` table names configs literally**, in merge order \u2014 base first, environment config last, because a later configuration file overrides an earlier one.\n- **Every sub-project gets its own build folder.** Every invocation (compile or test) carries\n  `-b <sub-project>`, so one suite never overwrites a sibling's artifacts and after a single run\n  every sub-project's build is still on disk. The flag names a **subfolder of `build/`**: `-b fraud_store` writes to `<project-root>/build/fraud_store`.\n- **`-b` requires `-r`.** Pointing `-b` inside `build/` makes the compiler resolve `script.main`\n  against the wrong root unless the project root is given too: `-r .` for a project that is the\n  mount root, `-r <project-dir>` when it sits inside a repository. With `-r`, config paths are\n  relative to that root and carry no prefix.\n- **Keep it in sync**: a new sub-project means a new entry in `SUBPROJECTS` and a `suite` line per environment it supports.\n- `chmod +x run-tests.sh` after writing it.\n\n**Scope:** While iterating, pass the names of the sub-projects you are working on as arguments of `run-tests.sh` so only their suites run. Your run must cover everything that changed: every sub-project newly generated or edited, plus every sub-project affected by an edit to shared code or configuration. Before running the script, list the generated, edited and affected sub-projects, and pass exactly that list as the arguments.\n\n**DataSQRL CLI version.**  Currently used DataSQRL CLI version: 0.12.5. The code agent container runs specific DataSQRL version. The test shell scrip pins `SQRL_VERSION` to the same default so a CI or local run compiles and snapshots with the version the project was built against.\n\n### The script\n\nThis skill comes with a [run-tests.sh](run-tests.sh) template that follows the contract above. Copy it into the project root and make the copied file executable.\n\nThen edit only the three blocks marked `EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT`: the `ENVS` and `SUBPROJECTS` declarations, the `suite` table, and the optional setup and teardown hooks. Keep everything else unchanged.\n\nAfter completing the script, verify the script. First run `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations` and check that every sub-project appears with the configs you expect. Then run `./run-tests.sh` for only the sub-projects you changed (see **Scope** above).\n\nFor an example with two sub-projects (one of them has no prod config), see `/opt/datasqrl-examples/feature_store/run-tests.sh`.\n\n## One Invocation per Test Package\n\nA project folder may hold several **sub-projects** (deployments), each with its own\n`*-test-package.json`. The `suite` table in `run-tests.sh` carries one entry per\n(sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order:\n\n```bash\n    <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n```\n\nThe driver runs one `cmd.sh` invocation per selected sub-project per selected environment from those entries.\n\n- Multiple package arguments to one invocation always result in a merged configuration (last file overwrites). That is what makes base + overlay work.\n- Drop the `<project>-shared-package.json` argument only for a project that has no base config. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Base Config and Environment Overlays, Sub-projects) while setting the config files.\n- Listing two different sub-projects' test packages in one invocation merges them into one configuration and fails the build.\n- Keep the `-r <root> \u2026 -b <sub-project>` form on every direct invocation too, matching the suite's build folder, so a hand-run compile lands where the framework and the next suite run expect it.\n- Give each test package its own `test-runner.snapshot-folder`, set in its `-test-package.json` overlay (for example `snapshots/fraud_store/`, `snapshots/fraud_training/`); sharing one folder makes the runs clobber each other's snapshots.\n\n**Project inside a repository.** Package paths resolve at the compiler's mount root. When the project is a subdirectory of the repository (the compiler mounts the repository root), every package path carries the project directory as a prefix. The suite lines pick that prefix up from `SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX` (`${P}`), and the `sqrl()` function adds `-r <project-dir>` on the CI/local docker path. As a fallback, when you call `/opt/agent/cmd.sh` directly, qualify the paths yourself. The same form applies to `compile` and `run`:\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r {your-project-dir} {project}-shared-package.json {project}-test-package.json -b {sub-project}\n# equivalently\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . {project}-shared-package.json {project}-test-package.json -b {sub-project}   # project IS the mount root\n```\n\nThe `-b` value stays **un-prefixed**: it is resolved against the root given by `-r`, so `-r {your-project-dir} \u2026 -b x` writes `{your-project-dir}/build/x`.\n\nWhen the project *is* the repository root, the plain `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test {package.json}` form applies.\n\n**Direct CLI fallback.** When the script is broken, fix it. As a fallback, call `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r <root> <base config> <overlay config> -b <sub-project>`; `run-tests.sh` stays the command of record and the layering rules above apply to the direct call unchanged.\n\nExample: `/opt/datasqrl-examples/feature_store/` ships two sub-projects (deployments) over one shared feature computation (`fraud_features.sqrl`): a realtime feature store with an API (`fraud_store`) and a batch training run (`fraud_training`).\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud_store-shared-package.json fraud_store-test-package.json -b fraud_store\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud_training-shared-package.json fraud_training-test-package.json -b fraud_training\n```\n\n**Edge case: `-r` must match what is mounted at `/workspace`.**: \n* Wrong `-r`: If a compile fails with:\n\n```\nProject root does not exist or not a directory: /workspace/<path>\n```\n\nthe `-r` value points at the wrong place. `-r` is resolved against `/workspace`, and the mount at `/workspace` is fixed for the whole run, so the fix is to correct `-r`:\n\n- The project itself is mounted at `/workspace` (single-project run): use `-r .` and pass the package files without a prefix.\n- The repository is mounted at `/workspace` (the project sits in a subdirectory of the repo): use `-r <project-dir>` or prefix every package path with `<project-dir>/` too.\n\nThis error comes from a direct `cmd.sh` call. `run-tests.sh` script derives the root from `SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX` when it is provided.\n\n\n## Snapshot Lifecycle\n\n- **The first run of a new test always reports BUILD FAILURE** and prints `Snapshot created for test: <name> \u2014 Rerun to verify.` \u2014 this is expected, not an error. Re-run the same test immediately; the second run compares against the created snapshots and reports BUILD SUCCESS if the output is consistent.\n- After snapshot creation, read each snapshot file to verify the captured data is correct before accepting it.\n- Snapshots are generated compiled output, so never edit them by hand. When SQRL code, test data, or a `/*+test*/` table's name or output schema changes, delete the affected snapshot files and re-run the tests to regenerate them; then verify the new snapshots as above.\n\n# Test Automation (CI)\n\nAfter `run-tests.sh` works, you MUST read [github-test-automation.md](github-test-automation.md) and follow its steps to set up the CI workflow.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510988421}]}
