{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:04:04.090014+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_update", "usage": {"input": 108, "output": 2387, "cacheRead": 142208, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 1057, "totalTokens": 144703, "cost": {"input": 3.24e-05, "output": 0.0028644, "cacheRead": 0.000853248, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.003750048}}, "assistantMessageEvent": {"type": "thinking_end", "contentIndex": 0, "content": "Now step 3 done. Step 4: API schema and operations. Let me write the schema.\n\nCustom schema needs scalars Date, Long; maybe DateTime not needed. And the `@api` directive. Let me write.\n\n```graphql\n\"An RFC-3339 compliant date (no time component).\"\nscalar Date\n\n\"A 64-bit signed integer.\"\nscalar Long\n\n\"Per-loan totals and counts over the payments of one personal loan.\"\ntype LoanPaymentSummary {\n  loan_id: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  loan_purpose: String\n  applied_payment_count: Long!\n  returned_payment_count: Long!\n  total_paid_cents: Long!\n  principal_paid_cents: Long!\n  interest_paid_cents: Long!\n  late_fees_paid_cents: Long!\n  last_payment_date: Date\n}\n\n\"One payment of a personal loan.\"\ntype LoanPayments {\n  payment_id: String!\n  loan_id: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  loan_purpose: String\n  payment_amount_cents: Long!\n  principal_cents: Long\n  interest_cents: Long\n  late_fee_cents: Long\n  other_fees_cents: Long\n  payment_type: String!\n  payment_status: String!\n  due_date: Date!\n  received_date: Date\n  applied_date: Date\n  payment_method: String\n  principal_balance_after_cents: Long\n  days_past_due_at_payment: Int\n  confirmation_number: String\n  return_reason_code: String\n}\n\n\"One personal loan of the signed-in customer, with its payment summary.\"\ntype CustomerLoans {\n  loan_id: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  loan_purpose: String\n  loan_status: String!\n  original_amount_cents: Long!\n  current_balance_cents: Long!\n  monthly_payment_cents: Long!\n  next_payment_due_date: Date\n  applied_payment_count: Long!\n  returned_payment_count: Long!\n  total_paid_cents: Long!\n  principal_paid_cents: Long!\n  interest_paid_cents: Long!\n  late_fees_paid_cents: Long!\n  last_payment_date: Date\n}\n\ntype Query {\n  \"Payment summary of one loan owned by the signed-in customer. Returns null when the loan does not exist or belongs to another customer.\"\n  LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: String!): LoanPaymentSummary\n  \"Payments of one loan owned by the signed-in customer, newest due date first.\"\n  LoanPayments(loan_id: String!, limit: Int = 20, offset: Int = 0): [LoanPayments!]\n  \"All personal loans of the signed-in customer, each with its payment summary.\"\n  CustomerLoans(limit: Int = 20, offset: Int = 0): [CustomerLoans!]\n}\n\nenum _McpMethodType { NONE TOOL }\nenum _RestMethodType { NONE GET POST }\ndirective @api(mcp: _McpMethodType, rest: _RestMethodType, uri: String) on QUERY | MUTATION | FIELD_DEFINITION\n```\n\nWait, `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: String!)` returns single object. The function returns at most one row. Good.\n\nNow operations.v1.graphql:\n```graphql\n# Payment summary of one loan for the signed-in customer.\nquery LoanSummaryByLoan($loan_id: String!) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/loans/{loan_id}/payment-summary\") {\n  LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: $loan_id) { ... }\n}\n\n# Payments of one loan for the signed-in customer, newest due date first.\nquery LoanPaymentsByLoan($loan_id: String!, $limit: Int = 20, $offset: Int = 0) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/loans/{loan_id}/payments{?limit,offset}\") {\n  LoanPayments(loan_id: $loan_id, limit: $limit, offset: $offset) { ... }\n}\n\n# All personal loans of the signed-in customer.\nquery MyLoans @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/my-loans\") {\n  CustomerLoans { ... }\n}\n```\n\nHmm `MyLoans` has no arguments and returns a list. Fine. REST GET.\n\nNow, the `CustomerLoans` field has limit/offset defaults. Including limit/offset in the operation as variables isn't required.\n\nLet me write the schema file. Note the `@api` directive definition is already in the schema; operations file uses it.\n\nNow, is the `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: String!)` single object allowed by SQRL when the function returns a list? The function returns rows; the schema declares a single object. The design-api says cardinality changes are allowed. Good.\n\nLet me write both files."}}
