{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:04:26.672782+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccc9ed77de1a1b72ac5", "toolName": "edit", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced 4 block(s) in /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md."}], "details": {"diff": "     ...\n  39 - [x] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n  40 - [x] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n  41 - [x] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n  42 - [x] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- 43 - [*] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- 44 - [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- 45 - [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- 46 - [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n+ 43 - [x] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n+ 44 - [x] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n+ 45 - [x] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n+ 46 - [*] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n  47 - [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n  48 - [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n  49 - [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n  50 - [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n@@ -39,12 +39,12 @@\n - [x] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n - [x] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n - [x] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n - [x] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n-- [*] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n-- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n-- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n-- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n+- [x] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n+- [x] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n+- [x] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n+- [*] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n - [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n - [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n - [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n - [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n", "firstChangedLine": 43}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795511066672}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:04:26.673257+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccc9faf7822b6b71cbb", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: monitor-pipeline\ndescription: Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n---\n\n\n# Monitor the pipeline: data quality and observability tables\n\nMonitor things that matter to the result of this particular job. Balance the benefit against performance, storage cost, and noise. \n\n## Two kinds of monitoring tables\n\n**Data-quality table (`DQ_` prefix).** Collect unexpected records that matter to this job's processing or result. Use aggregate counters instead when individual records are unnecessary or too numerous to retain.  Examples include a transaction with no matching customer at event time, an address with a start date after its end date, or an event from an unknown sender. Each row should describe a problem that someone can investigate or fix.\n\n**Observability statistics table (`OB_` prefix).** Count records over time at a level that platform metrics do not show. Examples include hourly counts by sender, source table, or partition. Counts can also be split by valid records and rejection reason. A missing row or a lower count can indicate a problem.\n\n## Decide whether a table is needed for data quality or observability\n\n1. **Treat catalog assertions as guarantees.** Assertions such as `/*+test(no_rows) */` document what downstream jobs can rely on. Do not turn them into production checks because they run only in tests. Treat an assertion as unenforced only when the catalog or source explicitly documents that it is not enforced on source data and must be evaluated downstream.\n2. **Only put DQ on sources when both conditions hold:** invalid data would break this job's processing or intended result, and the invalid data can reach this job because the relevant upstream guarantee is absent or explicitly unenforced. Use cleaned catalog tables by default. A job whose purpose is cleaning or enriching raw records can read the raw layer and capture rejected records or counters. Shared cleanup and monitoring belong in the catalog or an upstream job.\n3. **Failures in this job that matter to consumers or downstream.** Inspect joins, filters, and derived results for credible failures. A missing customer at transaction time warrants a capture only when it can happen and silently dropping the transaction harms the result. Skip intentional exclusions and acceptable defaults.\n4. **Observe critical blind spots.** Add statistics/metrics only when they help assess the job's health. Do not count every source by default. Example: A department-level count is useful when independent departments send critical input and aggregate metrics would hide one department stopping. \n5. **Require an action and consider cost.** Before adding a table, state what it detects, how it affects this job, why existing guarantees or monitoring do not cover it, and who will act. Consider state, storage, retention, cardinality, and noise; use counters when keeping individual invalid records would be too expensive. An upstream source check may not cover a failure introduced by this job's own processing.\n\n\n## Use the patterns as examples\n\nApply a pattern only after it meets the rules above. These examples are not a checklist of tables to create. Investigate the actual sources and processing for the job-specific decision.\n\n## Pattern 1: collect events with missing master data\n\nIn this example, each transaction needs a matching customer record at event time, the catalog does not guarantee that temporal match, and losing a transaction would make the job's result incomplete. Operations needs the individual records to reconcile them. Use a LEFT temporal join so unmatched transactions remain available to a DQ table. Classify the result once, then use that classification for the main processing and the DQ table.\n\n```sql\n_Customer := DISTINCT cat.Customer ON customer_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n\n_TransactionClassified := SELECT t.*,\n    CASE\n      WHEN c.customer_id IS NULL THEN 'unknown_customer'\n      ELSE 'valid'\n    END AS ingestion_status\n  FROM cat.Card_Transaction t\n  LEFT JOIN _Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t.event_time AS c\n    ON t.customer_id = c.customer_id;\n\n-- The main processing reads only transactions with a matching customer.\nTransaction := SELECT * FROM _TransactionClassified WHERE ingestion_status = 'valid';\n\n/** DQ: Transactions with no matching customer version at event time.\n    These records are excluded from the main processing and remain available for investigation. */\n/*+query_by_any(customer_id) */\nDQ_Transaction_Unknown_Customer := SELECT transaction_id, customer_id, amount_cents, event_time\n  FROM _TransactionClassified WHERE ingestion_status = 'unknown_customer'\n  ORDER BY event_time DESC, transaction_id ASC;\n```\n\nUse this pattern when a missing match is a problem. If discarding the record or using a default is acceptable, document that choice. Do not add a DQ table for an intentional, harmless exclusion. If several rejection reasons are useful, extend the classification and explain how a record that fails several checks is handled.\n\n## Pattern 2: capture rejected records in a cleaning job\n\nPattern only in a job responsible for cleaning raw address records whose effective windows are not guaranteed valid. Invalid windows would break that job's intended output, and rejected records are needed for repair.\n\n```sql\n/** DQ: Address records with invalid effective window (effective_from > effective_to).\n    Raw records rejected by this cleaning job, retained for repair. */\nDQ_Address_Window_Issues := SELECT customer_id, address_type, effective_from, effective_to,\n    source_updated_at, ingested_at\n  FROM cat.Customer_Address_raw\n  WHERE effective_to IS NOT NULL AND effective_from > effective_to\n  ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC, customer_id ASC, address_type ASC;\n```\n\n## Pattern 3: find missing or inconsistent derived state\n\nA pipeline can keep a record even when related data is missing. Inspect derived tables for conditions that need attention, including conditions that affect compliance.\n\nHere, `_Customer` is the latest customer table from Pattern 1. `_CustomerRiskRating` contains the latest rating marked as current for each customer. Follow the actual source's rules for representing current ratings when adapting this example.\n\n```sql\n_CurrentRiskRating := SELECT * FROM cat.Customer_Risk_Rating WHERE is_current = true;\n_CustomerRiskRating := DISTINCT _CurrentRiskRating ON customer_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n\n/** DQ: Active customers without a current AML risk rating \u2014 compliance-sensitive.\n    Rows require investigation by this job's risk operations team. */\n/*+query_by_any(customer_id) */\nDQ_Unrated_Active_Customers := SELECT c.customer_id, c.customer_status,\n    c.source_updated_at, c.ingested_at\n  FROM _Customer c\n  LEFT JOIN _CustomerRiskRating r ON c.customer_id = r.customer_id\n  WHERE c.customer_status = 'ACTIVE' AND r.customer_id IS NULL\n  ORDER BY c.source_updated_at DESC, c.customer_id ASC;\n```\n\nThis table reports a current condition. A customer should leave the result when a current rating becomes available or the customer is no longer active.\n\n## Pattern 4: count received records by source\n\nCombine source records and count them by source and hour before filtering or deduplication.\n\n```sql\n_SourceRecords := SELECT 'Customer' AS source_name, ingested_at FROM cat.Customer\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'Customer_Address' AS source_name, ingested_at FROM cat.Customer_Address\n  UNION ALL SELECT 'Card_Transaction' AS source_name, ingested_at FROM cat.Card_Transaction;\n\n/** OB: Hourly counts of received records for each source.\n    Changes in counts or missing hours can indicate an input problem. */\n/*+query_by_any(source_name) */\nOB_SourceIngestionStats := SELECT source_name, window_start, window_time AS window_end,\n    COUNT(*) AS record_count\n  FROM TABLE(TUMBLE(TABLE _SourceRecords, DESCRIPTOR(ingested_at), INTERVAL '1' HOUR))\n  GROUP BY source_name, window_start, window_end, window_time\n  ORDER BY window_time DESC, source_name ASC;\n```\n\nWhen a source has independent senders, also group by a sender field, such as department or partner, where useful. Count the raw source records by arrival time in the same way. This helps users notice when one sender stops while others continue.\n\n\n## Names, API access, and documentation\n\n- Name DQ tables `DQ_<Subject>_<Condition>`. Name observability statistics tables `OB_<Subject>Stats`, for example `OB_SourceIngestionStats`. Both kinds are exposed, so do not start their names with `_`. Start intermediate table names with `_`.\n- Give each exposed table a deterministic `ORDER BY` and a `/*+query_by_any(...) */` hint for the columns users filter by. If a DQ table can grow without limit, serve it from Iceberg through a table function with a `from_time` argument. Follow `/design-api`.\n- Start each table's documentation comment by explaining what a row or count means. Explain what problem it helps detect.\n- Add a \"Data quality and observability\" section to the README. List the tables, explain what a row or missing row means, and link to tables in other projects that provide existing coverage. Follow `/write-documentation`.\n\n## Testing\n\nGive every DQ table and every observability statistics table its own `/*+test */` snapshot.\n\nIf the pipeline owns its test data, prepare it according to the project's data quality tables and observability metrics. Choose test cases that exercise what each table checks or measures. Adapt the cases to the project's logic.\n\nIf the pipeline reads clean test data from a catalog connector, expect empty DQ snapshots and record this expectation in the plan. OB snapshots should still contain the counts expected from that data. State which cases the available data does not cover. An empty DQ snapshot alone does not show that invalid records would be detected.\n```sql\n/*+test */\nDQTransactionUnknownCustomerTest := SELECT transaction_id, customer_id, event_time\n  FROM DQ_Transaction_Unknown_Customer ORDER BY event_time ASC, transaction_id ASC;\n\n/*+test */\nDQAddressWindowIssuesTest := SELECT * FROM DQ_Address_Window_Issues;\n\n/*+test */\nDQUnratedActiveCustomersTest := SELECT * FROM DQ_Unrated_Active_Customers;\n\n/*+test */\nOBSourceIngestionStatsTest := SELECT * FROM OB_SourceIngestionStats;\n```\n\n## Script layout\n\nAfter the main processing definitions, group DQ tables, observability statistics tables, and tests under the following comments, in this order. This makes them easier for readers and later repository surveys to find.\n\n```sql\n-- ============================================================\n-- DATA QUALITY TABLES\n-- ============================================================\n\n-- ============================================================\n-- OBSERVABILITY\n-- ============================================================\n\n-- ============================================================\n-- TEST QUERIES\n-- ============================================================\n```\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795511066672}}
