{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:04:31.352977+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The plan's Data Quality & Observability section already decided no new tables. Let me verify that reasoning against the skill. The one credible failure: personal payment whose loan record never arrives \u2192 inner join drops it. But the catalog asserts referential integrity (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test`). Treat catalog assertions as guarantees \u2192 no DQ needed. Also no independent senders \u2192 no ingestion counters. Intentional auto-loan exclusion not an anomaly. So no new tables. Good.\n\nThe plan already documents this in its `## Data Quality & Observability` section. So step 9 is a no-op; mark it done with the recorded rationale. Optionally update the README section later (step 13). The plan already has it.\n\nLet me mark step 9 done and move to step 10: table tests. I already added 5 table tests to the script. Step 10 wants a separate inferred-schema table-test package (created: loan-payments-table-test-package.json). The plan says \"Add ... package covering ... personal-only row count, absence of AL- loan ids, and one row per payment_id and per loan_id\". Done in the script.\n\nNow step 11: API tests. Let me create the test files and properties.\n\nLet me plan the API test files:\n1. `payment-returned.graphql` (CUST-439): LoanPayments for PL-115. Include all payment fields? At least payment_id, payment_status, return_reason_code. Also confirm LPMT-046 present.\n2. `loan-summary-returned.graphql` (CUST-439): LoanPaymentSummary for PL-115 \u2192 returned_payment_count=1, total_paid_cents=635360.\n3. `loan-summary-late-fees.graphql` (CUST-061): LoanPaymentSummary for PL-035 \u2192 late_fees_paid_cents=7500.\n4. `zero-payment-loan.graphql` (CUST-066): LoanPaymentSummary for PL-011.\n5. `customer-loans.graphql` (CUST-060): CustomerLoans \u2192 4 loans.\n6. `wrong-customer-summary.graphql` (CUST-060): LoanPaymentSummary for PL-115 \u2192 null.\n7. `wrong-customer-payments.graphql` (CUST-060): LoanPayments for PL-115 \u2192 empty list.\n8. `missing-token.graphql` (no properties): LoanPaymentSummary for PL-115 \u2192 auth error.\n\nWait, for the missing token test, if the test runner has no default headers in the API test package, and this test has no .properties, then no Authorization. Good. But all other tests need .properties. \n\nHmm, but the API test package `test-runner` doesn't set `headers`. Good.\n\nNow, one concern: the test runner executes queries; if no auth header, the server returns 401/403, and the test runner saves the error as expected result and reports success (per test-sqrl: \"the test server rejects a request that does not fit the custom schema, and sqrl test saves that error as the expected result and still reports success\"). So the missing-token snapshot will be an error JSON. Good.\n\nNow, for `LoanPayments` in the custom schema, the query field returns a list. Test query:\n\n```graphql\nquery PaymentReturned {\n  LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\") {\n    payment_id\n    payment_status\n    return_reason_code\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut this returns all 12 payments. Snapshot will include all. That's fine, but to specifically show LPMT-046, order is due_date desc, payment_id. Might be long. Could filter? No filter besides loan_id. Fine.\n\nActually, to keep snapshots focused and deterministic, I'll select all payment_ids and statuses. It's 12 rows.\n\nFor `customer-loans.graphql` CUST-060:\n```graphql\nquery CustomerLoansForCustomer {\n  CustomerLoans {\n    loan_id\n    customer_id\n    loan_status\n    total_paid_cents\n    applied_payment_count\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNow properties files with tokens. Let me write them.\n\nAlso, I should double-check the claim name mapping: `METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'` reads top-level claim `customer_id`. The token has `customer_id`. Good.\n\nLet me write the test files one per write call (rule: one file per writing call). Actually the rule says \"Write one file per writing call.\" So I must not batch. Let me do sequential writes. That's a lot of calls. I'll do them.\n\nLet me start.\n\nActually, wait. The API test package snapshot folder and test folder: `snapshots/loan-payments/` and `loan-payments-api/tests/`. Good.\n\nLet me write the files. I'll combine the .graphql and .properties in separate calls.\n\nLet me also consider: Do I need a test that queries the REST/MCP operations? No.\n\nLet me write.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Step 9: The plan's Data Quality section already reasons (and I confirmed) that catalog assertions cover the only credible silent-loss path, and there are no independent senders, so no new DQ tables are warranted. Marking done. Now step 10 table tests are already in the script; step 11 API tests."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cccaf577363b744c94b", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"edits": [{"newText": "- [x] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality", "oldText": "- [*] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality"}, {"newText": "- [x] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package", "oldText": "- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package"}, {"newText": "- [*] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package", "oldText": "- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package"}], "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cccb028770385da60f2", "name": "write", "arguments": {"content": "query PaymentReturned {\n  LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\") {\n    payment_id\n    payment_status\n    return_reason_code\n  }\n}\n", "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/loan-payments-api/tests/payment-returned.graphql"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "