{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:05:05.641087+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "source": "agent", "kind": "tool", "tool": "bash", "target": "cat /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"=== progress ===\"; head -40 /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt 2>/dev/null", "message": "bash cat /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"=== progress ===\"; head -40 /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt 2>/dev/null"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:05:05.642357+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "tool_execution_update", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccd37a57670897fecbb", "toolName": "bash", "args": {"command": "cat /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"=== progress ===\"; head -40 /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt 2>/dev/null"}, "partialResult": {"content": []}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:05:05.642895+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "tool_execution_update", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccd37a57670897fecbb", "toolName": "bash", "args": {"command": "cat /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"=== progress ===\"; head -40 /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt 2>/dev/null"}, "partialResult": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /execute-plan \u2014 Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\n"}], "details": {}}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:05:05.643326+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "tool_execution_update", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccd37a57670897fecbb", "toolName": "bash", "args": {"command": "cat /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"=== progress ===\"; head -40 /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt 2>/dev/null"}, "partialResult": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /execute-plan \u2014 Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\n=== progress ===\n+00:00:00  INFO   Image edition: pro (pro-agent)\n+00:00:00  INFO   Judges use together_ai/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash\n+00:00:00  INFO   Discovered 3 sibling entr(ies) (1 shared module(s))\n+00:00:00  RUN    Run started \u00b7 implementation\n+00:00:00  INFO   Wrote repo inventory to .datasqrl/repo-inventory.md\n+00:00:00  STEP   Iteration 0 \u00b7 implementing\n+00:00:02  THINK  Let me start by invoking the execute-plan skill, reading the plan and requirements. Let me also read the repo inventory and requirements file. Let me begin.\n+00:00:02  TEXT   I'll start by invoking the execute-plan skill and reading the plan and requirements.\n+00:00:02  TOOL   read /opt/agent/skills/execute-plan/SKILL.md\n+00:00:02  TOOL   read /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+00:00:03  THINK  Let me read the requirements file and repo inventory.\n+00:00:03  TOOL   read /workspace/loan-payments/adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md\n+00:00:03  TOOL   read /workspace/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md\n+00:00:04  THINK  Let me find the inventory file.\n+00:00:04  TOOL   bash ls -la /workspace/ && echo \"---\" && find /workspace -maxdepth 2 -name \"repo-inventory.md\" 2>/dev/null && echo \"---\" && ls -la /workspace/loan-payments/\n+00:00:04  TOOL   bash ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/ 2>/dev/null && echo \"=== lending ===\" && ls -la /workspace/lending/ && echo \"=== customer ===\" && ls -la /workspace/customer/\n+00:00:05  THINK  Let me look at the .datasqrl folder in loan-payments.\n+00:00:05  TOOL   bash find /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl -type f | head -50 && echo \"---adr---\" && ls -la /workspace/loan-payments/adr/\n+00:00:05  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360-shared-package.json\n+00:00:05  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360-test-package.json\n+00:00:05  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360-prod-package.json\n+00:00:06  THINK  Let me read the repo-inventory.md in loan-payments, and lending360.sqrl, and the api folder.\n+00:00:06  TOOL   read /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md\n+00:00:06  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360.sqrl\n+00:00:07  THINK  Now let me look at the lending API folder, the data catalog consumer_loans files, and test data.\n+00:00:07  TOOL   bash find /workspace/lending/lending360-api -type f | sort && echo \"=== snapshots ===\" && find /workspace/lending/snapshots -type f | sort && echo \"=== catalog consumer_loans ===\" && ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/\n+00:00:07  TOOL   read /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl\n+00:00:08  THINK  Now let me read the connector test files and the lending API schema/operations/tests.\n+00:00:08  TOOL   read /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-test.sqrl\n+00:00:08  TOOL   read /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-prod.sqrl\n+00:00:08  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360-api/schema.v1.graphqls\n+00:00:08  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/lending360-api/operations.v1.graphql\n+00:00:09  THINK  Now I need to understand the auth/claim pattern. Let me look for examples of JWT auth in the repo, particularly lending's prod package (no auth?), and maybe the\u2026\n+00:00:09  TOOL   bash grep -rl \"auth\" /workspace --include=\"*.json\" --include=\"*.sqrl\" --include=\"*.graphqls\" --include=\"*.graphql\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v build | head -30; echo \"=== examples ===\"; ls /opt/datasqrl-examples/ 2>/dev/null; echo \"=== readme ===\"; cat /opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md 2>/dev/null | head -80\n+00:00:09  TOOL   read /workspace/data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\n+00:00:09  TOOL   read /workspace/lending/README.md\n+00:00:10  THINK  The User Analytics example is the one for JWT auth. Let me look at it. Let me find the exact auth patterns. Let me read the user_analytics project and docs.\n+00:00:10  TOOL   bash find /opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics -type f | grep -v build | sort\n+00:00:10  TOOL   read /opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/README.md\n+00:00:12  THINK  This is the key example. Let me read the user_analytics main sqrl, test package, prod package, shared package, and test files/properties, and run-tests.sh.\n"}], "details": {}}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:05:05.644000+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccd37a57670897fecbb", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /execute-plan \u2014 Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\n=== progress ===\n+00:00:00  INFO   Image edition: pro (pro-agent)\n+00:00:00  INFO   Judges use together_ai/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash\n+00:00:00  INFO   Discovered 3 sibling entr(ies) (1 shared module(s))\n+00:00:00  RUN    Run started \u00b7 implementation\n+00:00:00  INFO   Wrote repo inventory to .datasqrl/repo-inventory.md\n+00:00:00  STEP   Iteration 0 \u00b7 implementing\n+00:00:02  THINK  Let me start by invoking the execute-plan skill, reading the plan and requirements. Let me also read the repo inventory and requirements file. Let me begin.\n+00:00:02  TEXT   I'll start by invoking the execute-plan skill and reading the plan and requirements.\n+00:00:02  TOOL   read /opt/agent/skills/