{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:07:25.055143+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "source": "agent", "kind": "tool", "tool": "edit", "target": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md", "message": "edit /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:07:25.056211+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccf57bc7003be8179c1", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: write-documentation\ndescription: Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n---\n\nInitialize the README.md if none exists and make sure it contains a) a high level description of the project, b) the motivation and requirements for the project, c) a concise description of the implementation choices and architecture and d) commands the user can use to compile, test, and run the project. \n\nImportant Rules:\n- Update documentation to reflect changes made. Document changes with motivation or requirements.\n- Keep track of progress and features not yet implemented.\n- Write the commands in the README as described in [Commands](#commands).\n\n## Commands\n\nWrite each command for the user's machine. The user runs Docker and has no `/opt/agent/cmd.sh`.\n\nGive the commands in two forms: the project's test script, and `docker run` commands that show the DataSQRL call.\n\n### The test script\n\n`./run-tests.sh` is the project's own runner, and CI uses it. It mounts the repository, passes `-r`, pins the DataSQRL image and runs any setup steps.\n\nStart the block from the repository root. When the project is in a subfolder, put `cd <project-path>` first. `<project-path>` is the `-r <root>` value that `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations` prints. When `<root>` is `.`, leave out the `cd` line.\n\n```bash\ncd <project-path>                                    # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                                       # test every sub-project\n./run-tests.sh <sub-project>                         # test one sub-project\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev <sub-project>     # compile one sub-project with a deployed environment\n```\n\n### The docker run commands\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  compile -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-dev-package.json -b <sub-project>\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  test -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-test-package.json -b <sub-project>\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  run -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-dev-package.json\n```\n\nFill in the values from the project:\n\n1. Run `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations <sub-project>` for the test configs, and `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev <sub-project>` for the compile and run configs. Use `--env local` when the project has a `local` environment and no `dev`. The option prints the invocations, runs nothing and needs no Docker.\n2. Copy `-r <root>` and the config files from those lines, in the same order.\n3. Read `SQRL_VERSION` in `run-tests.sh`.\n4. When the pipeline needs an environment variable, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`, add `-e <NAME>`.\n\nWhen `run-tests.sh` runs a setup step before the suites, state in the README that the `docker run` test command needs that step first.\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `-r <root>` | The project folder, relative to the repository root. `.` when the project is the repository root. |\n| `-b <name>` | Writes the output to `build/<name>`, the same folder that `run-tests.sh` uses. Optional. Keep it when the project has more than one sub-project, so that one build does not overwrite another. |\n| `-it` | Attaches a terminal, so the user can stop `test` or `run` with `Ctrl-C`. Leave it out for `compile`, which stops by itself. |\n| `-p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081` | Opens the API and the Flink WebUI for `run`. Add `-p 9092:9092` to reach Redpanda with Kafka tooling. |\n\nIn the README, explain `-b` in one sentence under the `docker run` commands: `-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nIn Windows PowerShell, use the same mount: `-v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel):/workspace\"`.\n\n### Environments\n\nPair each command with the environment it is made for. `./run-tests.sh --list-envs` prints the environments the project declares.\n\n| Command | Environment | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test` | `test` | The test config adds what the tests need: the snapshot folder, test settings and, in some projects, a test API schema that exposes the `/*+test */` tables. |\n| `compile` | a deployed environment, such as `dev`, `local` or `prod` | A compile builds the pipeline that you deploy. A connector can reach an external system while it compiles, for example S3 through AWS, so a `prod` compile can need production credentials. |\n| `run` | a local environment, such as `dev` or `local` | `run` starts the pipeline on the user's machine. The `prod` config points at production systems, so a local run would read from or write to them. |\n\nWrite every `compile` and every `run` command in the README with `dev`, or with `local` when the project has a `local` environment and no `dev`. The `dev` config needs no production credentials, and a deployed environment works in every project. A compile with the test config (`<sub-project>-test-package.json`) works only when that config leaves the API schema as it is. When the test config swaps in a test API schema, that schema asks for the `/*+test */` tables, which only the `test` command creates. A `compile` or `run` with the test config then fails with `Could not find table or function for field: <Name>Test`.\n\n### Where each command runs\n\n`$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)` mounts the repository root from any folder inside the repository, so `-r <root>` always finds the project. This also makes sibling modules that the project includes (such as `../data-catalog`) available in the container.\n\nA mount of `$PWD` works only from one folder: `-v $PWD` mounts the folder the user starts in, so `-r <root>` finds the project only from the repository root, and a command without `-r` works only from the project folder.\n\nState in the README where each command runs: the `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters. The `docker run` commands work from any folder inside the git repository. From a folder outside it, `git rev-parse` finds no repository and Docker stops with `invalid spec: :/workspace`.\n\nThe `run` command executes the entire pipeline locally and allows the user to interact with it:\n- Flink cluster is accessible through the WebUI at http://localhost:8081/.\n- The Redpanda cluster is accessible on port 9092 (via Kafka command line tooling) when port is mapped.\n- The GraphQL API is accessible at http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/. \n- The Swagger UI for the REST API is accessible at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui\n- The OpenAPI JSON for the REST API is served at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi\n- The MCP API is accessible at `http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795511245055}}
