{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:09:10.157202+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_update", "usage": {"input": 611, "output": 340, "cacheRead": 189312, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 0, "totalTokens": 190263, "cost": {"input": 0.00018329999999999998, "output": 0.000408, "cacheRead": 0.001135872, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001727172}}, "assistantMessageEvent": {"type": "text_end", "contentIndex": 0, "content": "The implementation is complete. All 13 tests pass (8 API tests + 5 table tests), both test and prod configs compile, and all plan steps are done.\n\nSummary of what was built in `loan-payments/`:\n- **Sources**: imports the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan tables via `script.include` \u2014 no copied or redeclared sources.\n- **Processing** (`loan-payments.sqrl`): latest-version CDC dedup by `source_updated_at`, personal-only payment filter, loan enrichment, and a left-joined applied/returned aggregation anchored on all personal loans (so payment-free loans get zero counts and a null last payment date).\n- **API**: custom GraphQL schema plus REST/MCP operations for `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`, `LoanPayments(loan_id)`, and `CustomerLoans`, with `customer_id` injected from the JWT claim and never caller-visible.\n- **Auth**: Vert.x `auth` providers in the test and prod overlays (HS256 test secret; `${AUTH_ISSUER}`/`${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`/`${JWT_SECRET}` in prod).\n- **Tests**: API tests for the returned payment, late fees, zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, wrong-customer and missing-token cases; plus inferred-schema table tests for row counts, no auto-loan leakage, and key uniqueness.\n- **Docs**: README covering sources, architecture, API, authorization, FR-8-by-construction, and the no-new-DQ decision.\n\n./run-tests.sh"}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:09:10.158451+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "source": "agent", "kind": "text", "message": "The implementation is complete. All 13 tests pass (8 API tests + 5 table tests), both test and prod configs compile, and all plan steps are done.\n\nSummary of what was built in `loan-payments/`:\n- **Sources**: imports the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan tables via `script.include` \u2014 no copied or redeclared sources.\n- **Processing** (`loan-payments.sqrl`): latest-version CDC dedup by `source_updated_at`, personal-only payment filter, loan enrichment, and a left-joined applied/returned aggregation anchored on all personal loans (so payment-free loans get zero counts and a null last payment date).\n- **API**: custom GraphQL schema plus REST/MCP operations for `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`, `LoanPayments(loan_id)`, and `CustomerLoans`, with `customer_id` injected from the JWT claim and never caller-visible.\n- **Auth**: Vert.x `auth` providers in the test and prod overlays (HS256 test secret; `${AUTH_ISSUER}`/`${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`/`${JWT_SECRET}` in prod).\n- **Tests**: API tests for the returned payment, late fees, zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, wrong-customer and missing-token cases; plus inferred-schema table tests for row counts, no auto-loan leakage, and key uniqueness.\n- **Docs**: README covering sources, architecture, API, authorization, FR-8-by-construction, and the no-new-DQ decision.\n\n./run-tests.sh"}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:09:10.159865+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The implementation is complete. All 13 tests pass (8 API tests + 5 table tests), both test and prod configs compile, and all plan steps are done.\n\nSummary of what was built in `loan-payments/`:\n- **Sources**: imports the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan tables via `script.include` \u2014 no copied or redeclared sources.\n- **Processing** (`loan-payments.sqrl`): latest-version CDC dedup by `source_updated_at`, personal-only payment filter, loan enrichment, and a left-joined applied/returned aggregation anchored on all personal loans (so payment-free loans get zero counts and a null last payment date).\n- **API**: custom GraphQL schema plus REST/MCP operations for `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`, `LoanPayments(loan_id)`, and `CustomerLoans`, with `customer_id` injected from the JWT claim and never caller-visible.\n- **Auth**: Vert.x `auth` providers in the test and prod overlays (HS256 test secret; `${AUTH_ISSUER}`/`${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`/`${JWT_SECRET}` in prod).\n- **Tests**: API tests for the returned payment, late fees, zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, wrong-customer and missing-token cases; plus inferred-schema table tests for row counts, no auto-loan leakage, and key uniqueness.\n- **Docs**: README covering sources, architecture, API, authorization, FR-8-by-construction, and the no-new-DQ decision.\n\n./run-tests.sh"}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 611, "output": 340, "cacheRead": 189312, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 0, "totalTokens": 190263, "cost": {"input": 0.00018329999999999998, "output": 0.000408, "cacheRead": 0.001135872, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001727172}}, "stopReason": "stop", "timestamp": 1795511347130, "responseId": "a3f7a6966982476b9b7d443fe8eb10fe-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "stop", "durationMs": 3028, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": []}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:09:10.177896+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_end", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "", "sections": {"preamble": "You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.", "tools": "<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- grep: Search file contents for patterns (respects .gitignore)\n- find: Find files by glob pattern (respects .gitignore)\n- ls: List directory contents\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>", "rules": "<rules>\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>", "docs": "<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>", "addendum": "<addendum>\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Starting an implementation run, resuming after a compaction, or choosing the next plan step                           | `/execute-plan`            | **MUST** \u2014 the loop that drives every step below (see \"Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\") |\n| Initializing a new project                                                                                            | `/init-sqrl`               | If applicable                                      |\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n## Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\n\nYour implementation plan is in `adr/plan_<ts>.md` (the exact path is in your \"## Requirements &\nplan checklist\" context). Its `## Implementation Checklist` is a list of markdown checkboxes and is\nyour **durable, on-disk progress state**. Invoke the `/execute-plan` skill at the start of the run and\nagain after any compaction \u2014 it is the loop for the rules below.\n\n- Resume at the in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several); otherwise take the first\n  `- [ ]` box whose `[deps: ...]` are all done.\n- Mark a box `- [*]` when you start its step; before the step, read its `\u2192 skill: /name` (and any\n  MUST skills for the files you touch).\n- Mark a box `- [x]` **only after** that step's work is done. Edit the plan file in place; mark one box at a time.\n- Never redo a `- [x]` step. Mark a `[cond: ...]` step that does not apply as `- [~]` with a short\n  reason (`- [ ]` = todo, `- [*]` = in progress, `- [x]` = done, `- [~]` = skipped).\n- After any compaction, re-read the checklist and resume at the first `- [*]` (or, if none, `- [ ]`).\n- Keep the checklist current: if the plan changes, add/re-scope `- [ ]` steps (never delete or\n  renumber existing steps \u2014 `[deps: N]` references them). Change only checkbox glyphs; never disturb the frontmatter or `## Assumptions` section.\n\nInvoke the `/execute-plan` skill for the full loop.\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nAn implementation run changes only what its plan checklist asks for. Code that already exists and works is **out of scope**, especially if the change is not necessary.\n\n- Touch a file only if a checklist step names it, or it must change for a named step to compile or pass its tests.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when necessary and a plan step requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n\n# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /execute-plan \u2014 Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\nThis repo contains other SQRL projects/modules. `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists them (paths, READMEs, SQRL files, package summaries) \u2014 read it before deciding how to build. They are mounted READ-ONLY: never edit them, never copy their files into this project (include shared modules via `script.include` in `*-package.json` files instead), and never read their `build/` directories.\n\n## Requirements & plan checklist\nYour requirements and implementation plan are in `adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` (authoritative). Read it before implementing and re-read it after any compaction. Its `## Implementation Checklist` is your durable progress state: resume at an in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several), else the first unchecked `- [ ]`; mark a step `- [*]` when you start it and `- [x]` only once it compiles/passes; never redo a `- [x]` step. Follow the `execute-plan` skill.\nSkill form takes precedence over step wording: where a step and its skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form and correct the step text. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n</addendum>", "project_context": "<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/workspace/loan-payments/AGENTS.md\">\nDataSQRL is a framework for building data pipelines and data products that ingest, process, store, and expose data as data lake/database views or through GraphQL, REST, or MCP APIs. It compiles SQL-like SQRL code into integrated data pipelines using Apache Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x server and other engines.\nDataSQRL runs and tests data pipelines locally or compiles deployment artifacts to deploy by the user or fully-managed by DataSQRL Cloud product offering.\n\n## Development Commands\n\nYou are running in **headless mode** inside a DataSQRL container using DataSQRL CLI version 0.11.x to:\n- compile a project with: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r <project root> <configs...> -b <sub-project>` \n- The `build/` folder contains the build artifacts for each sub-project seperately with `-b` flag and the build/<sub-project-name>/deploy/plan folder contains all deployment artifacts for each engine.\n\nYou are working on a git repository.\n\n**The compile command is slow and exclusive. Follow these rules without exception:**\n\n1. **Execute one command at a time.** Command invocations are synchronized on a shared lock. A second compile launched while one is running will queue and wait, it will NOT run in parallel. Never launch multiple compile from more than one place simultaneously.\n2. **Always run command synchronously in the foreground, and NEVER pipe it into anything.** No `run_in_background`, no `| tail`, `| grep`, `| head`, no `$(...)`. A run starts Postgres, Redpanda and a background helper that all inherit the command's stdout, and they can outlive the command (`head` also kills the build outright via SIGPIPE.) **Redirect to a file and read the file instead:** `/opt/agent/cmd.sh test x-test-package.json > /tmp/test.log 2>&1` then read `/tmp/test.log`.\n3. **Command can take multiple minutes.** Flink, Postgres, and Redpanda must start before tests begin. No output for the first minute or two is normal \u2014 do not assume failure.\n4. **Wait for `COMPILE_DONE: exit_code=N`.** This line marks the end of every compile. Only read results after seeing it. `exit_code=0` means success; any other value means failure.\n5. **If you see `COMPILE_QUEUED:`, stop.** Another compile is already running. Do not launch another. Wait for the current one to finish.\n\n## Writing Files\n\n**Write one file per writing call. Never assemble multiple files or an entire project in a single response.**\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}.sqrl                           # Main data processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-[dev/test/prod]-package.json   # Configuration per environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-connectors/                    # Project specific source/sink definitions, {name}- is optional\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 sources-[dev/test/prod].sqrl      # Source tables by environment\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/{name}/                     # Snapshot data for tests, subfolders for multiple projects\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {name}-api/                           # API definitions (optional)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql             # Defines persisted queries, REST endpoints or MCP tools\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                            # Test queries (.graphql)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh                          # Runs the test suites \u2014 the agent, CI and you all run this one script\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                             # Project documentation\n```\n\nLayouts vary \u2014 treat the above as the common shape, not a required one.\n\nYour project may be alone in its repository, or it may sit alongside other SQRL projects and shared\nmodules. Those are **siblings \u2014 outside your project, not part of it**. Their directory names are\nwhatever the repository author chose, so never assume a particular name: read\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` for what is actually present. For example:\n\n```\n{repo root}/                   # mounted READ-ONLY\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {your-project}/            # the ONLY directory you can write to\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 {another-project}/         # e.g. a second pipeline \u2014 reference only\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 {shared-module}/           # e.g. a data catalog or metrics layer \u2014 reference only\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe `*-package.json` files configure every aspect of a DataSQRL project (main script, engines, API, tests, compiler, deployment). \nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill before editing any configuration file (base config and environment overlays, `script.include` for included modules like data catalog). \nEach (sub-)project, and each environment of those (sub-)projects has its own configuration. \nWhen you create a pipeline project or sub-project, give it a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` environment overlay. Leave out `-dev` or `-prod` only when the requirements say that the environment is not needed. The `-dev` overlay uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine.\nTo prepare tests, compile and run tests; first invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and then apply the steps.\nAlways check the `package.json` files for authoritative file paths, and align `README.md` with the project-specific settings.\n\n## Skill Usage Policy\n\nBefore any file change, work through this checklist:\n\n1. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n2. Check each skill's trigger condition against your action and invoke every match:\n\n| Trigger condition                                                                                                     | Skill                      | Required?                                          |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n                                                                                                          | Starting an implementation run, resuming after a compaction, or choosing the next plan step                           | `/execute-plan`            | **MUST** \u2014 the loop that drives every step below (see \"Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\") |\n| Initializing a new project                                                                                            | `/init-sqrl`               | If applicable                                      |\n                                                                                                          | Writing or editing a `.sqrl` file                                                                                     | `/implement-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing any source/sink definition file (typically a `connectors/` folder, or a catalog you own like `data_catalog/`) | `/manage-connector`        | **MUST** \u2014 even when following an existing pattern |\n| Editing a `*-package.json`                                                                                            | `/configure-sqrl`          | If applicable                                      |\n| Adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` API schema                                 | `/design-api`              | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| When requirements specify the API schema that the SQRL project requires                                               | `/from-api`                | **MUST** \u2014 including on first creation             |\n| Writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, creating or editing `run-tests.sh`, or first test run on a project  | `/test-sqrl`               | **MUST** \u2014 no exceptions                           |\n| Creating or editing data catalog                                                                                      | `/build-catalog`           | **MUST** - no excpetions                           |\n| Test or compile error not immediately obvious                                                                         | `/troubleshoot-test`       | If applicable                                      |\n| Given provided source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling                                               | `/data-observation`        | If applicable                                      |\n| Writing or updating `README.md` or any other documentation                                                            | `/write-documentation`     | If applicable                                      |\n| A large data file is present, or a run reports `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`                                                   | `/handle-large-data-files` | If applicable \u2014 reactive                     |\n\n3. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n4. For **MUST** skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n5. **Skill form takes precedence over plan-step wording.** Where a plan step and a skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then correct the step's text to describe what you built. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler.\n\nInvoke skills immediately before the file change they apply to \u2014 not all at once at session start.\n\nConsult skills before reading templates \u2014 skills contain the essential patterns; templates are for structural reference only.\n\n## Plan Execution & Progress Tracking\n\nYour implementation plan is in `adr/plan_<ts>.md` (the exact path is in your \"## Requirements &\nplan checklist\" context). Its `## Implementation Checklist` is a list of markdown checkboxes and is\nyour **durable, on-disk progress state**. Invoke the `/execute-plan` skill at the start of the run and\nagain after any compaction \u2014 it is the loop for the rules below.\n\n- Resume at the in-progress `- [*]` step (the earliest, if several); otherwise take the first\n  `- [ ]` box whose `[deps: ...]` are all done.\n- Mark a box `- [*]` when you start its step; before the step, read its `\u2192 skill: /name` (and any\n  MUST skills for the files you touch).\n- Mark a box `- [x]` **only after** that step's work is done. Edit the plan file in place; mark one box at a time.\n- Never redo a `- [x]` step. Mark a `[cond: ...]` step that does not apply as `- [~]` with a short\n  reason (`- [ ]` = todo, `- [*]` = in progress, `- [x]` = done, `- [~]` = skipped).\n- After any compaction, re-read the checklist and resume at the first `- [*]` (or, if none, `- [ ]`).\n- Keep the checklist current: if the plan changes, add/re-scope `- [ ]` steps (never delete or\n  renumber existing steps \u2014 `[deps: N]` references them). Change only checkbox glyphs; never disturb the frontmatter or `## Assumptions` section.\n\nInvoke the `/execute-plan` skill for the full loop.\n\n## Other Projects in the Repository \u2014 Read-Only\n\nYour project can sit inside a repository that also holds other SQRL projects and shared modules\n(a data catalog is the common case, but there may be several, with any names). The whole repository\nis mounted **READ-ONLY**; only your own project directory is writable.\n`.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists what is actually there \u2014 rely on it rather than on assumed names\nor layouts.\n\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything outside your project.\n- **Never copy** a sibling's files into your project. If you need what a shared module provides,\n  declare it under `script.include` and import it by namespace.\n- **Never read another project's build folders** (`build/`, `build-*/`) \u2014 they are transient compiler output. The `.sqrl`, `package.json`, and `README.md` files in the project folder contain all relevant information\n- Only include modules that this project **actually needs**. \n- Sibling projects are useful as **worked examples**: how they configure `script.include`, how they\n  import a module, how they lay out connectors and tests. Follow those patterns where they fit.\n\n## Scope \u2014 Do Not Change What You Were Not Asked to Change\n\nAn implementation run changes only what its plan checklist asks for. Code that already exists and works is **out of scope**, especially if the change is not necessary.\n\n- Touch a file only if a checklist step names it, or it must change for a named step to compile or pass its tests.\n- **\"For consistency with the new code\" is never a reason to rewrite existing code.** Match the\n  existing code's conventions in what you add; never migrate the existing code to match yours unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Data Processing\n\nThe data ingestion, processing, and serving logic is implemented in the main script using a SQL dialect called SQRL which is an extension of Flink SQL 2.3. The main script may import additional SQRL scripts: local ones by relative path, scripts of another SQRL project (e.g. a shared data catalog) by the namespace declared under configuration file: invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement). SQRL scripts can import Flink functions implemented in Java for custom processing logic that cannot be expressed in SQL: invoke the `/implement-udf` skill.\n\nAll data processing is implemented in SQRL scripts and imported functions.\n\n## Sources and Sinks\n\nSource/sink tables are defined with `CREATE TABLE` statements - often in dedicated SQRL files separated by environment in a connectors folder and imported/exported from the main script.\n\nSources are used to ingest data for processing. Sinks are used to export data to external systems. `CREATE TABLE` statements with a preceding `/*+engine(..) */` hint are sources/sinks that are managed by DataSQRL with the external resources (e.g. Kafka topic or Iceberg table) created and managed automatically.\n\nSources and sinks local to the project (usually in a `connectors` folder) can be updated when necessary and a plan step requires it \u2014 see **Scope** above. Sources, sinks and imported view definitions that come from a shared module (a data catalog or similar, whatever it is named) belong to other teams/projects and cannot be changed \u2014 they are mounted read-only. Always check whether an import is local or comes from a shared module before making changes.\n\n## API\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL as the authoritative model for the data serving API which exposes the processed data if the server engine is enabled. The GraphQL API maps tables in the main script (and those imported inline) to queries/mutations/subscriptions plus associated types and relationships to fields. SQRL supports table functions to define custom endpoints.\nThe GraphQL schema is either inferred from SQRL or defined explicitly and configured in the package.json. The inferred GraphQL schema is written to `<build-folder>/inferred_schema.graphqls` after compilation (the folder named by `-b`, else `build/`).\n\nREST and MCP (model context protocol) endpoints are created automatically for each GraphQL query and mutation or defined explicitly as GraphQL operations.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe test command executes the GraphQL queries in the configured test folder and the table queries marked with the `/*+test*/` hint that the test server's schema exposes, and stores the results in the snapshots folder. Tests pass when snapshots are identical or no records are returned on a test marked `no_rows`. `${DATA_PATH}` is set by the framework and does not need to be configured. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for writing tests and test data, and for running the test packages.\n\n## Documentation\n\nClearly and concisely document any code you implement focusing on implementation choices and intent. Do not document what is implemented but focus on the *why?*.\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>", "skills": "<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>build-catalog</name>\n    <description>Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/build-catalog/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>configure-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when configuring a DataSQRL project&apos;s `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>data-observation</name>\n    <description>Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/data-observation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-api</name>\n    <description>Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/design-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>execute-plan</name>\n    <description>Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a &quot;your conversation was compacted&quot; reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_&lt;ts&gt;.md.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/execute-plan/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>from-api</name>\n    <description>Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/from-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>handle-large-data-files</name>\n    <description>Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/handle-large-data-files/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-udf</name>\n    <description>Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-udf/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-ui</name>\n    <description>Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>init-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/init-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>manage-connector</name>\n    <description>Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/manage-connector/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>monitor-pipeline</name>\n    <description>Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline&apos;s monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/monitor-pipeline/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize-deployment</name>\n    <description>Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.&lt;flink|postgres|vertx&gt;.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/optimize-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>troubleshoot-test</name>\n    <description>Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/troubleshoot-test/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>write-documentation</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/write-documentation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>", "cwd": "<cwd>\n/workspace/loan-payments\n</cwd>"}, "timestamp": 1795510933924, "toolsAdded": [{"name": "read", "description": "Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"}, "offset": {"type": "number", "description": "Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of lines to read"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "bash", "description": "Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["command"], "properties": {"command": {"type": "string", "description": "Shell command to execute"}, "timeout": {"type": "number", "description": "Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "edit", "description": "Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "edits"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"}, "edits": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["oldText", "newText"], "properties": {"oldText": {"type": "string", "description": "Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."}, "newText": {"type": "string", "description": "Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}}, "description": "One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "write", "description": "Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "content"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"}, "content": {"type": "string", "description": "Content to write to the file"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "grep", "description": "Search file contents for a pattern. Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 100 matches or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Long lines are truncated to 500 chars.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Search pattern (regex or literal string)"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory or file to search (default: current directory)"}, "glob": {"type": "string", "description": "Filter files by glob pattern, e.g. '*.ts' or '**/*.spec.ts'"}, "ignoreCase": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false)"}, "literal": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Treat pattern as literal string instead of regex (default: false)"}, "context": {"type": "number", "description": "Number of lines to show before and after each match (default: 0)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of matches to return (default: 100)"}}}}, {"name": "find", "description": "Search for files by glob pattern. Returns matching file paths relative to the search directory. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 1000 results or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Glob pattern to match files, e.g. '*.ts', '**/*.json', or 'src/**/*.spec.ts'"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to search in (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of results (default: 1000)"}}}}, {"name": "ls", "description": "List directory contents. Returns entries sorted alphabetically, with '/' suffix for directories. Includes dotfiles. Output is truncated to 500 entries or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to list (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of entries to return (default: 500)"}}}}]}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# DataSQRL Requirements Implementation Task\n\n## Context\nYou are implementing a DataSQRL project.\n\n## Implement User Requirements\n\nYour primary goal is to implement the following user requirements:\n---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\nA new DataSQRL pipeline in `loan-payments/` that reads the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan\nsources, builds a per-loan payment summary and a per-payment table, and serves them through a\nJWT-authorized GraphQL/REST/MCP API. It reuses the catalog connectors, its test data and the\nproject layout of the sibling `lending/` project, and adds no data of its own.\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\nThe specification lives in `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`: goal and scope, `### Sources`,\n`### Time semantics`, `### Entities and keys`, `### Transformations and aggregations`,\n`### API surface`, `### Test data and acceptance criteria`, `### Non-functional requirements`,\n`### Hard constraints`, and `### Open Questions`. Build the project to satisfy `FR-1`\u2013`FR-13`\nand the acceptance criteria there; the payload shapes and expected test values are quoted there\nand are not repeated in this plan.\n\nThe sibling `lending/` project (`lending/lending360.sqrl`, `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`,\n`lending/lending360-test-package.json`, `lending/lending360-prod-package.json`, and its\n`lending/lending360-api/` folder) is the layout and API-configuration reference. The shared\n`data-catalog` module (sibling directory, read-only) supplies the source tables `Personal_Loan`\nand `Loan_Payment` and their test data; the project imports them and never copies or edits them.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\nLegend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n- **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n  payments to `Personal_Loan`: a `PERSONAL` payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish\n  from `LoanPayments` and undercount its summary. The catalog already guarantees this cannot\n  happen (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture table is warranted. FR-8 is handled\n  by construction (latest-version dedup feeding an unwindowed re-aggregation), not by monitoring.\n- **Skip** \u2014 do not repeat the catalog's referential-integrity, enum, or numeric-sanity assertions;\n  do not add ingestion counters for the two sources, because the independent-sender failure mode\n  does not apply and the catalog's root snapshot queries already surface content changes; do not\n  monitor the intentional auto-loan exclusion, which is a required filter, not an anomaly.\n- **Reuse** \u2014 the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology snapshot queries, and the\n  `lending/` project's convention of carrying no pipeline-local DQ tables.\n\n## Technical Decisions\n- A custom GraphQL schema plus operations (not an inferred schema) is used, because `customer_id`\n  must be injected from the JWT rather than accepted from the caller and the exposed type names are\n  fixed; the API is configured like `lending/lending360-api/`.\n- The engine set is Flink + Postgres + Vert.x. No `kafka` engine is enabled: the API has no mutation\n  or subscription, and the catalog's Kafka sources are external Flink connectors that do not need\n  the DataSQRL-managed Kafka engine.\n- The catalog is included through one of its top-level package files with the batch mode disabled,\n  exactly like the sibling `lending/` base config; the consumer's per-environment configuration\n  selects which catalog connector file it imports, so the included module's own environment setting\n  never reaches the running pipeline.\n- CDC streams are reduced to the latest version per key ordered by the source update timestamp, and\n  the summary is an unwindowed re-aggregation, so a late or changed payment updates the API within\n  the streaming freshness target instead of waiting for a window boundary.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary` is anchored on the latest personal loans and left-joins the applied-payment\n  aggregates. Grouping only the payments would omit the loans that have none, which the accepted\n  Open-Question default requires to appear with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Authorization is implemented as claim-injected table functions and relationships over an internal\n  state table, with a per-environment Vert.x auth provider; a required claim argument produces the\n  required authorization error when the claim is absent.\n\n## Assumptions\n**FR-8 coverage**: Assumed FR-8 is verified by construction \u2014 the latest-version `DISTINCT` feeds an\nunwindowed re-aggregation \u2014 and documented in the README instead of by a snapshot. Rationale: no\ncatalog payment changes status, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data of our own.\n[impact: low]\n\n**Catalog include config**: Assumed the catalog is included with one of its top-level package files\nand `is_batch` false (as `lending/` does) while the consumer's `environment` selects the connector\nfile. Rationale: the consumer imports only the `-{{environment}}` connector, so the include's own\nenvironment setting is inert. [impact: medium]\n\n**Freshness**: Assumed new or changed payments appear within one minute through the streaming Flink\npipeline with no window. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`, and the\ncatalog watermarks (10 s test, 30 s prod) are used as-is. [impact: high]\n\n**JWT validation**: Assumed HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test` and a key from `${JWT_SECRET}`\nin `prod`, with issuer and audience supplied as environment variables. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault in `### Open Questions`. [impact: high]\n\n**Production workload**: Assumed up to 1,000,000 personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and a\n50 requests/second peak. Rationale: the recommended default; it only affects deployment sizing.\n[impact: medium]\n\n**Dev/local environment**: Assumed only `test` and `prod` overlays, because the scope names exactly\nthose two and follows `lending/`. [impact: low]\n\n**Loans without payments**: Assumed `LoanPaymentSummary` returns a row for every personal loan, with\nzero counts/sums and a null `last_payment_date` when no applied payment exists. Rationale: the\nrecommended default and the acceptance criterion for `PL-011`. [impact: medium]\n\n**Loan status**: Assumed the API exposes loans in every status, not only `ACTIVE`. Rationale: the\nrecommended default, and the scope keeps all statuses. [impact: medium]\n\n**Last payment date**: Assumed the latest `applied_date` among applied payments. Rationale: the\nrecommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Total amount paid**: Assumed `total_paid_cents` is the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied\npayments. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Customer-query loan details**: Assumed the signed-in customer's loans also show `loan_status`,\n`original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n`next_payment_due_date`. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**API protocols**: Assumed GraphQL, REST and MCP, matching `lending/`. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault and `### API surface`. [impact: low]\n\n**Budget**: Assumed the smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment size. Rationale: the recommended default;\nno budget was given. [impact: low]\n\n\n---\n\n## Pipeline Implementation\n\n### Self-Improvement Loop\n\nYou MUST follow this iterative development process:\n\n1. **Implement**: Write or modify the project code and configuration to meet the requirements.\n   Before any file change, work through this checklist:\n   a. State what you are about to do (file type, what it changes).\n   b. Match your action against the triggers below and invoke every skill that matches:\n      - Invoke the `/init-sqrl` skill when initializing a new project.\n      - Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill when writing or editing a `.sqrl` file.\n      - Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill when writing any file in `connectors/` or `data_catalog/`. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, even when following an existing pattern.\n      - Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill when editing a `*-package.json`.\n      - Invoke the `/design-api` skill when adding or modifying a SUBSCRIBE statement, mutation table, or `.graphql`/`.graphqls` file. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/from-api` skill when the requirements themselves specify the API to expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, MCP tools). **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally, including on first creation.\n      - Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill when writing a `/*+test*/` table, modifying snapshots, or running tests for the first time on a project. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally.\n      - Invoke the `/monitor-pipeline` skill when writing any data-quality capture or observability stats table. **MUST** \u2014 invoke unconditionally. Treat catalog assertions as upstream guarantees unless explicitly documented otherwise. Update the plan when monitoring should be added, narrowed, removed, or reused. \n      - Invoke the `/data-observation` skill when given source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling.\n      - Invoke the `/handle-large-data-files` skill when a large data file is present, or a run reported `LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED`.\n   c. For non-MUST skills: only skip if you explicitly state why it does not apply to this specific action.\n   d. For MUST skills: \"I already know the pattern\" is not a valid reason to skip.\n2. **Compile**: Run the DataSQRL compiler for every generated or affected (sub-)project with the help of the `/configure-sqrl` skill (How to Compile/Test).\n3. **Test**: Run the tests of every generated or affected (sub-)project using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests).\n4. **Review**: Analyze compiler output and test results\n5. **Iterate**: If there are errors or test failures, fix them and repeat from step 2\n\n**Continue this loop until**:\n- The project compiles without errors\n- All tests pass successfully\n- The implementation meets all user requirements\n- Code follows DataSQRL best practices\n- Test snapshots are validated\n- Documentation is updated\n\nYour last action should be running the tests successfully.\n\n### Critical Rules\n\n- Always review relevant reference projects in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md` before implementing new features.\n- Always create test cases for features you implement and make sure all tests pass. Validate new snapshots.\n- Always ensure all package.json files for the project compile successfully.\n- The project must have a runnable `run-tests.sh` at its root whose suite lines cover every test.\n- If the project consumes a shared SQRL module (e.g. a data catalog mounted as a read-only sibling), never modify the module; invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Included Projects) and the `/implement-sqrl` skill, and compile/test using the `/test-sqrl` skill (Running Tests, Project inside a repository).\n- Always review and create/update README.md with the `/write-documentation` skill.\n- Clean up at the end of the implementation: Delete copied files that are no longer needed.\n\n### Response\n\nYour last action must be a successful run of `./run-tests.sh`, scoped with the names of every sub-project you generated or edited plus the ones affected by an edit to shared code or configuration, use the script with no arguments when run all (sub-)projects test or the project is new. Output that exact invocation as your final line, no markdown, no explanation, just the bare command. \n\nExample: \n```\n./run-tests.sh [affected-sub-project-names] # only the affected sub-project(s) \n```\nand if change affect every sub-project, or completely new project:\n```\n./run-tests.sh\n```\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795510933924}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me start by invoking the execute-plan skill, reading the plan and requirements. Let me also read the repo inventory and requirements file.\n\nLet me begin.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I'll start by invoking the execute-plan skill and reading the plan and requirements."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cca9d6b7682a9dc4401", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/execute-plan/SKILL.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cca9d9b79f18d882492", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 16024, "output": 142, "cacheRead": 640, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 31, "totalTokens": 16806, "cost": {"input": 0.0048072, "output": 0.0001704, "cacheRead": 3.84e-06, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00498144}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510933937, "responseId": "579b3b6367524d428f6f29553bebdb50-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1242, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cca9d6b7682a9dc4401", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: execute-plan\ndescription: Use when you have implementation plan at the beginning of an implementation run and after every context compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder) to work through the plan in adr/plan_<ts>.md.\nmodes: [implementation]\n---\nThe plan's Implementation Checklist is durable, on-disk progress state. Resume at the in-progress step, mark boxes as you go, and never lose your place across compaction.\n\n# Execute the Implementation Plan\n\nYour implementation plan is in `adr/plan_<ts>.md` (the exact path is in your \"## Requirements & plan checklist\" context block). Its `## Implementation Checklist` is a list of markdown checkboxes, and that on-disk checklist is the single source of truth for what is done and what remains. Treat the plan file as your durable memory.\n\n## Box legend\n- `- [ ]` \u2014 not started (this is the work that remains)\n- `- [*]` \u2014 in progress: you have started this step but not finished it (one or more may be active,\n  e.g. parallel-candidate steps)\n- `- [x]` \u2014 complete and verified (its work compiles / its tests pass, as the step requires)\n- `- [~]` \u2014 skipped: a `[cond: ...]` step whose condition did not apply (append a short reason)\n\n## The loop\n1. **Read the checklist before doing anything** \u2014 at the start of the run and again after any compaction (a \"your conversation was compacted\" reminder, or a summarized transcript, means: re-read now). To stay cheap, re-read **only the `## Implementation Checklist` section** of the plan file, not the whole file; consult the other sections when you need their detail.\n2. **Find where to resume.** If any step is marked `- [*]`, resume at the earliest such step \u2014 that is where you were working. Otherwise take the first `- [ ]` step whose `[deps: ...]` are all `- [x]` (or `- [~]`). Never redo a `- [x]` step.\n3. **Mark the step `- [*]`** in the plan file before you start its work.\n4. **Honor `[cond: ...]`.** If the step is conditional and its condition cannot apply (e.g. `[cond: tests fail after step 5]` but step 5's tests passed), mark it as skipped (`- [~]`) with a one-line reason and move on. If the condition is not yet decided, leave it `- [ ]`, continue with the next actionable step, and return once the condition resolves.\n5. **Invoke the step's annotated skill (`\u2192 skill: /name`)** \u2014 read its SKILL.md in full before doing the step's work, plus any MUST skills the AGENTS.md skill policy requires for the files you touch.\n6. **Do the step**, follow the instructions giving in the step and complete the step.\n   **Skill form takes precedence over step wording.** Where a step and its skill differ on import form, connector pattern, API exposure, hint placement, or syntax, implement the skill's form, then edit the step text to describe what you built and append a one-line note of the change. Plan steps state intent and are written without a compiler; the skill and the compiler determine form.\n7. **Mark the box `- [x]` only after the step's work is actually done** \u2014 for code steps, that means it compiles and its tests pass; for a doc/cleanup step, that it is complete. Edit the plan file in place to change `- [*]` \u2192 `- [x]` for exactly that step. Mark one box at a time as you finish it. Never mark ahead of the work.\n8. **Repeat** from step 2 until no `- [ ]` or `- [*]` boxes remain (only `- [x]` / `- [~]`).\n\n## Keeping the checklist correct\n- If implementation shows the plan is wrong or incomplete, update the checklist: append new `- [ ]` steps (continue the numbering) or re-scope an existing step, then keep executing. The checklist must always reflect the real remaining work.\n- This applies to technical corrections as well as scope. When a step's stated approach differs from what the skill and the compiler accept, rewrite that step to describe what you built, so a post-compaction read of the checklist matches the code on disk.\n- The plan's `## Data Quality & Observability` section is kept correct the same way: when the step adds a capture or stats table the plan did not list, append it to that section with its one-line reason and append its checklist step.\n- Parallel-candidate steps (separate boxes sharing the same `[deps]`) may be worked in any order; mark each independently.\n- Do NOT delete or renumber existing steps \u2014 `[deps: N]` references them by number.\n- When marking a box, change ONLY the checkbox glyph (and, for `- [~]`, append a short reason). Never disturb the YAML frontmatter or the `## Assumptions` section of the plan file.\n\n## After compaction\nRe-read the `## Implementation Checklist`, scan it top-to-bottom, and resume at the `- [*]` step (or, if none, the first `- [ ]`). Everything above it (`- [x]` / `- [~]`) is already handled, so do not repeat it. Marking boxes as you go is exactly what makes this recovery reliable.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795510935190}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cca9d9b79f18d882492", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\nA new DataSQRL pipeline in `loan-payments/` that reads the shared `data-catalog` consumer-loan\nsources, builds a per-loan payment summary and a per-payment table, and serves them through a\nJWT-authorized GraphQL/REST/MCP API. It reuses the catalog connectors, its test data and the\nproject layout of the sibling `lending/` project, and adds no data of its own.\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\nThe specification lives in `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`: goal and scope, `### Sources`,\n`### Time semantics`, `### Entities and keys`, `### Transformations and aggregations`,\n`### API surface`, `### Test data and acceptance criteria`, `### Non-functional requirements`,\n`### Hard constraints`, and `### Open Questions`. Build the project to satisfy `FR-1`\u2013`FR-13`\nand the acceptance criteria there; the payload shapes and expected test values are quoted there\nand are not repeated in this plan.\n\nThe sibling `lending/` project (`lending/lending360.sqrl`, `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`,\n`lending/lending360-test-package.json`, `lending/lending360-prod-package.json`, and its\n`lending/lending360-api/` folder) is the layout and API-configuration reference. The shared\n`data-catalog` module (sibling directory, read-only) supplies the source tables `Personal_Loan`\nand `Loan_Payment` and their test data; the project imports them and never copies or edits them.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\nLegend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n- **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n  payments to `Personal_Loan`: a `PERSONAL` payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish\n  from `LoanPayments` and undercount its summary. The catalog already guarantees this cannot\n  happen (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture table is warranted. FR-8 is handled\n  by construction (latest-version dedup feeding an unwindowed re-aggregation), not by monitoring.\n- **Skip** \u2014 do not repeat the catalog's referential-integrity, enum, or numeric-sanity assertions;\n  do not add ingestion counters for the two sources, because the independent-sender failure mode\n  does not apply and the catalog's root snapshot queries already surface content changes; do not\n  monitor the intentional auto-loan exclusion, which is a required filter, not an anomaly.\n- **Reuse** \u2014 the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology snapshot queries, and the\n  `lending/` project's convention of carrying no pipeline-local DQ tables.\n\n## Technical Decisions\n- A custom GraphQL schema plus operations (not an inferred schema) is used, because `customer_id`\n  must be injected from the JWT rather than accepted from the caller and the exposed type names are\n  fixed; the API is configured like `lending/lending360-api/`.\n- The engine set is Flink + Postgres + Vert.x. No `kafka` engine is enabled: the API has no mutation\n  or subscription, and the catalog's Kafka sources are external Flink connectors that do not need\n  the DataSQRL-managed Kafka engine.\n- The catalog is included through one of its top-level package files with the batch mode disabled,\n  exactly like the sibling `lending/` base config; the consumer's per-environment configuration\n  selects which catalog connector file it imports, so the included module's own environment setting\n  never reaches the running pipeline.\n- CDC streams are reduced to the latest version per key ordered by the source update timestamp, and\n  the summary is an unwindowed re-aggregation, so a late or changed payment updates the API within\n  the streaming freshness target instead of waiting for a window boundary.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary` is anchored on the latest personal loans and left-joins the applied-payment\n  aggregates. Grouping only the payments would omit the loans that have none, which the accepted\n  Open-Question default requires to appear with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Authorization is implemented as claim-injected table functions and relationships over an internal\n  state table, with a per-environment Vert.x auth provider; a required claim argument produces the\n  required authorization error when the claim is absent.\n\n## Assumptions\n**FR-8 coverage**: Assumed FR-8 is verified by construction \u2014 the latest-version `DISTINCT` feeds an\nunwindowed re-aggregation \u2014 and documented in the README instead of by a snapshot. Rationale: no\ncatalog payment changes status, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data of our own.\n[impact: low]\n\n**Catalog include config**: Assumed the catalog is included with one of its top-level package files\nand `is_batch` false (as `lending/` does) while the consumer's `environment` selects the connector\nfile. Rationale: the consumer imports only the `-{{environment}}` connector, so the include's own\nenvironment setting is inert. [impact: medium]\n\n**Freshness**: Assumed new or changed payments appear within one minute through the streaming Flink\npipeline with no window. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`, and the\ncatalog watermarks (10 s test, 30 s prod) are used as-is. [impact: high]\n\n**JWT validation**: Assumed HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test` and a key from `${JWT_SECRET}`\nin `prod`, with issuer and audience supplied as environment variables. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault in `### Open Questions`. [impact: high]\n\n**Production workload**: Assumed up to 1,000,000 personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and a\n50 requests/second peak. Rationale: the recommended default; it only affects deployment sizing.\n[impact: medium]\n\n**Dev/local environment**: Assumed only `test` and `prod` overlays, because the scope names exactly\nthose two and follows `lending/`. [impact: low]\n\n**Loans without payments**: Assumed `LoanPaymentSummary` returns a row for every personal loan, with\nzero counts/sums and a null `last_payment_date` when no applied payment exists. Rationale: the\nrecommended default and the acceptance criterion for `PL-011`. [impact: medium]\n\n**Loan status**: Assumed the API exposes loans in every status, not only `ACTIVE`. Rationale: the\nrecommended default, and the scope keeps all statuses. [impact: medium]\n\n**Last payment date**: Assumed the latest `applied_date` among applied payments. Rationale: the\nrecommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Total amount paid**: Assumed `total_paid_cents` is the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied\npayments. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**Customer-query loan details**: Assumed the signed-in customer's loans also show `loan_status`,\n`original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n`next_payment_due_date`. Rationale: the recommended default in `### Open Questions`. [impact: low]\n\n**API protocols**: Assumed GraphQL, REST and MCP, matching `lending/`. Rationale: the recommended\ndefault and `### API surface`. [impact: low]\n\n**Budget**: Assumed the smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment size. Rationale: the recommended default;\nno budget was given. [impact: low]\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510935191}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me read the requirements file and repo inventory.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa2677782980553e9", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa2937812a4a86de5", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 20493, "output": 105, "cacheRead": 256, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 10, "totalTokens": 20854, "cost": {"input": 0.0061478999999999995, "output": 0.000126, "cacheRead": 1.536e-06, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.006275436}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510935195, "responseId": "98bce745910e46c8810852cd8050272b-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1131, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa2677782980553e9", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\ntype: user-requirements\n---\n\n# Requirements: Loan Payments (personal loans)\n\n## Goal\n\nGive each personal-loan customer an API to look up their own loans: the payment summary of each\nloan and the payments of each loan. The customer signs in through the SSO, and the API shows only\nthe loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\n## Scope\n\n**In scope:**\n\n- A new DataSQRL project in `loan-payments/` that reads the finance data catalog\n  (`data-catalog/`, a git submodule at the repository root) and does not change it.\n- The table `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer id and the\n  loan purpose from the loan record.\n- The table `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts and totals.\n- An API with the three lookups in [API surface](#api-surface), authorized by the JWT claim\n  `customer_id`.\n- Tests that use the test data of the catalog.\n- A `test` and a `prod` environment, with the same layout as the sibling project `lending/`\n  (`lending360-shared-package.json`, `lending360-test-package.json`, `lending360-prod-package.json`).\n\n**Out of scope:**\n\n- Auto loans and auto-loan payments. The catalog table `Loan_Payment` holds both, and this project\n  keeps only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n- Mortgages, credit cards and delinquency events.\n- Payment submission or any other write to the source systems. The API has no mutations.\n- Staff or admin access to the loans of other customers.\n- Changes to the data catalog, its schemas or its test data.\n- A web UI.\n\n## Functional requirements\n\n- **FR-1**: Read the personal loans from the catalog table `Personal_Loan` and the payments from\n  the catalog table `Loan_Payment` (dataset `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans`).\n- **FR-2**: Keep the latest version of each personal loan (key `loan_id`) and of each payment\n  (key `payment_id`), by `source_updated_at`. Both sources are CDC streams.\n- **FR-3**: Keep only the payments of personal loans: `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` and a `loan_id`\n  that exists in `Personal_Loan`.\n- **FR-4**: `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`. Each row has the payment fields listed in\n  [Transformations](#transformations-and-aggregations), plus `customer_id`\n  (= `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id`) and `loan_purpose` from the loan record.\n- **FR-5**: `LoanPayments` keeps payments of every status (`APPLIED`, `PENDING`, `RETURNED`,\n  `REVERSED`) so that a customer sees their returned payments.\n- **FR-6**: `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row per personal loan, with `loan_id`, `customer_id`,\n  `loan_purpose` and these values:\n  - `applied_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`.\n  - `returned_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'RETURNED'`.\n  - `total_paid_cents`: the sum of `payment_amount_cents`.\n  - `principal_paid_cents`: the sum of `principal_cents`.\n  - `interest_paid_cents`: the sum of `interest_cents`.\n  - `late_fees_paid_cents`: the sum of `late_fee_cents`.\n  - `last_payment_date`: the latest `applied_date`.\n- **FR-7**: The totals and `last_payment_date` in FR-6 use only the payments with\n  `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`. A `RETURNED`, `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment adds nothing to them.\n- **FR-8**: When a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary of its\n  loan uses only the latest status.\n- **FR-9**: Query `LoanPaymentSummary` by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-10**: Query the `LoanPayments` of a loan by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-11**: Query all loans of a customer, each with its summary, by `customer_id`.\n- **FR-12**: Every query returns only rows whose `customer_id` equals the JWT claim `customer_id`.\n  A request for a loan of another customer returns no rows.\n- **FR-13**: A request without a valid JWT, or with a JWT that has no `customer_id` claim, is\n  rejected.\n\n## Sources\n\nBoth sources are catalog tables that the project imports. The project manages no data itself.\n\n### `Personal_Loan` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external. The consumer lending system publishes it. The project only reads it.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl`\n  (`Personal_Loan_schema`), connectors `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` and `consumer_loans-prod.sqrl`.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.personal_loan` on `${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps. No schema registry.\n- **Sample payload** (test data):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\n   \"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\n   \"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\n   \"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\n   \"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\n   \"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\n   \"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\n   \"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\n   \"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\n   \"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields used by this project: `loan_purpose`. `loan_id` and\n  `primary_borrower_customer_id` are `NOT NULL`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 170 loans, 142 customers. Production volume is an open question.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once (Kafka, `kafka-safe` connector). Replay from the earliest\n  offset is possible.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Several versions of a `loan_id` can arrive. The latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC updates to prior state (for example `ACTIVE` to `PAID_OFF`).\n\n### `Loan_Payment` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external, the same consumer lending system.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `Loan_Payment_schema` in the same `consumer_loans.sqrl`, with the same\n  connector files.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.loan_payment`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps.\n- **Sample payload** (test data, a returned payment):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"payment_id\":\"LPMT-046\",\"loan_id\":\"PL-115\",\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\",\"payment_amount_cents\":57760,\n   \"principal_cents\":37108,\"interest_cents\":20652,\"late_fee_cents\":0,\"other_fees_cents\":0,\n   \"payment_type\":\"REGULAR\",\"payment_status\":\"RETURNED\",\"due_date\":\"2025-10-08\",\n   \"received_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"applied_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"payment_method\":\"ACH\",\n   \"principal_balance_after_cents\":1632892,\"days_past_due_at_payment\":0,\n   \"confirmation_number\":\"ACH-20251005-00046\",\"return_reason_code\":\"R01\",\n   \"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:36:25Z\",\n   \"ingested_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:39:42Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields: `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`,\n  `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`, `principal_balance_after_cents`,\n  `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`, `return_reason_code`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 1,942 payments. 1,010 are personal (1,001 `APPLIED`,\n  9 `RETURNED`). The test data has no `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Deduplicate on `payment_id`, the latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC. A payment can change status after it arrives.\n\n## Time semantics\n\n- **Event-time field**: `ingested_at` (`TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3)`, UTC). The catalog connectors set the\n  watermark on it: 10 seconds in test, 30 seconds in prod. The project uses these watermarks as\n  they are.\n- **Version order**: `source_updated_at` picks the latest version of a loan or a payment.\n- **Lateness**: a payment that arrives late still counts. The summary is not windowed, so a late\n  payment updates the summary of its loan.\n- **Processing time**: not used.\n\n## Entities and keys\n\n| Entity | Key | Source | Relations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Personal loan | `loan_id` | catalog `Personal_Loan` | belongs to one customer (`primary_borrower_customer_id`) |\n| Payment | `payment_id` | catalog `Loan_Payment` | belongs to one loan (`loan_id`) |\n| Customer | `customer_id` | the JWT claim, and `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id` | has zero or more personal loans |\n\nThe `customer_id` of a payment comes from its loan, not from the payment record.\n\n## Transformations and aggregations\n\n1. **Latest loans**: the latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `loan_id`. All loan statuses stay (`ACTIVE`, `PAID_OFF`, `CHARGED_OFF`, `SETTLED`).\n2. **Latest payments**: the latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n3. **`LoanPayments`**: latest payments joined to latest loans on `loan_id` (inner join). Columns:\n   `payment_id`, `loan_id`, `customer_id`, `loan_purpose`, `payment_amount_cents`,\n   `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`, `payment_type`,\n   `payment_status`, `due_date`, `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`,\n   `principal_balance_after_cents`, `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`,\n   `return_reason_code`. Default order: `due_date` descending, then `payment_id`.\n4. **`LoanPaymentSummary`**: group `LoanPayments` by `loan_id`, with the values of FR-6 and the\n   filter rule of FR-7. Window: none (unbounded, updated on each change). A loan without payments\n   gets a row with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date` (see Open Questions).\n\nAmounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`), the same unit as the catalog.\n\n## Results\n\n### `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`\n\n- **Kind**: API (served from the database of the project).\n- **Consumers**: personal-loan customers, through the client app of the bank.\n- **Authorization**: authorized. The JWT from the SSO carries the claim `customer_id`. Every query\n  filters on `customer_id = <claim customer_id>`. No other claim gives access.\n\n## API surface\n\n- **Queries** (all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`, see FR-12):\n  - `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`: the summary of one loan. Returns 0 or 1 row.\n  - `LoanPayments(loan_id)`: the payments of one loan, newest due date first, paged\n    (`limit`, `offset`).\n  - Loans of a customer: all personal loans of the signed-in customer, each with its summary.\n    The `customer_id` comes from the JWT claim, so the caller cannot ask for another customer.\n- **Mutations**: none.\n- **Subscriptions**: none.\n- **Protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, the same as the sibling project `lending/`.\n- **Access**: every query needs a valid JWT with the claim `customer_id`.\n\n## Test data and acceptance criteria\n\n- **Test data**: the catalog test data in\n  `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/`\n  (`consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`, `consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`), read through the\n  catalog test connectors. The project adds no source data of its own.\n- **Edge cases in that data**:\n  - Auto-loan payments (932 rows) that the filter must remove.\n  - 9 `RETURNED` personal payments, on loans `PL-023`, `PL-061`, `PL-062`, `PL-068`, `PL-071`,\n    `PL-079`, `PL-108`, `PL-115`, `PL-121`.\n  - 26 applied personal payments with `late_fee_cents > 0`.\n  - 35 personal loans without payments (for example `PL-011`, `PL-014`).\n  - 23 customers with more than one personal loan (for example `CUST-060` with `PL-006`,\n    `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`).\n  - Loans in every status (`ACTIVE` 114, `PAID_OFF` 51, `CHARGED_OFF` 4, `SETTLED` 1).\n- **Acceptance criteria**:\n  - *(FR-1, FR-3)* `LoanPayments` has 1,010 rows. No row has a `loan_id` that starts with `AL-`.\n  - *(FR-2)* `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`, and `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row\n    per `loan_id`.\n  - *(FR-4)* Each `LoanPayments` row has the `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` of its loan in\n    `Personal_Loan`.\n  - *(FR-5)* `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` includes payment `LPMT-046` with\n    `payment_status = \"RETURNED\"` and `return_reason_code = \"R01\"`.\n  - *(FR-6, FR-7)* For `PL-115`, `returned_payment_count = 1`, and `total_paid_cents` equals the\n    sum of `payment_amount_cents` of its `APPLIED` payments only (it excludes the 57,760 of\n    `LPMT-046`). The planner computes the expected values from the test data.\n  - *(FR-6)* For a loan with applied late fees, `late_fees_paid_cents` equals the sum of their\n    `late_fee_cents`.\n  - *(FR-6)* For `PL-011` (no payments), the summary row has counts and sums of 0 and a null\n    `last_payment_date`.\n  - *(FR-8)* Not testable with the catalog test data, because no payment changes status in it.\n    The plan says how it covers this rule.\n  - *(FR-9, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-439` (the customer of `PL-115`), `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: \"PL-115\")`\n    returns 1 row. With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the same query returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-10, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-11)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the customer query returns the 4 loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`,\n    `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary.\n  - *(FR-13)* A request without a JWT gets an authorization error.\n  - Snapshot tests store the API responses above under the test folder of the project.\n\n## Non-functional requirements\n\n- **Freshness**: a new or changed payment shows in `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary` within\n  1 minute of its arrival in Kafka (see Open Questions).\n- **API latency SLA**: p99 < 200 ms for each query, at up to 50 requests/second (see Open Questions).\n- **Workload**: unknown in production (see Open Questions). The test data has 170 loans and\n  1,010 personal payments.\n- **Budget**: none stated (see Open Questions).\n- **Reliability**: no data loss. After a failure, the pipeline restarts from its last checkpoint\n  and replays from Kafka.\n- **Retention**: the API keeps all payments of all personal loans, with no expiry.\n- **PII and sensitive fields**: `customer_id` is tagged `@pii`. The amounts are `@npi`. The API\n  shows them only to the customer that owns the loan (FR-12). The API leaves out `loan_number`\n  (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI ratio.\n- **Compliance**: GLBA, GDPR, CCPA, the same as the catalog dataset. No new storage outside the\n  database of the project.\n- **Availability**: no target stated. The default deployment of DataSQRL Cloud applies.\n\n## Hard constraints\n\n- Use the catalog tables `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` from `data-catalog/` through\n  `script.include`, the same way as `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`. Do not copy or\n  re-declare them. Reason: the user asked to use the finance data catalog.\n- Use the catalog test data for the tests. Reason: the user asked for it.\n- Use the table names `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. Reason: the user named them.\n- Authorize with the JWT claim `customer_id` from the SSO. Reason: the user stated it.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Freshness**: how fast must a payment show in the API? Recommended default: *within 1 minute,\n  with the streaming Flink pipeline of the sibling `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **JWT validation**: which signing algorithm, issuer, audience and key does the SSO use?\n  Recommended default: *HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test`, and the key from an environment\n  variable (for example `${JWT_SECRET}`) in `prod`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **Production workload**: how many loans and payments per day, and how many API requests per\n  second? Recommended default: *up to 1 million personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and\n  50 requests/second peak*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Last payment date**: is it the latest `applied_date` of an applied payment, or the latest\n  `received_date`? Recommended default: *the latest `applied_date` of an `APPLIED` payment*.\n  Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Total amount paid**: is it `payment_amount_cents` (the full payment, which includes fees)?\n  Recommended default: *yes, the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied payments*. Impact if\n  wrong: low.\n- **Loans without payments**: does `LoanPaymentSummary` show a row for a loan with no payments?\n  Recommended default: *yes, with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date`*. Impact if\n  wrong: medium.\n- **Loan status**: does the API show loans in every status, or only `ACTIVE` loans? Recommended\n  default: *every status*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Loan details in the customer query**: does the customer query show loan fields other than the\n  summary (for example `loan_status`, `current_balance_cents`)? Recommended default: *add\n  `loan_status`, `original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n  `next_payment_due_date`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **API protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, or GraphQL only? Recommended default: *all three, the\n  same as `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Budget**: what may the pipeline cost per month? Recommended default: *the smallest DataSQRL\n  Cloud deployment size*. Impact if wrong: low.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510936335}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa2937812a4a86de5", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/workspace/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md'"}], "details": {}, "isError": true, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510936335}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me find the inventory file.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa6167411a6072ee6", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "ls -la /workspace/ && echo \"---\" && find /workspace -maxdepth 2 -name \"repo-inventory.md\" 2>/dev/null && echo \"---\" && ls -la /workspace/loan-payments/"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa65f7f7088d73aa6", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/ 2>/dev/null && echo \"=== lending ===\" && ls -la /workspace/lending/ && echo \"=== customer ===\" && ls -la /workspace/customer/"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 5067, "output": 171, "cacheRead": 20736, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 7, "totalTokens": 25974, "cost": {"input": 0.0015201, "output": 0.0002052, "cacheRead": 0.000124416, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001849716}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510936337, "responseId": "34409f7b9b77408390d7459601d3c953-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1000, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa6167411a6072ee6", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "total 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root  352 Nov 24 08:28 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  1 root root 4096 Nov 24 09:02 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root  352 Nov 24 08:27 customer\ndrwxr-xr-x 14 root root  448 Nov 24 08:27 data-catalog\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  142 Nov 24 08:27 .git\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root   96 Nov 24 08:27 .github\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   55 Nov 24 08:27 .gitignore\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  119 Nov 24 08:27 .gitmodules\ndrwxr-xr-x  9 root root  288 Nov 24 08:27 lending\ndrwxr-xr-x  6 root root  192 Nov 24 09:02 loan-payments\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1064 Nov 24 08:27 README.md\n---\n---\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x  6 root root   192 Nov 24 09:02 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:28 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  4 root root   128 Nov 24 08:46 adr\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 15626 Nov 24 09:02 AGENTS.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 root root   224 Nov 24 09:02 .datasqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  1094 Nov 24 08:46 .datasqrl_agent_results.json\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 22, "timestamp": 1795510937360}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa65f7f7088d73aa6", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "total 60\ndrwxr-xr-x 14 root root   448 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:28 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  4 root root   128 Nov 24 08:27 customer\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 root root   160 Nov 24 08:27 deposits_payments\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root    96 Nov 24 08:27 .git\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24 08:27 .github\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   134 Nov 24 08:27 .gitignore\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 root root   160 Nov 24 08:27 lending\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   501 Nov 24 08:27 ontology-shared-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 18322 Nov 24 08:27 ontology.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   308 Nov 24 08:27 ontology-test-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  8589 Nov 24 08:27 README.md\n-rwxr-xr-x  1 root root  8752 Nov 24 08:27 run-tests.sh\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24 08:27 snapshots\n=== lending ===\ntotal 28\ndrwxr-xr-x  9 root root   288 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:28 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 root root   160 Nov 24 08:27 lending360-api\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root    72 Nov 24 08:27 lending360-prod-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   910 Nov 24 08:27 lending360-shared-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 10057 Nov 24 08:27 lending360.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   353 Nov 24 08:27 lending360-test-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  3417 Nov 24 08:27 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24 08:27 snapshots\n=== customer ===\ntotal 48\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:28 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   354 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_pipeline-batch-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   187 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_pipeline-prod-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   629 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_pipeline-shared-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 13863 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_pipeline.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   437 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_pipeline-test-package.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root    68 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_sinks-prod.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  1091 Nov 24 08:27 customer_enriched_sinks-test.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  7985 Nov 24 08:27 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24 08:27 snapshots\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 19, "timestamp": 1795510937361}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me look at the .datasqrl folder in loan-payments.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa8dc7c33a2c52c0d", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "find /workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl -type f | head -50 && echo \"---adr---\" && ls -la /workspace/loan-payments/adr/"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa91977a3a49f44e0", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360-shared-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa945724199554a4f", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360-test-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaa96c71e08372c0c7", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360-prod-package.json"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1379, "output": 211, "cacheRead": 25856, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 16, "totalTokens": 27446, "cost": {"input": 0.00041369999999999997, "output": 0.0002532, "cacheRead": 0.000155136, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.000822036}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510937362, "responseId": "3767f90e5e334f28ac6a41dd3bd793c6-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 723, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa8dc7c33a2c52c0d", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-context.txt\n/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-logs.jsonl_20261124-084627.bak\n/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-logs.jsonl.bak\n/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md\n/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/datasqrl-agent-progress.txt\n---adr---\ntotal 32\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 root root   128 Nov 24 08:46 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 6 root root   192 Nov 24 09:02 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 10703 Nov 24 08:46 plan_20261124-084455.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 17315 Nov 24 08:44 requirements_20261124-113440.md\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 10, "timestamp": 1795510938100}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa91977a3a49f44e0", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"kafka\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"lending360.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data-catalog\": {\n        \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\",\n        \"config\": {\n          \"is_batch\": false\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"lending360-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"lending360-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\n        \"GRAPHQL\",\n        \"REST\",\n        \"MCP\"\n      ],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 2,\n      \"default-limit\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 12, "timestamp": 1795510938100}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa945724199554a4f", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"1 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/lending360/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"lending360-api/tests\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 11, "timestamp": 1795510938100}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaa96c71e08372c0c7", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"prod\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 11, "timestamp": 1795510938101}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me read the repo-inventory.md in loan-payments, and lending360.sqrl, and the api folder.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaac8e7591a88126ad", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaacbb7101ac6bdef3", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360.sqrl"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 737, "output": 116, "cacheRead": 27392, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 25, "totalTokens": 28245, "cost": {"input": 0.00022109999999999998, "output": 0.0001392, "cacheRead": 0.000164352, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.000524652}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510938103, "responseId": "cf409f1f4a5f4125872e8dac8c8d368b-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 827, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaac8e7591a88126ad", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Repository inventory\n\nGenerated by the orchestrator for project `loan-payments`. Everything listed here is mounted **READ-ONLY** \u2014 it is reference material, not yours to change.\n\nRules:\n- **Never** create, edit, copy or delete anything under these directories.\n- **Never** copy their files into your project \u2014 include shared modules via `script.include`.\n- **Never** read their `build/` directories (compiler output; excluded from this inventory).\n- Read a directory's README first (when present), then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`.\n\n## `data-catalog` \u2014 SHARED MODULE\n\n_declared under script.include by customer_\n\n- Main script: `ontology.sqrl`\n- Engines: `flink`, `postgres`, `vertx`\n- Package configs: `ontology-shared-package.json`, `ontology-test-package.json`\n- Include via `script.include`: `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }` (any top-level package file of the module works)\n- SQRL files: `customer/customer-ontology.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_enriched-prod.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_enriched-test.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_enriched.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_master-prod.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_master-test.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/customer_master.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/kyc_aml-prod.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/kyc_aml-test.sqrl`, `customer/customer_data/kyc_aml.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_analytics-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_analytics-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_analytics.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_balances-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_balances-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/account_balances.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/deposit_accounts-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/deposit_accounts-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/accounts/deposit_accounts.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/core_transactions-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/core_transactions-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/core_transactions.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/enriched_transactions-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/enriched_transactions-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/enriched_transactions.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/wire_ach_transactions-prod.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/wire_ach_transactions-test.sqrl`, `deposits_payments/transactions/wire_ach_transactions.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-prod.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-test.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/credit_cards-prod.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/credit_cards-test.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/credit_cards.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/credit_risk_signals-prod.sqrl`, `lending/cards_consumer_credit/credit_risk_signals-test.sqrl` (+12 more not listed)\n\n<details><summary>README.md</summary>\n\n# Retail Bank Data Catalog\n\nA data catalog covering the **bronze** (source) and **silver** (enriched/conformed) layers of a retail bank's data mesh. Tables are documented as SQRL `CREATE TABLE` definitions using Flink SQL syntax, organized by line of business and team.\n\n## Catalog Index\n\n| Line of Business | Team | Folder | Key Datasets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Customer | Customer Data | [customer/customer_data/](customer/customer_data/) | Customer master, KYC/AML, Customer profiles |\n| Deposits & Payments | Accounts | [deposits_payments/accounts/](deposits_payments/accounts/) | Deposit accounts, Balances, Account analytics |\n| Deposits & Payments | Transactions | [deposits_payments/transactions/](deposits_payments/transactions/) | Core transactions, Card/Wire/ACH, Enriched transactions |\n| Lending | Mortgages | [lending/mortgages/](lending/mortgages/) | Originations, Servicing, Performance analytics |\n| Lending | Cards & Consumer Credit | [lending/cards_consumer_credit/](lending/cards_consumer_credit/) | Credit cards, Consumer loans, Credit risk signals |\n\n## Classification Levels\n\n| Level | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Public** | Disclosed publicly or intended for public use |\n| **In\n\n_(README truncated in this inventory \u2014 read the file for the rest.)_\n\n</details>\n\n## `customer` \u2014 SQRL PROJECT\n\n_has a main script / environment package configs_\n\n- Main script: `customer_enriched_pipeline.sqrl`\n- Engines: `flink`, `iceberg`, `duckdb`, `vertx`\n- Consumes shared module(s): `data-catalog`\n- Package configs: `customer_enriched_pipeline-batch-package.json`, `customer_enriched_pipeline-prod-package.json`, `customer_enriched_pipeline-shared-package.json`, `customer_enriched_pipeline-test-package.json`\n- SQRL files: `customer_enriched_pipeline.sqrl`, `customer_enriched_sinks-prod.sqrl`, `customer_enriched_sinks-test.sqrl`\n\n<details><summary>README.md</summary>\n\n# Customer Enrichment Pipeline\n\nA real-time streaming pipeline that produces silver-layer customer data products from bronze-layer source streams. It consolidates customer identity, contact, compliance, and relationship data into queryable `Customer_Profile` and `Customer_Household` tables exposed via GraphQL, REST, and MCP APIs.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n\nBanks and financial institutions maintain customer data across multiple authoritative systems \u2014 a core customer master (CDC stream), KYC verification records, AML risk ratings, and PEP screening results. These systems use append-only or CDC event streams, meaning the same customer can appear multiple times as records are updated.\n\nDownstream consumers (analytics, marketing, relationship management, compliance reporting) need:\n\n- **A single, deduplicated customer view** \u2014 one row per customer with the latest identity, contact, and address information.\n- **Compliance enrichment** \u2014 KYC verification status, AML risk rating, and PEP flag attached to each customer record.\n- **Household groupings** \u2014 customers linked via SPOUSE, JOINT_OWNER, or DEPENDENT relationships grouped into households for relationship-level analytics.\n- **\n\n_(README truncated in this inventory \u2014 read the file for the rest.)_\n\n</details>\n\n## `lending` \u2014 SQRL PROJECT\n\n_has a main script / environment package configs_\n\n- Main script: `lending360.sqrl`\n- Engines: `flink`, `postgres`, `kafka`, `vertx`\n- Consumes shared module(s): `data-catalog`\n- Package configs: `lending360-prod-package.json`, `lending360-shared-package.json`, `lending360-test-package.json`\n- SQRL files: `lending360.sqrl`\n\n<details><summary>README.md</summary>\n\n# Lending 360\n\nA customer-facing data application that provides a unified view of all lending instruments a customer holds, including mortgages, consumer loans (personal and auto), and credit cards. Customers can see active balances, terms, and a consolidated payment history across all products.\n\n## Features\n\n- **Mortgage**: Active mortgage loans with key terms (rate, balance, payment, maturity)\n- **CreditCard**: Active credit card accounts with balance, credit limit, APR, and rewards\n- **ConsumerLoan**: Active personal and auto loans in a unified view\n- **Customer**: Basic customer profile without sensitive PII (no SSN, TIN, or date of birth)\n- **ConsolidatedLoanPayments**: All payments across mortgages, consumer loans, and credit cards in one table\n\n## Data Sources\n\nData is sourced from the shared [`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) submodule at the repository root, wired in via `script.include` in `lending360-shared-package.json`:\n- `data-catalog/lending/mortgages` \u2014 mortgage originations and servicing\n- `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit` \u2014 consumer loans and credit cards\n- `data-catalog/customer/customer_data` \u2014 customer master records\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nlending\n\n_(README truncated in this inventory \u2014 read the file for the rest.)_\n\n</details>\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510938937}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaacbb7101ac6bdef3", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "-- Lending 360: Unified customer-facing view of all active lending instruments.\n-- Brings together mortgages, consumer loans (personal + auto), and credit cards\n-- into a coherent picture for each customer, including consolidated payment history.\n\nIMPORT data-catalog.lending.mortgages.mortgage_originations-{{environment}} AS mort_orig;\nIMPORT data-catalog.lending.mortgages.mortgage_servicing-{{environment}} AS mort_svc;\nIMPORT data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}} AS cons_loans;\nIMPORT data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.credit_cards-{{environment}} AS cc;\nIMPORT data-catalog.customer.customer_data.customer_master-{{environment}} AS cust_data;\n\n-- Deduplicate CDC streams: retain only the latest record per primary key\n\n_LatestMortgageLoan := DISTINCT mort_orig.Mortgage_Loan ON loan_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n_LatestCreditCardAccount := DISTINCT cc.Credit_Card_Account ON credit_card_account_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n_LatestPersonalLoan := DISTINCT cons_loans.Personal_Loan ON loan_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n_LatestAutoLoan := DISTINCT cons_loans.Auto_Loan ON loan_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n_LatestCustomer := DISTINCT cust_data.Customer ON customer_id ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC;\n\n/** Active mortgage loans with key terms for customer display. */\n/*+query_by_any(loan_id, customer_id) */\nMortgage := SELECT loan_id, loan_number,\n                   primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id,\n                   loan_status, loan_program, lien_position, loan_purpose, occupancy_type,\n                   rate_type, interest_rate, original_principal_cents, current_principal_cents,\n                   original_term_months, remaining_term_months, monthly_pi_payment_cents,\n                   total_monthly_payment_cents, funding_date, first_payment_date,\n                   maturity_date, next_payment_due_date\n            FROM _LatestMortgageLoan\n            WHERE loan_status = 'ACTIVE'\n            ORDER BY funding_date DESC, loan_id;\n\n/** Active credit card accounts with current balance and credit terms. */\n/*+query_by_any(credit_card_account_id, customer_id) */\nCreditCard := SELECT credit_card_account_id, account_number,\n                     primary_customer_id AS customer_id,\n                     product_name, account_status, credit_limit_cents, available_credit_cents,\n                     current_balance_cents, minimum_payment_due_cents, payment_due_date,\n                     purchase_apr, opened_date, autopay_enrolled, rewards_program, rewards_balance\n              FROM _LatestCreditCardAccount\n              WHERE account_status = 'OPEN'\n              ORDER BY opened_date DESC, credit_card_account_id;\n\n-- Combine personal and auto loans into a common schema for the ConsumerLoan table.\n-- loan_purpose is null for auto loans since that concept maps to loan_type there.\n_ConsumerLoanRaw :=\n    SELECT loan_id, loan_number,\n           primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id,\n           'PERSONAL' AS loan_type, loan_purpose,\n           original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents,\n           interest_rate, rate_type, monthly_payment_cents,\n           original_term_months, remaining_term_months,\n           funding_date, maturity_date, next_payment_due_date, autopay_enrolled\n    FROM _LatestPersonalLoan\n    WHERE loan_status = 'ACTIVE'\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT loan_id, loan_number,\n           primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id,\n           'AUTO' AS loan_type, CAST(NULL AS STRING) AS loan_purpose,\n           original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents,\n           interest_rate, rate_type, monthly_payment_cents,\n           original_term_months, remaining_term_months,\n           funding_date, maturity_date, next_payment_due_date, autopay_enrolled\n    FROM _LatestAutoLoan\n    WHERE loan_status = 'ACTIVE';\n\n/** Active personal and auto loans in a unified view with common lending attributes. */\n/*+query_by_any(loan_id, customer_id) */\nConsumerLoan := SELECT * FROM _ConsumerLoanRaw ORDER BY funding_date DESC, loan_id;\n\n/** Basic customer profile without sensitive PII (SSN, TIN, and date of birth are excluded). */\n/*+query_by_any(customer_id) */\nCustomer := SELECT customer_id, first_name, last_name, name_suffix,\n                   customer_type, customer_status, relationship_start_date,\n                   citizenship_country, preferred_language\n            FROM _LatestCustomer\n            WHERE customer_status = 'ACTIVE'\n            ORDER BY customer_id;\n\n/** Active mortgages owned by this customer. */\nCustomer.mortgages := SELECT * FROM Mortgage m WHERE m.customer_id = this.customer_id ORDER BY m.funding_date DESC;\n/** Active credit card accounts owned by this customer. */\nCustomer.credit_cards := SELECT * FROM CreditCard c WHERE c.customer_id = this.customer_id ORDER BY c.opened_date DESC;\n/** Active consumer loans (personal and auto) owned by this customer. */\nCustomer.consumer_loans := SELECT * FROM ConsumerLoan l WHERE l.customer_id = this.customer_id ORDER BY l.funding_date DESC;\n\n-- Lookup table mapping loan_id to customer_id across both personal and auto loan types.\n-- Used to enrich payment records, which do not carry customer_id directly.\n_ConsumerLoanLookup :=\n    SELECT loan_id, primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id\n    FROM _LatestPersonalLoan\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT loan_id, primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id\n    FROM _LatestAutoLoan;\n\n-- Mortgage payments enriched with customer_id from the loan record\n_MortgagePayments :=\n    SELECT mp.payment_id,\n           'MORTGAGE' AS instrument_type,\n           mp.loan_id AS instrument_id,\n           ml.primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id,\n           mp.payment_amount_cents,\n           mp.payment_type,\n           mp.payment_status,\n           mp.received_date AS payment_date,\n           mp.principal_cents,\n           mp.interest_cents,\n           mp.ingested_at\n    FROM mort_svc.Mortgage_Payment mp\n    JOIN _LatestMortgageLoan ml ON mp.loan_id = ml.loan_id;\n\n-- Consumer loan payments (personal + auto) enriched with customer_id\n_ConsumerLoanPayments :=\n    SELECT lp.payment_id,\n           CASE lp.loan_type WHEN 'PERSONAL' THEN 'PERSONAL_LOAN' ELSE 'AUTO_LOAN' END AS instrument_type,\n           lp.loan_id AS instrument_id,\n           cl.customer_id,\n           lp.payment_amount_cents,\n           lp.payment_type,\n           lp.payment_status,\n           lp.received_date AS payment_date,\n           lp.principal_cents,\n           lp.interest_cents,\n           lp.ingested_at\n    FROM cons_loans.Loan_Payment lp\n    JOIN _ConsumerLoanLookup cl ON lp.loan_id = cl.loan_id;\n\n-- Credit card payments enriched with customer_id from the account record\n_CreditCardPayments :=\n    SELECT ccp.payment_id,\n           'CREDIT_CARD' AS instrument_type,\n           ccp.credit_card_account_id AS instrument_id,\n           cca.primary_customer_id AS customer_id,\n           ccp.payment_amount_cents,\n           ccp.payment_type,\n           ccp.payment_status,\n           ccp.received_date AS payment_date,\n           CAST(NULL AS BIGINT) AS principal_cents,\n           CAST(NULL AS BIGINT) AS interest_cents,\n           ccp.ingested_at\n    FROM cc.Credit_Card_Payment ccp\n    JOIN _LatestCreditCardAccount cca ON ccp.credit_card_account_id = cca.credit_card_account_id;\n\n_AllPayments :=\n    SELECT payment_id, instrument_type, instrument_id, customer_id,\n           payment_amount_cents, payment_type, payment_status,\n           payment_date, principal_cents, interest_cents, ingested_at\n    FROM _MortgagePayments\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT payment_id, instrument_type, instrument_id, customer_id,\n           payment_amount_cents, payment_type, payment_status,\n           payment_date, principal_cents, interest_cents, ingested_at\n    FROM _ConsumerLoanPayments\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT payment_id, instrument_type, instrument_id, customer_id,\n           payment_amount_cents, payment_type, payment_status,\n           payment_date, principal_cents, interest_cents, ingested_at\n    FROM _CreditCardPayments;\n\n/** All loan payments across mortgages, consumer loans, and credit cards.\n    Allows customers to see a unified payment history across all their lending instruments.\n    instrument_type values: MORTGAGE, PERSONAL_LOAN, AUTO_LOAN, CREDIT_CARD */\n/*+query_by_all(customer_id) */\nConsolidatedLoanPayments := SELECT * FROM _AllPayments ORDER BY payment_date DESC, payment_id;\n\n-- Test queries: validate key tables using known test data values\n\n/*+test */\nMortgage_Test := SELECT loan_id, loan_number, customer_id, loan_status, loan_program,\n                        interest_rate, current_principal_cents, total_monthly_payment_cents,\n                        next_payment_due_date\n                 FROM Mortgage WHERE customer_id = 'CUST-001' ORDER BY loan_id;\n\n/*+test */\nCreditCard_Test := SELECT credit_card_account_id, account_number, customer_id, product_name,\n                          current_balance_cents, credit_limit_cents, payment_due_date, purchase_apr\n                   FROM CreditCard WHERE customer_id = 'CUST-001' ORDER BY credit_card_account_id;\n\n/*+test */\nConsumerLoan_Test := SELECT loan_id, loan_number, customer_id, loan_type, loan_purpose,\n                            current_balance_cents, interest_rate, monthly_payment_cents,\n                            next_payment_due_date\n                     FROM ConsumerLoan WHERE customer_id = 'CUST-001' ORDER BY loan_id;\n\n/*+test */\nCustomer_Test := SELECT customer_id, first_name, last_name, customer_type, customer_status,\n                        relationship_start_date, preferred_language\n                 FROM Customer WHERE customer_id = 'CUST-001' ORDER BY customer_id;\n\n/*+test */\nConsolidatedLoanPayments_Test := SELECT payment_id, instrument_type, instrument_id, customer_id,\n                                        payment_amount_cents, payment_type, payment_status, payment_date\n                                 FROM ConsolidatedLoanPayments WHERE customer_id = 'CUST-001'\n                                 ORDER BY payment_id;\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 4, "timestamp": 1795510938937}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now let me look at the lending API folder, the data catalog consumer_loans files, and test data.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab0647350b1ba8348", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "find /workspace/lending/lending360-api -type f | sort && echo \"=== snapshots ===\" && find /workspace/lending/snapshots -type f | sort && echo \"=== catalog consumer_loans ===\" && ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab0d77672946c51e1", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 4473, "output": 176, "cacheRead": 28160, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 23, "totalTokens": 32809, "cost": {"input": 0.0013419, "output": 0.00021119999999999998, "cacheRead": 0.00016896, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00172206}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510938940, "responseId": "48902171ba234449b72b6ee5f43e62d0-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1057, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab0647350b1ba8348", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/workspace/lending/lending360-api/operations.v1.graphql\n/workspace/lending/lending360-api/schema.v1.graphqls\n/workspace/lending/lending360-api/tests/customer-lending.graphql\n/workspace/lending/lending360-api/tests/payment-history.graphql\n=== snapshots ===\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/ConsolidatedLoanPayments_Test.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/ConsumerLoan_Test.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/CreditCard_Test.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/customer-lending.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/Customer_Test.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/Mortgage_Test.snapshot\n/workspace/lending/snapshots/lending360/payment-history.snapshot\n=== catalog consumer_loans ===\ntotal 72\ndrwxr-xr-x 13 root root   416 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 root root   160 Nov 24 08:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  3400 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-prod.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 13558 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  2556 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-test.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  4089 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-prod.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 13309 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  3075 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-test.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  2269 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-prod.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  9145 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  1798 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-test.sqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  1537 Nov 24 08:27 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 root root   544 Nov 24 08:27 testdata\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 13, "timestamp": 1795510940010}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab0d77672946c51e1", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "-- ## Consumer Loans\n--\n-- Personal and auto loan data including loan applications, funded loans,\n-- payment history, and delinquency tracking. Supports the full consumer\n-- lending lifecycle from application through payoff or charge-off.\n-- Data is a CDC stream from source system.\n--\n-- Layer:            bronze\n-- Classification:   Restricted\n-- Regulatory scope: GLBA, GDPR, CCPA, FCRA, TILA, ECOA, SCRA\n\n/** Personal/unsecured loan records.\n    Grain: one row per loan_id.\n\n    # Columns:\n    * loan_id: Unique identifier for the loan\n    * application_id: Reference to application\n    * primary_borrower_customer_id: Reference to primary borrower @pii\n    * loan_number: Loan number displayed to customer @npi @mask\n    * loan_status: Loan status: ACTIVE, PAID_OFF, CHARGED_OFF, SETTLED\n    * loan_purpose: Loan purpose: DEBT_CONSOLIDATION, HOME_IMPROVEMENT, MAJOR_PURCHASE, MEDICAL, VACATION, OTHER\n    * original_amount_cents: Original loan amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * current_balance_cents: Current principal balance in cents @npi @confidential\n    * interest_rate: Interest rate (APR) @confidential\n    * rate_type: Rate type: FIXED, VARIABLE\n    * original_term_months: Original term in months\n    * remaining_term_months: Remaining term in months\n    * monthly_payment_cents: Monthly payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * funding_date: Date loan was funded\n    * first_payment_date: First payment due date\n    * maturity_date: Maturity date\n    * next_payment_due_date: Next payment due date\n    * paid_off_date: Date loan was paid off\n    * origination_fee_cents: Origination fee in cents\n    * credit_score_at_origination: Credit score at origination @npi @confidential\n    * dti_at_origination: DTI ratio at origination @npi @confidential\n    * autopay_enrolled: Autopay enrolled\n    * disbursement_account_id: Disbursement account @npi\n    * payment_account_id: Payment account @npi\n    * origination_channel: Origination channel: ONLINE, BRANCH, PHONE, PARTNER\n    * source_system: Source system identifier\n    * source_updated_at: Timestamp when record was last updated in source system */\nCREATE TABLE Personal_Loan_schema (\n  loan_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  application_id STRING,\n  primary_borrower_customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_number STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_status STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_purpose STRING,\n  original_amount_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  current_balance_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  interest_rate DECIMAL(6, 4) NOT NULL,\n  rate_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  original_term_months INT NOT NULL,\n  remaining_term_months INT,\n  monthly_payment_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  funding_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  first_payment_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  maturity_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  next_payment_due_date DATE,\n  paid_off_date DATE,\n  origination_fee_cents BIGINT,\n  credit_score_at_origination INT,\n  dti_at_origination DECIMAL(5, 2),\n  autopay_enrolled BOOLEAN NOT NULL,\n  disbursement_account_id STRING,\n  payment_account_id STRING,\n  origination_channel STRING,\n  source_system STRING NOT NULL,\n  source_updated_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL\n);\n\n/** Auto loan records with vehicle collateral information.\n    Grain: one row per loan_id.\n\n    # Columns:\n    * loan_id: Unique identifier for the loan\n    * application_id: Reference to application\n    * primary_borrower_customer_id: Reference to primary borrower @pii\n    * loan_number: Loan number displayed to customer @npi @mask\n    * loan_status: Loan status: ACTIVE, PAID_OFF, CHARGED_OFF, REPOSSESSED\n    * loan_type: Loan type: NEW_VEHICLE, USED_VEHICLE, REFINANCE, LEASE_BUYOUT\n    * original_amount_cents: Original loan amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * current_balance_cents: Current principal balance in cents @npi @confidential\n    * interest_rate: Interest rate (APR) @confidential\n    * rate_type: Rate type: FIXED, VARIABLE\n    * original_term_months: Original term in months\n    * remaining_term_months: Remaining term in months\n    * monthly_payment_cents: Monthly payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * funding_date: Date loan was funded\n    * first_payment_date: First payment due date\n    * maturity_date: Maturity date\n    * next_payment_due_date: Next payment due date\n    * paid_off_date: Date loan was paid off\n    * vehicle_vin: Vehicle VIN @pii\n    * vehicle_year: Vehicle year\n    * vehicle_make: Vehicle make\n    * vehicle_model: Vehicle model\n    * vehicle_trim: Vehicle trim\n    * mileage_at_origination: Vehicle mileage at origination\n    * vehicle_condition: Vehicle condition: NEW, USED, CERTIFIED\n    * vehicle_value_at_origination_cents: Vehicle value at origination in cents @npi @confidential\n    * current_vehicle_value_cents: Current estimated vehicle value in cents @npi @confidential\n    * ltv_at_origination: Loan-to-value ratio at origination\n    * current_ltv: Current LTV\n    * dealer_name: Dealer name @pii\n    * dealer_id: Dealer ID\n    * gap_insurance: GAP insurance included\n    * extended_warranty: Extended warranty included\n    * credit_score_at_origination: Credit score at origination @npi @confidential\n    * dti_at_origination: DTI ratio at origination @npi @confidential\n    * title_status: Title status: HELD, RELEASED, DEFECTIVE\n    * lien_state: Lien holder state\n    * autopay_enrolled: Autopay enrolled\n    * source_system: Source system identifier\n    * source_updated_at: Timestamp when record was last updated in source system */\nCREATE TABLE Auto_Loan_schema (\n  loan_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  application_id STRING,\n  primary_borrower_customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_number STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_status STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  original_amount_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  current_balance_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  interest_rate DECIMAL(6, 4) NOT NULL,\n  rate_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  original_term_months INT NOT NULL,\n  remaining_term_months INT,\n  monthly_payment_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  funding_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  first_payment_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  maturity_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  next_payment_due_date DATE,\n  paid_off_date DATE,\n  vehicle_vin STRING NOT NULL,\n  vehicle_year INT NOT NULL,\n  vehicle_make STRING NOT NULL,\n  vehicle_model STRING NOT NULL,\n  vehicle_trim STRING,\n  mileage_at_origination INT,\n  vehicle_condition STRING NOT NULL,\n  vehicle_value_at_origination_cents BIGINT,\n  current_vehicle_value_cents BIGINT,\n  ltv_at_origination DECIMAL(5, 2),\n  current_ltv DECIMAL(5, 2),\n  dealer_name STRING,\n  dealer_id STRING,\n  gap_insurance BOOLEAN,\n  extended_warranty BOOLEAN,\n  credit_score_at_origination INT,\n  dti_at_origination DECIMAL(5, 2),\n  title_status STRING,\n  lien_state STRING,\n  autopay_enrolled BOOLEAN NOT NULL,\n  source_system STRING NOT NULL,\n  source_updated_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL\n);\n\n/** Consumer loan payment records (personal and auto).\n    Grain: one row per payment_id.\n\n    # Columns:\n    * payment_id: Unique identifier for the payment\n    * loan_id: Reference to loan\n    * loan_type: Loan type: PERSONAL, AUTO\n    * payment_amount_cents: Payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * principal_cents: Principal portion in cents @npi @confidential\n    * interest_cents: Interest portion in cents @npi @confidential\n    * late_fee_cents: Late fee amount in cents\n    * other_fees_cents: Other fees in cents\n    * payment_type: Payment type: REGULAR, EXTRA_PRINCIPAL, PAYOFF, PARTIAL\n    * payment_status: Payment status: APPLIED, PENDING, RETURNED, REVERSED\n    * due_date: Date payment was due\n    * received_date: Date payment was received\n    * applied_date: Date payment was applied\n    * payment_method: Payment method: ACH, CHECK, DEBIT_CARD, ONLINE\n    * principal_balance_after_cents: Principal balance after payment in cents @npi @confidential\n    * days_past_due_at_payment: Days past due at time of payment\n    * confirmation_number: Confirmation number\n    * return_reason_code: Return reason code (if returned)\n    * source_system: Source system identifier\n    * source_updated_at: Timestamp when record was last updated in source system */\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Payment_schema (\n  payment_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  payment_amount_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  principal_cents BIGINT,\n  interest_cents BIGINT,\n  late_fee_cents BIGINT,\n  other_fees_cents BIGINT,\n  payment_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  payment_status STRING NOT NULL,\n  due_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  received_date DATE,\n  applied_date DATE,\n  payment_method STRING,\n  principal_balance_after_cents BIGINT,\n  days_past_due_at_payment INT,\n  confirmation_number STRING,\n  return_reason_code STRING,\n  source_system STRING NOT NULL,\n  source_updated_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL\n);\n\n/** Consumer loan application records.\n    Grain: one row per application_id.\n\n    # Columns:\n    * application_id: Unique identifier for the application\n    * primary_applicant_customer_id: Reference to primary applicant @pii\n    * co_applicant_customer_id: Reference to co-applicant (if applicable) @pii\n    * loan_type: Loan type: PERSONAL, AUTO\n    * loan_purpose: Loan purpose: DEBT_CONSOLIDATION, HOME_IMPROVEMENT, VEHICLE_PURCHASE, REFINANCE, OTHER\n    * requested_amount_cents: Requested loan amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * requested_term_months: Requested term in months\n    * application_status: Application status: SUBMITTED, IN_REVIEW, APPROVED, COUNTER_OFFER, DENIED, WITHDRAWN, FUNDED\n    * submitted_at: Date application was submitted\n    * decision_date: Date decision was made\n    * decision_type: Decision type: APPROVED, DENIED, COUNTER_OFFER\n    * approved_amount_cents: Approved amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * approved_rate: Approved rate @confidential\n    * approved_term_months: Approved term in months\n    * denial_reasons: Denial reason codes (if denied) @confidential\n    * credit_score: Credit score at application @npi @confidential\n    * annual_income_cents: Annual income stated @npi @confidential\n    * monthly_debt_cents: Monthly debt obligations @npi @confidential\n    * dti_ratio: DTI ratio calculated @npi @confidential\n    * employment_status: Employment status: EMPLOYED, SELF_EMPLOYED, RETIRED, OTHER\n    * employer_name: Employer name @pii\n    * years_employed: Years at current employer\n    * origination_channel: Origination channel: ONLINE, BRANCH, PHONE, DEALER, PARTNER\n    * promo_code: Promo code applied\n    * funded_date: Date application was funded\n    * source_system: Source system identifier\n    * source_updated_at: Timestamp when record was last updated in source system */\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Application_schema (\n  application_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  primary_applicant_customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  co_applicant_customer_id STRING,\n  loan_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_purpose STRING,\n  requested_amount_cents BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  requested_term_months INT,\n  application_status STRING NOT NULL,\n  submitted_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  decision_date DATE,\n  decision_type STRING,\n  approved_amount_cents BIGINT,\n  approved_rate DECIMAL(6, 4),\n  approved_term_months INT,\n  denial_reasons STRING,\n  credit_score INT,\n  annual_income_cents BIGINT,\n  monthly_debt_cents BIGINT,\n  dti_ratio DECIMAL(5, 2),\n  employment_status STRING,\n  employer_name STRING,\n  years_employed INT,\n  origination_channel STRING,\n  promo_code STRING,\n  funded_date DATE,\n  source_system STRING NOT NULL,\n  source_updated_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL\n);\n\n/** Consumer loan delinquency events.\n    Grain: one row per delinquency_event_id.\n\n    # Columns:\n    * delinquency_event_id: Unique identifier for the delinquency event\n    * loan_id: Reference to loan\n    * loan_type: Loan type: PERSONAL, AUTO\n    * customer_id: Reference to customer @pii\n    * event_type: Event type: MISSED_PAYMENT, DELINQUENCY_START, DELINQUENCY_CURED, CHARGE_OFF, REPOSSESSION, COLLECTION_ASSIGNED, RECOVERY\n    * days_past_due: Days past due at event\n    * delinquency_bucket: Delinquency bucket: CURRENT, 30_DAY, 60_DAY, 90_DAY, 120_PLUS\n    * amount_past_due_cents: Amount past due in cents @npi @confidential\n    * total_balance_cents: Total balance at event in cents @npi @confidential\n    * event_date: Event date\n    * cured_date: Date cured (if applicable)\n    * collection_action: Collection action taken @confidential\n    * collection_agency: Collection agency assigned\n    * repossession_date: Repossession date (for auto)\n    * repossession_agency: Repossession agency\n    * sale_date: Sale date (for repo)\n    * sale_amount_cents: Sale amount in cents\n    * deficiency_balance_cents: Deficiency balance in cents @npi @confidential\n    * charge_off_date: Charge-off date\n    * charge_off_amount_cents: Charge-off amount in cents @npi @confidential\n    * recovery_amount_cents: Recovery amount in cents\n    * source_system: Source system identifier\n    * source_updated_at: Timestamp when record was last updated in source system */\nCREATE TABLE Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event_schema (\n  delinquency_event_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  loan_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  event_type STRING NOT NULL,\n  days_past_due INT NOT NULL,\n  delinquency_bucket STRING NOT NULL,\n  amount_past_due_cents BIGINT,\n  total_balance_cents BIGINT,\n  event_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  cured_date DATE,\n  collection_action STRING,\n  collection_agency STRING,\n  repossession_date DATE,\n  repossession_agency STRING,\n  sale_date DATE,\n  sale_amount_cents BIGINT,\n  deficiency_balance_cents BIGINT,\n  charge_off_date DATE,\n  charge_off_amount_cents BIGINT,\n  recovery_amount_cents BIGINT,\n  source_system STRING NOT NULL,\n  source_updated_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL\n);\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510940010}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now let me read the connector test files and the lending API schema/operations/tests.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab4c57f13aa330f82", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-test.sqrl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab5127a3382b8ef55", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans-prod.sqrl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab5567b83bf0e5fe6", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360-api/schema.v1.graphqls"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab59274328a27cadb", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/lending360-api/operations.v1.graphql"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 4257, "output": 211, "cacheRead": 32768, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 18, "totalTokens": 37236, "cost": {"input": 0.0012771, "output": 0.0002532, "cacheRead": 0.000196608, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001726908}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510940012, "responseId": "cf1f30c88a9d4238ab8ed2aae3744802-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1219, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab4c57f13aa330f82", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "-- ## Consumer Loans - Test Data\n--\n-- Use local filesystem data for testing\n\nIMPORT consumer_loans.*;\n\nCREATE TABLE Personal_Loan (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '10' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'filesystem',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl',\n{{^is_batch}}\n    'source.monitor-interval' = '10 min',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'format' = 'json',\n    'json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Personal_Loan_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Auto_Loan (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '10' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'filesystem',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl',\n{{^is_batch}}\n    'source.monitor-interval' = '10 min',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'format' = 'json',\n    'json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Auto_Loan_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Payment (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '10' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'filesystem',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl',\n{{^is_batch}}\n    'source.monitor-interval' = '10 min',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'format' = 'json',\n    'json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Loan_Payment_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Application (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '10' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'filesystem',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl',\n{{^is_batch}}\n    'source.monitor-interval' = '10 min',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'format' = 'json',\n    'json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Loan_Application_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL,\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '10' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'filesystem',\n    'path' = '${DATA_PATH}/consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl',\n{{^is_batch}}\n    'source.monitor-interval' = '10 min',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'format' = 'json',\n    'json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event_schema;\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510941239}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab5127a3382b8ef55", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "-- ## Consumer Loans - Production\n--\n-- Ingests source data from Kafka\n\nIMPORT consumer_loans.*;\n\nCREATE TABLE Personal_Loan (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '30' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n    'properties.bootstrap.servers' = '${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}',\n    'properties.group.id' = '${DEPLOYMENT_ID}',\n    'topic' = 'consumer_loans.personal_loan',\n    'scan.startup.mode' = 'earliest-offset',\n{{#is_batch}}\n    'scan.bounded.mode' = 'latest-offset',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'value.format' = 'json',\n    'value.json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Personal_Loan_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Auto_Loan (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '30' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n    'properties.bootstrap.servers' = '${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}',\n    'properties.group.id' = '${DEPLOYMENT_ID}',\n    'topic' = 'consumer_loans.auto_loan',\n    'scan.startup.mode' = 'earliest-offset',\n{{#is_batch}}\n    'scan.bounded.mode' = 'latest-offset',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'value.format' = 'json',\n    'value.json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Auto_Loan_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Payment (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '30' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n    'properties.bootstrap.servers' = '${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}',\n    'properties.group.id' = '${DEPLOYMENT_ID}',\n    'topic' = 'consumer_loans.loan_payment',\n    'scan.startup.mode' = 'earliest-offset',\n{{#is_batch}}\n    'scan.bounded.mode' = 'latest-offset',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'value.format' = 'json',\n    'value.json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Loan_Payment_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Loan_Application (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '30' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n    'properties.bootstrap.servers' = '${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}',\n    'properties.group.id' = '${DEPLOYMENT_ID}',\n    'topic' = 'consumer_loans.loan_application',\n    'scan.startup.mode' = 'earliest-offset',\n{{#is_batch}}\n    'scan.bounded.mode' = 'latest-offset',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'value.format' = 'json',\n    'value.json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Loan_Application_schema;\n\nCREATE TABLE Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event (\n  /** Timestamp when record was ingested into the data platform */\n  ingested_at TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR ingested_at AS ingested_at - INTERVAL '30' SECOND\n) WITH (\n    'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n    'properties.bootstrap.servers' = '${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}',\n    'properties.group.id' = '${DEPLOYMENT_ID}',\n    'topic' = 'consumer_loans.consumer_loan_delinquency_event',\n    'scan.startup.mode' = 'earliest-offset',\n{{#is_batch}}\n    'scan.bounded.mode' = 'latest-offset',\n{{/is_batch}}\n    'value.format' = 'json',\n    'value.json.timestamp-format.standard' = 'ISO-8601'\n) LIKE Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event_schema;\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510941240}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab5567b83bf0e5fe6", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "type Customer {\n  customer_id: String!\n  first_name: String!\n  last_name: String!\n  name_suffix: String\n  customer_type: String!\n  customer_status: String!\n  relationship_start_date: Date!\n  citizenship_country: String\n  preferred_language: String\n  mortgages(limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [Mortgage!]\n  credit_cards(limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [CreditCard!]\n  consumer_loans(limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [ConsumerLoan!]\n}\n\ntype Mortgage {\n  loan_id: String!\n  loan_number: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  loan_status: String!\n  loan_program: String\n  lien_position: String!\n  loan_purpose: String\n  occupancy_type: String\n  rate_type: String!\n  interest_rate: Float!\n  original_principal_cents: Long!\n  current_principal_cents: Long!\n  original_term_months: Int!\n  remaining_term_months: Int\n  monthly_pi_payment_cents: Long!\n  total_monthly_payment_cents: Long!\n  funding_date: Date!\n  first_payment_date: Date!\n  maturity_date: Date!\n  next_payment_due_date: Date\n}\n\ntype CreditCard {\n  credit_card_account_id: String!\n  account_number: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  product_name: String\n  account_status: String!\n  credit_limit_cents: Long!\n  available_credit_cents: Long\n  current_balance_cents: Long!\n  minimum_payment_due_cents: Long\n  payment_due_date: Date\n  purchase_apr: Float!\n  opened_date: Date!\n  autopay_enrolled: Boolean!\n  rewards_program: String\n  rewards_balance: Long\n}\n\ntype ConsumerLoan {\n  loan_id: String!\n  loan_number: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  loan_type: String!\n  loan_purpose: String\n  original_amount_cents: Long!\n  current_balance_cents: Long!\n  interest_rate: Float!\n  rate_type: String!\n  monthly_payment_cents: Long!\n  original_term_months: Int!\n  remaining_term_months: Int\n  funding_date: Date!\n  maturity_date: Date!\n  next_payment_due_date: Date\n  autopay_enrolled: Boolean!\n}\n\ntype ConsolidatedLoanPayments {\n  payment_id: String!\n  instrument_type: String!\n  instrument_id: String!\n  customer_id: String!\n  payment_amount_cents: Long!\n  payment_type: String!\n  payment_status: String!\n  payment_date: Date\n  principal_cents: Long\n  interest_cents: Long\n}\n\n\"An RFC-3339 compliant Full Date Scalar\"\nscalar Date\n\n\"A DateTime scalar that handles both full RFC3339 and shorter timestamp formats\"\nscalar DateTime\n\n\"A 64-bit signed integer\"\nscalar Long\n\ntype Query {\n  \"Basic customer profile (no sensitive PII) with relationships to all active lending instruments.\"\n  Customer(customer_id: String, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [Customer!]\n  \"Active mortgage loans, queryable by loan or customer.\"\n  Mortgage(loan_id: String, customer_id: String, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [Mortgage!]\n  \"Active credit card accounts, queryable by account or customer.\"\n  CreditCard(credit_card_account_id: String, customer_id: String, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [CreditCard!]\n  \"Active consumer loans (personal and auto), queryable by loan or customer.\"\n  ConsumerLoan(loan_id: String, customer_id: String, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [ConsumerLoan!]\n  \"Unified payment history across all lending instruments for a customer.\"\n  ConsolidatedLoanPayments(customer_id: String!, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [ConsolidatedLoanPayments!]\n}\n\nenum _McpMethodType {\n  NONE\n  TOOL\n  RESOURCE\n}\n\nenum _RestMethodType {\n  NONE\n  GET\n  POST\n}\n\ndirective @api(mcp: _McpMethodType, rest: _RestMethodType, uri: String) on QUERY | MUTATION | FIELD_DEFINITION\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510941240}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab59274328a27cadb", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Retrieve a customer's full lending overview including all active mortgages,\n# credit cards, and consumer loans in a single call\nquery GetCustomerLendingOverview($customer_id: String) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/customer/{customer_id}/lending\") {\n  Customer(customer_id: $customer_id) {\n    customer_id\n    first_name\n    last_name\n    customer_type\n    customer_status\n    relationship_start_date\n    mortgages {\n      loan_id\n      loan_number\n      loan_program\n      loan_purpose\n      interest_rate\n      rate_type\n      current_principal_cents\n      total_monthly_payment_cents\n      next_payment_due_date\n      maturity_date\n    }\n    credit_cards {\n      credit_card_account_id\n      account_number\n      product_name\n      credit_limit_cents\n      current_balance_cents\n      available_credit_cents\n      minimum_payment_due_cents\n      payment_due_date\n      purchase_apr\n      rewards_program\n      rewards_balance\n    }\n    consumer_loans {\n      loan_id\n      loan_number\n      loan_type\n      loan_purpose\n      original_amount_cents\n      current_balance_cents\n      interest_rate\n      monthly_payment_cents\n      next_payment_due_date\n      maturity_date\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# Retrieve all payment activity across a customer's lending instruments\nquery GetCustomerPaymentHistory($customer_id: String!, $limit: Int = 50) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/customer/{customer_id}/payments\") {\n  ConsolidatedLoanPayments(customer_id: $customer_id, limit: $limit) {\n    payment_id\n    instrument_type\n    instrument_id\n    payment_amount_cents\n    payment_type\n    payment_status\n    payment_date\n    principal_cents\n    interest_cents\n  }\n}\n\n# Retrieve details for a specific mortgage loan\nquery GetMortgage($loan_id: String, $customer_id: String) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/mortgage/{loan_id}\") {\n  Mortgage(loan_id: $loan_id, customer_id: $customer_id) {\n    loan_id\n    loan_number\n    customer_id\n    loan_status\n    loan_program\n    lien_position\n    loan_purpose\n    occupancy_type\n    rate_type\n    interest_rate\n    original_principal_cents\n    current_principal_cents\n    original_term_months\n    remaining_term_months\n    monthly_pi_payment_cents\n    total_monthly_payment_cents\n    funding_date\n    first_payment_date\n    maturity_date\n    next_payment_due_date\n  }\n}\n\n# Retrieve details for a specific credit card account\nquery GetCreditCard($credit_card_account_id: String, $customer_id: String) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/credit-card/{credit_card_account_id}\") {\n  CreditCard(credit_card_account_id: $credit_card_account_id, customer_id: $customer_id) {\n    credit_card_account_id\n    account_number\n    customer_id\n    product_name\n    account_status\n    credit_limit_cents\n    available_credit_cents\n    current_balance_cents\n    minimum_payment_due_cents\n    payment_due_date\n    purchase_apr\n    opened_date\n    autopay_enrolled\n    rewards_program\n    rewards_balance\n  }\n}\n\n# Retrieve details for a specific consumer loan (personal or auto)\nquery GetConsumerLoan($loan_id: String, $customer_id: String) @api(mcp: TOOL, rest: GET, uri: \"/consumer-loan/{loan_id}\") {\n  ConsumerLoan(loan_id: $loan_id, customer_id: $customer_id) {\n    loan_id\n    loan_number\n    customer_id\n    loan_type\n    loan_purpose\n    original_amount_cents\n    current_balance_cents\n    interest_rate\n    rate_type\n    monthly_payment_cents\n    original_term_months\n    remaining_term_months\n    funding_date\n    maturity_date\n    next_payment_due_date\n    autopay_enrolled\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510941240}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I need to understand the auth/claim pattern. Let me look for examples of JWT auth in the repo, particularly lending's prod package (no auth?), and maybe the customer project. Let me check the lending README and the data-catalog ontology shared package, and search for auth in repo.\n\nActually the plan says \"Configure the Vert.x auth provider\". Let me search for auth patterns in the examples directory /opt/datasqrl-examples and skills.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab8ff75128c27849f", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "grep -rl \"auth\" /workspace --include=\"*.json\" --include=\"*.sqrl\" --include=\"*.graphqls\" --include=\"*.graphql\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v build | head -30; echo \"=== examples ===\"; ls /opt/datasqrl-examples/ 2>/dev/null; echo \"=== readme ===\"; cat /opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md 2>/dev/null | head -80"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab9b07e43b24f88ef", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccab9ea7b50885c3051", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/lending/README.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 3796, "output": 310, "cacheRead": 37120, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 95, "totalTokens": 41226, "cost": {"input": 0.0011388, "output": 0.000372, "cacheRead": 0.00022272, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00173352}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510941242, "responseId": "4e2794c843584891aa20799538830715-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1060, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab8ff75128c27849f", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/transactions/core_transactions.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions-test.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/transactions/card_transactions-prod.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/accounts/deposit_accounts.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/ontology.sqrl\n/workspace/data-catalog/customer/customer_data/customer_master.sqrl\n=== examples ===\ncontent_recommendation\ncustomer360\nevent_processing\nfeature_store\nfinance\nfinance-catalog\nincremental_batch_kafka\nlibrary_loans\nREADME.md\nsupport_tickets\nuser_analytics\n=== readme ===\n# SQRL Project Templates\n\nUse the following project templates and examples to initialize a new project by copying all project files or to evolve an existing project by adapting pieces from these templates.\n\n## Data Products\n\nUse these templates for building data products, data pipelines, and data APIs.\n\n* [Generic Event Processing](event_processing/README.md): Use this template as a starting point for realtime event processing when none of the more specific templates apply. Ingests generic events that are enriched with dimensional data (like users and resources), aggregated (cumulate-, session-, and tumble windows and data cubes), stored in a database, archived in Apache Iceberg, and served through GraphQL, REST, and MCP APIs. Supports mutations for event ingestion and subscriptions for real-time alerts. Demonstrates how to structure the exposed API, provide information on the health of the job (monitoring), and add test coverage.\n* [Customer Lending 360](finance/lending/): Use this template for customer 360 use cases. Consolidates all customer lending vehicles (credit cards, loans, mortgages) for a bank into one application for customer servicing. Uses data catalog for sources. A classic enterprise customer 360 examples. Demonstrates how to build a customer 360 API.\n* [Customer 360](customer360/): A more advanced customer 360 use case to build a unified customer view by deduplicating CDC streams, resolving customer identities across channels, and enriching profiles with purchase history and message interactions. Uses LLM-based sentiment analysis to extract offering preferences from customer messages. Defines an explicit customer 360 GraphQL API with REST and MCP endpoints.\n* [User Analytics](user_analytics/README.md): Use this template to build user-facing analytics and to build authenticated data APIs with JWT that use claims for data access authorization and data masking. Enriches and processes financial transactions in realtime to compute customer rewards and rewards analytics exposed through user-facing API. Demonstrates how to secure APIs, build flexible authorization and RBAC, and how to test the security model.\n* [Customer Enrichment Pipelint](finance/customer): Use this template for streaming data enrichment pipelines that take raw/bronze layer data and enrich it into a data product for downstream consumers (i.e. silver layer). Enriches customer master data from a bank into a clean and normalized customer profile and customer household data stream. Uses data sources and sinks defined in a central data catalog.\n* [Feature Store](feature_store/README.md): Use this template to build a streaming feature store and training dataset batch job that reuses the same feature vector computation logic for both. Processes ISO 20022 PAIN messages to compute multiple features for fraudulent payment identification. Demonstrates how to implement test coverage for both the feature store and training dataset, explicitly define the exposed feature store API with both GraphQL schema and limiting access to explicitly defined operations that configure the REST URI template and MCP endpoints. Demonstrates how to combine stream and batch processing.\n* [Content Processing and Recommendation](content_recommendation/README.md): Use this template to augment with unstructured content (e.g. text) processing and building recommendation features. Processes clickstream events to collaborative filtering recommendations, uses vector embeddings to compute user-specific interest vectors for relevant content retrieval, and LLM based text completions to extract structured data from unstructured text. Demonstrates how to explicitly define and fine-tune the exposed API of the recommendation engine.\n* [Incremental Batch to Kafka](incremental_batch_kafka/README.md): Use this template for scheduled batch jobs that process only a recent time window of data and publish the results to a Kafka topic. Reads AWS S3 Inventory Parquet exports through the filesystem connector, parses the Iceberg warehouse structure out of the object keys, and computes a storage metrics cube (object count and total size) with `ROLLUP` over data group, database, and table. Runs Flink in `BATCH` mode on a daily cron schedule with a look-back window configured in the package, and writes to a Kafka-engine topic partitioned by key with a retention TTL, with no database or API. Demonstrates how to configure a scheduled batch deployment, bound each run to a configurable time window, and test a batch pipeline whose Kafka sink is replaced by a blackhole in tests.\n* [Library Loans](library_loans/README.md): Use this template when a project has a custom GraphQL schema, and most of all when the schema has mutations. A client records book loans through a mutation with an enum, and the pipeline counts the loans by genre and by member. Shows how to test such a project: API tests against the custom schema, and table tests in a second test package for data that the API does not return.\n* [Support Tickets](support_tickets/README.md): Use this template when a project has a custom GraphQL schema with mutations, and every table that needs a test has a field in that schema. A client records ticket events through a mutation with enums, and the pipeline serves the open tickets by priority and the tickets of each customer. Shows the default way to test such a project: API tests only, against the custom schema.\n\n## Data Catalog\n\nUse these templates only for initializing or updating data catalogs.\n\n* [Finance Data Catalog](finance-catalog): Example data catalog of a large bank for multiple lines of business (customer, deposits, and lending) with multiple teams in each. Demonstrates how to structure a data catalog and define datasets at both the source/raw/bronze layer and the enriched/silver layer.\n\n## Bug Reproductions\n\nProjects in this section reproduce DataSQRL defects for reporting and verification. They are not templates and must not be copied into a pipeline.\n\n* [Relation alias as ROW in a Postgres-stage definition](bug_repro_relation_alias_row/README.md): `c AS customer` in a plain-join table or a parameterized function compiles but fails at runtime with `cannot cast type record to jsonb` (DataSQRL/sqrl PR #2340 follow-up).\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 38, "timestamp": 1795510942341}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab9b07e43b24f88ef", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"ontology.sqrl\"\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"max-result-depth\": 1,\n      \"add-prefix\": true\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510942342}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccab9ea7b50885c3051", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Lending 360\n\nA customer-facing data application that provides a unified view of all lending instruments a customer holds, including mortgages, consumer loans (personal and auto), and credit cards. Customers can see active balances, terms, and a consolidated payment history across all products.\n\n## Features\n\n- **Mortgage**: Active mortgage loans with key terms (rate, balance, payment, maturity)\n- **CreditCard**: Active credit card accounts with balance, credit limit, APR, and rewards\n- **ConsumerLoan**: Active personal and auto loans in a unified view\n- **Customer**: Basic customer profile without sensitive PII (no SSN, TIN, or date of birth)\n- **ConsolidatedLoanPayments**: All payments across mortgages, consumer loans, and credit cards in one table\n\n## Data Sources\n\nData is sourced from the shared [`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) submodule at the repository root, wired in via `script.include` in `lending360-shared-package.json`:\n- `data-catalog/lending/mortgages` \u2014 mortgage originations and servicing\n- `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit` \u2014 consumer loans and credit cards\n- `data-catalog/customer/customer_data` \u2014 customer master records\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nlending/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lending360.sqrl                  # Main processing logic\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lending360-shared-package.json   # Shared configuration between all environments\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lending360-test-package.json     # Test configuration\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lending360-prod-package.json     # Prod configuration\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lending360-api/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls           # GraphQL API schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql        # API endpoints (GraphQL, REST, MCP)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                       # GraphQL test queries\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 customer-lending.graphql\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 payment-history.graphql\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/lending360/            # Test snapshots (auto-generated)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## API Endpoints\n\n| Operation | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| `GetCustomerLendingOverview` | Full lending overview for a customer including all active instruments |\n| `GetCustomerPaymentHistory` | Consolidated payment history across all lending instruments |\n| `GetMortgage` | Mortgage loan details by loan_id or customer_id |\n| `GetCreditCard` | Credit card account details by account_id or customer_id |\n| `GetConsumerLoan` | Consumer loan details by loan_id or customer_id |\n\n## Running Tests\n\nRun from the **repository root**: the whole repository is mounted so the shared `data-catalog` submodule is visible, and `-r lending` selects this project.\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v $PWD:/workspace datasqrl/cmd \\\n  test lending360-shared-package.json lending360-test-package.json -r lending\n```\n\n## Design Notes\n\n- CDC streams are deduplicated with `DISTINCT ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC` to retain the latest record per entity\n- ConsumerLoan unifies personal and auto loans via UNION ALL; `loan_purpose` is null for auto loans (they use `loan_type` for vehicle category instead)\n- ConsolidatedLoanPayments joins payment records with their respective loan/account tables to enrich with `customer_id`, which payments do not carry directly\n- The Customer table excludes SSN, TIN, and date of birth to avoid exposing sensitive PII\n- `instrument_type` values in ConsolidatedLoanPayments: `MORTGAGE`, `PERSONAL_LOAN`, `AUTO_LOAN`, `CREDIT_CARD`\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510942342}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The User Analytics example is the one for JWT auth. Let me look at it. Let me find the exact auth patterns. Let me read the user_analytics project and docs.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccabe3b73719eb3dee0", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "find /opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics -type f | grep -v build | sort"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccabe8478d1bac53402", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/README.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 2474, "output": 139, "cacheRead": 41216, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 38, "totalTokens": 43829, "cost": {"input": 0.0007421999999999999, "output": 0.0001668, "cacheRead": 0.000247296, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0011562959999999998}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510942344, "responseId": "c1ce5f86ff5f403cbf18d412bd049dda-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1151, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccabe3b73719eb3dee0", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/sources-prod.sqrl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/sources.sqrl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/sources-test.sqrl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/test-data/card_assignment.jsonl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/test-data/merchant.jsonl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/test-data/merchant_reward.jsonl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/connectors/test-data/transaction.jsonl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequestCustomer2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequestCustomer2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/CardRequest.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/MyCardRequests.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/PotentialRewards.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsByDay.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsWithSupportRole.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/RewardsWithSupportRole.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/test-customerId-2.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/test-customerId-2.properties\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests/TotalPotentialReward.graphql\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-local-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards.sqrl\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-test-package.json\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/README.md\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/run-tests.sh\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CardRequestCustomer2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CardRequest.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/CustomerRewardsTest.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/MyCardRequestsCustomer2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/MyCardRequests.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/PotentialRewards.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/RewardsByDay.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/RewardsWithSupportRole.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/test-customerId-2.snapshot\n/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/snapshots/TotalPotentialReward.snapshot\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795510943503}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccabe8478d1bac53402", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# User Analytics for Financial Transactions\n\nThis project computes credit card rewards for each customer from card transactions.\nIt joins each transaction with the card assignment, the merchant and the merchant's reward program that were valid at the time of the transaction.\nIt then computes the rewards the customer earned with their card, and the rewards they could have earned with other card types.\nA card issuer can use the second result to recommend a card.\n\nA user-facing GraphQL API serves the results. JWT authentication makes sure that each customer sees only their own data.\n\nThe sub-project name is `creditcard_rewards`.\n\n## What this example shows\n\n- Deduplication of CDC streams to the latest version of each record (`DISTINCT`).\n- Temporal joins (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) with the card, merchant and reward state at transaction time.\n- `CROSS JOIN UNNEST` of an array of rewards, filtered by the validity period of each reward.\n- Daily tumbling windows.\n- Access control from JWT claims: table functions take `customerId` and `roles` from `METADATA FROM 'auth.*'`.\n- A GraphQL subscription for new rewards.\n- A mutation that takes the customer from a JWT claim: the `CardRequest` column `customerId` comes from `METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId'`.\n- Tests that use different JWT tokens.\n\n## Sources\n\nThe source schemas are in `connectors/sources.sqrl`.\nThe main script imports `connectors/sources-{{environment}}.sqrl`, so each environment picks its own connectors.\n\n| Table | One record is | Key | Test data (`connectors/test-data/`) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `CardAssignmentStream` | a card assigned to a customer, with its card type | `customerId`, `cardNo` | `card_assignment.jsonl`, 14 records for customers 1 to 11 |\n| `MerchantStream` | one version of a merchant profile | `merchantId` | `merchant.jsonl`, 101 records |\n| `MerchantRewardStream` | the reward program of a merchant: a reward percentage for each card type, with start and end times | `merchantId` | `merchant_reward.jsonl`, 468 records |\n| `Transaction` | one card payment | `transactionId` | `transaction.jsonl`, 5184 records |\n\n- **test** (`connectors/sources-test.sqrl`): reads the JSONL files with the filesystem connector.\n- **prod** (`connectors/sources-prod.sqrl`): reads the Kafka topics `cardassignment`, `merchant`, `merchantreward` and `transaction`. Needs `KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS`.\n\n## Processing\n\nThe main script is `creditcard_rewards.sqrl`.\n\n| Table | What it computes |\n|---|---|\n| `_CustomerTransactionRewardsByCard` | Each transaction with the customer, the merchant, and one row for each card type that the merchant rewards at that time. `reward = amount \u00d7 percentage / 100`. |\n| `CustomerRewards` | The rows where the reward card type is the customer's card type: the rewards the customer earned. |\n| `CustomerPotentialRewards` | The rows with other card types: the rewards the customer could have earned. |\n\nThe API functions read these two tables. Each function filters by the `customerId` claim of the caller.\n\n| Function | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `Rewards(fromTime, toTime)` | The rewards of the customer in a time range. A caller with the `support` role sees the rewards of all customers. |\n| `TotalReward` | The total reward of the customer and the time of the first reward. |\n| `RewardsByDay` | The daily reward totals of the customer. |\n| `PotentialRewards(cardType, fromTime, toTime)` | The rewards the customer could have earned with one card type. |\n| `TotalPotentialReward` | The total potential reward of the customer for each card type. |\n| `PotentialRewardsByDay` | The daily potential reward totals of the customer. |\n| `NewCustomerRewards` | A subscription: each new reward of the customer. |\n| `MyCardRequests` | The card requests of the customer, starting from the most recent one. |\n\nThe mutation `CardRequest(event: {cardType})` records a request for a new card type.\nThe client sends only `cardType`. The server sets `requestId`, the `customerId` from the token, and `requestTime`.\nThe table `CardRequest` has `no_query`, so a customer reads requests only through `MyCardRequests`.\n\n## Authorization\n\nThe API accepts only requests with a valid JWT.\nThe functions read two claims:\n\n- `customerId`: every function returns only the data of this customer, and `CardRequest` writes it into each request.\n- `roles`: `Rewards` also returns the data of all customers when `roles` contains `support`.\n\nFor an array claim like `roles`, check it with `array_contains` and cast the argument, for example `array_contains(cast(:roles as ARRAY<STRING>), 'support')`.\n\nThe JWT settings are in the `engines.vertx.config.auth` block of each environment config, not in the shared config.\nThe script reads claims, so each config that compiles it needs an `auth` block. Without one, the compile fails.\nEach `auth` block has one issuer with its audience and one `oct` key. The server accepts a token only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature match that issuer.\nThe `k` value of the key is the signing key in base64url without padding.\nThe test and local configs use the key `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` with issuer `dummy-issuer` and audience `dummy-audience`.\nTo make the `k` value, run `printf '%s' 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' | base64 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=\\n'`. Use `printf`, not `echo`, because `echo` adds a newline to the key.\n\nTo make a token for another customer (1 to 11 in the test data), run:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret \"dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string\" --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"dummy-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"dummy-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customerId\": 6, \"roles\": [\"user\"]}'\n```\n\nThe prod config uses the same dummy key with issuer `my-issuer` and audience `my-audience`. Before you deploy, replace the issuer entry with your identity provider: its issuer URL and your audiences, without `keys`. The server then gets the signing keys from the issuer.\n\n## Environments\n\n| Config | Environment | What it adds |\n|---|---|---|\n| `creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json` | base | The engines (Vert.x, Postgres, Kafka, Flink) and the main script. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-test-package.json` | `test` | The JWT settings, the test runner and the default test token. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-local-package.json` | `test` connectors | The JWT settings. Use it to run the pipeline on your machine with the test data. |\n| `creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json` | `prod` | The Kafka sources and the prod JWT settings. |\n\n## Tests\n\nThe API tests are in `creditcard_rewards-api/tests/`.\nThe test config sets a default token for customer 9 in `test-runner.headers`.\nA test with its own `.properties` file uses that file's header instead:\n\n- `test-customerId-2.graphql` runs as customer 2.\n- `RewardsWithSupportRole.graphql` runs with the `support` role and gets the rewards of several customers.\n- `CardRequest.graphql` and `CardRequestCustomer2.graphql` send one card request as customer 9 and one as customer 2.\n- `MyCardRequests.graphql` and `MyCardRequestsCustomer2.graphql` show that each customer gets only their own request.\n\nThe script also has one test table, `CustomerRewardsTest`.\nThe expected results are in `snapshots/`.\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from this project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root of the code-agent repository.\n\n```bash\ncd datasqrl-examples/user_analytics                         # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                                              # test every sub-project\n./run-tests.sh creditcard_rewards                           # test one sub-project\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env local creditcard_rewards     # compile with the local environment\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod creditcard_rewards      # compile with the prod environment\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands below make the same DataSQRL calls as the `./run-tests.sh` compile and test lines.\nThey work from any folder inside the git repository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-local-package.json -b creditcard_rewards\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-test-package.json -b creditcard_rewards\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, the same folder that `./run-tests.sh` uses. It is optional. Without it, the output goes to `build/`.\n\nThe `run` command starts the whole pipeline on your machine with the `local` environment.\nIt works from any folder inside the git repository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 \\\n  -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r datasqrl-examples/user_analytics creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-local-package.json\n```\n\nThe pipeline loads the test data at start.\nEvery request needs a token. In GraphiQL, add this header to query as customer 9:\n\n```json\n{\"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6OSwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNDcsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.317UGknkS-dke26iQs3H6_Oj2WsoKO78H300hRsePrc\"}\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs:\n\n- The Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/.\n- The GraphQL API and GraphiQL are at http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/.\n- The Swagger UI for the REST API is at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui.\n- The MCP API is at `http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795510943503}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "This is the key example. Let me read the user_analytics main sqrl, test package, prod package, shared package, and test files/properties, and run-tests.sh.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac3087d809f5652a5", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards.sqrl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac35176f18f50b7d8", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-test-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac38b76e2996234a2", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac3c67141b54382b4", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 3525, "output": 239, "cacheRead": 43776, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 39, "totalTokens": 47540, "cost": {"input": 0.0010574999999999998, "output": 0.0002868, "cacheRead": 0.000262656, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0016069559999999999}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510943506, "responseId": "108bf48bc15944e8b6117eba85db1df8-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1395, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac3087d809f5652a5", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources;\n\n/* Deduplicate CDC Streams */\n_Merchant       := DISTINCT sources.MerchantStream ON merchantId ORDER BY updatedTime DESC;\n_CardAssignment := DISTINCT sources.CardAssignmentStream ON cardNo ORDER BY `timestamp` DESC;\n_MerchantReward := DISTINCT sources.MerchantRewardStream ON merchantId ORDER BY updatedTime DESC;\n\n/* Part 1: Compute customer rewards */\n_CustomerTransaction := SELECT t.transactionId, t.cardNo, t.`time`, t.amount, m.name AS merchantName,\n                               m.merchantId, m.category, c.customerId, c.cardType\n                        FROM sources.Transaction t\n                        JOIN _CardAssignment FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t.`time` c ON t.cardNo = c.cardNo\n                        JOIN _Merchant FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t.`time` m ON t.merchantId = m.merchantId;\n\n_CustomerTransactionRewards := SELECT t.*, r.rewardsByCard AS rewards\n                               FROM _CustomerTransaction t\n                               JOIN _MerchantReward FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t.`time` r ON r.merchantId = t.merchantId;\n\n_CustomerTransactionRewardsByCard := SELECT t.*, t.amount * (r.rewardPercentage / 100.0) as reward, r.cardType AS rewardCardType\n                                     FROM _CustomerTransactionRewards t CROSS JOIN UNNEST(t.rewards) r\n                                     WHERE UNIX_TIMESTAMP(DATE_FORMAT(t.`time`,'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')) BETWEEN r.startTimestamp AND r.expirationTimestamp;\n\n/*+no_query */\nCustomerRewards := SELECT transactionId, customerId, cardNo, cardType, `time`, amount, reward, merchantName\n                   FROM _CustomerTransactionRewardsByCard\n                   WHERE cardType = rewardCardType;\n\n/** Returns all the rewards that a customer (identified by customerId) has earned since fromTime (inclusive) until toTime (exclusive)..\n  fromTime and toTime must be RFC-3339 compliant date time scalar. Both must be the start of a day, e.g. 2024-01-19T00:00:00-00:00.\n */\n/* Part 2a: Query and Analyze Rewards */\nRewards(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId', roles ARRAY<STRING> METADATA FROM 'auth.roles',\n       fromTime TIMESTAMP NOT NULL, toTime TIMESTAMP NOT NULL) :=\n  SELECT * FROM CustomerRewards WHERE :fromTime <= `time` AND :toTime > `time` AND\n      (customerId = :customerId OR array_contains(cast(:roles as ARRAY<STRING>), 'support')) -- support role has super access for this endpoint\n  ORDER BY `time` DESC;\n\n_TotalReward := SELECT customerId, SUM(reward) AS total_reward,\n                      MIN(`time`) as since_time\n                FROM CustomerRewards GROUP BY customerId LIMIT 1;\n\n/** Returns the total amount of rewards the customer has earned to date and the time since when they eared rewards */\nTotalReward(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') :=\n  SELECT * FROM _TotalReward WHERE customerId = :customerId;\n\n_RewardsByDay := SELECT customerId, window_time as timeDay, SUM(reward) as total_reward\n                FROM TABLE(TUMBLE(TABLE CustomerRewards, DESCRIPTOR(`time`), INTERVAL '1' DAY))\n                GROUP BY customerId, window_start, window_end, window_time\n                ORDER BY window_time DESC;\n\n/** Returns the total awards a customer earned by day starting from the most recent day.\n  Use limit and offset to control how many days to return and when to start. */\nRewardsByDay(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') :=\n  SELECT * FROM _RewardsByDay WHERE customerId = :customerId ORDER BY customerId DESC, timeDay DESC LIMIT 5;\n\n/* Part 2b: Compute potential rewards for personalized sales */\n/*+no_query */\nCustomerPotentialRewards := SELECT transactionId, customerId, rewardCardType, `time`, amount, reward, merchantName\n                            FROM _CustomerTransactionRewardsByCard\n                            WHERE cardType <> rewardCardType;\n\n/** Returns all the potential rewards a customer could have earned since fromTime (inclusive) and until toTime (exclusive) for the given card type.\n  Use this function to show customers the rewards they would have earned if they had the given card.\n  cardType must be one of [travel, sports, business, family].\n  fromTime and toTime must be RFC-3339 compliant date time scalar. Both must be the start of a day, e.g. 2024-01-19T00:00:00-00:00. */\nPotentialRewards(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId',\n                 cardType STRING NOT NULL,\n                 fromTime TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n                 toTime TIMESTAMP NOT NULL) :=\n  SELECT * FROM CustomerPotentialRewards\n  WHERE customerId = :customerId AND :fromTime <= `time` AND :toTime > `time` AND rewardCardType = :cardType\n  ORDER BY `time` DESC;\n\n/** Returns the total amount of rewards the customer could have earned for each type of credit card the customer does not yet have.\n  Use this function to determine which credit card type to recommend to a customer. */\nTotalPotentialReward(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') :=\n  SELECT customerId, rewardCardType AS cardType, SUM(reward) AS total_reward, MIN(`time`) as since_time\n  FROM CustomerPotentialRewards\n  WHERE customerId = :customerId\n  GROUP BY customerId, rewardCardType\n  ORDER BY cardType DESC;\n\n_PotentialRewardsByDay := SELECT customerId, rewardCardType AS cardType, window_time as timeDay, SUM(reward) as total_reward\n                          FROM TABLE(TUMBLE(TABLE CustomerPotentialRewards, DESCRIPTOR(`time`), INTERVAL '1' DAY))\n                          GROUP BY customerId, window_start, window_end, window_time, rewardCardType\n                          ORDER BY window_time DESC, rewardCardType ASC;\n\n/** Returns the total rewards a customer could have earned for a given card type by day starting from the most recent day. Use this function to show the customer what their reward earnings would have looked like, if they had a given card.\n cardType must be one of [travel, sports, business, family]. Use limit and offset to control how many days to return when to start.\n*/\nPotentialRewardsByDay(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') :=\n  SELECT * FROM _PotentialRewardsByDay WHERE customerId = :customerId LIMIT 5;\n\n/** Returns the rewards for a given customer immediately */\nNewCustomerRewards(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') := SUBSCRIBE SELECT * FROM CustomerRewards WHERE customerId = :customerId;\n\n/* Part 3: Card requests from customers */\n/** A request from the customer for a new card type. The server takes the customer from the token, so a customer cannot request a card for somebody else.\n  cardType must be one of [travel, sports, business, family]. */\n/*+no_query, engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE CardRequest (\n  requestId STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'uuid',\n  customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId',\n  cardType STRING NOT NULL,\n  requestTime TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp'\n);\n\n/** Returns the card requests of the customer, starting from the most recent one. */\nMyCardRequests(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customerId') :=\n  SELECT * FROM CardRequest WHERE customerId = :customerId ORDER BY requestTime DESC;\n\n/* ==== TEST CASES ==== */\n\n/*+test */\nCustomerRewardsTest := SELECT * FROM CustomerRewards ORDER BY `time` DESC, transactionId ASC LIMIT 10;\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510944905}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac35176f18f50b7d8", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\" : {\n    \"vertx\" : {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"dummy-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"dummy-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"test-folder\": \"creditcard_rewards-api/tests/\",\n    \"headers\": {\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6OSwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNDcsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.317UGknkS-dke26iQs3H6_Oj2WsoKO78H300hRsePrc\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510944905}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac38b76e2996234a2", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"prod\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\" : {\n      \"config\" : {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"10 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\" : {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"my-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"my-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510944906}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac3c67141b54382b4", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"vertx\", \"postgres\", \"kafka\", \"flink\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"creditcard_rewards.sqrl\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510944906}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now let me read the test files and properties, and run-tests.sh. Also the config skill and from-api/design-api skills. Let me first look at the tests.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac8817983a4e9801b", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/creditcard_rewards-api/tests && for f in *.graphql *.properties; do echo \"=== $f ===\"; cat \"$f\"; echo; done"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccac8e07b4387117def", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/datasqrl-examples/user_analytics/run-tests.sh"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 2430, "output": 163, "cacheRead": 47488, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 36, "totalTokens": 50081, "cost": {"input": 0.0007289999999999999, "output": 0.00019559999999999998, "cacheRead": 0.000284928, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0012095279999999999}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510944908, "responseId": "d3763c3673b046378cf45862949f4da2-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1237, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac8817983a4e9801b", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== CardRequestCustomer2.graphql ===\nmutation {\n  CardRequest(event: {cardType: \"sports\"}) {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n  }\n}\n\n=== CardRequest.graphql ===\nmutation {\n  CardRequest(event: {cardType: \"travel\"}) {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n  }\n}\n\n=== MyCardRequestsCustomer2.graphql ===\nquery {\n  MyCardRequests {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n  }\n}\n\n=== MyCardRequests.graphql ===\nquery {\n  MyCardRequests {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n  }\n}\n\n=== PotentialRewards.graphql ===\nquery {\n  PotentialRewardsByDay {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n    timeDay\n    total_reward\n  }\n}\n\n=== RewardsByDay.graphql ===\nquery {\n  RewardsByDay {\n    customerId\n    timeDay\n    total_reward\n  }\n}\n\n=== RewardsWithSupportRole.graphql ===\nquery {\n  Rewards(fromTime: \"2024-04-26T00:00:00Z\", toTime: \"2024-06-26T00:00:00Z\") {\n    customerId\n    cardNo\n    amount\n    reward\n    time\n  }\n}\n\n=== test-customerId-2.graphql ===\nquery {\n  RewardsByDay {\n    customerId\n    timeDay\n    total_reward\n  }\n  PotentialRewardsByDay {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n    timeDay\n    total_reward\n  }\n  TotalPotentialReward {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n    total_reward\n    since_time\n  }\n}\n\n=== TotalPotentialReward.graphql ===\nquery {\n  TotalPotentialReward {\n    customerId\n    cardType\n    total_reward\n    since_time\n  }\n}\n\n=== CardRequestCustomer2.properties ===\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6MiwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNzEsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.7-rSd-Q1_e7BMc90SRzauti3F8VsIjcNiNjcqpBH4_8\n\n=== MyCardRequestsCustomer2.properties ===\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6MiwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNzEsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.7-rSd-Q1_e7BMc90SRzauti3F8VsIjcNiNjcqpBH4_8\n\n=== RewardsWithSupportRole.properties ===\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6MCwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgyOTEsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInN1cHBvcnQiXX0.X2ubZCFBmT-vwoemMWRYuxk2M9F6MgWqsg2uuekP6Uc\n=== test-customerId-2.properties ===\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJhdWQiOlsiZHVtbXktYXVkaWVuY2UiXSwiY3VzdG9tZXJJZCI6MiwiZXhwIjo5OTk5OTk5OTk5LCJpYXQiOjE3NzAwOTgzNzEsImlzcyI6ImR1bW15LWlzc3VlciIsInJvbGVzIjpbInVzZXIiXX0.7-rSd-Q1_e7BMc90SRzauti3F8VsIjcNiNjcqpBH4_8\n\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 15, "timestamp": 1795510946161}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccac8e07b4387117def", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# This project's suites \u2014 one command, deterministic exit code.\n# Used by the DataSQRL agent and by CI.\n#\n# USAGE\n#   ./run-tests.sh [options] [<sub-project> ...]\n#\n#   <sub-project>       zero or more names, in any order, selecting which suites run.\n#                       Give none to run them all. Options may appear before, after or\n#                       between the names.\n#\n#   --compile           compile instead of running tests\n#   --test              run tests (the default)\n#   --env <name>        which environment's configs to use (default: test).\n#                       `--env=<name>` is accepted too.\n#   --list-envs         print the environments this project declares, one line\n#   --list-subprojects  print the sub-projects this project ships, one line\n#   --list-invocations  print what would run and run nothing, one\n#                       `SQRL_INVOCATION <sub-project> <verb> -r <root> <config...> -b <name>`\n#                       line per suite \u2014 exactly the arguments a real run would use\n#\n# EXAMPLES (using this project's own sub-project, creditcard_rewards)\n#   ./run-tests.sh                                     test every suite, env test\n#   ./run-tests.sh creditcard_rewards                         test just that sub-project\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env local               compile every suite as local\n#   ./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod creditcard_rewards    compile just that one, as prod\n#   ./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env local   show what would run\n#\n#   A project with several sub-projects names as many as it likes:\n#   ./run-tests.sh <sub-project> <sub-project>\n#\n# An unknown sub-project name, or an environment this project does not declare, is an error\n# (exit 2) \u2014 never a silent no-op that runs nothing and reports success.\n#\n# WRITING THIS FILE FOR A PROJECT\n#   Three blocks below are marked \"EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT\": the ENVS/SUBPROJECTS declarations,\n#   the `suite` table, and the optional setup/teardown hooks. Everything else is boilerplate \u2014\n#   copy it verbatim.\n#\n#   The `suite` table is the ONE place a project's config layering is written down: for each\n#   sub-project and each environment, the exact configs to layer, in merge order.\n#\nset -uo pipefail\n# `set -e` (exit on the first error) is not used to allow for more granular error handling.\n\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\n\n# Where this project sits relative to the compiler's mount root. Inside the DataSQRL agent the mount\n# root is the repository, so a project in a subdirectory is reached with the prefix the\n# orchestrator exports; empty everywhere else, meaning the project IS the mount root.\n#\n# It is passed as `-r <root>` rather than glued onto every config path. Both forms are equivalent\n# to the compiler, but `-r` is REQUIRED once `-b` points inside build/: without it the compiler\n# resolves script.main against the wrong root and fails with \"Could not find main SQRL script\".\nP=\"${SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX:-}\"\nif [ -n \"$P\" ]; then\n  ROOT=\"${P%/}\"                                   # the DataSQRL agent told us where we are\nelif ROOT=\"$(git rev-parse --show-prefix 2>/dev/null)\"; then\n  ROOT=\"${ROOT%/}\"; ROOT=\"${ROOT:-.}\"             # a repo checkout: our path within it\nelse\n  ROOT=\".\"                                        # not in a repo: this project is the root\nfi\n\n# The DataSQRL CLI version the DataSQRL agent container ships; CI and local runs default to the same\n# one so snapshots match. Override with SQRL_VERSION.\nSQRL_VERSION=\"${SQRL_VERSION:-0.12.5}\"\n# Fallback tag for when that version is not published yet (a release in flight). Resolved once,\n# on the first docker invocation, and never inside the DataSQRL agent \u2014 the container has its own CLI.\nSQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE=\"${SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE:-datasqrl/cmd:dev}\"\nIMAGE=\"\"\n\nresolve_image() {\n  [ -n \"$IMAGE\" ] && return 0\n  local want=\"datasqrl/cmd:${SQRL_VERSION}\"\n  if docker image inspect \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker pull -q \"$want\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    IMAGE=\"$want\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  IMAGE=\"$SQRL_FALLBACK_IMAGE\"\n  # Worth a warning, not a failure: snapshots were generated with the pinned version, so a\n  # different CLI can legitimately produce different output and fail tests that are fine.\n  printf 'warning: datasqrl/cmd:%s is not available; falling back to %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SQRL_VERSION\" \"$IMAGE\" >&2\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# The environments this project's configs cover, and the sub-projects it ships. Both are\n# declared rather than discovered, so that asking for something this project does not have\n# fails loudly instead of matching nothing, running zero suites and still exiting 0.\nENVS=\"test local prod\"\nSUBPROJECTS=\"creditcard_rewards\"\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nVERB=test\nENV=test\nLIST=\"\"\nSELECTED=\"\"\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n  case \"$1\" in\n    --compile)   VERB=compile ;;\n    --test)      VERB=test ;;\n    --env)       ENV=\"${2:-}\"; shift ;;\n    --env=*)     ENV=\"${1#--env=}\" ;;\n    --list-invocations) LIST=1 ;;\n    --list-envs) echo \"$ENVS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    --list-subprojects) echo \"$SUBPROJECTS\"; exit 0 ;;\n    -*)          echo \"unknown option: $1\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n    *)           SELECTED=\"$SELECTED $1\" ;;\n  esac\n  shift\ndone\ncase \" $ENVS \" in\n  *\" $ENV \"*) ;;\n  *) echo \"unknown env '$ENV' \u2014 this project declares: $ENVS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\nfor s in $SELECTED; do\n  case \" $SUBPROJECTS \" in\n    *\" $s \"*) ;;\n    *) echo \"unknown sub-project '$s' \u2014 this project ships: $SUBPROJECTS\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n  esac\ndone\n\nselected() {\n  [ -z \"$SELECTED\" ] && return 0\n  case \" $SELECTED \" in *\" $1 \"*) return 0 ;; esac\n  return 1\n}\n\n# Each suite writes its own build folder (the -b flag in the driver loop below), so one suite's\n# artifacts never overwrite a sibling's. `-b <name>` names a subfolder of build/: DataSQRL\n# resolves `-b fraud_store` to <project-root>/build/fraud_store\n\nsqrl() {\n  local verb=\"$1\"; shift\n  # Every named config must exist. A missing one means the suite line and this environment\n  # disagree, and compiling a partial argument list would quietly review the wrong thing.\n  local a missing=0\n  for a in \"$@\"; do\n    case \"$a\" in\n      *.json) [ -f \"$a\" ] || { echo \"missing config: $a\" >&2; missing=1; } ;;\n    esac\n  done\n  [ \"$missing\" = 1 ] && return 1\n  # List mode: report what would run and return. Bash has already expanded the environment and\n  # the path prefix, so the caller reads the real argument list without parsing this file. The\n  # sub-project is named first so each line stands on its own.\n  if [ -n \"$LIST\" ]; then\n    printf 'SQRL_INVOCATION %s %s -r %s %s\\n' \"$SUITE\" \"$verb\" \"$ROOT\" \"$*\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n\n  if [ -x /opt/agent/cmd.sh ]; then\n    /opt/agent/cmd.sh \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"     # inside the DataSQRL agent container\n    return\n  fi\n  # CI / local developer machine: run the DataSQRL CLI image. Mount the REPOSITORY root at\n  # /workspace (not just this project) so a sibling shared module this project consumes exists\n  # inside the container too; `-r <project>` then points the compiler at this project.\n  local root\n  root=\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\" || root=\"$PWD\"\n  # The image runs in UTC, and every template's snapshots are generated in UTC. The container's\n  # default is left alone. TZ passes through only when set explicitly.\n  resolve_image\n  docker run --rm ${TZ:+-e TZ=\"$TZ\"} -v \"$root\":/workspace \\\n    \"$IMAGE\" \"$verb\" -r \"$ROOT\" \"$@\"\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): setup \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# Anything the suites need first \u2014 generating or downloading test data too large for git,\n# starting a fixture. Guard it with $LIST so listing stays instant and side-effect free.\n# [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || ./scripts/generate-testdata.sh || exit 1\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT: the suite table \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# One line per (sub-project, environment), naming that combination's configs in merge order \u2014\n# base first, environment config last, since a later file overrides an earlier one. Write the\n# filenames out literally; any names work. Cover every pair of SUBPROJECTS x ENVS that this\n# project actually has, and leave out the ones it does not: asking for a pair that is missing\n# reports an error rather than compiling something half-declared.\n#\n#   <sub-project>/<env>) echo \"<base config> <env config>\" ;;\n#\nsuite() {\n  case \"$1/$2\" in\n    creditcard_rewards/test) echo \"creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-test-package.json\" ;;\n    creditcard_rewards/local) echo \"creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-local-package.json\" ;;\n    creditcard_rewards/prod) echo \"creditcard_rewards-shared-package.json creditcard_rewards-prod-package.json\" ;;\n    *) return 1 ;;\n  esac\n}\n\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# Every selected sub-project runs even after one fails, so a single run reports every failure.\nfail=0\nSUITE=\"\"\nfor sub in $SUBPROJECTS; do\n  selected \"$sub\" || continue\n  SUITE=\"$sub\"\n  args=\"$(suite \"$sub\" \"$ENV\")\" || {\n    echo \"no '$ENV' configs declared for sub-project '$sub'\" >&2\n    fail=1\n    continue\n  }\n  # shellcheck disable=SC2086  # the declaration is a space-separated argument list, on purpose\n  sqrl \"$VERB\" $args -b \"$sub\" || fail=1\ndone\n\n# \u2500\u2500 EDIT FOR YOUR PROJECT (optional): teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n# [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || ./scripts/teardown.sh\n# \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nexit $fail\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510946161}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have a good understanding. Let me look at the configure-sqrl, from-api, design-api, test-sqrl, implement-sqrl, init-sqrl, monitor-pipeline, data-observation, write-documentation skills. That's a lot. Let me read the key ones. First data-observation since step 1.\n\nActually, let me be efficient. Let me start step 1: inspect test data. First invoke data-observation skill. But I should be mindful of time. Let me read skills as needed.\n\nStep 1: data-observation. Let me read the skill and inspect the test data.\n\nLet me first mark step 1 in progress. Actually let me read data-observation skill and inspect the data.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccace2f7033b731b783", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md", "edits": [{"oldText": "- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data", "newText": "- [*] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data"}]}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaceb57242a2b18671", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/data-observation/SKILL.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccacee87963b593abb9", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/ && echo \"=== personal_loan ===\" && wc -l /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl && echo \"=== loan_payment ===\" && wc -l /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 3756, "output": 438, "cacheRead": 50048, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 155, "totalTokens": 54242, "cost": {"input": 0.0011267999999999999, "output": 0.0005256, "cacheRead": 0.000300288, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0019526879999999997}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510946163, "responseId": "0af2ce8be7e94bd68606898aeff09d06-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1653, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccace2f7033b731b783", "toolName": "edit", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md."}], "details": {"diff": "     ...\n  34 \n  35 ## Implementation Checklist\n  36 Legend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n  37 \n- 38 - [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n+ 38 - [*] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n  39 - [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n  40 - [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n  41 - [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n  42 - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n@@ -34,9 +34,9 @@\n \n ## Implementation Checklist\n Legend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n \n-- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n+- [*] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n - [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n - [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n - [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n", "firstChangedLine": 38}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 10, "timestamp": 1795510947830}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaceb57242a2b18671", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: data-observation\ndescription: Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge. \n---\n\n# Observe provided data before modeling it\n\nAlways inspect provided data (real, sample, or test data) before designing schemas, connectors, or test data. Do not infer the schema from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge alone. Always read the provided data (or subsets thereof if too large) first. Always consider provided data as authoritative and flag any inconsistencies with READMEs, requirements, or other provided instructions. Never resolve such inconsistencies without user direction.\n\nFor each provided data file:\n\n1. **Check the size first.** Use the following commands: `ls -la <file>` or `du -h <file>`. The size decides how you inspect it and whether it needs special handling (step 4).\n2. **Read a bounded prefix \u2014 never the whole file \u2014 in a format-aware way:**\n   - Text (`.jsonl`, `.ndjson`, `.csv`, `.tsv`): use `head -100 <file>` command\n   - Gzipped (`.gz`, e.g. `.csv.gz`): use `zcat <file> | head -100` command \u2014 decompress only a prefix, never the whole file\n   - Columnar Parquet: use `duckdb -c \"SELECT * FROM '<file>' LIMIT 100\"` command. For `.orc`/`.avro`, DuckDB needs a dedicated reader (e.g. `INSTALL avro; LOAD avro;` then `read_avro('<file>')`) or use another tool \u2014 a bare `FROM 'file.orc'` will not work\n   - Archives (`.zip`): use `unzip -l <file>` command to list members, then inspect a single small member\n3. **Understand the data from what you actually saw:** column names and order, types, real value formats, null/optional fields, header rows, and edge cases. Base the schema and test data on the observed rows, not on assumptions.\n4. **If a file is large** (roughly tens of MB or more), do **not** wire a connector to it or leave it unchanged in the project. As DataSQRL copies every data file into `build/` on each compile/test command, so a large file makes every run extremely slow (and `compile`/`test` commands used in downstream tasks will be blocked). **Invoke the `/handle-large-data-files` skill to sample it down or exclude it.**\n5. **For CSV specifically:** confirm the delimiter, quoting, and header. For CSV sources, the header data only lives in the schema: the column names go into the `CREATE TABLE` statement, and the CSV file contains only data rows. If the file has a header row, carry its column names into the `CREATE TABLE` statement in order, and remove the line from the file. Check that rows have a **consistent field count** \u2014 provided CSV might be ragged (trailing delimiters, extra empty columns, the odd malformed row), which passes compile and fails the Flink job at runtime. Nail the column count from the real data, then configure the source with the `/manage-connector` skill ([formats/csv.md](../manage-connector/formats/csv.md), Ragged rows).\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 4, "timestamp": 1795510947831}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccacee87963b593abb9", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "total 12244\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 root root     544 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 13 root root     416 Nov 24 08:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  157145 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   38474 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  312922 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1149330 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  142600 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   45087 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-card_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  229821 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_account.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  188425 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1040770 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1511049 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_statement.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  206287 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_limit_history.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1236995 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-credit_utilization.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 3174132 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-customer_credit_exposure.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  378776 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-delinquency_rollforward.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 2689373 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-payment_behavior.jsonl\n=== personal_loan ===\n170 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n=== loan_payment ===\n1942 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 12, "timestamp": 1795510947831}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The files are small enough (< 2MB). Let me inspect the data. Let me look at PL-115, PL-011, and compute some aggregates with jq / python.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccad4537b939d00ecb7", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata\necho \"=== personal_loan first 2 ===\"; head -2 consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\necho \"=== loan_payment first 2 ===\"; head -2 consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\necho \"=== PL-011 ===\"; grep '\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\"' consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\necho \"=== PL-115 loan ===\"; grep '\"loan_id\":\"PL-115\"' consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\necho \"=== LPMT-046 ===\"; grep '\"payment_id\":\"LPMT-046\"' consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccad54172f182482be7", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json, collections\npay=[json.loads(l) for l in open('consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl')]\nloan=[json.loads(l) for l in open('consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl')]\nprint(\"payments total\", len(pay))\nprint(\"by loan_type\", collections.Counter(p['loan_type'] for p in pay))\nprint(\"personal by status\", collections.Counter(p['payment_status'] for p in pay if p['loan_type']=='PERSONAL'))\nprint(\"personal total\", sum(1 for p in pay if p['loan_type']=='PERSONAL'))\nprint(\"late fee applied count\", sum(1 for p in pay if p['loan_type']=='PERSONAL' and p['payment_status']=='APPLIED' and (p.get('late_fee_cents') or 0)>0))\nprint(\"loans\", len(loan))\nprint(\"statuses\", collections.Counter(l['loan_status'] for l in loan))\n# null purposes\nprint(\"null purpose\", sum(1 for l in loan if l['loan_purpose'] is None))\n# distinct loan ids\nprint(\"distinct loan ids\", len(set(l['loan_id'] for l in loan)))\nprint(\"distinct payment ids\", len(set(p['payment_id'] for p in pay)))\n# PL-115 summary\npl115=[p for p in pay if p['loan_id']=='PL-115' and p['loan_type']=='PERSONAL']\nprint(\"PL-115 payments\", len(pl115), collections.Counter(p['payment_status'] for p in pl115))\nprint(\"PL-115 applied sum\", sum(p['payment_amount_cents'] for p in pl115 if p['payment_status']=='APPLIED'))\n# loans with applied late fees\nlate=[p for p in pay if p['loan_type']=='PERSONAL' and p['payment_status']=='APPLIED' and (p.get('late_fee_cents') or 0)>0]\nprint(\"late loans\", sorted(set(p['loan_id'] for p in late))[:10])\n# CUST-060 loans\nc60=[l['loan_id'] for l in loan if l['primary_borrower_customer_id']=='CUST-060']\nprint(\"CUST-060 loans\", sorted(c60))\n# PL-115 customer\nfor l in loan:\n    if l['loan_id']=='PL-115': print(\"PL-115 customer\", l['primary_borrower_customer_id'])\nEOF"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1502, "output": 825, "cacheRead": 54144, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 38, "totalTokens": 56471, "cost": {"input": 0.0004506, "output": 0.00099, "cacheRead": 0.000324864, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001765464}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510947833, "responseId": "33b9dcde1c8b4b25ad5b06aad468f105-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2176, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccad4537b939d00ecb7", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== personal_loan first 2 ===\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_d