i\n  42 - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n@@ -34,9 +34,9 @@\n \n ## Implementation Checklist\n Legend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n \n-- [*] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n+- [x] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n - [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n - [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n - [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n", "firstChangedLine": 38}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795510951956}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccadf8e79d2bb5f9c09", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: init-sqrl\ndescription: Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\nmodes: [planning, implementation, setup, question]\n---\n\nFollow these steps to initialize a DataSQRL project:\n1. When an available examples directory contains a useful starting point, review its `README.md` and relevant projects. Treat its contents as optional reference material, not required templates.\n2. Adapt relevant example files into the working directory only when they fit the task. Don't overwrite existing files. If no example fits, scaffold the project with `/opt/agent/cmd.sh init <type> <name>` instead (it creates the `<name>/` folder; see `CLI_REFERENCE.md`). Pick the type by the pipeline's output:\n   * `api`: data served through a GraphQL API (Flink, PostgreSQL, Kafka, Vert.x). The production source `connectors/sources-prod.sqrl` is an internal Kafka table (`/*+ engine(kafka) */`), exposed as a mutation.\n   * `stream`: results written to Kafka topics (Flink, Kafka). The production source is an internal Kafka table, and the script exports its result with `EXPORT HelloWorld TO log.HelloWorld;`. Without an API, only exported tables are written anywhere: export each result the user needs, and the script must have at least one `EXPORT` to compile.\n   * `dataset`: results written to Iceberg and queried through DuckDB (Flink, Iceberg, DuckDB). The production source is an existing Iceberg table (`'connector' = 'iceberg'`): set its catalog options (`catalog-type`, `catalog-name`, `warehouse`, `catalog-table`) to the user's lakehouse.\n   * The test package of every type reads the sample data in `connectors/test-data/` and compiles and tests out of the box. Replace the `Messages` sources and the sample data with the real sources (step 4).\n3. Review provided source/sink table definitions in sub-folders to identify the sqrl files that define the sources and sinks required for the implementation. If the requirements reference a provided data catalog, include that catalog and delete copied connectors unless the requirements specifically ask to create additional sources/sinks outside the catalog.\n4. Update copied files to match user requirements by:\n   * **ALWAYS** invoke the relevant skill before updating or implementing anything\n   * Use user-provided source/sink definitions from sub-folders or an included data catalog if they exist (include/import them, reusing what exists), otherwise update the template connectors. Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill (Connector Organization) before writing or editing any source/sink definition.\n   * Updating the SQRL files to use those sources & sinks and adjust implementation by matching the code to the updated table schemas and removing features that are not required. Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill before writing or significantly modifying any SQRL logic during this step.\n   * When renaming the main `.sqrl` script, update `script.main` key in the (sub-)project's base config so every package.json reference matches the actual filename before the first compile. When creating or updating any configuration, invoke `/configure-sqrl` skill.\n   * Update the project configuration if necessary.\n   * Update the GraphQL API definition and defined operations to match the new schema.\n   * Create or update the configuration files: a `*-shared-package.json` base plus `*-<env>-package.json` thin per-environment overlays. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Base Config and Environment Overlays).\n   * Add `/*+test */` annotated SQL test queries at the end of the main SQRL script(s) for each exposed table. These test queries should SELECT from the table with a WHERE filter on a known test data value and produce deterministic results. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for the test and test-data rules, then delete the template's existing snapshots and run the tests to generate fresh ones (Running Tests, Snapshot Lifecycle).\n   * Create or update the test runner script `run-tests.sh` which is the entry point for the project's tests. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for details.\n   * Update `README.md` with user requirements and project description. Add a list of features that are required by the user but not part of the template and need to be implemented\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510951957}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Our project isn't a plain `init`; it's modeled on lending. We should scaffold manually following lending layout. The plan step 2 says: scaffold the project next to lending with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README.\n\nGiven the guidance, I'll create:\n- loan-payments.sqrl (main script placeholder, but actually step 6/7/8 will fill it)\n- loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls, operations.v1.graphql, tests/\n- snapshots/loan-payments/\n- README.md\n\nThe plan calls the project \"loan-payments\". The base config naming: loan-payments-shared-package.json, loan-payments-test-package.json, loan-payments-prod-package.json.\n\nBut should I call it init? The init skill says use /opt/agent/cmd.sh init <type> <name> when no example fits. But we have a clear example (lending). Let me follow lending layout manually rather than using init. Actually the skill says \"Adapt relevant example files into the working directory only when they fit the task.\" Since lending is nearly a perfect fit for the config/API structure, and we can't copy its files (read-only, but we can create our own based on it). \"Never copy their files into your project\" refers to sibling projects in the repo. The examples in /opt/datasqrl-examples are templates we adapt. Hmm, but the lending sibling is a project in this repo; the rule says never copy sibling files into your project. But we are allowed to follow the pattern. I'll write our own files.\n\nLet me not scaffold empty files prematurely? The plan steps 3-8 will create the files. Step 2 is \"scaffold ... with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README.\" I'll create the directory structure and placeholder files. But maybe it's cleaner to create the main script skeleton and folders. I'll create the API folder and tests dir; README at step 13. Let me create the directories now and the main script will be written in step 6.\n\nActually, creating a main script with content that isn't complete could break. But step 2 is just scaffolding. Let me create the directory structure with a minimal main script? The next steps edit it. Let me create the directories and a placeholder main script, then step 3 writes configs.\n\nLet me create directories. I'll create loan-payments.sqrl later. Let me create directories now via bash mkdir.\n\nActually the plan says scaffold with main script, API folder, snapshots folder, README. Let me create:\n- loan-payments.sqrl (placeholder with header comment; will be filled in step 6)\n- loan-payments-api/tests/\n- snapshots/loan-payments/\n- README.md (will be properly written step 13, but create skeleton now)\n\nLet me do that. But careful: don't overwrite. Let me use write for the main script and README, mkdir for folders.\n\nActually, scaffold step is non-MUST skill (init-sqrl). I'm invoking it. Let me create.\n\nLet me create the main script placeholder and README. I'll write the main script content in step 6 anyway. Maybe write a comment-only file now.\n\nLet me proceed.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Step 1 confirmed the data. Now step 2: scaffold the project. I'll create the directory structure modeled on `lending/`."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaed627c629a02d6df", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && mkdir -p loan-payments-api/tests snapshots/loan-payments && ls -la"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1074, "output": 800, "cacheRead": 58880, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 706, "totalTokens": 60754, "cost": {"input": 0.00032219999999999997, "output": 0.0009599999999999999, "cacheRead": 0.00035328, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00163548}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510951959, "responseId": "9498c0b42e384fcf972ac3a27802cb2c-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 3591, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaed627c629a02d6df", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "total 20\ndrwxr-xr-x  8 root root   256 Nov 24  2026 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 11 root root   352 Nov 24 08:28 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  4 root root   128 Nov 24 08:46 adr\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 15626 Nov 24 09:02 AGENTS.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 root root   224 Nov 24 09:02 .datasqrl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  1094 Nov 24 08:46 .datasqrl_agent_results.json\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24  2026 loan-payments-api\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root    96 Nov 24  2026 snapshots\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 10, "timestamp": 1795510955560}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now create the main script placeholder and README skeleton. Let me write a minimal placeholder main script. Actually, I'll write the full implementation now? No\u2014plan says step 6. But scaffolding step just needs the main script. I'll create a placeholder.\n\nActually, better: create the main script now with just comments? The config's script.main will point at it. Then step 6 fills it. Let me write a minimal placeholder.\n\nLet me create README skeleton too. Actually README will be fully written at step 13. I'll create a basic placeholder.\n\nLet me create main script.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaf2d77a728cb6c30a", "name": "write", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/loan-payments.sqrl", "content": "-- Loan Payments API: personal-loan payment summaries and payment history for customers.\n-- Sources come from the shared data-catalog module; see loan-payments-shared-package.json.\n"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaf37d7c71a789dfe7", "name": "write", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/loan-payments/README.md", "content": "# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for personal loans. It reads the shared finance\n[`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) sources `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment`, builds a payment\nhistory and a per-loan payment summary, and exposes them through a JWT-authorized GraphQL, REST and\nMCP API.\n\nSee the documentation section at the end of this file (completed during implementation).\n"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 281, "output": 366, "cacheRead": 60672, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 123, "totalTokens": 61319, "cost": {"input": 8.43e-05, "output": 0.0004392, "cacheRead": 0.000364032, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0008875319999999999}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510955563, "responseId": "d093616c604e4ecf83e368be06e2b4b6-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1771, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaf2d77a728cb6c30a", "toolName": "write", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully wrote to /workspace/loan-payments/loan-payments.sqrl"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510957339}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaf37d7c71a789dfe7", "toolName": "write", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully wrote to /workspace/loan-payments/README.md"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510957339}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Step 2 done. Mark [x], start step 3: write the config files. First invoke configure-sqrl skill. Let me read it.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccaf9977421a6f372c8", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"edits": [{"newText": "- [x] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/`", "oldText": "- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/`"}, {"newText": "- [*] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base", "oldText": "- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base"}], "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccafa6c735291becbec", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/SKILL.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 58, "output": 242, "cacheRead": 61312, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 32, "totalTokens": 61612, "cost": {"input": 1.74e-05, "output": 0.0002904, "cacheRead": 0.000367872, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.000675672}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510957341, "responseId": "0dadfd6c590d4dc4b54c517866106eed-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1497, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccaf9977421a6f372c8", "toolName": "edit", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md."}], "details": {"diff": "     ...\n  35 ## Implementation Checklist\n  36 Legend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n  37 \n  38 - [x] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- 39 - [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- 40 - [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n+ 39 - [x] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n+ 40 - [*] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n  41 - [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n  42 - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n  43 - [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n  44 - [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n@@ -35,10 +35,10 @@\n ## Implementation Checklist\n Legend: `- [ ]` not started \u00b7 `- [*]` in progress \u00b7 `- [x]` done/passing \u00b7 `- [~]` blocked.\n \n - [x] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data for `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` to confirm the payload shapes and the edge cases the acceptance criteria rely on (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, the auto-loan rows). [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n-- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n-- [ ] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n+- [x] 2. Scaffold the project next to `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n+- [*] 3. Write the `loan-payments-shared-package.json` base plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n - [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n - [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n - [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and enrich them from their loan record (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n - [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` anchored on all latest personal loans and left-joined to the applied-payment aggregates, so only `APPLIED` payments contribute totals and a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n", "firstChangedLine": 39}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510958846}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccafa6c735291becbec", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: configure-sqrl\ndescription: Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n---\nUpdate the configuration of a DataSQRL project by editing its `*-package.json` files or creating new ones. Read **Configuration Files** first (how the files are split and layered), then look up the key you need under **Configuration Content**.\n\nThe configuration supports environment placeholders in the configuration fields that process them. Read **Environment Variables** before adding credentials or tokens: the normal and secret forms have different compile-time behavior, and support is not universal across every configuration field.\n\n# Configuration Files\n\n## Base Config and Environment Overlays\n\nFor projects that need multiple deployment profiles, split configuration into a **base** (shared) and thin **per-environment overlays** to reduce duplication. This is a recommended project convention: SQRL accepts any ordered list of package JSON files and has no special meaning for the filenames or environment names.\n\n- **`<project>-shared-package.json`** is the base config file that includes common settings to every environment which could be `version`, `enabled-engines`, `script.main`, `script.api`, and the `engines`/`compiler` settings.\n- **`<project>-<env>-package.json`** (one per environment `-test`, `-prod`, `-local`, `-dev`) \u2014 add or overwrite ONLY what differs from the base configuration file (e.g. `script.config.environment`, `test-runner`, an engine override). Note that `-dev` is a local development environment, like `-local`.\n\nExample for the base config and environment overlays:\nThe `lending360` project with a test and a production environment:\n\n**`lending360-shared-package.json`** \u2014 the base: everything both environments share.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"kafka\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"lending360.sqrl\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"lending360-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"lending360-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"api\": { \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"], \"endpoints\": \"FULL\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**`lending360-test-package.json`** the test overlay: ONLY what test changes.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": { \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"0 s\" } // Disabling source idleness is advised for tests, see the `/test-sqrl` skill\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/lending360/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"lending360-api/tests\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**`lending360-prod-package.json`** the production overlay.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": { \"environment\": \"prod\" }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"deployment\": {\n        \"jobmanager-size\": \"small\",\n        \"taskmanager-size\": \"large.mem\",\n        \"taskmanager-count\": 3\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRules for writing the configuration files:\n\n* **Merging is per field, last file wins.** In the example above, the test overlay's `table.exec.source.idle-timeout: \"0 s\"` replaces the base's `\"30 s\"`, while `execution.runtime-mode: \"STREAMING\"` from the base is kept. An overlay does not replace the whole `engines.flink.config` object.\n* **`test-runner` belongs only in the `-test` overlay**.\n* **Every independently compiled or tested project and sub-project MUST have a named package file for each environment it uses:** `<project-or-sub-project>-<env>-package.json` (for example, `fraud_store-test-package.json`). Do not use an unqualified standalone package file for a deployable sub-project. The name lets the agent's package-discovery fallback identify the package that belongs to each build.\n* If settings are common across environments or sibling sub-projects, share them through a `*-shared-package.json` base; keep only environment- or sub-project-specific settings in the named package files.\n* **Fix duplicated configuration:** move common settings to `<project>-shared-package.json` and retain only differing fields in `<project>-<env>-package.json`.\n* **Give every compiled combination an auth provider when the script reads a claim.** The compiler checks the merged files of each compile. A script with `METADATA FROM 'auth.<claim>'` fails to compile when the merged `engines.vertx.config` has no `auth` block. So put `auth` in the shared base, or in each overlay that compiles the script. Use `${...}` placeholders for values that differ per environment. This also applies when a deployment mounts its own `vertx-config.json`.\n\n## Sub-projects (multiple deployments in one project)\n\nOne project folder may hold several **sub-projects**, meaning separate deployments over the same source files, each with its own configuration.\n\nEvery sub-project has **its own per-environment overlays** and layers them under a base. The base is either **its own** or **shared with sibling sub-projects**. Never combine sub-project specific configurations from different sub-projects.\n\n| File | Role |\n|------|------|\n| `<sub-project>-shared-package.json` | own base \u2014 `enabled-engines`, `script.main`, `script.api`, settings common to all of this sub-project's environments. Use one base per sub-project when the settings the sub-projects share differ (e.g. different engine sets). |\n| `<project>-shared-package.json` | shared base \u2014 only what is common to **every** sub-project layering under it (`version`, common `engines`/`compiler` settings, `enabled-engines` if identical). Whatever differs \u2014 at least `script.main` \u2014 is declared in the sub-project's own files, so the base stays valid for each of them. |\n| `<sub-project>-test-package.json` | test overlay \u2014 `test-runner` with its **own** `snapshot-folder`, plus test-only overwrites |\n| `<sub-project>-prod-package.json` | production overlay \u2014 production `script.config`, API protocols, deployment sizing and prod-only overwrites |\n\nAn available examples directory may include a useful multi-deployment reference. For example, `healthcare-study/` in `datasqrl-examples` illustrates separate stream, API, and analytics deployments alongside a shared data catalog; use it only when it is present and relevant.\n\n```\nfraud_store-shared-package.json      fraud_training-shared-package.json\nfraud_store-test-package.json        fraud_training-test-package.json\nfraud_store-prod-package.json\n```\n\nExample with **one shared base** for two sub-projects that differ only in their main script and snapshot folder:\n\n```\nfraud-shared-package.json            # version, enabled-engines, common engines/compiler settings \u2014 no script.main\nfraud_store-test-package.json        # \"script\": { \"main\": \"fraud_store.sqrl\" }, test-runner with snapshots/fraud_store/\nfraud_store-prod-package.json        # \"script\": { \"main\": \"fraud_store.sqrl\" }, prod settings\nfraud_training-test-package.json     # \"script\": { \"main\": \"fraud_training.sqrl\" }, test-runner with snapshots/fraud_training/\nfraud_training-prod-package.json     # \"script\": { \"main\": \"fraud_training.sqrl\" }, prod settings\n```\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud-shared-package.json fraud_store-test-package.json -b fraud_store\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test -r . fraud-shared-package.json fraud_training-test-package.json -b fraud_training\n```\n\nEach sub-project's `-test-package.json` is its own test run. To setup a test and run, invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill.\n\n## How to Compile/Test\n\n**Compile**\n\nCompile by layering base then overlay (merged last-wins):\n\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r . lending360-shared-package.json lending360-test-package.json -b lending360\n```\n\nThe `-b <sub-project>` flag gives each sub-project its own build folder *inside* `build/`, so a compile keeps sub-project's build artifacts intact.\n\n**Test**\nInvoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for which test packages to run and how.\n\n# Configuration Content\n\n## Engines (`enabled-engines`, `engines`)\n\n**Enable the minimal set of engines the project actually uses \u2014 nothing more.** Enabled engines determine what pipeline and deployment artifacts SQRL produces; some engines also provision or run services. Start from `flink` and add an engine only when a concrete capability below requires it.\nAt the end of the implementation, **always** check your engine set whether if is minimal or not, and prune engines that are no longer used.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"]\n}\n```\n\nEnable each engine **only if** its trigger is present:\n\n| Engine                                 | Enable ONLY IF                                                                                             | Omit when                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **flink**                              | always \u2014 streaming/batch data processor                                                                    | never omit                                                                  |\n| **postgres**                           | the API serves data from database-backed tables (query/point-lookup endpoints, full-text or vector search) | there is no served DB query \u2014 e.g. the only output is a data-lake or EXPORT |\n| **kafka**                              | there is a `SUBSCRIBE` subscription or a CREATE TABLE annotated with `/*+engine(kafka)*/` hint             | the API is plain request/response with no subscriptions or mutations        |\n| **iceberg**                            | a table is written to a data lake \u2014 must be paired with at least one Iceberg query engine (the two rows below) | there is no data-lake output                                                |\n| **duckdb**                             | iceberg is enabled and the project serves an API, runs tests or runs locally over the Iceberg tables \u2014 the only *full* query engine, the one the server queries | iceberg is not used \u2014 never enable duckdb on its own                        |\n| **snowflake, sparksql, redshift, trino**      | iceberg is enabled and the project needs to query Iceberg tables through that external engine( shallow query engines): the compiler only generates that engine's table definitions and views | iceberg is not used, or the project does not use that external engine |\n| **vertx**                              | the project exposes a GraphQL/REST/MCP API                                                                 | it is a pure ETL/export pipeline with no served API                         |\n\nCommon minimal recipes (pick the smallest that fits, then adjust):\n* **API over a database, no streaming pub/sub** \u2192 `[\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"]`\n* **API with subscriptions or mutations** \u2192 `[\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"kafka\", \"vertx\"]`\n* **Data lake queried from Snowflake, no API** \u2192 base `[\"flink\", \"iceberg\", \"duckdb\"]` for tests and local runs, prod overlay `[\"flink\", \"iceberg\", \"snowflake\"]`\n* **API over a data lake** \u2192 `[\"flink\", \"iceberg\", \"duckdb\", \"vertx\"]`\n* **API over a data lake that Redshift also queries** \u2192 `[\"flink\", \"iceberg\", \"duckdb\", \"redshift\", \"vertx\"]`\n* **Catalog / schema test** \u2192 `[\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"]`\n\nOnly enable iceberg plus query engines and postgres if both high-volume & high latency and low latency querying is needed in the same project.\n\nIceberg query engines come in two kinds. `duckdb` is the full engine: it is connected to the server and runs the API queries and the tests. `snowflake`, `sparksql`, `redshift` and `trino` are shallow engines: the compiler generates table definitions and query SQL to run separately in those external engines, and they are not integrated with the DataSQRL server. Read [iceberg-query.md](iceberg-query.md) before choosing between them.\n\n### Configuring the selected engines\n\nEnabled engines are configured under the `engines` field. Engine settings that hold for every environment go in the base `<project>-shared-package.json`; only the ones that differ go in an environment overlay, checkout [Base Config and Environment Overlays](#base-config-and-environment-overlays).\n\n**Before writing the configuration for an engine, ALWAYS read its reference file below.** Option names, nesting and value formats are engine-specific and are not guessable. Read the file for **every** engine in your set first, then write the `engines` block. \n\n| Engine in your set | Read this before configuring it |\n|--------------------|---------------------------------|\n| `flink` | [flink.md](flink.md) |\n| `vertx` | [vertx.md](vertx.md) |\n| `kafka` | [kafka.md](kafka.md) |\n| `postgres` | [postgres.md](postgres.md) |\n| `iceberg` | [iceberg.md](iceberg.md) |\n| `duckdb`, `snowflake`, `sparksql`, `redshift`, `trino` | [iceberg-query.md](iceberg-query.md) |\n\nImportant rules:\n* An enabled engine with **no** entry under `engines` runs on its defaults. Add a setting only when a requirement, a connector, or the engine's reference file calls for it.\n* Keep `engines` entries aligned with `enabled-engines`. SQRL removes a configuration for a disabled engine and warns when that configuration came from a user package; delete it yourself when pruning the engine.\n* Compulsory Slow-query logging (required with `vertx`): Every project that enables `vertx` must enable slow-query logging. Read [Slow-Query Logging](vertx.md#slow-query-logging) in vertx.md, then set `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig` in the base config (project base `<project>-shared-package.json`, or the own base of each sub-project `<sub-project>-shared-package.json` that enables `vertx`) with thresholds you chose for this API.\n\n### Deployment (`engines.<engine>.deployment`)\n\nThe `deployment` section under each `engines` configuration specifies what resources (i.e. instance type and number of instances) to use for deploying a DataSQRL pipeline in DataSQRL Cloud.\n\nAlways read [Cloud Deployment Configuration](cloud-deployment.md) when configuring deployment size and resources, or when upgrading an existing deployment to a new DataSQRL version.\n\n## Script (`script`)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"my-project.sqrl\", // Main SQRL script for pipeline\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": { //default API version\n        \"schema\": \"fraud_store-api/schema.v1.graphqls\", // GraphQL schema defines the API\n        \"operations\": [\"fraud_store-api/operations.v1.graphql\"], //List of GraphQL queries that define operations which are exposed as API endpoints\n        \"openapi\": \"fraud_store-api/openapi.v1.json\", //Optional OpenAPI baseline. See the /from-api skill\n        \"mcp-apps\": \"fraud_store-api/apps-v1\" //Optional directory of MCP apps (HTML UIs for MCP tools). See the /design-api skill\n      }\n    },\n    \"config\": { //Arbitrary JSON object used by the mustache templating engine to instantiate SQRL file templating variables\n      \"environment\": \"kafka\" \n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### API Versioning and Compatibility\n\n`script.api` serves one or more versioned APIs. Version keys must be `v` followed by a number (`v1`, `v2`); each version requires a `schema`, `operations`, an `openapi` compatibility specification, or an `mcp-apps` directory. SQRL infers the schema from the SQRL script when one is not configured, and generates an OpenAPI specification for every version. Set `openapi` to a previously generated specification when compilation must reject backward-incompatible API changes. Set `mcp-apps` to the directory with the self-contained `.html` files of the version's MCP apps; there is no top-level `script.mcp-apps`.\n\nFor one `v1` API only, the legacy top-level fields are also supported. The top-level `operations` field is deprecated, so configure operations under `script.api` in new projects:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"my-project.sqrl\",\n    \"graphql\": \"api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n    \"operations\": [\"api/operations.v1.graphql\"],\n    \"database\": \"my-mutation-database.json\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse **either** top-level `graphql`/`operations` or `script.api`, never both. `script.database` is an optional mutation-database JSON emitted by a previous compile; keeping it in the config makes SQRL check mutation-schema backward compatibility.\n\n### Included Projects (`script.include`)\n\nReuse scripts from **another SQRL project** (e.g. a shared data catalog) by declaring it under `script.include`. \nEach key under `script.include` is the **namespace** the consumer imports it under; `package` is the path, **relative to the current project root**, to **any package JSON file at the included project's top level** (`package.json`, `<name>-shared-package.json`, \u2026). DataSQRL takes that file's parent directory as the include root and walks it and all its subfolders, loading every `.sqrl` script under the namespace.\n\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"consumer.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n    \"<namespace>\": { \"package\": \"../other-project/package.json\", \"config\": { } }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"consumer.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": {                                                   // namespace \u2192 IMPORT data_catalog.<script>\n        \"package\": \"../other-project/other-project-shared-package.json\",  // path; relative from this project's root to the other project's TOP-LEVEL package file (its <name>-shared-package.json, or package.json); that file's folder + all subfolders is the include root\n        \"config\": { \"environment\": \"prod\" }                               // optional mustache overrides for that project's {{...}} template vars\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n* The **key** (`<namespace>`) is the import namespace. Name it with **underscores** (`data_catalog`), because a dash has a different meaning in SQL. `package` is the path **relative to the current project** to the other project's top-level package JSON file \u2014 its parent directory is the include root, and the SQRL files in that directory and its subdirectories are loaded; `config` supplies Mustache override values for that project's `script.config` template variables (`{{...}}`).\n- **Template values for the included scripts.** An included project's scripts may use `{{...}}` placeholders \u2014 variables such as `{{environment}}`, sections such as `{{#is_batch}}\u2026{{/is_batch}}`. DataSQRL renders them from two sources: the `script.config` of the package file named in `package`, and the include entry's own `config`, which overrides it. Add `config` only when you have values to pass: it must hold at least one property, so an empty `{}` is rejected (`must have at least 1 properties at location [/script/include/<namespace>/config]`).\n- **Declare `script.include` in the config that declares `script.main`** \u2014 the base config (or, with a base shared by several sub-projects, each sub-project's file that declares its `script.main`). Overlays that only add engine or test-runner settings need no `script.include`.\n- **Declare only the projects this project actually needs**, not everything the repository happens to contain: every declared project is copied into `build/` at compile time, so an unnecessary declaration are expensive.\n- **`standalone` includes.** By default the consumer owns the included project's internal tables like its own. Set `\"standalone\": true` on the include entry only when the included project has its own deployment; it can go in the deployment's package file alone, but that entry must repeat the include's `package`, because every include entry requires it. Then the included project owns its internal **Kafka** tables, and its deployment writes what the statements it plans write: the compiler follows the imports of the `script.main` in the include's `package` file, so point `package` at the file that sets the deployment's `script.main`, not at a `<name>-shared-package.json` without one (the compiler then warns, and the consumer writes everything itself). The consumer reads the topics of the subscriptions that deployment exposes (`SUBSCRIBE` tables and the tables `SUBSCRIBE` functions read, in its main script and inline imports) instead of writing them, and skips the include's `EXPORT`/`INSERT` statements into the log, its internal Kafka tables and external tables, with a notice per statement. `kafka.json` lists the topics the consumer uses by name under `includedTopics`; their settings, such as the partition count, belong in the included project's own package files. Both projects must resolve the same topic names and key/value formats, and the `kafka` topic template must not use `${sqrl:table-id}`. Internal Iceberg tables are not covered yet. See *Standalone Includes* in the configuration docs.\n- At compile time the included project is **copied into `build/<namespace>/`** from which IMPORT statements are resolved. Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec \u2192 IMPORT Statement \u2192 Included projects / multi-project).\n\n## Test Runner (`test-runner`)\n\nPut test-runner settings in a test overlay when using base/overlay packages. For streaming pipelines, prefer `required-checkpoints` with `delay-sec: -1`; otherwise use a wall-clock `delay-sec`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/myproject/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"api/tests/\",\n    \"use-inferred-schema\": true,\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"mutation-delay-sec\": 0,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"create-topics\": [\"input-topic\"],\n    \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <test-token>\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDefaults are `./snapshots`, `./tests`, `true`, `30`, `0`, and `0` respectively for the first six fields. `create-topics` and `headers` are optional, non-empty values. Header values are sent literally: environment placeholders such as `${TEST_TOKEN}` are not resolved in `test-runner`, so put a test-only token in the test package (or in per-query `.properties` header files).\n\n## Connector Templates (`connectors`)\n\nThe `connectors` field holds the templates that decide how the engines in the pipeline exchange table data with each other: the Kafka topic for a mutation table, the Postgres table behind a materialized query, the Iceberg table for a lake sink, the log/print sink.\n**No `connectors` field result in default usage.** Add or overwrite a template only when a requirement forces it: a fixed topic naming scheme, a non-default format, extra Kafka client properties, or credentials that do not come from the standard environment variables. **Before any change, ALWAYS read [connector-templates.md](connector-templates.md) first.** It lists every default template in full and the fields that can be overwritten. An overwritten template replaces only the fields you name.\n\nNote that these connector templates apply to table definition managed by DataSQRL are different from the source/sink connectors explicitly defined in SQRL (`CREATE TABLE ... WITH ('connector' = ...)`). For those, invoke the `/manage-connector` skill.\n\n## Compiler (`compiler`)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"logger\": \"print\",\n    \"compile-flink-plan\": true,\n    \"extended-scalar-types\": true,\n    \"cost-model\": \"DEFAULT\",\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES_NO_SOURCE\",\n    \"explain\": {\n      \"sql\": false,\n      \"logical\": false,\n      \"physical\": false,\n      \"sorted\": true\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 3,\n      \"default-limit\": 10,\n      \"paginated-results\": false\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`logger` is `print` or `none`. `extended-scalar-types` enables extended generated GraphQL scalars. `compile-flink-plan` controls Flink physical-plan generation and is not supported for batch pipelines. `cost-model` is `DEFAULT`, `READ`, or `WRITE`, and the default is `DEFAULT`. `DEFAULT` favors Flink unless joins make Flink too expensive, `WRITE` always favors Flink, and `READ` always favors the database. The compiler assigns each table to the cheapest engine. An `/*+ engine(...) */` hint limits the engines that the compiler can pick. With `DEFAULT`, Flink and the database can have the same cost for a table. Then the compiler assigns the table to Flink. To see the engine of each table, read its `Stage:` line in `build/pipeline_explain.txt`.\n\n`explain` controls plan output under `build/`: use `sql`, `logical`, or `physical` to include each representation, and `sorted` for deterministic order. Although the current schema accepts `text` and `visual`, the current compiler configuration interface does not consume them; do not rely on those keys.\n\n### Optimizer configuration\n\n`predicate-pushdown-rules` applies only to Flink streaming when `compile-flink-plan` is enabled:\n\n* `DEFAULT` uses the normal Flink optimizer rules.\n* `LIMITED_RULES_NO_SOURCE` strips downstream predicate-pushdown rules to maximize subgraph elimination.\n* `LIMITED_RULES` additionally strips table-source pushdown rules.\n* `LIMITED_TABLE_SOURCE_RULES` is also accepted by the configuration schema; use it only when a specific SQRL version/workload requires it, as it's deprecated.\n\n### API protocol configuration\n\nDataSQRL's authoritative API model is GraphQL; REST and MCP are generated from it. `protocols` may contain `GRAPHQL`, `REST`, and/or `MCP` and defaults to all three. `endpoints` is one of:\n\n* `FULL`: generated REST/MCP operations for schema queries and mutations, plus explicit operations.\n* `GRAPHQL`: flexible GraphQL but only explicit operations for other protocols.\n* `OPS_ONLY`: explicit GraphQL operations only.\n\n`add-prefix` prefixes generated operation names to avoid collisions. `max-result-depth` controls generated REST/MCP result traversal depth, `default-limit` is the generated query limit, and `paginated-results: true` wraps multi-row query results in a page with pagination metadata.\n\n### Tags (`compiler.tags`)\n\nConfigure tags only when the requirements ask the compiler to enforce data classification or similar tags, and use the tag names and values they define. When `compiler.tags` is present, the compiler validates the `@name` and `@name=value` tags in doc strings and propagates them from the tables a query reads to the table it defines. Without it, doc strings are plain text.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"tags\": {\n      \"classification\": { \"values\": [\"unclassified\", \"secret\", \"top_secret\"] },\n      \"gdpr\": {},\n      \"jurisdiction\": { \"required-columns\": { \"jurisdiction\": \"STRING\" } }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nEach key is a tag name. `values` lists the allowed values from lowest to highest; a tag without `values`, like `gdpr`, is a boolean tag that is written `@gdpr`. `required-columns` maps each column that every table carrying the tag must have to its type. Tag names and values may only contain letters, digits, underscores and dashes, and are case-insensitive. A required column name matches a column ignoring case, unless it is in backticks, such as ``\"`jurisdiction`\": \"STRING\"``, which only matches the column with exactly that name. A plain name that matches several columns whose names differ only in case is a compile error. Values must be unique within a tag, also ignoring case, and so must required column names, unless both are in backticks. Put the tags in the shared base: combined package files merge them per tag. To write tags and `declassify` hints in scripts, invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill (Tags in doc-strings). The compiler lists the tags of every table in `build/pipeline_explain.txt` and in the deployment plans of the database and Kafka.\n\n## Environment Variables (`${VAR}`)\n\n`${NAME}` is a normal environment-variable template; `${NAME:-fallback}` and `${NAME:=fallback}` both use `fallback` when `NAME` is absent. In supported compile-time fields, a present value is resolved and can be written into `build/` or `build/deploy/`; otherwise the normal template remains for runtime resolution. Do not use this form for a credential that must stay out of compile artifacts.\n\n`${{NAME}}` is a secret template and accepts only a variable name. Even if `NAME` is available at compile time, SQRL does not read it: in supported paths it rewrites `${{NAME}}` to `${NAME}` in the generated artifact for runtime resolution. This conversion applies only to user `connectors` configuration and Flink `CREATE TABLE ... WITH (...)` properties\u2014not arbitrary JSON, including `engines.vertx.config`.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510958846}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The configure-sqrl skill references vertx.md, flink.md, postgres.md for engine config, and says always read them before writing the config. Let me read vertx.md (for auth and slow-query logging) and flink.md. Also I need to know about the catalog include config: since catalog scripts contain `{{environment}}` and `{{#is_batch}}`, when we include via a package file, which one? lending uses `../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json` with config `is_batch: false`. But our inventory says declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`. Hmm the AGENTS addendum says top-level package. lending uses ontology-test-package.json. Let me check what ontology-test-package.json contains.\n\nLet me read the vertx.md, flink.md, and data-catalog ontology-test-package.json and ontology.sqrl (to see the consumer_loans import). Actually the main concern: the catalog's `consumer_loans-{{environment}}.sqrl` uses `{{environment}}` and that's the consumer's script.config, not the included module's. Let me check how lending imports: `IMPORT data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}}`. The `{{environment}}` is resolved from the consumer's script.config. Right. But the catalog scripts might have their own imports. Let me check ontology.sqrl and the consumer_loans.sqrl's imports (it had no imports, just CREATE TABLE). The `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` does `IMPORT consumer_loans.*;`. So the namespace resolution: `data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-test` \u2192 the file imports `consumer_loans.*` (relative within the catalog). That's fine.\n\nWait - but the included package determines the include root and the `config`. lending includes `ontology-test-package.json` with config `is_batch:false`. Let me look.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb03517c809cfcb5ab", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/workspace/data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb037e7552b9ffa648", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/vertx.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb03b27070a20f5cc7", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/flink.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 6918, "output": 576, "cacheRead": 61568, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 444, "totalTokens": 69062, "cost": {"input": 0.0020754, "output": 0.0006912, "cacheRead": 0.000369408, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0031360079999999996}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510958849, "responseId": "dc1d9e432bff4a7d882b7ed2733a24d0-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2345, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03517c809cfcb5ab", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": {\n      \"environment\": \"test\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"1 s\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/ontology/\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510961200}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb037e7552b9ffa648", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Vert.x Engine Configuration\n\n| Key        | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                 |\n|------------|------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n\n## Server Configuration Overrides\n\nThe compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|-----|-------|\n| `httpServerOptions.port` | HTTP port (default `8888`). Keep `8888` for the `test` command, whose test runner connects to that port |\n| `corsHandlerOptions` | CORS policy. Defaults allow all origins; restrict `allowedOrigin`/`allowedOrigins` in production |\n| `poolOptions.maxSize` | PostgreSQL connection pool size |\n| `poolOptions.maxLifetime` | Seconds before a pooled PostgreSQL connection is closed and replaced (default `600`), so connections rebalance across replicas; `0` keeps them forever |\n| `pgConnectOptions.cachePreparedStatements` | Cache prepared statements per connection so each query is planned once (default `true`) |\n| `servletConfig` | Endpoint paths (`graphQLEndpoint`, `restEndpoint`, `mcpEndpoint`, `graphiQLEndpoint`), prefixed with the API version, e.g. `/v1/graphql` |\n| `publicGraphQLEndpointEnabled` | Set to `false` to disable the public GraphQL endpoint; REST and MCP endpoints keep working |\n| `onlyConfiguredGraphQLOperations` | Set to `true` to only accept the predefined API operations instead of arbitrary GraphQL queries |\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"corsHandlerOptions\": {\n          \"allowedOrigin\": \"https://app.example.com\",\n          \"allowCredentials\": true\n        },\n        \"poolOptions\": {\n          \"maxSize\": 16\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n\n## Slow-Query Logging\n\nEnable slow-query logging in every project that enables `vertx`. The server then logs each API request that takes longer than its threshold or that fails. A request's threshold is the lowest threshold among the fields it runs. A slow query then appears in the logs, not only as a client timeout.\n\nPut the block in the base config, so every deployed environment has it:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the logging on |\n| `defaultThresholdMs` | `60000` | The threshold for fields without an entry in `thresholds` |\n| `thresholds` | `{}` | A threshold for requests that run this field, keyed `Type.field`, e.g. `{\"Query.OrdersByCustomer\": 5000}` or `{\"Customers.orders\": 5000}`. A request uses the lowest threshold among the fields it runs |\n| `alwaysLogErrors` | `true` | Also logs failed requests, such as a timeout while waiting for a database connection |\n| `maxLoggedTracesPerMinute` | `60` | Limits the log volume during an incident |\n| `maxFieldTraces` | `50` | Maximum number of query timings in one log line |\n\n### Choosing the threshold\n\nMake an educated guess for each threshold from how the API is used. The values in the example are a starting point, not a default to copy.\n\n- If the requirements state a latency target for the API, use that target. Otherwise, start from the consumer of the API: 1000 to 2000 ms for user interfaces and dashboards, 2000 to 5000 ms for service-to-service calls.\n- Look for heavy queries, such as a large aggregate. If their latency is much higher than that of the other queries, give each one its own entry in `thresholds`, keyed by its field (`Query.<Field>` for a table or table function, `<Type>.<field>` for a relationship). As a request uses the lowest threshold among its fields, the entry applies only when a request runs that field alone. Any other field in the same request, including a nested relationship field without its own entry, brings the lower threshold back.\n\n- Keep every threshold well below the client request timeout. The server writes the log line after a request ends, and it does not stop the request. A threshold below the timeout logs the requests that are slow but still succeed.\n\n## Large-Result Logging\n\nThe server logs a warning for each database query that returns many rows, before it converts the rows to JSON. Converting a very large result blocks the server, so the warning is written even when the pod is killed afterwards. The warning names the GraphQL field path, the row count, the elapsed time and the SQL (cut after 2000 characters), and never the parameter values. The elapsed time counts from issuing the query to receiving its rows, including any wait for a pool connection, so it is not the database time. Also when this warning is off, the server logs an error for a failed query, with its field path and SQL, and for rows it cannot convert to JSON, with the field path.\n\nThis warning is off by default and only covers large results. Slow requests are logged by [Slow-Query Logging](#slow-query-logging), which has per-field thresholds.\n\nAll keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `enabled` | `false` | Turns the warning on |\n| `rowCountThreshold` | `10000` | Warns when a query returns at least this many rows. `0` or less turns the warning off |\n\n## Authentication\n\nConfigure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\ntoken is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n            },\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key                       | Required | Default                          | Meaning                                                                          |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n| `resource`                | No       | derived from the request         | Public MCP endpoint URL for RFC 9728 metadata                                    |\n| `scopesSupported`         | No       | `[\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"]` | Scopes advertised to MCP clients                                                 |\n| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n\nWith neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\nbe fetched, and must be restarted after a key rotation. Each key verifies tokens signed with its own `alg`, or with the\ndefault for its type (`RS256` for `RSA`, `ES256` for `EC`, `EdDSA` for `OKP`, `HS256` for `oct`); tokens signed with\nany other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\na key whose `alg` is not listed is ignored, and a key without `alg` is used with each listed algorithm of its type (for\nexample `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\ndoes not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\nshapes are compile errors.\n\nThe MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\nwhen no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n\nFor a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n\nString values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\nserver starts.\n\nPackage layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\nlayer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n\n## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"jwtAuth\": {\n          \"pubSecKeys\": [{\n            \"algorithm\": \"HS256\",\n            \"buffer\": \"<base64-encoded signer secret>\"\n          }],\n          \"jwtOptions\": {\n            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n            \"expiresInSeconds\": 3600,\n            \"leeway\": 30\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor asymmetric algorithms such as `ES256`, `buffer` must contain the appropriately formatted public key (normally PEM).\n\n## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n\n`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"oauthConfig\": {\n          \"oauth2Options\": {\n            \"site\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\",\n            \"clientId\": \"my-client-id\"\n          },\n          \"authorizationServerUrl\": \"${AUTH_EXTERNAL_URL}\",\n          \"scopesSupported\": [\"mcp:tools\", \"mcp:resources\"],\n          \"resource\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/mcp\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`oauth2Options.site` is required. `clientId` defaults to `datasqrl-mcp`; `authorizationServerUrl`, `scopesSupported`, and `resource` are optional. Both deprecated blocks can be set together, but then a token that either one accepts is accepted; use one `auth` block that lists every issuer with its audiences instead.\n\n`resource` is the public URL of the MCP endpoint. Without it, the server derives the discovery `resource` and the `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"...\"` URL of `401` responses from each request (`X-Forwarded-Proto` or the request scheme, plus `Host`). With it, the discovery metadata returns it unchanged, and `resource_metadata` points to `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` on its origin. Set it behind a proxy that terminates TLS, so MCP clients always receive `https` URLs.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961200}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb03b27070a20f5cc7", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Flink Engine Configuration\n\n| Key          | Type       | Default   | Notes                                                                                              |\n|--------------|------------|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------| \n| `config`     | **object** | see below | Copied verbatim into the generated Flink SQL job (e.g. `\"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"5 s\"`). |\n\n```json5\n{\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\", //or \"BATCH\" for batch pipelines\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 sec\" // so pipeline can advance when some sources are idle\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn STREAMING mode, DataSQRL sets `execution.checkpointing.interval: 30 s`, `execution.checkpointing.min-pause: 20 s`, and `table.exec.source.idle-timeout: 1 s` unless the package configuration sets them explicitly.\n\nFrequently configured options:\n* `execution.runtime-mode`: `BATCH` or `STREAMING` which controls how the pipeline is executed.\n* `taskmanager.memory.network.max`: How much memory to assign to the network buffers.\n\n\n## Common Flink Table Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure                                                                                              |\n|---|---|---|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enables mini-batch optimization to reduce state access overhead in aggregations and more efficient processing |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.allow-latency` | `0 ms` | `1s` \u2013 `5s` | Max wait time to accumulate a mini-batch; trade-off between latency and throughput. >0 when enabled.          |\n| `table.exec.mini-batch.size` | `-1` | `1000` \u2013 `10000` | Max number of records per mini-batch bundle. >0 when enabled                                                  |\n| `table.exec.state.ttl` | `0 ms` (no expiry) | `1d`, `7d`, `24h` | Caps state size for unbounded joins and aggregations to prevent OOM                                           |\n| `table.optimizer.agg-phase-strategy` | `AUTO` | `TWO_PHASE`, `ONE_PHASE` | Forces two-phase (partial + final) aggregation for better parallelism                                         |\n| `table.optimizer.distinct-agg.split.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Splits `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` into two phases to avoid data skew                                               |\n| `table.optimizer.join-reorder-enabled` | `false` | `true` | Lets the optimizer reorder joins based on table statistics for better plans                                   |\n| `table.exec.source.idle-timeout` | `1 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL); `0 s` (disabled) for `test` | `30s`, `60s` | Marks idle sources so watermarks can advance when some partitions go quiet                                    |\n| `table.exec.sink.not-null-enforcer` | `ERROR` | `DROP` | Controls whether null-constraint violations throw errors or silently drop rows                                |\n| `table.exec.sink.upsert-materialize` | `AUTO` | `NONE`, `FORCE` | Controls materialization of upsert streams before writing to non-upsert sinks                                 |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-sub-plan-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabling can help when shared sub-plans cause unexpected state sharing                                       |\n| `table.optimizer.reuse-source-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls whether identical source scans are shared across the plan                                            |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.buffer-capacity` | `100` | `10` \u2013 `1000` | Max number of in-flight async lookup requests for async lookup joins                                          |\n| `table.exec.async-lookup.timeout` | `3 min` | `30s`, `1min` | Timeout for each async lookup request before it fails                                                         |\n| `table.exec.resource.default-parallelism` | `-1` (inherit) | `4`, `8`, `16` | Sets operator-level default parallelism for table/SQL jobs                                                    |\n| `table.dynamic-table-options.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Allows per-query connector option overrides via SQL hints (`/*+ OPTIONS(...) */`)                             |\n| `table.optimizer.multiple-input-enabled` | `true` | `false` | Controls chaining of multiple operators into a single task for reduced overhead                               |\n| `table.exec.rank.topn-cache-size` | `10000` | `1000` \u2013 `100000` | Cache size for TopN operator; larger cache reduces state reads but uses more heap                             |\n| `table.optimizer.bushy-join-reorder-threshold` | `12` | `4` \u2013 `20` | Max number of joins considered for bushy tree reordering                                                      |\n| `table.exec.window-agg.buffer-size-limit` | `100000` | `10000` \u2013 `500000` | Row buffer limit for window aggregation before spilling; affects memory vs. speed                             |\n\n## Common Flink Execution Settings\n\n| Configuration Key | Default | Common Values / Range | How to Configure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `execution.runtime-mode` | `STREAMING` | `BATCH` | Switched for bounded DataStream batch jobs |\n| `execution.checkpointing.interval` | `30 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `1min`, `5min` | Checkpoint frequency; trades recovery time and end-to-end latency of transactional sinks against checkpoint overhead |\n| `execution.checkpointing.mode` | `EXACTLY_ONCE` | `AT_LEAST_ONCE` | Relaxed for higher throughput when exactly-once isn't required |\n| `execution.checkpointing.timeout` | `10min` | `2min` \u2013 `30min` | Increased when checkpoints are slow due to large state or slow storage |\n| `execution.checkpointing.unaligned.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to speed up checkpointing under heavy backpressure |\n| `execution.checkpointing.min-pause` | `20 s` in STREAMING mode (set by DataSQRL) | `10s` \u2013 `1min` | Prevents checkpoint storms by enforcing a gap between checkpoints |\n| `execution.checkpointing.max-concurrent-checkpoints` | `1` | `1` | Rarely increased; relevant when unaligned checkpoints are disabled |\n| `execution.checkpointing.tolerable-failed-checkpoints` | `0` | `3` \u2013 `5` | Prevents job failure from transient checkpoint issues |\n| `taskmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `2gb` \u2013 `16gb` | Primary memory sizing knob for containerized deployments |\n| `taskmanager.memory.managed.fraction` | `0.4` | `0.3` \u2013 `0.6` | Adjusted when RocksDB needs more or less managed memory |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.fraction` | `0.1` | `0.05` \u2013 `0.2` | Increased for jobs with high shuffle/network traffic |\n| `taskmanager.memory.network.max` | `infinite`; `800m` for `run` | `1gb`, `2gb`, `4gb` | Upper bound on network memory; set explicitly to cap buffer memory usage |\n| `jobmanager.memory.process.size` | (none) | `1gb` \u2013 `4gb` | Required sizing for containerized JobManager deployments |\n| `parallelism.default` | `1` | `4` \u2013 `256` | Sets job-wide default parallelism |\n| `pipeline.auto-watermark-interval` | `200ms` | `500ms`, `1s`, `5s` | Increased to reduce watermark overhead in high-throughput jobs |\n| `pipeline.max-parallelism` | `-1` | `128` \u2013 `4096` | Set explicitly to control key-group count and future rescaling |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.size` | `64mb` | `128mb` \u2013 `256mb` | Increased for write-heavy workloads to reduce flush frequency |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.writebuffer.count` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased alongside writebuffer.size for write throughput |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.thread.num` | `2` | `4` \u2013 `8` | Increased to parallelize RocksDB compaction and flush |\n| `state.backend.rocksdb.checkpoint.transfer.thread.num` | `4` | `8` \u2013 `16` | Increased to speed up checkpoint upload/download |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.interval` | `100ms` | `0`, `200ms`, `500ms` | Lowered for latency, raised or disabled for throughput |\n| `execution.buffer-timeout.enabled` | `true` | `false` | Disabled to maximize throughput at cost of latency |\n| `taskmanager.network.memory.buffer-debloat.enabled` | `false` | `true` | Enabled to automatically tune in-flight buffer sizes |\n| `heartbeat.timeout` | `50s` | `30s` \u2013 `120s` | Increased in flaky network or cloud environments |\n| `heartbeat.interval` | `10s` | `5s` \u2013 `30s` | Tuned alongside timeout for failure detection sensitivity |\n| `pekko.ask.timeout` | `10s` | `30s` \u2013 `60s` | Increased when RPC timeouts occur under load |\n| `pekko.framesize` | `10mb` | `50mb` \u2013 `200mb` | Increased when large serialized objects are passed via RPC |\n| `execution.checkpointing.aligned-checkpoint-timeout` | `0ms` | `30s` \u2013 `5min` | Set when using unaligned checkpoints with fallback to aligned |\n| `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state` | `false` | `true` | Set only for one restore from a savepoint whose plan had operators the new plan removed; drops their state (see below) |\n\n## Upgrading from a Savepoint\n\nFlink refuses to restore a savepoint that holds state for an operator the new `flink-compiled-plan.json` no longer has.\nCompare the compiled plans of both versions before upgrading. If stateful operators were removed, restore once with `execution.state-recovery.ignore-unclaimed-state: true` (`job.allowNonRestoredState: true` with the Flink Kubernetes Operator), then remove the option so it cannot hide an unexpected state mismatch later.\nThe state of the removed operators is dropped. For example, plans compiled by earlier DataSQRL versions write `STREAM` tables through `WatermarkTimestampAssigner` and `SinkMaterializer` operators that current plans no longer have; rows still held in their state are not written to the database after the restore.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510961201}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I understand. The catalog include: lending uses `ontology-test-package.json` because that file has `script.config.environment: test`. Wait, but the note says the consumer import `data-catalog...consumer_loans-{{environment}}` uses consumer's environment. The include's own config environment is used to render the catalog's own scripts? But we don't load the catalog's main script; we only load the SQRL files under the include root (loaded as scripts, but only the ones we import matter?). Actually DataSQRL loads every .sqrl under include root, and unresolved placeholders become parse errors. Hmm, the warning in the AGENTS addendum: \"If a module's OWN scripts contain {{...}} placeholders, supply those values in that module's script.include entry via a config object\". The catalog's consumer_loans-test.sqrl and the `{{environment}}` in the filename pattern. Wait the include root is catalog top-level. All .sqrl files loaded. The catalog's own scripts use `{{...}}` placeholders? Let me check ontology.sqrl and whether there are `{{environment}}` placeholders in catalog files. consumer_loans-test.sqrl has `{{^is_batch}}`. That's resolved by `is_batch` config. So we must supply `is_batch: false`. The `{{environment}}` placeholder\u2014the file names contain `-test`/`-prod`, not `{{environment}}`. So `environment` might not be used in catalog files. Let me grep.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb0b967071901490f2", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/data-catalog && grep -rn \"{{\" --include=\"*.sqrl\" . | grep -v \"is_batch\" | head -30; echo \"=== is_batch usages ===\"; grep -rln \"is_batch\" --include=\"*.sqrl\" . | wc -l; echo \"=== ontology.sqrl head ===\"; head -40 ontology.sqrl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb0c5c78d2be1b02b4", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 5828, "output": 495, "cacheRead": 68992, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 328, "totalTokens": 75315, "cost": {"input": 0.0017484, "output": 0.000594, "cacheRead": 0.000413952, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.002756352}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510961203, "responseId": "676c6b04085644c48f201ccd06afad81-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2233, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb0b967071901490f2", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:1:IMPORT accounts.deposit_accounts-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:2:IMPORT accounts.account_balances-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:3:IMPORT accounts.account_analytics-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:4:IMPORT transactions.core_transactions-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:5:IMPORT transactions.card_transactions-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:6:IMPORT transactions.wire_ach_transactions-{{environment}}.*;\n./deposits_payments/deposits_payments-ontology.sqrl:7:IMPORT transactions.enriched_transactions-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:1:IMPORT cards_consumer_credit.credit_cards-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:2:IMPORT cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:3:IMPORT mortgages.mortgage_originations-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:4:IMPORT mortgages.mortgage_servicing-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:5:IMPORT cards_consumer_credit.credit_risk_signals-{{environment}}.*;\n./lending/lending-ontology.sqrl:6:IMPORT mortgages.mortgage_performance-{{environment}}.*;\n./customer/customer-ontology.sqrl:1:IMPORT customer_data.customer_master-{{environment}}.*;\n./customer/customer-ontology.sqrl:2:IMPORT customer_data.kyc_aml-{{environment}}.*;\n./customer/customer-ontology.sqrl:3:IMPORT customer_data.customer_enriched-{{environment}}.*;\n=== is_batch usages ===\n27\n=== ontology.sqrl head ===\nIMPORT customer.customer-ontology.*;\nIMPORT lending.lending-ontology.*;\nIMPORT deposits_payments.deposits_payments-ontology.*;\n\n-- =====================================================\n-- Customer to Credit Card Relationships\n-- =====================================================\n\n/** Credit card accounts where customer is the primary account holder */\nCustomer.credit_card_accounts := SELECT * FROM Credit_Card_Account a WHERE this.customer_id = a.primary_customer_id;\n\n/** Open credit card accounts for this customer */\nCustomer.open_credit_card_accounts := SELECT * FROM Credit_Card_Account a WHERE this.customer_id = a.primary_customer_id\n                                                                      AND account_status = 'OPEN';\n\n/** Credit cards where customer is the cardholder (including authorized user cards) */\nCustomer.credit_cards := SELECT * FROM Credit_Card c WHERE this.customer_id = c.cardholder_customer_id;\n\n/** Card delinquency events for this customer */\nCustomer.card_delinquency_events := SELECT * FROM Card_Delinquency_Event d WHERE this.customer_id = d.customer_id\n                                                                       ORDER BY event_date DESC;\n\n-- =====================================================\n-- Customer to Consumer Loan Relationships\n-- =====================================================\n\n/** Personal loans where customer is the primary borrower */\nCustomer.personal_loans := SELECT * FROM Personal_Loan l WHERE this.customer_id = l.primary_borrower_customer_id;\n\n/** Active personal loans for this customer */\nCustomer.active_personal_loans := SELECT * FROM Personal_Loan l WHERE this.customer_id = l.primary_borrower_customer_id\n                                                            AND loan_status = 'ACTIVE';\n\n/** Auto loans where customer is the primary borrower */\nCustomer.auto_loans := SELECT * FROM Auto_Loan l WHERE this.customer_id = l.primary_borrower_customer_id;\n\n/** Active auto loans for this customer */\nCustomer.active_auto_loans := SELECT * FROM Auto_Loan l WHERE this.customer_id = l.primary_borrower_customer_id\n                                                    AND loan_status = 'ACTIVE';\n\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 26, "timestamp": 1795510963463}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb0c5c78d2be1b02b4", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: implement-sqrl\ndescription: Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n---\n\n# Implement SQRL scripts\n\nSQRL is an extension of Flink SQL that adds support for table functions and convenience syntax to build reactive data processing and serving applications.  \nThe \u201cR\u201d in **SQRL** stands for *Reactive* and *Relationships*.\n\nThis document focuses only on features **unique to SQRL**; when SQRL accepts Flink SQL verbatim we simply refer to the upstream spec.\n\n## Reference Templates\n\nReview project examples in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md` before implementing new features to identify related projects and copy features from those reference implementations.\n\n## Script Structure\n\nA SQRL script is an **ordered list** of statements separated by semicolons (`;`).  \nOnly one statement is allowed per line, but a statement may span multiple lines.\n\nTypical order of statements:\n\n```text\nIMPORT ...            -- import other SQRL scripts\nCREATE TABLE ...      -- define internal & external sources\nMyTable := SELECT ... -- define tables or functions (with hints)\nEXPORT MyTable TO ... -- write table data to sinks\n```\n\nAt compile time the statements form a *directed-acyclic graph* (DAG).  \nEach node is then assigned to an enabled execution engine according to the optimizer and the compiler generates the data processing code for that engine. See [Where a table runs](#where-a-table-runs).\n\n## Flink SQL\n\nSQRL inherits full Flink SQL grammar for\n\n* `CREATE {TABLE | VIEW | FUNCTION | CATALOG | DATABASE}`\n* `SELECT` queries inside any of the above\n* `USE ...`\n* `INSERT INTO`\n\nSQRL currently tracks Flink 2.3; use its compatible SQL syntax. Refer to the Flink SQL documentation for the detailed specification.\n\n## Type System\nIn SQRL, every table and function has a type based on how the table represents data.\nThe type determines the semantic validity of queries against tables and how data is processed by different engines.\n\nSQRL assigns one of the following types to tables based on the definition:\n- **STREAM**: Represents a stream of immutable records with an assigned timestamp (often referred to as the \"event time\"). Streams are append-only. Stream tables represent events or actions over time.\n- **VERSIONED_STATE**: Contains records with a natural primary key and a timestamp, tracking changes over time to each record, thereby creating a change-stream.\n- **STATE**: Similar to VERSIONED_STATE but without tracking the history of changes. Each record is uniquely identified by its natural primary key.\n- **LOOKUP**: Supports lookup operations using a primary key against external data systems but does not allow further processing of the data.\n- **STATIC**: For data that does not change over time, such as constants.\n\nThe table type determines what operators a table supports and how those operators are applied.\n\n## IMPORT Statement\n\nImports another SQRL script into the current script.\n```\nIMPORT qualifiedPath (AS identifier)?;\nIMPORT qualifiedPath.*;             -- wildcard\n```\n\n### Path resolution\n\nA `qualifiedPath` resolves in exactly one of three ways:\n\n* **Relative (default)** \u2014 against the directory of the importing script: `IMPORT my.custom.script` maps to `./my/custom/script.sqrl`.\n* **`root.` (reserved)** \u2014 resolves the rest of the path against the **build root of the top-level project being compiled**, no matter how deeply nested the importing script is. SQRL dot-paths have no `..`, so a script in a **subfolder** cannot climb upward relatively; `root.` is the only way for it to reach something at the project root (`IMPORT root.data_catalog.identity.customers;`, `IMPORT root.functions.my_func;`).\n* **`stdlib.` (reserved)** \u2014 resolves to compiler built-ins (e.g. `IMPORT stdlib.math.*;`), not the file system. Import a whole library with `.*` or a single function by name.\n\n\n- The path may continue one segment past a SQRL script to import a **single `CREATE TABLE`** from it. Standard-library paths may similarly select a function, e.g. `IMPORT stdlib.math.hypot AS hypotenuse`. Hyphenated segments are written as they are (`IMPORT connectors.kafka-source.*`); backticking a segment is also accepted (`` IMPORT `data_catalog`.`sources` ``). A `{{...}}` template variable in the path is substituted from `script.config` before the path is resolved (`IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources;` \u2014 see [Config Templating](guides/templating.md)).\n- Imports that end in `.*` are imported inline which means that the statement from that script are executed verbatim in the current script.\nOtherwise, imports are available within a namespace that's equal to the name of the script or the optional `AS` identifier.\n\n### Examples\n\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script.*`: All table definitions from the script are imported inline and can be referenced directly as `MyTable` in `FROM` clauses.\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script`: Tables are imported into the `script` namespace and can be referenced as `script.MyTable`\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script AS myNamespace`: Tables are imported into the `myNamespace` namespace and can be referenced as `myNamespace.MyTable`\n* `IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources`: Imports the connector file selected by the `environment` config variable (`sources-test.sqrl`, `sources-prod.sqrl`, \u2026) into the `sources` namespace\n* `IMPORT functions.*` / `IMPORT functions.my_func`: Imports Java UDFs from the project's `functions/` folder (invoke the `/implement-udf` skill); `IMPORT stdlib.math.*` imports built-in functions (see [functions.md](functions.md))\n\n### Import style controls API exposure\n\nBecause a wildcard import executes the statements verbatim in the current script (above), its tables become definitions of this script and follow the normal exposure rules (see *Interfaces*). A namespaced import keeps its tables **private**: they can be referenced in `FROM` clauses as `namespace.Table`, but they are not exposed in the interface directly.\n\n```sql\nIMPORT connectors.sources AS sources;   -- private: sources.Customer usable in FROM, absent from the API directly\nIMPORT connectors.enablement.*;         -- inline: these tables are exposed in the API\n```\n\nTo publish only what the API needs, import the sources aliased and re-declare the tables that should be exposed:\n\n```sql\nIMPORT connectors.sources AS sources;\n/** Purchases with computed line total. */\nPurchase := SELECT *, quantity * unit_price AS total_price FROM sources.Purchase;\n```\n\nWhen a single table must be a **mutation**, put it in its own connector file and wildcard-import that file, while the read-only sources stay aliased.\n\n### Included projects / multi-project (`script.include`)\n\nA project can import scripts from **another SQRL project**, e.g. a shared data catalog or a metrics layer reused by several pipelines, declared under `script.include` in the project's configuration file. To include another SQRL project, invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill for configuration. What matters for the language is how the imports resolve:\n\n* At compile time the included project (the directory holding the top-level package file, together with all its subfolders, every `.sqrl` in them is loaded) is **copied into `build/<namespace>/`**, alongside the consumer's own scripts, where `<namespace>` is the key of its `script.include` entry in the configuration file. A script reaches it with a plain **relative** import, like `IMPORT <namespace>.sources AS sources;`, and that is the preferred form. Name include namespaces with underscores (`data_catalog`), because a dash has a different meaning in SQL. When the importing script is in a subdirectory (e.g. `./my-module/module-script.sqrl`), use `IMPORT root.<namespace>.sources;` (see *Path resolution*). Below the namespace the path follows the included project's own directory structure at any depth. Example:`IMPORT data_catalog.customer.customer_data.customer_master-{{environment}} AS cs;`.\n* `{{...}}` in the *consumer's* import statement is substituted from the consumer's own `script.config`; `{{...}}` inside the *included project's* scripts (e.g. a catalog's `{{environment}}` or a `{{^is_batch}}\u2026{{/is_batch}}` section) is rendered with the `script.config` of the package file named by `package`, overridden by `script.include.<namespace>.config`.\n* **Inside an included project, reference sibling scripts with *relative* imports**, so they travel with the copied files. A `root.` import inside an included project breaks once it is consumed: `root` then points at the consumer's build root, not the included project's.\n* A **`standalone`** include (see `/configure-sqrl`) is deployed on its own, so the consumer does not repeat what that deployment writes: the consumer's subscriptions on tables that the include's main script (or its inline imports) subscribes to read the include's topics, and the include's `EXPORT`/`INSERT` statements into the log, its internal Kafka tables and external tables do not run in the consumer (a NOTICE names each). Write your own subscriptions and exports in the consumer's scripts; those always run.\n\n## CREATE TABLE (internal vs external)\n\nSQRL understands the complete Flink SQL `CREATE TABLE` syntax, but distinguishes between **internal** and **external** source tables.\nExternal source tables are standard Flink SQL tables that connect to an external data source (e.g. database or Kafka cluster).\nInternal tables are managed by SQRL through an engine hint (`kafka` or `iceberg`). Kafka internal sources are exposed for data ingestion in the interface.\n\n| Feature           | Internal source (managed by SQRL)                                   | External Source (connector)    |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| Connector options | **omitted**; other engine-specific `WITH (...)` options are allowed | **required**                   |\n| Engine hint       | **required**                                                        | Not used to select a connector |\n| Metadata columns  | `METADATA FROM 'uuid'`, `'timestamp'`, `'auth.<claim>'` only        | Passed through                 |\n| Watermark spec    | **generated**                                                       | **required**                   |\n| Primary key       | *Unenforced* upsert semantics                                       | Same as Flink                  |\n\nOn an internal table, use only the metadata keys `'uuid'`, `'timestamp'` and `'auth.<claim>'`. A `METADATA` column without `FROM` uses its column name as the key. Any other key stops the compile with an `Unknown metadata key` error, for example `'auht.sub'` or the Flink Kafka key `'offset'`. If you see that error, correct the key to one of the three supported keys.\n\nExample (internal): An internal table managed by DataSQRL as a Kafka topic carries the `engine(kafka)` hint (see the `/manage-connector` skill):\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE Customer (\n  customerid BIGINT,\n  email STRING,\n  ts TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  PRIMARY KEY (customerid) NOT ENFORCED\n);\n```\n\nThe internal table does not need `WATERMARK` declaration. SQRL generates the watermark for it. \nDeclare a `WATERMARK` explicitely on an internal table only when the user asks for one.\n\nWith `engine(kafka)`, `PARTITIONED BY (col, ...)` on an internal table without a `PRIMARY KEY` keys each record by those columns, both the records the server writes for mutations and the records the pipeline exports into the table, so records with the same key stay on one Kafka partition in order.\nThe key columns can be `uuid` or `auth` metadata columns, but not the `timestamp` metadata column.\nThe Flink source ignores that key and reads every column from the message value, so adding `PARTITIONED BY` to an existing internal table is safe: records written before the table had a key are still read, also when the topic is read again from the earliest offset.\nThis relies on the default `kafka-safe` connector. Do not switch internal Kafka tables to the plain `kafka` connector, which drops records that have no key.\nUse `PARTITIONED BY` when records only need a Kafka key for partitioning and ordering. A `PRIMARY KEY` also keys the records, but it makes the table an upsert table (`VERSIONED_STATE`), and its upsert source drops records that were written without a key.\n\nExample (external): An external table connects to a data system through a `WITH (...)` clause and declares its own `WATERMARK`:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE kafka_json_table (\n  user_id    INT,\n  name       STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR event_time AS event_time - INTERVAL '1' SECOND\n) WITH (\n  'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n  'topic'     = 'users',\n  'format'    = 'flexible-json'\n);\n```\n\n- Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill before writing or editing any table with a `WITH (...)` clause i.e. every external source and sink. This section covers the statement syntax only. Use that skill to decide which connector and format to use, the watermark rules for the source type, whether the schema belongs in a separate `_schema` table referenced with `LIKE`, and which values become `${VARIABLE}` references.\n- With the `_schema` + `LIKE` source pattern, the `WATERMARK`, any `METADATA FROM '...'` column, and any computed (`AS ...`) column belong to the **connector table**, never the `_X_schema` table. Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill for those rules.\n\n### File-based sources: watermarks & snapshot aggregation\n\nFile-based sources (`filesystem`, `iceberg`, `hudi`, `deltalake`) behave differently from Kafka streams.\n\n**(A) Watermark column.** A file-based source whose data timestamp is not quasi-monotonic, or whose files do not arrive frequently, watermarks a computed `ingestion_time AS now()` column instead of a data column. The one exception to \"prefer event time\". The rule that decides this belongs to the source table: invoke the `/manage-connector` skill (Event-Time Processing \u2192 Watermarks on production file-based sources).\n\n**(B) Complete-snapshot sources.** If a file source re-lists every object each cycle (e.g. S3 Inventory, full dumps), a global (non-windowed) `GROUP BY` **double-counts** \u2014 it counts each object once per snapshot. Aggregate each snapshot independently with a `SESSION` window over the `ingestion_time AS now()` column from (A), `PARTITION BY` the group key (read [SESSION Windows](guides/session-tvf.md) first): each snapshot's records arrive as one ingestion burst and form one session, so they are counted once. A short session gap (e.g. 1 minute) closes the window shortly after a snapshot finishes loading, while the much larger gap between snapshots keeps them in separate sessions.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** a `PRIMARY KEY` on a filesystem source does **not** dedup (the source is append-only); even a correct `DISTINCT`-dedup mishandles deletions (an object disappears by absence in the next snapshot) and grows unbounded state.\n\n## Definition statements\n\n### Table definition\n\n```\nTableName := SELECT ... ;\n```\n\nEquivalent to a `CREATE VIEW` in SQL.\n\n```sql\nValidCustomer := SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE customerid > 0 AND email IS NOT NULL;\n```\n\nA common pattern is stream enrichment with a temporal join:\n\n```sql\n-- Temporal join for stream enrichment \u2014 intermediate result uses _ prefix\n_EnrichedOrders := SELECT o.*, c.name, c.email\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n\n-- If the enriched table is exposed directly in the API, use a public name:\n/** Orders enriched with customer details */\nEnrichedOrders := SELECT o.*, c.name, c.email\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\n### Timestamp of a join result\n\nFor every join, decide the timestamp of the result: the time when the result row last changed. Choose it by the type of join:\n\n| Join | Timestamp of the result |\n|---|---|\n| Temporal join (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) or interval join | The timestamp of the stream side. Each result row is an event at that time, and the join never changes it later. |\n| Regular join (an inner or outer join without `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` or a time bound) | Reason about how the result row changes over time. In the common case, both sides change independently. The row then last changed at the later of the two times: `GREATEST(l.ts, r.ts)`. Give `GREATEST` one argument for each joined table that has a timestamp. A table without a timestamp, such as a table of fixed values, never changes, so it adds no argument. `GREATEST` returns `NULL` when one argument is `NULL`. In an outer join, wrap the timestamp of each side that can be missing in `COALESCE` with the timestamp of another side. For example, in a `LEFT JOIN`, write `GREATEST(l.ts, COALESCE(r.ts, l.ts))`. |\n\nA regular join emits a new version of a result row each time either side changes. A row that keeps the timestamp of one side only looks out of date when the other side changes. API clients that sort or filter by that timestamp then get wrong results.\n\nThe Postgres sink writes a new version of a `STATE` row only when this timestamp is not older than the stored one. `GREATEST` goes back to an older timestamp when the optional side of an outer join stops matching, for example because its row is deleted or filtered out. The sink then keeps the old joined values. For such a join, use only the timestamp of the preserved side, such as `l.ts` in a `LEFT JOIN`. It does not show changes of the optional side, but it never goes back.\n\nA `FULL JOIN` has no preserved side. If its sides can stop matching, `CAST` the result timestamp to its type, for example `CAST(GREATEST(...) AS TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3))`, to remove the row-time marker. The sink then writes every version without comparing timestamps.\n\n### DISTINCT operator\n\n```\nDistinctTbl := DISTINCT SourceTbl\n               ON pk_col [, ...]\n               ORDER BY ts_col [ASC|DESC] [NULLS LAST] ;\n```\n\n* Deduplicates a **STREAM** of changelog data into a **VERSIONED_STATE** table.\n* The SQRL `DISTINCT` statement is not SQL `SELECT DISTINCT`.\n* Hint `/*+filtered_distinct_order*/` (see hints) may precede the statement to push filters before deduplication for optimization.\n\n**Use `DISTINCT` when the source table is a `STREAM` of updates to an entity that needs to be converted to a state table, i.e. changelog.** A `DISTINCT` statement adds a stateful operator and changes the semantics of the table.\nCheck the type of the source table before you write one:\n\n| Source table | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| A `STREAM` that carries several records for the same key, for example an append-only change feed | Write the `DISTINCT` statement where a consumer needs a single version per entity, either the current one or the one valid at an event's timestamp in a temporal join. It turns the stream into `VERSIONED_STATE`. |\n| A table that already declares a `PRIMARY KEY` with upsert semantics, for example an `upsert-kafka` source or an internal table with an engine hint and a primary key | Read the table directly. It is `VERSIONED_STATE` already, so a `DISTINCT` on that same key returns the same rows and only costs state. |\n\nRead the `STREAM` directly where a consumer needs the changes themselves, such as a history endpoint, an aggregation over every update, an alert per change, or a stream-to-stream join. When both consumers exist, keep the `STREAM` and define the `DISTINCT` table, if needed, beside it, so each keeps its own semantics.\n\n```sql\n-- Intermediate dedup step (feeds into another table) \u2014 use _ prefix\n_DistinctProducts := DISTINCT Products ON id ORDER BY updated DESC;\n\n-- Final dedup result exposed directly in the API \u2014 use public name\n/** Latest version of each product, deduplicated by id */\nDistinctProducts := DISTINCT Products ON id ORDER BY updated DESC;\n```\n\n### Primary key columns\n\nA table gets a primary key from a `PRIMARY KEY` in its `CREATE TABLE`, or from a `DISTINCT ... ON` or `GROUP BY` on the key columns. Such a table has the type `VERSIONED_STATE` or `STATE` (see [Type System](#type-system)).\n\nClean the primary key columns before the first table that has that primary key, and select them unchanged in every table that reads from that table, directly or through other tables.\nFor example, when `Customer` has the primary key `customer_id`, a later table selects `customer_id`, and never `TRIM(customer_id) AS customer_id`.\n\nA function on a key column in such a later table (`TRIM`, `LOWER`, `REGEXP_REPLACE`, `CAST`, `NULLIF`) hides the key from the Flink planner. A function can turn two different keys into the same value, so the planner treats the result as a normal column, not as a key. Every join that reads the later table stores each full row in its state and finds a row by comparing all of its columns (`NoUniqueKey`), instead of finding it by its key. This makes the state larger and every update slower.\nColumns that are not part of the key can be cleaned in any table.\n\nThese rules apply to entity data (see the `/manage-connector` skill, Entity Data), where each new record replaces the previous version of the same entity, such as a customer or an account.\n\n**Writing entity data.** When the pipeline writes entity data into a table that other tables or projects read, clean the key before the `EXPORT`.\nSuch a table is a mutation table with a `PRIMARY KEY` (an internal table with an engine hint) or a Kafka sink with a `PRIMARY KEY`.\nEvery reader then receives the final key value and can select it unchanged.\nFollow these steps:\n\n1. Clean the key in a view, and keep only the rows whose cleaned key is not empty.\n2. Make the cleaned key unique with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key.\n3. Export the `DISTINCT` table into the table with the primary key.\n\nIn this example, `RawCustomerUpdate` is the source table the pipeline reads. It has no key, and its `customer_id` values can contain extra spaces or be empty.\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE Customer (\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  email STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  PRIMARY KEY (customer_id) NOT ENFORCED\n);\n\n_CleanCustomerUpdate := SELECT NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') AS customer_id,\n                               email, event_time\n                        FROM RawCustomerUpdate\n                        WHERE NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') IS NOT NULL;\n\n_LatestCustomerUpdate := DISTINCT _CleanCustomerUpdate ON customer_id ORDER BY event_time DESC;\n\nEXPORT _LatestCustomerUpdate TO Customer;\n```\n\nStep 2 is required.\nIt keeps the latest version of each key by `event_time`, and makes the cleaned key the upsert key of the exported query.\nAn `EXPORT` into a table declared with `CREATE TABLE` gets no generated `ON CONFLICT` clause, so when the upsert key of the exported query differs from the `PRIMARY KEY` of that table, Flink rejects the `EXPORT` with the error `The query has an upsert key that differs from the primary key of the sink table`.\nA view that only cleans and filters a source without a key has no upsert key at all, so exporting it directly fails with this error. Export the `DISTINCT` table instead.\n\n**Reading keys that arrive dirty.** When the writer is outside this project, for example an external client that calls a mutation endpoint, the pipeline cannot clean the key before it is stored.\nClean it in the first table after the mutation instead:\n\n1. Declare the mutation without a `PRIMARY KEY`, so that each write is stored as an event with the key value exactly as the client sent it.\n   A `PRIMARY KEY` on the raw key would make the raw value the key, and cleaning it in a later table would hide that key from the planner.\n2. Clean the key in a view, and keep only the rows whose cleaned key is not empty.\n3. Make the cleaned key unique with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key. Every later table reads this `DISTINCT` table.\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE CustomerUpdate (\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  email STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp'\n);\n\n_CleanCustomerUpdate := SELECT NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') AS customer_id,\n                               email, event_time\n                        FROM CustomerUpdate\n                        WHERE NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') IS NOT NULL;\n\n/** Current version of each customer, with the cleaned id as primary key */\nCustomer := DISTINCT _CleanCustomerUpdate ON customer_id ORDER BY event_time DESC;\n```\n\nFollow the same steps for an external source whose keys can arrive dirty.\nWhen an external source's keys always arrive clean, read its key unchanged and skip the cleaning.\n\nClean the key in a view, as shown above, and not inside the `CREATE TABLE`.\nFlink accepts a `PRIMARY KEY` only on physical columns, so a computed column such as `customer_id AS TRIM(raw_customer_id)` cannot be the key.\n\nThe `/*+primary_key(...)*/` hint does not fix this: it declares the key for DataSQRL, but does not deduplicate the data and is not visible to Flink.\nWhen the hint is placed on a state table whose rows the query does not keep unique on the hinted columns, the compiler warns with `PRIMARY_KEY_NOT_UNIQUE`. Do not ignore this warning: replace the hint with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key.\n\n### Function definition\n\n```\nFuncName(arg1 TYPE [NOT NULL] [, ...]) :=\n  SELECT ... WHERE col = :arg1 ;\n```\n\nArguments are referenced with `:name` in the `SELECT` query. Argument definitions are identical to column definitions in `CREATE TABLE` statements.\n\n```sql\nCustomerByEmail(email STRING) := SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE email = :email;\n```\n\n#### Accessing JWT payload\n\nTo access the JWT payload included in the Authorization HTTP header, you can use the `METADATA` expression within the function definition. The text after `auth.` is the name of a claim in the JWT payload:\n\n* `'auth.val'` reads the top-level claim `val`.\n* `'auth.org.id'` reads the field `id` of the object claim `org`. A path can go to any depth, for example `'auth.org.location.zip'`.\n* A key can contain dots, at the top level and inside an object, for example the namespaced claim `https://example.com/org`. At each level, the server uses the longest key that it finds, and then follows the rest of the path. So `'auth.https://example.com/org_id'` reads a top-level claim, and `'auth.https://example.com/org.id'` reads the field `id` of the claim `https://example.com/org`. In the same way, `'auth.resource_access.com.example.app.roles'` reads the field `roles` of the key `com.example.app` inside the claim `resource_access`.\n* A path can end on an array. In the SQRL script, declare the function argument with an `ARRAY<...>` type, for example `roles ARRAY<STRING> NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.realm_access.roles'`.\n* Array indexing does not work. For example, `'auth.realm_access.roles.0'` does not read the first role. The server treats it as a missing claim. To check for one item, read the full array and use `array_contains` in the query.\n* Give each segment of the path a name. A key with an empty segment, such as `'auth.org.'` or `'auth.org..id'`, stops the compile with an `Invalid metadata key` or `Unknown metadata key` error.\n* When the token has no claim at the path, a request fails with `403 Forbidden` for a `NOT NULL` argument. A nullable argument gets `null`.\n\nIn the example below, the JWT payload contains a val field, which is an integer.\n\n```sql\nAuthFilter(mySecretId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.val') :=\n  SELECT c.* \n  FROM Customer c \n  WHERE c.customerId = :mySecretId;\n```\n\nA script that reads a claim needs an auth provider in `engines.vertx.config`. This applies to a function argument and to a mutation column. The provider is an `auth` block with at least one issuer. The deprecated `jwtAuth`, or `oauthConfig` with `oauth2Options.site`, also gives a provider. Without a provider, the server checks no token. The compile then fails with the error `The script reads JWT claims (...), but engines.vertx.config has no auth provider.` If you see that error, add an `auth` block with the `/configure-sqrl` skill, and keep the `METADATA FROM 'auth.<claim>'` columns. The check also covers a claim function that a custom GraphQL schema leaves out. If the API must not expose that function, start its name with `_`. The `/*+no_query*/` hint does not help here, because it works only on tables.\n\n### Relationship definition\n\n```\nParentTable.RelName(arg TYPE, ...) :=\n  SELECT ...\n  FROM Child c\n  WHERE this.id = c.parentId\n  [AND c.col = :arg ...] ;\n```\n\n`this.` is the alias for the parent table to reference columns from the parent row.\n\n```sql\nCustomer.highValueOrders(minAmount BIGINT) := SELECT * FROM Orders o WHERE o.customerid = this.id AND o.amount > :minAmount;\n```\n\n### Column-addition statement\n\n```\nTableName.new_col := expression;\n```\n\nMust appear **immediately after** the table definition it extends, and may reference previously added columns of the same table. Cannot be applied after `CREATE TABLE` statements.\n\n\n### Relation alias as ROW\n\nA relation alias is the alias of any `FROM` item: a table, a view, a subquery or a CTE. Used by itself in a `SELECT` list, it produces one `ROW` value holding every column of that relation. The API exposes the column as a nested object. \nThe compiler expands the alias to `CAST(ROW(c.col1, ...) AS ROW<...>)` from the relation's validated columns, so the alias replaces writing that cast by hand.\n\nExamples:\n```sql\nTableName := SELECT o.id, c AS customer FROM Orders o JOIN Customer c ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\n```sql\n/** Orders with the customer as it was when the order was placed */\nOrderWithCustomer := SELECT o.id, o.amount, o.event_time, c AS customer\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\nThe `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` clause selects the customer version valid at `o.event_time`. The alias itself packs whichever row the join produced.\n\n* The flink row carries every column of the relation, including its timestamp column. A `_`-prefixed column is stored inside the row and omitted from the API type.\n* A column named like the alias takes precedence, and the alias then resolves to that column. Give the alias a name no column uses when you want the row.\n* A bare alias, `SELECT customerid, c FROM Customer c`, names the column after the alias. `c AS customer` names it explicitly.\n* The alias combines with `o.*`, works inside CTEs and subqueries, and works with `LEFT JOIN`, where the row stays non-null and every field inside it becomes nullable.\n* Read a field back downstream with dot access: `SELECT x.id, x.customer.name FROM OrderWithCustomer x`.\n* Copy a field out as its own column as well, `c.tier AS customer_tier`, when the API filters or sorts by it. The nested value is one JSON column in the database and carries no relationships or filter arguments of its own.\n\nRead the [Stream Enrichment](guides/stream-enrichment.md) guide to choose between the whole-row alias, copied scalar columns, and a relationship.\n\n### Passthrough definitions\n\nPassthrough definitions allow you to bypass SQRL's analysis and translation, passing SQL queries directly to the underlying database engine.\nThis serves as a \"backdoor\" for SQL constructs that SQRL does not yet support natively.\n\nPassthrough table, function, or relationship definition have a `RETURNS` clause in their signature that defines the result type of the query.\n\n```\nTableName RETURNS (column TYPE [NOT NULL], ...) := \n  SELECT raw_sql_query;\n```\n\n**\u26a0\ufe0f Important considerations:**\n- SQL must be written in the exact syntax of the target database engine\n- SQRL will not validate, parse, or optimize the query\n- Only use when SQRL lacks native support for the required functionality\n- Return type must be explicitly declared using the `RETURNS` clause\n- Passthrough queries must be assigned to a database engine for execution\n\nThe following defines a relationship definition with a recursive CTE that is passed through to the\ndatabase engine for execution.\nUse `this` to reference parent fields in a relationship definition or `:name` to reference function\narguments as in standard definitions. The only thing that changes is that adding a `RETURNS` declaration\nbypasses the SQRL analysis, optimization, and query rewriting.\n\n```sql\nEmployees.allReports RETURNS (employeeid BIGINT NOT NULL, name STRING NOT NULL, level INT NOT NULL) :=\n  WITH RECURSIVE employee_hierarchy AS (\n    SELECT r.employeeid, r.managerid, 1 as level\n    FROM \"Reporting\" r\n    WHERE r.managerid = this.employeeid\n    \n    UNION ALL\n    \n    SELECT r.employeeid, r.managerid, eh.level + 1 as level\n    FROM \"Reporting\" r\n    INNER JOIN employee_hierarchy eh ON r.managerid = eh.employeeid\n  )\n  SELECT e.employeeid, e.name, eh.level\n  FROM employee_hierarchy eh\n  JOIN \"Employees\" e ON eh.employeeid = e.employeeid\n  ORDER BY eh.level, e.employeeid;\n```\n\n## Functions\n\nSQRL supports the Flink 2.3-compatible built-in functions plus its own scalar, aggregate, and table functions. [Look up the required function](functions.md).\n\n## Interfaces\n\nThe tables and functions defined in a SQRL script are exposed through an interface.\nThe term \"interface\" is used generically to describe a means by which a client, user, or external system can access the processed data.\nThe interface depends on the configured engines: API endpoints for servers, queries and views for databases, and topics for logs.\nAn interface is a sink in the data processing DAG that's defined by a SQRL script.\n\nHow a table or function is exposed in the interface depends on the access type. The access type is one of the following:\n\n| Access type              | How to declare               | Surface                                                                                              |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Query** (default)      | no modifier                  | GraphQL query / SQL view / log topic (pull)                                                          |\n| **Subscription**         | prefix body with `SUBSCRIBE` | GraphQL subscription / push topic                                                                    |\n| **No direct query**        | `/*+no_query*/` hint         | still **in** the interface, reachable through a relationship or an explicit function but cannot be queryable directly |\n| **Not in the interface but intermediate step** | object name starts with `_`  | not exposed at all                                                                                   |\n\nExample:\n\n```sql\nHighTempAlert := SUBSCRIBE\n                 SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines a subscription endpoint that is exposed as a GraphQL subscription or Kafka topic depending on engine configuration.\nThe assignment operator comes first and the `SUBSCRIBE` keyword comes after it (`Name := SUBSCRIBE SELECT ...`).\n\n```sql\nHighTemperatures := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines a query table that is exposed as a query in the API or view in the database.\n\n```sql\nHighTemperatures(temp BIGINT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > :temp;\n```\nDefines a query function that is exposed as a parametrized query in the API.\n\n```sql\n_HighTemps := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines an internal table that is not exposed in the interface.\n\n### `_` prefix vs. `/*+no_query*/` \u2014 different semantics, never interchangeable\n\n* **`_` prefix** \u2014 the object is **not part of the interface at all**: no endpoint, no generated type, unreachable from any client. This is what pipeline intermediates use (dedup, enrichment, filter and join steps).\n* **`/*+no_query*/`** \u2014 the object **is** part of the interface through relationship but it cannot be queryable directly. Use it for a table clients reach only through a relationship from its parent.\n\n```sql\n-- Clients reach Purchase only through Order, so it has no query endpoint of its own.\n/** A line of a purchase order, with its total price (quantity times unit price). */\n/*+no_query */\nPurchase := SELECT *, quantity * unit_price AS total_price FROM sources.Purchase;\nPurchase.offering := SELECT * FROM Offering o WHERE o.offering_id = this.offering_id LIMIT 1;\nPurchase.customer := SELECT * FROM Customer c WHERE c.customer_id = this.customer_id LIMIT 1;\n\n/** The lines of one purchase order, sorted by offering id. Empty when no order has this id. */\nOrder(order_id STRING NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Purchase WHERE order_id = :order_id ORDER BY offering_id ASC;\n```\n\nApplying `no_query` to a `_`-prefixed table is illogical. Pick one: `_` for a pipeline intermediate, `no_query` for an interface object that must not have its own query endpoint.\n\n### CREATE TABLE\n\nCREATE TABLE statements that define an internal data source are exposed as topics in the log, or GraphQL mutations in the server.\nThe input type is defined by mapping all column types to native data types of the interface schema. Computed and metadata columns are not included in the input type since those are computed on insert.\n\n## EXPORT statement\n\n```\nEXPORT source_identifier TO sinkPath.QualifiedName ;\n```\n\n* `sinkPath` maps to a connector table definition when present, or one of the **built-in** sinks:\n    * `print.*` \u2013 stdout\n    * `logger.*` \u2013 uses configured logger\n    * `log.*` \u2013 topic in configured log engine\n\n```sql\nEXPORT CustomerTimeWindow TO print.TimeWindow;\nEXPORT MyAlerts          TO log.AlertStream;\n```\n\nAn export may also target one table in another script, for example `EXPORT CustomerSubset TO connectors.sinks.CustomerExport;`. The target must be either an external connector table or an engine-managed internal table.\n\n\n## Hints\n\nHints live in a `/*+ ... */` comment placed **immediately before** the definition they apply to.\n\n| Hint                        | Form                                                                       | Applies to     | Effect                                                                                                                                                                             |\n|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **primary_key**             | `primary_key(col, ...)`                                                    | table          | declare the key when the compile fails with `Could not determine primary key for table [...]`; does not deduplicate (see Primary key columns)                                                                                             |\n| **index**                   | `index(type, col [ASC\\|DESC], ...)` <br/> Multiple `index(...)` can be comma-separated | table          | replaces automatic index selection for this table. `type` \u2208 `HASH`, `BTREE`, `PBTREE`, `TEXT`, `VECTOR_COSINE`, `VECTOR_EUCLID`; `DESC` is supported only for `BTREE` and `PBTREE`. <br />`index` *alone* disables all automatic indexes, except the row-time index of a table behind a paginated query |\n| **partition_key**           | `partition_key(col, ...)`                                                  | table          | define partition columns for sinks that support partitioning                                                                                                                       |\n| **vector_dim**              | `vector_dim(col, 1536)`                                                    | table          | declare fixed vector length. This is required when using vector indexes.                                                                                                           |\n| **query_by_all**            | `query_by_all(col, ...)`                                                   | table          | generate interface with *required* filter arguments for all listed columns                                                                                                         |\n| **query_by_any**            | `query_by_any(col, ...)`                                                   | table          | generate interface with *optional* filter arguments for all listed columns                                                                                                         |\n| **no_query**                | `no_query`                                                                 | table          | prevent the table from being directly queryable but reachable through relationships and explicit functions (see *Interfaces*)                        |\n| **insert**                  | `insert(type)`                                                             | table          | controls the way how mutations will be written to their target sink. `type` \u2208 `SINGLE` (default), `BATCH`, `TRANSACTION`                                                           |\n| **ttl**                     | `ttl(duration)`                                                            | table          | specifies how long the records for this table are retained in the underlying data system before it can be discarded. Expects a duration string with a unit between minute and day, e.g. `30 min`, `36 hours`, `14 days`. Disabled by default. |\n| **cache**                   | `cache(duration)`                                                          | table          | how long the results retrieved from this table can be cached on the server before they are refreshed. Expects a duration string like `10 seconds`. Disabled by default.            |\n| **filtered_distinct_order** | flag                                                                       | DISTINCT table | eliminate updates on order column only before dedup                                                                                                                                |\n| **engine**                  | `engine(engine_id)`                                                        | table          | on a table definition, select the engine that computes the table, such as `flink` or `postgres` (see [Where a table runs](#where-a-table-runs)). On a `CREATE TABLE`, name the engine that manages the internal table, such as `kafka` (see [CREATE TABLE](#create-table-internal-vs-external)). `exec` is a deprecated alias of `engine` |\n| **maintenance**             | `maintenance(type)`                                                        | table          | table maintenance for engines that support it. `type` \u2208 `NONE`, `REGULAR`; `REGULAR` on an Iceberg table adds the `iceberg-maintenance` connector options |\n| **row_count**               | `row_count(count)` or `row_count(col, ..., count)`                         | table          | estimated total row count (e.g. `1e6`), or the number of distinct values for the listed column combination. Used for query optimization and index selection: a table with a total row count of at most 100 (`small-table-row-limit` of the `postgres` engine) gets no automatic index (see [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md)) |\n| **test**                    | `test` or `test(no_rows)`                                                  | table          | marks test case, only executed with `test` command.                                                                                                       |\n| **workload**                | `workload`                                                                 | table          | retained as sink for DAG optimization but hidden from interface                                                                                                                    |\n\nMultiple hints are comma-separated inside one comment. This example configures the vector length and a vector index for the `SensorTempByHour` table:\n\n```sql\n/*+vector_dim(embedding, 1536), index(VECTOR_COSINE, embedding) */\nSensorTempByHour := SELECT ... ;\n```\n\nSQL matches column names case-sensitively, but hints do not. A plain column name in a hint matches the column with the same name, ignoring case, so `primary_key(CUSTOMERID)` matches `customerId`. A name in backticks only matches the column with exactly that name. When a table has columns whose names differ only in case, such as `cnt` and `CNT`, a plain name fails the compile with `matches columns that differ only in case`: write the name in backticks, like ``index(BTREE, `CNT`)``, or rename one of the columns. The required columns of a tag in `compiler.tags` match the same way.\n\nFor choosing between `query_by_all` (required filter arguments) and `query_by_any` (optional filter arguments) when designing table access, invoke the `/design-api` skill (Table Hints for Table Access).\n\n### Where a table runs\n\nThe compiler runs each table in Flink or in the database, and picks the cheaper of the two. The `engine` hint replaces this choice for one table:\n\n- `/*+engine(flink) */`: Flink engine computes the rows when the data arrives. When the API reads the table, Flink writes the rows to a Postgres table, and the compiler can index its columns.\n- `/*+engine(postgres) */`: Postgres engine stores no rows for the table. When the API reads the table directly, the compiler creates a view with the query of the table. Otherwise, it copies the query into each served query that reads the table. Postgres computes the rows on every request.\n\nA table definition accepts only an enabled engine that can compute a table:\n\n| Engine     | Engine type |\n|------------|-------------|\n| `flink`    | `process`   |\n| `postgres` | `database`  |\n| `iceberg`  | `database`  |\n\nThe hint also accepts the engine type like `engine(database)`, but compiler selects the engine. Use the engine name. Other engines, such as `kafka`, `vertx`, `duckdb` or `snowflake`, cannot compute a table. For what each engine does and when to enable it, see the `/configure-sqrl` skill.\n\nLeave the choice to the compiler, unless a table needs an index that the compiler cannot give it. A served query can use an index only on rows that the database stores. A table that runs in Postgres stores no rows, so no index can serve a column that it computes, such as a `CONCAT_WS` text for `text_search` or a `CAST` value for a filter. In that case, add `/*+engine(flink) */` to the table: Flink computes the column ahead of time, and Postgres stores it in a table that can have an index. The hint also moves every table that this table reads into Flink, because a table in Flink can read only from Flink or Kafka. So the hint costs Flink state for this table and for each upstream table that ran in Postgres before, plus one more database table. Read the `Stage:` lines of the upstream tables after the compile.\n\nAfter compile, read the `Stage:` line of the table in `pipeline_explain.txt` in the build folder to see which engine computes it.\n\nPut the `engine` hint on a table definition. An API function always runs in the database, so `engine(flink)` on a function fails with `Provided execution stages could not be found or are not configured`.\nA table in Postgres cannot feed a table in Flink. Pinning such a table to `postgres` fails with `Could not find execution stage for [table ...]`.\n\n### Filters in API queries\n\nDataSQRL creates the database indexes for the tables that the API queries. The database (Postgres) can use an index only when the filtered column stands alone in the comparison, so write every served query in the following form:\n\n- Compare the bare column: `customer_id = :customerId`.\n- Do a type conversion or a derived value in a table that Flink computes, then filter on the result: add `/*+engine(flink) */` to the table, select `CAST(c.customer_id AS STRING) AS customer_id` in it, and filter on `customer_id = :customerId`. A table that runs in the database (an `engine(database)` hint, or the planner's choice) is copied into the served query, so its `CAST` ends up in the filter again. A function on the column in the filter, such as `CAST(customer_id AS STRING) = :customerId` or `LOWER(email) = :email`, makes the database read the whole table.\n- Put arithmetic on the parameter side: `day_index = :day - 1`.\n- In a `JOIN`, put the table that the `WHERE` clause filters with a parameter first. With the other order, the database reads every row of the table that the `WHERE` clause does not filter. Write `FROM AccountOwners o JOIN Orders c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.account_id = :accountId`, not `FROM Orders c JOIN AccountOwners o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id WHERE o.account_id = :accountId`.\n- To filter on another table, put `EXISTS (...)` or `IN (SELECT ...)` in the `WHERE` clause and combine it with the other conditions by `AND`: `FROM Orders c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM AccountOwners o WHERE o.account_id = :accountId AND o.customer_id = c.customer_id)`. When the subquery compares with the outer columns only by `=`, the compiler indexes both tables, so the database can start from either one. With `NOT EXISTS (...)`, `NOT IN (SELECT ...)`, or a subquery that is part of an `OR`, the database reads every row of the outer table that the other conditions leave.\n\nThe compiler creates no index for a filter that wraps its column in an expression, and warns `The filter ... cannot use an index on table ...`, with the fix. It does not warn about the order of the tables in a `JOIN`.\n\n### Indexes for API queries\n\nThe compiler selects the indexes for the tables that the API queries. An `index` hint replaces the automatic index selection for its whole table, so a hint that one query does not need can remove the indexes that other queries need. Leave the index selection to the compiler. Read [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md) and fix the query only in these cases:\n\n- The requirements, the plan or a review issue name a slow query or an index.\n- The compiler warns that query filter patterns of a table \"have no supporting index and require a full table scan\". Read the guide before you add the `index` hint that the warning suggests.\n\n\n### Constraints and mutual exclusions\n\n**Never combine `/*+no_query*/` with a `_`-prefixed name.** They are used for different purposes (see *Interfaces*): `_` removes the object from the interface entirely and it is used for intermediate processes. On the other hand, table with `no_query` hint cannot be queried directly while is accessible through relationship from other tables.\n\n**`query_by_all` / `query_by_any` need a queryable table.** They generate interface filter arguments, so they are meaningless on a `_`-prefixed table.\n\n\n\n[Showing lines 1-643 of 762 (50.0KB limit). Use offset=644 to continue.]"}], "details": {"truncation": {"content": "---\nname: implement-sqrl\ndescription: Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n---\n\n# Implement SQRL scripts\n\nSQRL is an extension of Flink SQL that adds support for table functions and convenience syntax to build reactive data processing and serving applications.  \nThe \u201cR\u201d in **SQRL** stands for *Reactive* and *Relationships*.\n\nThis document focuses only on features **unique to SQRL**; when SQRL accepts Flink SQL verbatim we simply refer to the upstream spec.\n\n## Reference Templates\n\nReview project examples in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/README.md` before implementing new features to identify related projects and copy features from those reference implementations.\n\n## Script Structure\n\nA SQRL script is an **ordered list** of statements separated by semicolons (`;`).  \nOnly one statement is allowed per line, but a statement may span multiple lines.\n\nTypical order of statements:\n\n```text\nIMPORT ...            -- import other SQRL scripts\nCREATE TABLE ...      -- define internal & external sources\nMyTable := SELECT ... -- define tables or functions (with hints)\nEXPORT MyTable TO ... -- write table data to sinks\n```\n\nAt compile time the statements form a *directed-acyclic graph* (DAG).  \nEach node is then assigned to an enabled execution engine according to the optimizer and the compiler generates the data processing code for that engine. See [Where a table runs](#where-a-table-runs).\n\n## Flink SQL\n\nSQRL inherits full Flink SQL grammar for\n\n* `CREATE {TABLE | VIEW | FUNCTION | CATALOG | DATABASE}`\n* `SELECT` queries inside any of the above\n* `USE ...`\n* `INSERT INTO`\n\nSQRL currently tracks Flink 2.3; use its compatible SQL syntax. Refer to the Flink SQL documentation for the detailed specification.\n\n## Type System\nIn SQRL, every table and function has a type based on how the table represents data.\nThe type determines the semantic validity of queries against tables and how data is processed by different engines.\n\nSQRL assigns one of the following types to tables based on the definition:\n- **STREAM**: Represents a stream of immutable records with an assigned timestamp (often referred to as the \"event time\"). Streams are append-only. Stream tables represent events or actions over time.\n- **VERSIONED_STATE**: Contains records with a natural primary key and a timestamp, tracking changes over time to each record, thereby creating a change-stream.\n- **STATE**: Similar to VERSIONED_STATE but without tracking the history of changes. Each record is uniquely identified by its natural primary key.\n- **LOOKUP**: Supports lookup operations using a primary key against external data systems but does not allow further processing of the data.\n- **STATIC**: For data that does not change over time, such as constants.\n\nThe table type determines what operators a table supports and how those operators are applied.\n\n## IMPORT Statement\n\nImports another SQRL script into the current script.\n```\nIMPORT qualifiedPath (AS identifier)?;\nIMPORT qualifiedPath.*;             -- wildcard\n```\n\n### Path resolution\n\nA `qualifiedPath` resolves in exactly one of three ways:\n\n* **Relative (default)** \u2014 against the directory of the importing script: `IMPORT my.custom.script` maps to `./my/custom/script.sqrl`.\n* **`root.` (reserved)** \u2014 resolves the rest of the path against the **build root of the top-level project being compiled**, no matter how deeply nested the importing script is. SQRL dot-paths have no `..`, so a script in a **subfolder** cannot climb upward relatively; `root.` is the only way for it to reach something at the project root (`IMPORT root.data_catalog.identity.customers;`, `IMPORT root.functions.my_func;`).\n* **`stdlib.` (reserved)** \u2014 resolves to compiler built-ins (e.g. `IMPORT stdlib.math.*;`), not the file system. Import a whole library with `.*` or a single function by name.\n\n\n- The path may continue one segment past a SQRL script to import a **single `CREATE TABLE`** from it. Standard-library paths may similarly select a function, e.g. `IMPORT stdlib.math.hypot AS hypotenuse`. Hyphenated segments are written as they are (`IMPORT connectors.kafka-source.*`); backticking a segment is also accepted (`` IMPORT `data_catalog`.`sources` ``). A `{{...}}` template variable in the path is substituted from `script.config` before the path is resolved (`IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources;` \u2014 see [Config Templating](guides/templating.md)).\n- Imports that end in `.*` are imported inline which means that the statement from that script are executed verbatim in the current script.\nOtherwise, imports are available within a namespace that's equal to the name of the script or the optional `AS` identifier.\n\n### Examples\n\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script.*`: All table definitions from the script are imported inline and can be referenced directly as `MyTable` in `FROM` clauses.\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script`: Tables are imported into the `script` namespace and can be referenced as `script.MyTable`\n* `IMPORT my.custom.script AS myNamespace`: Tables are imported into the `myNamespace` namespace and can be referenced as `myNamespace.MyTable`\n* `IMPORT connectors.sources-{{environment}} AS sources`: Imports the connector file selected by the `environment` config variable (`sources-test.sqrl`, `sources-prod.sqrl`, \u2026) into the `sources` namespace\n* `IMPORT functions.*` / `IMPORT functions.my_func`: Imports Java UDFs from the project's `functions/` folder (invoke the `/implement-udf` skill); `IMPORT stdlib.math.*` imports built-in functions (see [functions.md](functions.md))\n\n### Import style controls API exposure\n\nBecause a wildcard import executes the statements verbatim in the current script (above), its tables become definitions of this script and follow the normal exposure rules (see *Interfaces*). A namespaced import keeps its tables **private**: they can be referenced in `FROM` clauses as `namespace.Table`, but they are not exposed in the interface directly.\n\n```sql\nIMPORT connectors.sources AS sources;   -- private: sources.Customer usable in FROM, absent from the API directly\nIMPORT connectors.enablement.*;         -- inline: these tables are exposed in the API\n```\n\nTo publish only what the API needs, import the sources aliased and re-declare the tables that should be exposed:\n\n```sql\nIMPORT connectors.sources AS sources;\n/** Purchases with computed line total. */\nPurchase := SELECT *, quantity * unit_price AS total_price FROM sources.Purchase;\n```\n\nWhen a single table must be a **mutation**, put it in its own connector file and wildcard-import that file, while the read-only sources stay aliased.\n\n### Included projects / multi-project (`script.include`)\n\nA project can import scripts from **another SQRL project**, e.g. a shared data catalog or a metrics layer reused by several pipelines, declared under `script.include` in the project's configuration file. To include another SQRL project, invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill for configuration. What matters for the language is how the imports resolve:\n\n* At compile time the included project (the directory holding the top-level package file, together with all its subfolders, every `.sqrl` in them is loaded) is **copied into `build/<namespace>/`**, alongside the consumer's own scripts, where `<namespace>` is the key of its `script.include` entry in the configuration file. A script reaches it with a plain **relative** import, like `IMPORT <namespace>.sources AS sources;`, and that is the preferred form. Name include namespaces with underscores (`data_catalog`), because a dash has a different meaning in SQL. When the importing script is in a subdirectory (e.g. `./my-module/module-script.sqrl`), use `IMPORT root.<namespace>.sources;` (see *Path resolution*). Below the namespace the path follows the included project's own directory structure at any depth. Example:`IMPORT data_catalog.customer.customer_data.customer_master-{{environment}} AS cs;`.\n* `{{...}}` in the *consumer's* import statement is substituted from the consumer's own `script.config`; `{{...}}` inside the *included project's* scripts (e.g. a catalog's `{{environment}}` or a `{{^is_batch}}\u2026{{/is_batch}}` section) is rendered with the `script.config` of the package file named by `package`, overridden by `script.include.<namespace>.config`.\n* **Inside an included project, reference sibling scripts with *relative* imports**, so they travel with the copied files. A `root.` import inside an included project breaks once it is consumed: `root` then points at the consumer's build root, not the included project's.\n* A **`standalone`** include (see `/configure-sqrl`) is deployed on its own, so the consumer does not repeat what that deployment writes: the consumer's subscriptions on tables that the include's main script (or its inline imports) subscribes to read the include's topics, and the include's `EXPORT`/`INSERT` statements into the log, its internal Kafka tables and external tables do not run in the consumer (a NOTICE names each). Write your own subscriptions and exports in the consumer's scripts; those always run.\n\n## CREATE TABLE (internal vs external)\n\nSQRL understands the complete Flink SQL `CREATE TABLE` syntax, but distinguishes between **internal** and **external** source tables.\nExternal source tables are standard Flink SQL tables that connect to an external data source (e.g. database or Kafka cluster).\nInternal tables are managed by SQRL through an engine hint (`kafka` or `iceberg`). Kafka internal sources are exposed for data ingestion in the interface.\n\n| Feature           | Internal source (managed by SQRL)                                   | External Source (connector)    |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| Connector options | **omitted**; other engine-specific `WITH (...)` options are allowed | **required**                   |\n| Engine hint       | **required**                                                        | Not used to select a connector |\n| Metadata columns  | `METADATA FROM 'uuid'`, `'timestamp'`, `'auth.<claim>'` only        | Passed through                 |\n| Watermark spec    | **generated**                                                       | **required**                   |\n| Primary key       | *Unenforced* upsert semantics                                       | Same as Flink                  |\n\nOn an internal table, use only the metadata keys `'uuid'`, `'timestamp'` and `'auth.<claim>'`. A `METADATA` column without `FROM` uses its column name as the key. Any other key stops the compile with an `Unknown metadata key` error, for example `'auht.sub'` or the Flink Kafka key `'offset'`. If you see that error, correct the key to one of the three supported keys.\n\nExample (internal): An internal table managed by DataSQRL as a Kafka topic carries the `engine(kafka)` hint (see the `/manage-connector` skill):\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE Customer (\n  customerid BIGINT,\n  email STRING,\n  ts TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  PRIMARY KEY (customerid) NOT ENFORCED\n);\n```\n\nThe internal table does not need `WATERMARK` declaration. SQRL generates the watermark for it. \nDeclare a `WATERMARK` explicitely on an internal table only when the user asks for one.\n\nWith `engine(kafka)`, `PARTITIONED BY (col, ...)` on an internal table without a `PRIMARY KEY` keys each record by those columns, both the records the server writes for mutations and the records the pipeline exports into the table, so records with the same key stay on one Kafka partition in order.\nThe key columns can be `uuid` or `auth` metadata columns, but not the `timestamp` metadata column.\nThe Flink source ignores that key and reads every column from the message value, so adding `PARTITIONED BY` to an existing internal table is safe: records written before the table had a key are still read, also when the topic is read again from the earliest offset.\nThis relies on the default `kafka-safe` connector. Do not switch internal Kafka tables to the plain `kafka` connector, which drops records that have no key.\nUse `PARTITIONED BY` when records only need a Kafka key for partitioning and ordering. A `PRIMARY KEY` also keys the records, but it makes the table an upsert table (`VERSIONED_STATE`), and its upsert source drops records that were written without a key.\n\nExample (external): An external table connects to a data system through a `WITH (...)` clause and declares its own `WATERMARK`:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE kafka_json_table (\n  user_id    INT,\n  name       STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  WATERMARK FOR event_time AS event_time - INTERVAL '1' SECOND\n) WITH (\n  'connector' = 'kafka-safe',\n  'topic'     = 'users',\n  'format'    = 'flexible-json'\n);\n```\n\n- Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill before writing or editing any table with a `WITH (...)` clause i.e. every external source and sink. This section covers the statement syntax only. Use that skill to decide which connector and format to use, the watermark rules for the source type, whether the schema belongs in a separate `_schema` table referenced with `LIKE`, and which values become `${VARIABLE}` references.\n- With the `_schema` + `LIKE` source pattern, the `WATERMARK`, any `METADATA FROM '...'` column, and any computed (`AS ...`) column belong to the **connector table**, never the `_X_schema` table. Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill for those rules.\n\n### File-based sources: watermarks & snapshot aggregation\n\nFile-based sources (`filesystem`, `iceberg`, `hudi`, `deltalake`) behave differently from Kafka streams.\n\n**(A) Watermark column.** A file-based source whose data timestamp is not quasi-monotonic, or whose files do not arrive frequently, watermarks a computed `ingestion_time AS now()` column instead of a data column. The one exception to \"prefer event time\". The rule that decides this belongs to the source table: invoke the `/manage-connector` skill (Event-Time Processing \u2192 Watermarks on production file-based sources).\n\n**(B) Complete-snapshot sources.** If a file source re-lists every object each cycle (e.g. S3 Inventory, full dumps), a global (non-windowed) `GROUP BY` **double-counts** \u2014 it counts each object once per snapshot. Aggregate each snapshot independently with a `SESSION` window over the `ingestion_time AS now()` column from (A), `PARTITION BY` the group key (read [SESSION Windows](guides/session-tvf.md) first): each snapshot's records arrive as one ingestion burst and form one session, so they are counted once. A short session gap (e.g. 1 minute) closes the window shortly after a snapshot finishes loading, while the much larger gap between snapshots keeps them in separate sessions.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** a `PRIMARY KEY` on a filesystem source does **not** dedup (the source is append-only); even a correct `DISTINCT`-dedup mishandles deletions (an object disappears by absence in the next snapshot) and grows unbounded state.\n\n## Definition statements\n\n### Table definition\n\n```\nTableName := SELECT ... ;\n```\n\nEquivalent to a `CREATE VIEW` in SQL.\n\n```sql\nValidCustomer := SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE customerid > 0 AND email IS NOT NULL;\n```\n\nA common pattern is stream enrichment with a temporal join:\n\n```sql\n-- Temporal join for stream enrichment \u2014 intermediate result uses _ prefix\n_EnrichedOrders := SELECT o.*, c.name, c.email\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n\n-- If the enriched table is exposed directly in the API, use a public name:\n/** Orders enriched with customer details */\nEnrichedOrders := SELECT o.*, c.name, c.email\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\n### Timestamp of a join result\n\nFor every join, decide the timestamp of the result: the time when the result row last changed. Choose it by the type of join:\n\n| Join | Timestamp of the result |\n|---|---|\n| Temporal join (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) or interval join | The timestamp of the stream side. Each result row is an event at that time, and the join never changes it later. |\n| Regular join (an inner or outer join without `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` or a time bound) | Reason about how the result row changes over time. In the common case, both sides change independently. The row then last changed at the later of the two times: `GREATEST(l.ts, r.ts)`. Give `GREATEST` one argument for each joined table that has a timestamp. A table without a timestamp, such as a table of fixed values, never changes, so it adds no argument. `GREATEST` returns `NULL` when one argument is `NULL`. In an outer join, wrap the timestamp of each side that can be missing in `COALESCE` with the timestamp of another side. For example, in a `LEFT JOIN`, write `GREATEST(l.ts, COALESCE(r.ts, l.ts))`. |\n\nA regular join emits a new version of a result row each time either side changes. A row that keeps the timestamp of one side only looks out of date when the other side changes. API clients that sort or filter by that timestamp then get wrong results.\n\nThe Postgres sink writes a new version of a `STATE` row only when this timestamp is not older than the stored one. `GREATEST` goes back to an older timestamp when the optional side of an outer join stops matching, for example because its row is deleted or filtered out. The sink then keeps the old joined values. For such a join, use only the timestamp of the preserved side, such as `l.ts` in a `LEFT JOIN`. It does not show changes of the optional side, but it never goes back.\n\nA `FULL JOIN` has no preserved side. If its sides can stop matching, `CAST` the result timestamp to its type, for example `CAST(GREATEST(...) AS TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3))`, to remove the row-time marker. The sink then writes every version without comparing timestamps.\n\n### DISTINCT operator\n\n```\nDistinctTbl := DISTINCT SourceTbl\n               ON pk_col [, ...]\n               ORDER BY ts_col [ASC|DESC] [NULLS LAST] ;\n```\n\n* Deduplicates a **STREAM** of changelog data into a **VERSIONED_STATE** table.\n* The SQRL `DISTINCT` statement is not SQL `SELECT DISTINCT`.\n* Hint `/*+filtered_distinct_order*/` (see hints) may precede the statement to push filters before deduplication for optimization.\n\n**Use `DISTINCT` when the source table is a `STREAM` of updates to an entity that needs to be converted to a state table, i.e. changelog.** A `DISTINCT` statement adds a stateful operator and changes the semantics of the table.\nCheck the type of the source table before you write one:\n\n| Source table | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| A `STREAM` that carries several records for the same key, for example an append-only change feed | Write the `DISTINCT` statement where a consumer needs a single version per entity, either the current one or the one valid at an event's timestamp in a temporal join. It turns the stream into `VERSIONED_STATE`. |\n| A table that already declares a `PRIMARY KEY` with upsert semantics, for example an `upsert-kafka` source or an internal table with an engine hint and a primary key | Read the table directly. It is `VERSIONED_STATE` already, so a `DISTINCT` on that same key returns the same rows and only costs state. |\n\nRead the `STREAM` directly where a consumer needs the changes themselves, such as a history endpoint, an aggregation over every update, an alert per change, or a stream-to-stream join. When both consumers exist, keep the `STREAM` and define the `DISTINCT` table, if needed, beside it, so each keeps its own semantics.\n\n```sql\n-- Intermediate dedup step (feeds into another table) \u2014 use _ prefix\n_DistinctProducts := DISTINCT Products ON id ORDER BY updated DESC;\n\n-- Final dedup result exposed directly in the API \u2014 use public name\n/** Latest version of each product, deduplicated by id */\nDistinctProducts := DISTINCT Products ON id ORDER BY updated DESC;\n```\n\n### Primary key columns\n\nA table gets a primary key from a `PRIMARY KEY` in its `CREATE TABLE`, or from a `DISTINCT ... ON` or `GROUP BY` on the key columns. Such a table has the type `VERSIONED_STATE` or `STATE` (see [Type System](#type-system)).\n\nClean the primary key columns before the first table that has that primary key, and select them unchanged in every table that reads from that table, directly or through other tables.\nFor example, when `Customer` has the primary key `customer_id`, a later table selects `customer_id`, and never `TRIM(customer_id) AS customer_id`.\n\nA function on a key column in such a later table (`TRIM`, `LOWER`, `REGEXP_REPLACE`, `CAST`, `NULLIF`) hides the key from the Flink planner. A function can turn two different keys into the same value, so the planner treats the result as a normal column, not as a key. Every join that reads the later table stores each full row in its state and finds a row by comparing all of its columns (`NoUniqueKey`), instead of finding it by its key. This makes the state larger and every update slower.\nColumns that are not part of the key can be cleaned in any table.\n\nThese rules apply to entity data (see the `/manage-connector` skill, Entity Data), where each new record replaces the previous version of the same entity, such as a customer or an account.\n\n**Writing entity data.** When the pipeline writes entity data into a table that other tables or projects read, clean the key before the `EXPORT`.\nSuch a table is a mutation table with a `PRIMARY KEY` (an internal table with an engine hint) or a Kafka sink with a `PRIMARY KEY`.\nEvery reader then receives the final key value and can select it unchanged.\nFollow these steps:\n\n1. Clean the key in a view, and keep only the rows whose cleaned key is not empty.\n2. Make the cleaned key unique with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key.\n3. Export the `DISTINCT` table into the table with the primary key.\n\nIn this example, `RawCustomerUpdate` is the source table the pipeline reads. It has no key, and its `customer_id` values can contain extra spaces or be empty.\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE Customer (\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  email STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp',\n  PRIMARY KEY (customer_id) NOT ENFORCED\n);\n\n_CleanCustomerUpdate := SELECT NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') AS customer_id,\n                               email, event_time\n                        FROM RawCustomerUpdate\n                        WHERE NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') IS NOT NULL;\n\n_LatestCustomerUpdate := DISTINCT _CleanCustomerUpdate ON customer_id ORDER BY event_time DESC;\n\nEXPORT _LatestCustomerUpdate TO Customer;\n```\n\nStep 2 is required.\nIt keeps the latest version of each key by `event_time`, and makes the cleaned key the upsert key of the exported query.\nAn `EXPORT` into a table declared with `CREATE TABLE` gets no generated `ON CONFLICT` clause, so when the upsert key of the exported query differs from the `PRIMARY KEY` of that table, Flink rejects the `EXPORT` with the error `The query has an upsert key that differs from the primary key of the sink table`.\nA view that only cleans and filters a source without a key has no upsert key at all, so exporting it directly fails with this error. Export the `DISTINCT` table instead.\n\n**Reading keys that arrive dirty.** When the writer is outside this project, for example an external client that calls a mutation endpoint, the pipeline cannot clean the key before it is stored.\nClean it in the first table after the mutation instead:\n\n1. Declare the mutation without a `PRIMARY KEY`, so that each write is stored as an event with the key value exactly as the client sent it.\n   A `PRIMARY KEY` on the raw key would make the raw value the key, and cleaning it in a later table would hide that key from the planner.\n2. Clean the key in a view, and keep only the rows whose cleaned key is not empty.\n3. Make the cleaned key unique with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key. Every later table reads this `DISTINCT` table.\n\n```sql\n/*+engine(kafka) */\nCREATE TABLE CustomerUpdate (\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  email STRING,\n  event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp'\n);\n\n_CleanCustomerUpdate := SELECT NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') AS customer_id,\n                               email, event_time\n                        FROM CustomerUpdate\n                        WHERE NULLIF(TRIM(REGEXP_REPLACE(customer_id, '\\s+', ' ')), '') IS NOT NULL;\n\n/** Current version of each customer, with the cleaned id as primary key */\nCustomer := DISTINCT _CleanCustomerUpdate ON customer_id ORDER BY event_time DESC;\n```\n\nFollow the same steps for an external source whose keys can arrive dirty.\nWhen an external source's keys always arrive clean, read its key unchanged and skip the cleaning.\n\nClean the key in a view, as shown above, and not inside the `CREATE TABLE`.\nFlink accepts a `PRIMARY KEY` only on physical columns, so a computed column such as `customer_id AS TRIM(raw_customer_id)` cannot be the key.\n\nThe `/*+primary_key(...)*/` hint does not fix this: it declares the key for DataSQRL, but does not deduplicate the data and is not visible to Flink.\nWhen the hint is placed on a state table whose rows the query does not keep unique on the hinted columns, the compiler warns with `PRIMARY_KEY_NOT_UNIQUE`. Do not ignore this warning: replace the hint with `DISTINCT ... ON` the cleaned key.\n\n### Function definition\n\n```\nFuncName(arg1 TYPE [NOT NULL] [, ...]) :=\n  SELECT ... WHERE col = :arg1 ;\n```\n\nArguments are referenced with `:name` in the `SELECT` query. Argument definitions are identical to column definitions in `CREATE TABLE` statements.\n\n```sql\nCustomerByEmail(email STRING) := SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE email = :email;\n```\n\n#### Accessing JWT payload\n\nTo access the JWT payload included in the Authorization HTTP header, you can use the `METADATA` expression within the function definition. The text after `auth.` is the name of a claim in the JWT payload:\n\n* `'auth.val'` reads the top-level claim `val`.\n* `'auth.org.id'` reads the field `id` of the object claim `org`. A path can go to any depth, for example `'auth.org.location.zip'`.\n* A key can contain dots, at the top level and inside an object, for example the namespaced claim `https://example.com/org`. At each level, the server uses the longest key that it finds, and then follows the rest of the path. So `'auth.https://example.com/org_id'` reads a top-level claim, and `'auth.https://example.com/org.id'` reads the field `id` of the claim `https://example.com/org`. In the same way, `'auth.resource_access.com.example.app.roles'` reads the field `roles` of the key `com.example.app` inside the claim `resource_access`.\n* A path can end on an array. In the SQRL script, declare the function argument with an `ARRAY<...>` type, for example `roles ARRAY<STRING> NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.realm_access.roles'`.\n* Array indexing does not work. For example, `'auth.realm_access.roles.0'` does not read the first role. The server treats it as a missing claim. To check for one item, read the full array and use `array_contains` in the query.\n* Give each segment of the path a name. A key with an empty segment, such as `'auth.org.'` or `'auth.org..id'`, stops the compile with an `Invalid metadata key` or `Unknown metadata key` error.\n* When the token has no claim at the path, a request fails with `403 Forbidden` for a `NOT NULL` argument. A nullable argument gets `null`.\n\nIn the example below, the JWT payload contains a val field, which is an integer.\n\n```sql\nAuthFilter(mySecretId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.val') :=\n  SELECT c.* \n  FROM Customer c \n  WHERE c.customerId = :mySecretId;\n```\n\nA script that reads a claim needs an auth provider in `engines.vertx.config`. This applies to a function argument and to a mutation column. The provider is an `auth` block with at least one issuer. The deprecated `jwtAuth`, or `oauthConfig` with `oauth2Options.site`, also gives a provider. Without a provider, the server checks no token. The compile then fails with the error `The script reads JWT claims (...), but engines.vertx.config has no auth provider.` If you see that error, add an `auth` block with the `/configure-sqrl` skill, and keep the `METADATA FROM 'auth.<claim>'` columns. The check also covers a claim function that a custom GraphQL schema leaves out. If the API must not expose that function, start its name with `_`. The `/*+no_query*/` hint does not help here, because it works only on tables.\n\n### Relationship definition\n\n```\nParentTable.RelName(arg TYPE, ...) :=\n  SELECT ...\n  FROM Child c\n  WHERE this.id = c.parentId\n  [AND c.col = :arg ...] ;\n```\n\n`this.` is the alias for the parent table to reference columns from the parent row.\n\n```sql\nCustomer.highValueOrders(minAmount BIGINT) := SELECT * FROM Orders o WHERE o.customerid = this.id AND o.amount > :minAmount;\n```\n\n### Column-addition statement\n\n```\nTableName.new_col := expression;\n```\n\nMust appear **immediately after** the table definition it extends, and may reference previously added columns of the same table. Cannot be applied after `CREATE TABLE` statements.\n\n\n### Relation alias as ROW\n\nA relation alias is the alias of any `FROM` item: a table, a view, a subquery or a CTE. Used by itself in a `SELECT` list, it produces one `ROW` value holding every column of that relation. The API exposes the column as a nested object. \nThe compiler expands the alias to `CAST(ROW(c.col1, ...) AS ROW<...>)` from the relation's validated columns, so the alias replaces writing that cast by hand.\n\nExamples:\n```sql\nTableName := SELECT o.id, c AS customer FROM Orders o JOIN Customer c ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\n```sql\n/** Orders with the customer as it was when the order was placed */\nOrderWithCustomer := SELECT o.id, o.amount, o.event_time, c AS customer\n  FROM Orders o\n  JOIN Customer FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF o.event_time AS c\n    ON o.customerid = c.customerid;\n```\n\nThe `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` clause selects the customer version valid at `o.event_time`. The alias itself packs whichever row the join produced.\n\n* The flink row carries every column of the relation, including its timestamp column. A `_`-prefixed column is stored inside the row and omitted from the API type.\n* A column named like the alias takes precedence, and the alias then resolves to that column. Give the alias a name no column uses when you want the row.\n* A bare alias, `SELECT customerid, c FROM Customer c`, names the column after the alias. `c AS customer` names it explicitly.\n* The alias combines with `o.*`, works inside CTEs and subqueries, and works with `LEFT JOIN`, where the row stays non-null and every field inside it becomes nullable.\n* Read a field back downstream with dot access: `SELECT x.id, x.customer.name FROM OrderWithCustomer x`.\n* Copy a field out as its own column as well, `c.tier AS customer_tier`, when the API filters or sorts by it. The nested value is one JSON column in the database and carries no relationships or filter arguments of its own.\n\nRead the [Stream Enrichment](guides/stream-enrichment.md) guide to choose between the whole-row alias, copied scalar columns, and a relationship.\n\n### Passthrough definitions\n\nPassthrough definitions allow you to bypass SQRL's analysis and translation, passing SQL queries directly to the underlying database engine.\nThis serves as a \"backdoor\" for SQL constructs that SQRL does not yet support natively.\n\nPassthrough table, function, or relationship definition have a `RETURNS` clause in their signature that defines the result type of the query.\n\n```\nTableName RETURNS (column TYPE [NOT NULL], ...) := \n  SELECT raw_sql_query;\n```\n\n**\u26a0\ufe0f Important considerations:**\n- SQL must be written in the exact syntax of the target database engine\n- SQRL will not validate, parse, or optimize the query\n- Only use when SQRL lacks native support for the required functionality\n- Return type must be explicitly declared using the `RETURNS` clause\n- Passthrough queries must be assigned to a database engine for execution\n\nThe following defines a relationship definition with a recursive CTE that is passed through to the\ndatabase engine for execution.\nUse `this` to reference parent fields in a relationship definition or `:name` to reference function\narguments as in standard definitions. The only thing that changes is that adding a `RETURNS` declaration\nbypasses the SQRL analysis, optimization, and query rewriting.\n\n```sql\nEmployees.allReports RETURNS (employeeid BIGINT NOT NULL, name STRING NOT NULL, level INT NOT NULL) :=\n  WITH RECURSIVE employee_hierarchy AS (\n    SELECT r.employeeid, r.managerid, 1 as level\n    FROM \"Reporting\" r\n    WHERE r.managerid = this.employeeid\n    \n    UNION ALL\n    \n    SELECT r.employeeid, r.managerid, eh.level + 1 as level\n    FROM \"Reporting\" r\n    INNER JOIN employee_hierarchy eh ON r.managerid = eh.employeeid\n  )\n  SELECT e.employeeid, e.name, eh.level\n  FROM employee_hierarchy eh\n  JOIN \"Employees\" e ON eh.employeeid = e.employeeid\n  ORDER BY eh.level, e.employeeid;\n```\n\n## Functions\n\nSQRL supports the Flink 2.3-compatible built-in functions plus its own scalar, aggregate, and table functions. [Look up the required function](functions.md).\n\n## Interfaces\n\nThe tables and functions defined in a SQRL script are exposed through an interface.\nThe term \"interface\" is used generically to describe a means by which a client, user, or external system can access the processed data.\nThe interface depends on the configured engines: API endpoints for servers, queries and views for databases, and topics for logs.\nAn interface is a sink in the data processing DAG that's defined by a SQRL script.\n\nHow a table or function is exposed in the interface depends on the access type. The access type is one of the following:\n\n| Access type              | How to declare               | Surface                                                                                              |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Query** (default)      | no modifier                  | GraphQL query / SQL view / log topic (pull)                                                          |\n| **Subscription**         | prefix body with `SUBSCRIBE` | GraphQL subscription / push topic                                                                    |\n| **No direct query**        | `/*+no_query*/` hint         | still **in** the interface, reachable through a relationship or an explicit function but cannot be queryable directly |\n| **Not in the interface but intermediate step** | object name starts with `_`  | not exposed at all                                                                                   |\n\nExample:\n\n```sql\nHighTempAlert := SUBSCRIBE\n                 SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines a subscription endpoint that is exposed as a GraphQL subscription or Kafka topic depending on engine configuration.\nThe assignment operator comes first and the `SUBSCRIBE` keyword comes after it (`Name := SUBSCRIBE SELECT ...`).\n\n```sql\nHighTemperatures := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines a query table that is exposed as a query in the API or view in the database.\n\n```sql\nHighTemperatures(temp BIGINT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > :temp;\n```\nDefines a query function that is exposed as a parametrized query in the API.\n\n```sql\n_HighTemps := SELECT * FROM SensorReading WHERE temperature > 50;\n```\nDefines an internal table that is not exposed in the interface.\n\n### `_` prefix vs. `/*+no_query*/` \u2014 different semantics, never interchangeable\n\n* **`_` prefix** \u2014 the object is **not part of the interface at all**: no endpoint, no generated type, unreachable from any client. This is what pipeline intermediates use (dedup, enrichment, filter and join steps).\n* **`/*+no_query*/`** \u2014 the object **is** part of the interface through relationship but it cannot be queryable directly. Use it for a table clients reach only through a relationship from its parent.\n\n```sql\n-- Clients reach Purchase only through Order, so it has no query endpoint of its own.\n/** A line of a purchase order, with its total price (quantity times unit price). */\n/*+no_query */\nPurchase := SELECT *, quantity * unit_price AS total_price FROM sources.Purchase;\nPurchase.offering := SELECT * FROM Offering o WHERE o.offering_id = this.offering_id LIMIT 1;\nPurchase.customer := SELECT * FROM Customer c WHERE c.customer_id = this.customer_id LIMIT 1;\n\n/** The lines of one purchase order, sorted by offering id. Empty when no order has this id. */\nOrder(order_id STRING NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Purchase WHERE order_id = :order_id ORDER BY offering_id ASC;\n```\n\nApplying `no_query` to a `_`-prefixed table is illogical. Pick one: `_` for a pipeline intermediate, `no_query` for an interface object that must not have its own query endpoint.\n\n### CREATE TABLE\n\nCREATE TABLE statements that define an internal data source are exposed as topics in the log, or GraphQL mutations in the server.\nThe input type is defined by mapping all column types to native data types of the interface schema. Computed and metadata columns are not included in the input type since those are computed on insert.\n\n## EXPORT statement\n\n```\nEXPORT source_identifier TO sinkPath.QualifiedName ;\n```\n\n* `sinkPath` maps to a connector table definition when present, or one of the **built-in** sinks:\n    * `print.*` \u2013 stdout\n    * `logger.*` \u2013 uses configured logger\n    * `log.*` \u2013 topic in configured log engine\n\n```sql\nEXPORT CustomerTimeWindow TO print.TimeWindow;\nEXPORT MyAlerts          TO log.AlertStream;\n```\n\nAn export may also target one table in another script, for example `EXPORT CustomerSubset TO connectors.sinks.CustomerExport;`. The target must be either an external connector table or an engine-managed internal table.\n\n\n## Hints\n\nHints live in a `/*+ ... */` comment placed **immediately before** the definition they apply to.\n\n| Hint                        | Form                                                                       | Applies to     | Effect                                                                                                                                                                             |\n|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **primary_key**             | `primary_key(col, ...)`                                                    | table          | declare the key when the compile fails with `Could not determine primary key for table [...]`; does not deduplicate (see Primary key columns)                                                                                             |\n| **index**                   | `index(type, col [ASC\\|DESC], ...)` <br/> Multiple `index(...)` can be comma-separated | table          | replaces automatic index selection for this table. `type` \u2208 `HASH`, `BTREE`, `PBTREE`, `TEXT`, `VECTOR_COSINE`, `VECTOR_EUCLID`; `DESC` is supported only for `BTREE` and `PBTREE`. <br />`index` *alone* disables all automatic indexes, except the row-time index of a table behind a paginated query |\n| **partition_key**           | `partition_key(col, ...)`                                                  | table          | define partition columns for sinks that support partitioning                                                                                                                       |\n| **vector_dim**              | `vector_dim(col, 1536)`                                                    | table          | declare fixed vector length. This is required when using vector indexes.                                                                                                           |\n| **query_by_all**            | `query_by_all(col, ...)`                                                   | table          | generate interface with *required* filter arguments for all listed columns                                                                                                         |\n| **query_by_any**            | `query_by_any(col, ...)`                                                   | table          | generate interface with *optional* filter arguments for all listed columns                                                                                                         |\n| **no_query**                | `no_query`                                                                 | table          | prevent the table from being directly queryable but reachable through relationships and explicit functions (see *Interfaces*)                        |\n| **insert**                  | `insert(type)`                                                             | table          | controls the way how mutations will be written to their target sink. `type` \u2208 `SINGLE` (default), `BATCH`, `TRANSACTION`                                                           |\n| **ttl**                     | `ttl(duration)`                                                            | table          | specifies how long the records for this table are retained in the underlying data system before it can be discarded. Expects a duration string with a unit between minute and day, e.g. `30 min`, `36 hours`, `14 days`. Disabled by default. |\n| **cache**                   | `cache(duration)`                                                          | table          | how long the results retrieved from this table can be cached on the server before they are refreshed. Expects a duration string like `10 seconds`. Disabled by default.            |\n| **filtered_distinct_order** | flag                                                                       | DISTINCT table | eliminate updates on order column only before dedup                                                                                                                                |\n| **engine**                  | `engine(engine_id)`                                                        | table          | on a table definition, select the engine that computes the table, such as `flink` or `postgres` (see [Where a table runs](#where-a-table-runs)). On a `CREATE TABLE`, name the engine that manages the internal table, such as `kafka` (see [CREATE TABLE](#create-table-internal-vs-external)). `exec` is a deprecated alias of `engine` |\n| **maintenance**             | `maintenance(type)`                                                        | table          | table maintenance for engines that support it. `type` \u2208 `NONE`, `REGULAR`; `REGULAR` on an Iceberg table adds the `iceberg-maintenance` connector options |\n| **row_count**               | `row_count(count)` or `row_count(col, ..., count)`                         | table          | estimated total row count (e.g. `1e6`), or the number of distinct values for the listed column combination. Used for query optimization and index selection: a table with a total row count of at most 100 (`small-table-row-limit` of the `postgres` engine) gets no automatic index (see [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md)) |\n| **test**                    | `test` or `test(no_rows)`                                                  | table          | marks test case, only executed with `test` command.                                                                                                       |\n| **workload**                | `workload`                                                                 | table          | retained as sink for DAG optimization but hidden from interface                                                                                                                    |\n\nMultiple hints are comma-separated inside one comment. This example configures the vector length and a vector index for the `SensorTempByHour` table:\n\n```sql\n/*+vector_dim(embedding, 1536), index(VECTOR_COSINE, embedding) */\nSensorTempByHour := SELECT ... ;\n```\n\nSQL matches column names case-sensitively, but hints do not. A plain column name in a hint matches the column with the same name, ignoring case, so `primary_key(CUSTOMERID)` matches `customerId`. A name in backticks only matches the column with exactly that name. When a table has columns whose names differ only in case, such as `cnt` and `CNT`, a plain name fails the compile with `matches columns that differ only in case`: write the name in backticks, like ``index(BTREE, `CNT`)``, or rename one of the columns. The required columns of a tag in `compiler.tags` match the same way.\n\nFor choosing between `query_by_all` (required filter arguments) and `query_by_any` (optional filter arguments) when designing table access, invoke the `/design-api` skill (Table Hints for Table Access).\n\n### Where a table runs\n\nThe compiler runs each table in Flink or in the database, and picks the cheaper of the two. The `engine` hint replaces this choice for one table:\n\n- `/*+engine(flink) */`: Flink engine computes the rows when the data arrives. When the API reads the table, Flink writes the rows to a Postgres table, and the compiler can index its columns.\n- `/*+engine(postgres) */`: Postgres engine stores no rows for the table. When the API reads the table directly, the compiler creates a view with the query of the table. Otherwise, it copies the query into each served query that reads the table. Postgres computes the rows on every request.\n\nA table definition accepts only an enabled engine that can compute a table:\n\n| Engine     | Engine type |\n|------------|-------------|\n| `flink`    | `process`   |\n| `postgres` | `database`  |\n| `iceberg`  | `database`  |\n\nThe hint also accepts the engine type like `engine(database)`, but compiler selects the engine. Use the engine name. Other engines, such as `kafka`, `vertx`, `duckdb` or `snowflake`, cannot compute a table. For what each engine does and when to enable it, see the `/configure-sqrl` skill.\n\nLeave the choice to the compiler, unless a table needs an index that the compiler cannot give it. A served query can use an index only on rows that the database stores. A table that runs in Postgres stores no rows, so no index can serve a column that it computes, such as a `CONCAT_WS` text for `text_search` or a `CAST` value for a filter. In that case, add `/*+engine(flink) */` to the table: Flink computes the column ahead of time, and Postgres stores it in a table that can have an index. The hint also moves every table that this table reads into Flink, because a table in Flink can read only from Flink or Kafka. So the hint costs Flink state for this table and for each upstream table that ran in Postgres before, plus one more database table. Read the `Stage:` lines of the upstream tables after the compile.\n\nAfter compile, read the `Stage:` line of the table in `pipeline_explain.txt` in the build folder to see which engine computes it.\n\nPut the `engine` hint on a table definition. An API function always runs in the database, so `engine(flink)` on a function fails with `Provided execution stages could not be found or are not configured`.\nA table in Postgres cannot feed a table in Flink. Pinning such a table to `postgres` fails with `Could not find execution stage for [table ...]`.\n\n### Filters in API queries\n\nDataSQRL creates the database indexes for the tables that the API queries. The database (Postgres) can use an index only when the filtered column stands alone in the comparison, so write every served query in the following form:\n\n- Compare the bare column: `customer_id = :customerId`.\n- Do a type conversion or a derived value in a table that Flink computes, then filter on the result: add `/*+engine(flink) */` to the table, select `CAST(c.customer_id AS STRING) AS customer_id` in it, and filter on `customer_id = :customerId`. A table that runs in the database (an `engine(database)` hint, or the planner's choice) is copied into the served query, so its `CAST` ends up in the filter again. A function on the column in the filter, such as `CAST(customer_id AS STRING) = :customerId` or `LOWER(email) = :email`, makes the database read the whole table.\n- Put arithmetic on the parameter side: `day_index = :day - 1`.\n- In a `JOIN`, put the table that the `WHERE` clause filters with a parameter first. With the other order, the database reads every row of the table that the `WHERE` clause does not filter. Write `FROM AccountOwners o JOIN Orders c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.account_id = :accountId`, not `FROM Orders c JOIN AccountOwners o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id WHERE o.account_id = :accountId`.\n- To filter on another table, put `EXISTS (...)` or `IN (SELECT ...)` in the `WHERE` clause and combine it with the other conditions by `AND`: `FROM Orders c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM AccountOwners o WHERE o.account_id = :accountId AND o.customer_id = c.customer_id)`. When the subquery compares with the outer columns only by `=`, the compiler indexes both tables, so the database can start from either one. With `NOT EXISTS (...)`, `NOT IN (SELECT ...)`, or a subquery that is part of an `OR`, the database reads every row of the outer table that the other conditions leave.\n\nThe compiler creates no index for a filter that wraps its column in an expression, and warns `The filter ... cannot use an index on table ...`, with the fix. It does not warn about the order of the tables in a `JOIN`.\n\n### Indexes for API queries\n\nThe compiler selects the indexes for the tables that the API queries. An `index` hint replaces the automatic index selection for its whole table, so a hint that one query does not need can remove the indexes that other queries need. Leave the index selection to the compiler. Read [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md) and fix the query only in these cases:\n\n- The requirements, the plan or a review issue name a slow query or an index.\n- The compiler warns that query filter patterns of a table \"have no supporting index and require a full table scan\". Read the guide before you add the `index` hint that the warning suggests.\n\n\n### Constraints and mutual exclusions\n\n**Never combine `/*+no_query*/` with a `_`-prefixed name.** They are used for different purposes (see *Interfaces*): `_` removes the object from the interface entirely and it is used for intermediate processes. On the other hand, table with `no_query` hint cannot be queried directly while is accessible through relationship from other tables.\n\n**`query_by_all` / `query_by_any` need a queryable table.** They generate interface filter arguments, so they are meaningless on a `_`-prefixed table.\n\n", "truncated": true, "truncatedBy": "bytes", "totalLines": 761, "totalBytes": 61477, "outputLines": 643, "outputBytes": 51198, "lastLinePartial": false, "firstLineExceedsLimit": false, "maxLines": 2000, "maxBytes": 51200}}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 6, "timestamp": 1795510963463}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me continue reading implement-sqrl from line 644. But that might be mostly guides. I'll skip if needed. I have enough. But let me check the `DISTINCT`, function, relationship definitions. Yes I have those.\n\nImportant: the catalog files contain `{{environment}}` placeholders (in the ontology files). If we include the catalog at its top level, DataSQRL loads every .sqrl file. Those include `lending-ontology.sqrl` with `{{environment}}`. Unresolved placeholder \u2192 parse error? The AGENTS addendum warns exactly this. So we must supply `environment` in the include's config. The include's config overrides the package's script.config. So `config: { is_batch: false, environment: \"test\" }`? But wait, the environment should differ per consumer environment (test/prod). The include config is set in the base config which is shared. Hmm.\n\nActually, lending sets `package: ../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json` with config `{is_batch: false}`. The ontology-test-package.json provides `script.config.environment: test`. But wait, the include config overrides the package's config only if supplied. Since lending doesn't supply `environment`, the package's `environment: test` is used for the catalog's own scripts. That means even in prod, lending includes the catalog with environment=test? But that only affects the catalog's own scripts that we're not actually using (the ontology files). The important file, `consumer_loans-{{environment}}.sqrl`, is imported by the consumer with the consumer's `{{environment}}`. Right: the consumer's import is rendered with the consumer's script.config. So the include's environment only affects catalog's internal `{{environment}}` placeholders.\n\nBut if the catalog ontology files load and reference `consumer_loans-test` connectors, they'd be loaded as part of the include. That's fine\u2014they're just definitions in the included namespace, not part of our API since we don't wildcard import them. However, all files being loaded means the source tables get defined in build/data_catalog. That's the lending pattern and it works.\n\nNow the plan/AGENTS addendum says to declare `data_catalog` with `../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json`. But ontology-shared-package.json has no `script.config.environment`. If we include that and supply `config: {environment: \"test\", is_batch: false}`... but environment differs per consumer env. Hmm. The plan's technical decision: \"The catalog is included through one of its top-level package files with the batch mode disabled, exactly like the sibling lending/ base config; the consumer's per-environment configuration selects which catalog connector file it imports, so the included module's own environment setting never reaches the running pipeline.\"\n\nSo \"never reaches the running pipeline\" \u2014 but it still must render without parse errors. If we use `ontology-shared-package.json` (no environment) and don't supply environment in include config, the catalog's `lending-ontology.sqrl` has `{{environment}}` unresolved \u2192 parse error. Unless DataSQRL only parses imported files? Actually the skill says \"every .sqrl in them is loaded\". The warning in AGENTS explicitly says unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors. So we must supply environment.\n\nOption: include `../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json` with config `{is_batch: false}` (like lending) \u2014 then environment=test comes from the package. This is what lending does and it works. The consumer's own import uses the consumer's environment. So use `ontology-test-package.json`. But the AGENTS addendum example lists `ontology-shared-package.json`. Both are top-level and valid. The lending pattern uses `ontology-test-package.json`. Since plan says \"exactly like sibling lending/ base config\", I'll use `ontology-test-package.json` with `is_batch:false`. But hmm, that says environment:test even in prod include. It's inert for our pipeline. Fine.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: the included catalog's `lending-ontology.sqrl` imports `cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}}` = consumer_loans-test. And `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` imports `consumer_loans.*` (schema) and creates source tables with `is_batch` false. Those tables become part of the included project's namespace. Our main script imports `data-catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}} AS cons_loans` using our own environment. In prod, that would import `consumer_loans-prod` (Kafka). Good. So both `consumer_loans-test` and `consumer_loans-prod` might be loaded? Only if referenced by imports. The catalog's `lending-ontology` imports consumer_loans-test. Our main imports consumer_loans-{our env}. So in prod, both test and prod connector files get loaded into build (test from catalog's ontology, prod from us). That could define duplicate table names? They're in separate namespaces? Actually imports are namespace-scoped in catalog, but the catalog ontology wildcard-imports into the ontology file... The point is lending does exactly this and compiles. Let me not overthink\u2014follow lending exactly.\n\nActually wait, does the catalog's ontology even get loaded? DataSQRL loads all files under include root, but only resolves imports transitively from script.main? The skill says \"the directory holding the top-level package file, together with all its subfolders, every .sqrl in them is loaded\". So yes all loaded. And lending includes ontology-test-package.json. But lending's include doesn't point at ontology.sqrl as main; actually the include's package `ontology-test-package.json` has no script.main either, so the compiler doesn't follow its main. It just loads all files. Fine, lending works, so mimic it.\n\nHmm, but there's a concern: `environment` in the catalog scripts being \"test\" while our consumer env is prod: the include config could override. I'll mimic lending exactly: package ontology-test-package.json, config is_batch false. Actually wait\u2014lending's include is `ontology-test-package.json` with config `{ is_batch: false }`. Since the package already has environment test, no `environment` needed. Good.\n\nBut actually there might be an issue: `{{^is_batch}}` sections in `consumer_loans-test.sqrl`. The include config sets is_batch false, so the section is included. Good.\n\nNow, one more issue: since we include the whole catalog, all the other datasets' test connectors with `is_batch` false will load too. That's fine.\n\nActually, alternatively I could set `\"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\"` and config `{\"environment\":\"test\",\"is_batch\":false}`. That would be more explicit. But the plan says \"exactly like the sibling\". Hmm, AGENTS addendum says prefer package path example `../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json` but notes any top-level file works. I'll go with the lending pattern because it's proven and the plan references it.\n\nWait, but the plan also says the AGENTS addendum explicitly gives `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`. However, since environment placeholder needs resolving and the package config would provide it via ontology-test, I think lending pattern is safer. Let me verify by checking if there's a prior agent result or progress file. Let me look at .datasqrl_agent_results.json.\n\nLet me now write the configs. Base config:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"enabled-engines\": [\"flink\", \"postgres\", \"vertx\"],\n  \"script\": {\n    \"main\": \"loan-payments.sqrl\",\n    \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": {\n        \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\",\n        \"config\": { \"is_batch\": false }\n      }\n    },\n    \"api\": {\n      \"v1\": {\n        \"schema\": \"loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n        \"operations\": [\"loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql\"]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"execution.runtime-mode\": \"STREAMING\",\n        \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"30 s\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n          \"enabled\": true,\n          \"defaultThresholdMs\": 2000\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"compiler\": {\n    \"predicate-pushdown-rules\": \"LIMITED_RULES\",\n    \"api\": {\n      \"protocols\": [\"GRAPHQL\", \"REST\", \"MCP\"],\n      \"endpoints\": \"FULL\",\n      \"add-prefix\": true,\n      \"max-result-depth\": 2,\n      \"default-limit\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, the plan says no kafka engine. But the catalog ontology files use `kafka` internally? No, external connector tables with `'connector'='kafka-safe'` don't need the DataSQRL kafka engine. But the catalog's own scripts might define internal tables with engine(kafka)? Let me check: grep for `engine(kafka)` in catalog. Actually ontology.sqrl has subscriptions/mutations? Let me grep.\n\nActually, the enabled-engines just determines what artifacts SQRL produces. Since we don't use kafka engine tables, we don't need it. But the included catalog might have `SUBSCRIBE` or internal kafka tables. If loaded, could that force kafka engine? The include's main script isn't the compilation main, so its interface isn't built. Actually the include loads files but only our main defines the pipeline. Tables in included files are only pulled in via import. Our import pulls consumer_loans connector (external) and schema. So no internal kafka. Good. The plan explicitly says no kafka. Lending enabled kafka because it may use internal tables? Actually lending had kafka enabled though it has no mutation. Hmm, lending includes kafka. But the plan says our engine set is Flink+Postgres+Vertx, no kafka. Let me trust the plan.\n\nWait, but if we don't enable kafka, and the catalog base config has `version`, will the include compile? The include's enabled-engines doesn't matter; only the outer config's enabled-engines determines the pipeline. So fine.\n\nNow, `predicate-pushdown-rules: LIMITED_RULES` like lending.\n\nNow for the test overlay:\n```json\n{\n  \"script\": {\n    \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" }\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"flink\": {\n      \"config\": { \"table.exec.source.idle-timeout\": \"1 s\" }\n    },\n    \"vertx\": {\n      \"config\": {\n        \"auth\": {\n          \"issuers\": [\n            {\n              \"issuer\": \"dummy-issuer\",\n              \"audiences\": [\"dummy-audience\"],\n              \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw\" }]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"delay-sec\": -1,\n    \"required-checkpoints\": 1,\n    \"snapshot-folder\": \"snapshots/loan-payments/\",\n    \"test-folder\": \"loan-payments-api/tests\",\n    \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <token for CUST-060>\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWe need JWT tokens with `customer_id` claim. The user_analytics uses customerId as integer. Our claim is `customer_id` (string, e.g. CUST-060). Need to generate tokens. We'll use `jwt` CLI if available, or generate with python. But the test runner needs a fixed token. The keys: test secret \"dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string\" base64url = ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw. \n\nWhat issuer/audience should we use? The plan says \"HS256 with a fixed test secret in test and a key from ${JWT_SECRET} in prod, with issuer and audience supplied as environment variables.\" The test should use fixed issuer/audience. I'll pick issuer `dummy-issuer`, audience `dummy-audience` for simplicity in test; prod uses `${AUTH_ISSUER}` and `${AUTH_AUDIENCE}` with `${JWT_SECRET}` key. But key must be base64url. Using `${JWT_SECRET}` directly as `k` requires the env var to contain base64url. Hmm. The plan says \"a key from ${JWT_SECRET} in prod\". We'll set `\"k\": \"${JWT_SECRET}\"`.\n\nNow auth config is in both test and prod overlays per step 5. But the configure-sqrl skill says \"Give every compiled combination an auth provider\". The base config doesn't have auth, only test/prod overlays. Since we only compile test and prod, that's fine. But the skill says put it in base to be safe. The user_analytics puts it in each overlay. The plan step 5 says \"Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the test and prod overlays\". So do that.\n\nBut wait: step 3 writes base+test+prod including \"the versioned API, and the test runner\". Then step 5 configures auth in test/prod. To keep it simpler, I could write the full test/prod now including auth. But the plan separates. I'll follow: step 3 writes base + test + prod with test runner and non-auth; step 5 adds auth. Actually to avoid double-editing, but the plan says step 5. I'll just include auth in step 3? The plan says step 3 declares engine set and test runner, step 5 auth. I'll do step 3 configs without auth, then step 5 edit. But compilation of the main script (which reads auth claims) requires auth in merged configs, so we can't compile until step 5. Fine.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety with the base config: `script.include` config `is_batch:false`, and the catalog package already has environment test. But if we use `ontology-test-package.json`, then for our prod environment, the catalog's own scripts still render test. That's what lending does. But is it a problem that the catalog's test connectors reference `${DATA_PATH}`? They only get compiled if imported. Our prod import uses consumer_loans-prod. The catalog's ontology imports consumer_loans-test (because environment=test), but that file is loaded as part of include. It might define the `Personal_Loan` table in the included namespace with filesystem connector referencing `${DATA_PATH}`. In prod, `${DATA_PATH}` unresolved at runtime could be an issue\u2014but since we don't import that namespace, it shouldn't be in our pipeline. Lending prod works that way presumably. I'll trust it.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider: maybe better to use `ontology-shared-package.json` and pass `environment` in include config. But environment must be per consumer env; include config is in base shared. Unless we put the include in both test and prod overlays (each declaring script.main? no). The skill says declare script.include in the config that declares script.main (base). So base can't know env. Therefore using ontology-test-package.json (environment test) is the lending way. But then in prod, the included catalog renders test connectors. Does that matter? In lending prod, the include is in the shared base with ontology-test-package.json, so prod also renders catalog's own scripts as test. And lending compiles in prod. So it's fine.\n\nOK. Let me now write the three config files. But first, let me check the design-api and from-api skills, since step 4 (API schema) depends on them, and I need to design the API before writing config? Config references the schema files. I'll write configs in step 3, then API files in step 4.\n\nLet me write the base config now.\n\nActually, wait: we need to decide on the API surface. Let me read design-api and from-api skills first to design correctly, then write configs and API. Since step 4 is separate but the config references the schema path. I'll write configs first with correct paths.\n\nLet me write base config.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Now I'll write the base config (step 3). Let me first check the from-api and design-api skills briefly, since the API paths go in the config."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb2ff871508f7a9895", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/from-api/SKILL.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb30207e50bc89c873", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/design-api/SKILL.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 12474, "output": 3815, "cacheRead": 75264, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 3691, "totalTokens": 91553, "cost": {"input": 0.0037421999999999998, "output": 0.004578, "cacheRead": 0.000451584, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.008771784}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510963466, "responseId": "96e6964e1fa54bac87c2509b076e04ae-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 9104, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb2ff871508f7a9895", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: from-api\ndescription: Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n---\n\nWhen the requirements specify an API contract (partial or complete) it is critical that the implemented DataSQRL project exposes that API exactly or as closely as possible.\n\nThis inverts the default workflow. `/design-api` builds the API *forward* from SQRL: define tables, let the compiler infer the schema. Here you work *backwards* \u2014 the contract is fixed and the SQRL must be shaped to produce it. Follow this derivation chain and do not skip ahead:\n\n```\nAPI spec \u2192 GraphQL schema \u2192 result types \u2192 tables & columns \u2192 source data\n   (1)          (1)              (2)           (2)              (2)\n```\n\nEach arrow constrains the next step and they must always be followed in this exact sequence.\n\n## 1. Create GraphQL Schema (and operations)\n\nDefine the complete GraphQL schema in the file `{project-name}-api/schema.v1.graphqls` and register it under `script.api` in the project's package.json files (in the shared package.json if there are multiple per environment).\n\nReview `/opt/datasqrl-examples/customer360/customer360-api/schema.v1.graphqls` as reference for supported GraphQL scalars, required directives, and suggested structure. A custom schema must declare custom scalars and directives it uses.\n\n### Mapping spec types to schema types\n\nPick the SQRL column type that produces the scalar the spec requires:\n\n| Spec type (JSON/OpenAPI)      | SQRL column type          | GraphQL scalar                          |\n|-------------------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| `string`                      | `STRING`                  | `String`                                |\n| `integer`                     | `BIGINT` / `INT`          | `Long` (`Int` is an accepted narrowing) |\n| `number`                      | `DECIMAL(p,s)` / `DOUBLE` | `Float`                                 |\n| `boolean`                     | `BOOLEAN`                 | `Boolean`                               |\n| `string`, `format: date-time` | `TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3)`        | `DateTime`                              |\n| free-form object              | `RAW_JSON`                | `JSON`                                  |\n| nested object                 | `ROW<...>`                | child object type                       |\n| array of objects              | `ARRAY<ROW<...>>`         | list of a child object type             |\n\nThe compiler accepts *compatible* substitutions (widening/narrowing scalars, changing cardinalities), so this table is a starting point, not a strict contract \u2014 confirm the result by compiling.\n\nName each object type that has relationship fields after the SQRL table that owns those relationships. Step 2, \"Name types for relationships\", gives the rule.\n\n### From GraphQL\n\nIf the requirements specify the API in GraphQL schema or as example GraphQL queries, design the GraphQL schema directly from those requirements.\n\nNote that SQRL has some limitations you may need to work around:\n- Mutations must have a single InputType argument and the ResultType is identical plus (optional) fields for uuid, timestamp or JWT claim values.\n- SQRL supports all GraphQL scalars but only Long, DateTime, JSON extended scalars.\n- Unions are not supported.\n\nNote that standard GraphQL directive like `@oneOf` are supported and enforced by the server.\n\n### From REST (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI)\n\nIf the requirements specify a REST endpoint (e.g. via Swagger, OpenAPI, or JSON), map those requirements back to GraphQL schema.\n\nDataSQRL uses GraphQL schema as the authoritative API interface and generates REST endpoints either implicitly from the queries and mutations in the schema, or from explicit operations in `{project-name}-api/operations.v1.graphql`. Invoke the `/design-api` skill for that forward mapping \u2014 your job is to invert it.\n\nPrefer the implicit definition of REST endpoints by following queries and mutations. Use explicit operation definitions if the specified endpoints cannot be mapped implicitly, or when the spec fixes the URI, HTTP method, endpoint name, or result body shape.\n\nWhen inverting, note specifically that complex/nested response bodies map to GraphQL relationship fields with referenced types and - similarly - complex/nested request payloads map to GraphQL input type relationships to inner input types.\n\nFollow best practices in defining the GraphQL schema as much as possible while meeting the requirements. Matching the user provided spec as closely as possible takes priority.\n\nIdentify the simplest, normalized GraphQL schema to support all required REST endpoints. For example, if the requirements specify \"a REST endpoint called `UsersByHandles` that takes a username and phone number as URL parameters and returns two fields `byName` and `byPhone` which are arrays of all user records that match by the respective argument\", canonicalize the GraphQL schema to:\n```graphql\ntype User {\n  id: String!\n  username: String!\n  phoneNumber: String\n  email: String\n}\n\ntype Query {\n  \"\"\"\n  Look up users by personal information. Supplied filters are ANDed; omitted\n  arguments are ignored rather than matched against `null`.\n  \"\"\"\n  usersByPersonalInformation(\n    username: String\n    phoneNumber: String\n    limit: Int = 20\n    offset: Int = 0\n  ): [User!]\n}\n```\n\nAnd define an explicit operation:\n```graphql\nquery UsersByHandles($username: String!, $phoneNumber: String!, $limit: Int)\n  @api(rest: GET, uri: \"/queries/usersByHandles{?username,phoneNumber,limit}\") {\n  byName: usersByPersonalInformation(username: $username, limit: $limit) {\n    id\n    username\n    phoneNumber\n    email\n  }\n  byPhone: usersByPersonalInformation(phoneNumber: $phoneNumber, limit: $limit) {\n    id\n    username\n    phoneNumber\n    email\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Every argument of a `GET` operation must appear in the `uri` template.** Any argument missing from the template is treated as request payload, which forces the method to `POST`. Use RFC 6570 form-style query expansion (`{?a,b,c}`) for arguments the spec passes as URL query parameters, and simple expansion (`/{a}`) for those it passes as path segments \u2014 `$limit` above has to be in the template too, or the endpoint could not be `GET`.\n\n`POST` operations support path parameters together with a JSON body of GraphQL variables. A variable supplied in both the path and body must agree or the server returns HTTP 400. POST query-string parameters are ignored; do not use them to satisfy required arguments. The REST bridge supports GET and POST, so document requirements for other HTTP methods as discrepancies.\n\nDeclare `limit`/`offset` arguments only where the spec exposes paging; a query field may also return a single object rather than a list when the spec demands it.\n\nIf a clean mapping from REST to GraphQL schema is not possible, approximate the required API specification as closely as possible and clearly document the discrepancies in the README.\n\n### OpenAPI contract validation and artifacts\n\nPackage.json files support an `openapi` reference for each `script.api` version. When requirements provide an OpenAPI document, retain it as the compatibility baseline and register it alongside the GraphQL schema and any operations:\n\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"api\": {\n    \"v1\": {\n      \"schema\": \"{project-name}-api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n      \"operations\": [\"{project-name}-api/operations.v1.graphql\"],\n      \"openapi\": \"{project-name}-api/openapi.v1.json\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOmit `operations` when using only implicit endpoints. Keep the baseline as a JSON file in the project and reference its project-relative path. Convert a supplied YAML document to JSON without changing its contract.\n\nThe compiler compares the supplied baseline with its generated OpenAPI document. An incompatible change or a failure to read/compare the baseline fails compilation. This checks **backwards compatibility**, not exact equality: compatible additions can pass. The baseline is a validation contract; continue deriving the GraphQL schema and operations from it, and do not weaken it just to make compilation pass.\n\nInspect the generated specification at `deploy/plan/vertx-<version>-openapi.json` under the build folder (`build/` for a plain compile, `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). With REST and OpenAPI serving enabled, it is also available at `/v1/openapi`, with Swagger UI at `/v1/swagger-ui` by default.\n\nAccount for the REST `{\"data\": ...}` envelope when matching the required response. Generated response schemas can be incomplete for operations with multiple root fields (such as `UsersByHandles` above), fragments, or custom result formats, and do not capture response requiredness/nullability. Verify actual REST bodies separately; a passing compatibility check cannot establish an exact response contract.\n\n### From MCP\n\nIf the requirements specify an MCP tool with (optional) arguments and JSON result body you must first map those requirements back to GraphQL schema.\n\nFollow the same steps as for REST APIs above, using explicitly defined operations only when needing to customize the tool name or payload body structure.\n\nAuto-generated tools are named with a `Get` (queries) or `Add` (mutations) prefix. If the spec fixes exact tool names, either define explicit operations (operation name = tool name) or set `\"add-prefix\": false` under `compiler.api` to drop the prefixes.\n\n### Partial specifications\n\nWhen the spec covers only part of the API, treat the specified part as frozen, invoke the `/design-api` skill, and design the rest with its conventions. Do not silently widen a specified endpoint (extra arguments, extra result fields) \u2014 a specified endpoint must match exactly. Decide deliberately whether unspecified endpoints should be exposed at all (see step 3) and record that decision in the README.\n\n## 2. Map GraphQL schema to Tables & Relationships\n\nDefine the tables and relationships in SQRL so that they map to the GraphQL schema by name.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- For queries with no arguments (other than limit & offset which are handled automatically) or simple AND/OR filters, define a table with the same name and add a `query_by_all` or `query_by_any` hint with those arguments as column names.\n- For queries with more complex retrieval logic or claims-based authorization, define a table function with required arguments (other than limit & offset) and implement the retrieval logic in SQL. Use `METADATA FROM 'auth.claim-name'` to annotate arguments that are injected from authorized JWT claims. A script that reads a claim compiles only with an auth provider in `engines.vertx.config`. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill and add the `auth` block.\n- Define SQRL relationships for GraphQL relationship fields with arguments defined like above.\n- For mutations, define a `CREATE TABLE` statement with an `/*+engine(kafka)*/` hint and **no connector `WITH` clause**, where the columns match the mutation InputType definition. If the InputType has relationships to other InputTypes, define nested columns types via `ROW<column definitions...>` or `ARRAY<ROW<columns...>>` depending on multiplicity.\n- For each ResultType field that is not in the InputType, add a metadata column: `METADATA FROM 'uuid'` for a generated id, `'timestamp'` for the time of the call, and `'auth.<claim>'` for a JWT claim. Take the caller identity, such as the owner of the record, from a claim column, so that a caller cannot write a record for somebody else. A mutation table accepts only these three metadata keys. Any other key stops the compile with an `Unknown metadata key` error. Use the `/implement-sqrl` skill (Accessing JWT payload) for the claim rules.\n  In this spec, `ticket_id` and `customer_id` are in `Ticket` but not in `TicketInput`, and the caller is the JWT claim `sub`:\n\n  ```graphql\n  type Mutation { OpenTicket(event: TicketInput!): Ticket! }\n  input TicketInput { subject: String!  body: String! }\n  type Ticket { ticket_id: String!  customer_id: String!  subject: String!  body: String! }\n  ```\n\n  ```sql\n  /*+no_query, engine(kafka) */\n  CREATE TABLE OpenTicket (\n    ticket_id   STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'uuid',\n    customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.sub',\n    subject     STRING NOT NULL,\n    body        STRING NOT NULL\n  );\n  ```\n- For subscriptions, define the table with the `SUBSCRIBE` keyword. This also requires kafka (see below).\n\n### Name types for relationships\n\nThe compiler matches each schema field by name, and the name it looks up depends on the kind of field:\n\n| Schema field | SQRL item the compiler looks up | Role of the GraphQL type name |\n|---|---|---|\n| Field of `type Query` (or `Subscription`) | Table or table function with the field name | None |\n| Field that is a column | Column with the field name in the rows of the parent table or function | None, so any type name works |\n| Field that is a relationship | Relationship with the full name `<GraphQL type name>.<field name>` | It is the first half of that full name |\n\nGive every object type that has relationship fields the name of the SQRL table that owns those relationships.\nA table function cannot own a relationship, so define the relationships on its base table and use that table name as the function's result type.\nThis also applies to every nested type that has relationship fields: name it after the table that owns its relationships.\n\n```sql\n/*+no_query */\nOrderRecord := DISTINCT Orders ON id ORDER BY ts DESC;\nOrderRecord.items := SELECT sku, qty FROM Items i WHERE i.order_id = this.id;\n\nOrderById(id BIGINT NOT NULL, caller STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.sub') :=\n  SELECT o.* FROM OrderRecord o WHERE o.id = :id AND o.customer = :caller;\n```\n\n```graphql\ntype Query {\n  OrderById(id: Long!): OrderRecord\n}\n\ntype OrderRecord {\n  id: Long!\n  items: [Item!]\n}\n```\n\nThe query field `OrderById` matches the function by name. The field `items` matches the relationship `OrderRecord.items` because the type is named `OrderRecord`.\nWith `type Order`, the compiler looks for `Order.items`, finds no such relationship, and fails with `Unknown field at location items. Possible scalars are [...]`.\nThe compatible substitutions in step 1 cover field types and cardinalities. A relationship lookup always uses the exact type name.\n\nA nested type can have its own relationship fields only when its parent field is a relationship.\nIf the parent field is a column with nested data (`ROW<...>` or `ARRAY<ROW<...>>`), the compiler looks only at the values inside that column. It does not look for relationships there.\nExample: `OrderRecord.items` is a relationship to the table `Item`, and the table `Item` has the relationship `Item.supplier`. Then the field `supplier` in `type Item` works.\nIf `items` is an `ARRAY<ROW<sku STRING, qty INT>>` column instead, the field `supplier` fails with `Unknown field at location supplier`.\n\nWhen the compiler reports `Unknown field at location <field>` for a field that you defined as a relationship, compare the GraphQL type name with the table name in the relationship definition `<Table>.<field> :=`.\nRename the GraphQL type to that table name, and keep the SQRL script as it is.\n\n### Mutation shape\n\nThe generated mutation shape follows a fixed convention. For `/*+no_query, engine(kafka)*/ CREATE TABLE Clickstream (event_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'uuid', url STRING NOT NULL, userid STRING NOT NULL, event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'timestamp');`:\n\n```graphql\ntype Mutation {\n  Clickstream(event: ClickstreamInput!): ClickstreamResultOutput!\n}\ninput ClickstreamInput { url: String!  userid: String! }\ntype ClickstreamResultOutput { event_id: String! url: String!  userid: String!  event_time: DateTime! }\n```\n\nInput type is `{Table}Input` (metadata and computed columns excluded), result type is `{Table}ResultOutput` (input fields plus the metadata columns), and the argument is named `event`. The argument name and the type names are customizable in the schema; the structure is not.\n\nThree things are required for a mutation to appear at all, and omitting any of them yields a successful compile with the mutation silently missing:\n1. the `/*+engine(kafka)*/` hint (add `no_query` hint if the table should not also be a query, and always when the table holds the records of all callers),\n2. `kafka` in `enabled-engines` in package.json files,\n3. the table must be visible in the main script \u2014 wildcard-import it (`IMPORT connectors.clickstream.*;`); an aliased import keeps it private.\n\nThe same `kafka` requirement applies to any `SUBSCRIBE` statement.\n\n### Reconciling with available source data\n\nOnce the tables are derived, check each required column against the data the project actually has. Where a specified field has no source:\n- if it can be computable from existing sources, derive it (e.g. aggregate, join, computed column) in existing or separate table joined back in;\n- otherwise invoke the `/manage-connector` skill and add the source it needs, if requirements ask to add a source;\n- otherwise fill column with placeholder content and document the missing data.\n\n## 3. Configure the Exposed Surface\n\nThe schema decides what *can* be served; `compiler.api` decides what is actually exposed. Set these deliberately \u2014 the defaults rarely match a fixed spec:\n\n```json\n\"compiler\": {\n  \"api\": {\n    \"protocols\": [\"REST\", \"MCP\", \"GRAPHQL\"],\n    \"endpoints\": \"OPS_ONLY\",\n    \"add-prefix\": false,\n    \"max-result-depth\": 3\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- **`protocols`** must include every protocol the spec requires (defaults to all).\n- **`endpoints`**: `FULL` (default) auto-generates an endpoint for *every* query and mutation in the schema *in addition to* the explicit operations \u2014 so a spec-exact surface almost always needs `OPS_ONLY`. Use `FULL` only when the spec's endpoints are exactly the schema's queries and mutations.\n- **`add-prefix`**: `false` to suppress the `Get`/`Add` MCP tool-name prefixes when the spec fixes tool names.\n- **`max-result-depth`**: relationship traversal depth when building REST/MCP result bodies implicitly from schema (does not apply to explicitly specified ops). \n\nInvoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill for the full configuration reference.\n\n## 4. Verify Against the Spec\n\n1. Add one test operation per spec'd endpoint under `{project-name}-api/tests/` and run the tests (invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill,read `Projects with a Custom GraphQL Schema` section). Set `\"use-inferred-schema\": false` in the test package, so that these tests exercise the actual contract. With the default `true`, the tests run against the inferred schema instead. Snapshot the response bodies and check their *shape* against the spec, not just their values.\n2. For OpenAPI requirements, compile with the per-version `openapi` baseline registered and compare the generated specification against the required paths (including the configured REST prefix), methods, parameter locations, request bodies, response envelopes, and schemas. Check for extra endpoints explicitly because backwards-compatible additions may pass compilation. Exercise REST requests to verify HTTP behavior and response shape, especially POST path/body handling and operations affected by the response-schema limitations above.\n3. Check that the SQRL script adds no endpoint to the spec. A public table that you define for the mapping in step 2, such as the base table of a table function, gives a query of its own unless it has the `no_query` hint. Run the `compile` command and look for the warning `No GraphQL schema uses these endpoints, but they are still computed and written to the database or log: <names>`. It names each query, relationship and subscription that the SQRL script defines and the GraphQL schema does not have. Fix each name as the `/design-api` skill says (Schema Customization). The `test` command prints this warning only with `\"use-inferred-schema\": false`.\n4. Document every remaining discrepancy in the README: what the spec asked for, what is exposed instead, and why.\n\n## Critical Points\n\n* Always invoke the `/design-api` skill to retrieve the mappings from SQRL->GraphQL->REST/MCP and adhere to those mappings \u2014 this skill only tells you how to invert them.\n* If the compiler fails because it cannot map the tables & relationships in SQRL to the GraphQL schema, try to adjust the table/relationship definitions first. Only if that doesn't work, modify the GraphQL schema.\n* A provided GraphQL schema is static: a column added to a table later does **not** appear in the API until it is added to the schema file too.\n* If you remove the API schema and operations from `script.api` the compiler generates the GraphQL schema in `build/inferred_schema.graphqls`. Use this to partially generate schema from the SQRL files and to discover discrepancies when the compiler fails to match the provided schema.\n* Exactly match the specified API unless DataSQRL limitations require modifications or workarounds. Clearly document discrepancies from the requirements in the README.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510972575}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb30207e50bc89c873", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: design-api\ndescription: Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n---\n\nPrefer designing the API implicitly by defining tables, functions, and relationships in SQRL that map to the desired API. After compiling, the generated API schema is found in `build/inferred_schema.graphqls`.\nOnly configure an explicit schema in the `package.json` configuration if:\n- the schema needs to be static (e.g. to align with consumers of the API)\n- the user wants to adjust the schema (e.g. introduce enums, add interfaces, remove fields from result types, or rename/consolidate types). To remove an endpoint, change the SQRL script instead (see Schema Customization below)\n\nUse the `/configure-sqrl` skill to configure the API, which protocols to expose, and how the exposed API endpoints are generated.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nDataSQRL generates APIs from SQRL scripts with support for **GraphQL**, **REST**, **MCP**, and **Data Products**.\n\n**Critical:** GraphQL is the authoritative model. All API requests (REST, MCP) are executed by the GraphQL query engine.\n\n## SQRL to GraphQL Mapping\n\n**Tables/Functions \u2192 Endpoints:**\n- Default: Query endpoints (name and arguments match)\n- With `SUBSCRIBE`: Subscription endpoints. **Before compiling any SUBSCRIBE statement, add `kafka` to `enabled-engines` in ALL package.json files.** Omitting kafka produces BUILD SUCCESS but the Subscription type will be silently absent from the generated schema.\n- `CREATE TABLE` with `+engine` hint: Mutations (input excludes computed/metadata columns)\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Only tables defined in the main script (or imported inline with `IMPORT otherscript.*`) are exposed in the API. \n\n**Schema Generation:**\n- Tables \u2192 GraphQL types (columns \u2192 fields, nested rows \u2192 child objects)\n- Relationships \u2192 relationship fields on types\n- Compiler infers base tables to avoid redundant result types\n- Hidden columns (starting with `_`) excluded from result types\n\n**Example:**\n```sql\nCustomer := SELECT customerId, email, name FROM RawCustomer WHERE email IS NOT NULL;\n```\nMaps to GraphQL query `Customer(limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [Customer!]` with type containing the three fields. Every query that returns multiple rows gets `limit`/`offset` pagination arguments; the `limit` default is the table's own `LIMIT` if it has one, otherwise `compiler.api.default-limit` (10).\n\n**Schema Customization (preserving object-relationship mapping):**\n- Change cardinalities, scalar types, mutation argument names, field types\n- Add enums and interfaces\n- Compiler validates compatibility with SQRL script(s)\n- Leaving a query, relationship, or subscription out of the schema does not stop the compiler from computing it. The compiler lists such endpoints in a warning, except those on Iceberg: an Iceberg table is a data product that other engines read from the catalog. A Postgres endpoint is listed even when something reads its view directly. `test` uses the inferred schema by default, so it does not warn: check the output of `compile`. To stop computing an endpoint that nothing in the schema reads, remove it or start its name with `_`. If the table has to stay, because it is a mutation table or a relationship or table function in the schema returns its rows, hint it `/*+no_query*/` instead (see [interface.md](interface.md)).\n- A relationship field resolves to `<GraphQL type name>.<field name>` or, if there is neither such a relationship nor a column with that name, to the relationship on the base table. All tables/functions that return a type must resolve its relationship fields the same way (see [interface.md](interface.md)).\n\n## REST and MCP Endpoints\n\n**Auto-generated from GraphQL:**\n- Queries \u2192 MCP tools with `Get` prefix, REST GET endpoints under `rest/queries`\n- Mutations \u2192 MCP tools with `Add` prefix, REST POST endpoints under `rest/mutations` (nested inputs become nested JSON tool arguments)\n- `@api(mcp: NONE)` on a query or mutation field omits its generated operation from MCP\n- Results follow relationships in GraphQL up to configured `max-result-depth`\n- Mutation input types are followed to the leaf input types to construct the expected request payload. \n- MCP apps (interactive HTML UIs for MCP tools) are self-contained `.html` files in the directory set by `script.api.<version>.mcp-apps`. A tool is linked to the app with its name, or to the one its `appName` names; names match regardless of case (see [interface.md](interface.md))\n- MCP is served over Streamable HTTP at `/<version>/mcp` (e.g. `/v1/mcp`) and supports MCP protocol versions `2024-11-05`, `2025-03-26`, `2025-06-18`, and `2025-11-25`. The server echoes the client's requested version if supported, otherwise it responds with `2025-11-25` (see [interface.md](interface.md)).\n\n### Custom Operations\n\nDefined in `.graphql` files and configured in `package.json` under `script.api.<version>.operations`. The top-level `script.operations` is deprecated.\n\n```graphql\n# Returns up to 10 people of the given age.\nquery GetPersonByAge($age: Int!)\n  @api(rest: GET, mcp: TOOL, uri: \"/queries/personByAge/{age}\") {\n  Person(age: $age, limit: 10, offset: 0) { name email }\n}\n```\n\n**`@api` Directive:**\n- `rest`: `NONE` | `GET` | `POST` (HTTP method or disable)\n- `mcp`: `NONE` | `TOOL` (not exposed, or MCP tool)\n- `uri`: RFC 6570 template (args in template = path params, others = payload requiring POST)\n- `appName`: links the tool to the MCP app of that name; `appName: \"\"` links none (see MCP Apps in [interface.md](interface.md))\n- `visibility`: `[MODEL]` | `[APP]` | `[MODEL, APP]` (who an MCP tool is for when MCP apps are used; default both; applied by the host, not an access restriction)\n\n**Rules:**\n- Operation name = MCP tool name = REST endpoint name (must be unique)\n- `compiler.api.endpoints: OPS_ONLY` to expose only custom operations and restrict a public GraphQL endpoint to those stored named operations. `GRAPHQL` keeps public GraphQL flexible while exposing only explicit REST/MCP operations; `FULL` is the default and adds generated operations.\n\n## API Descriptions\n\nAPI consumers read these descriptions in GraphQL, REST (OpenAPI) and MCP. Each one comes from a single source:\n\n| API part | Source of the description |\n|---|---|\n| Generated schema | The SQRL `/** ... */` doc-strings, with their `# Columns:` and `# Arguments:` sections (see `/implement-sqrl`, Comments & Doc-strings) |\n| Custom schema | The `\"...\"` or `\"\"\"...\"\"\"` descriptions in the schema file. A `#` line above a type, field, argument, input field or enum value without a `\"...\"` description also becomes its description. The SQRL doc-strings do not reach the API |\n| Custom operation | All `#` lines above the operation, back to the end of the previous operation or to the top of the file, joined with spaces into one text. So a `#` note between two operations, or in the file header, becomes part of the next operation's description. They describe the REST operation and the MCP tool. A `#` line above a variable describes that argument in MCP, and in the REST request body of a `POST` operation. A `\"\"\"` description before an operation fails the compile with `Invalid syntax` |\n\nWrite every description for the API consumer. Describe each field, argument, input field and enum value whose name does not show its full meaning. For a mutation field in a custom schema, state what it creates or changes and what it returns.\n\nExample from a custom schema:\n\n```graphql\n\"The status of an event or action item.\"\nenum UpdateStatus {\n  \"Not started or in progress. The default when no update has set a status.\"\n  open\n  \"Completed.\"\n  done\n}\n\ntype Query {\n  \"One event by its key, or null when it does not exist or the caller cannot see it.\"\n  Event(\n    \"Subject identifier of the event's author.\"\n    author_sub: String!,\n    \"Business time of the event in epoch seconds.\"\n    event_time: Long!\n  ): EventData\n}\n\ntype Mutation {\n  \"\"\"\n  Creates an event, or replaces the existing event with the same key, and returns the stored event.\n  \"\"\"\n  upsertEvent(event: upsertEventInput!): upsertEventResultOutput!\n}\n```\n\nPut maintainer notes where DataSQRL does not publish them. In an operations file, write them as `#` lines inside the operation body. For a custom schema, write them as `--` comments in the SQRL script.\n\n## API Entrypoints\n\nTo specify how the tables defined in the SQRL script are accessed you either use access hints or explicit table functions.\n\n### Table Hints for Table Access\n\nThe `query_by_*` hints add the listed columns as arguments to the table query endpoint.\n\n- `/*+query_by_all(col, ...)*/`: Required filter arguments: the argument must be supplied in every API call. Use it only when that column is always required to retrieve the data.\n- `/*+query_by_any(col, ...)*/`: Optional filter arguments. Use it when requirements say \"also by X\", \"queryable by X\", or \"filter by X\".\n- `/*+no_query*/`: no query endpoint of its own. The table stays in the API and is reached through a relationship or an explicit table function\n- `_` prefix: not in the API at all (pipeline intermediate). \n\nNote: Never use `_` prefix with `no_query` as they are used for different purposes. See the `/implement-sqrl` skill (SQRL Language Spec, Interfaces) for the distinction.\n\n### Explicit Table Function\n\nTable functions are exposed as separate query endpoints in the API.\n\n```sql\nCustomerByIdRange(fromId BIGINT NOT NULL, toId BIGINT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE customerId >= :fromId AND customerId < :toId ORDER BY customerId;\n```\n\nThis definition maps to query endpoint `CustomerByIdRange(fromId: Long!, toId: Long!, limit: Int = 10, offset: Int = 0): [Customer!]`.\n\n### Authentication & Authorization\n\nDataSQRL authenticates JWT tokens as configured in the server engine and maps claims to function arguments with `METADATA FROM 'auth.[claim-name]'`. A dot in the claim name steps into a nested object claim: `'auth.org.id'` reads the field `id` of the claim `org`. A key can also contain dots, at the top level and inside an object. At each level, the server uses the longest key that it finds in the token, and steps into objects only for the rest of the path. For example, `'auth.https://example.com/org_id'` reads the top-level claim `https://example.com/org_id`, `'auth.https://example.com/org.id'` reads the field `id` of the claim `https://example.com/org`, and `'auth.resource_access.com.example.app.roles'` reads the field `roles` of the key `com.example.app` inside the claim `resource_access`.\n\n```sql\nMyUser(customerId BIGINT NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.userId') :=\n  SELECT c.* FROM Customer c WHERE c.customerId = :customerId LIMIT 1;\n```\n\nMaps to query endpoint `MyUser: Customer`; `customerId` is populated from the `userId` claim and is not a caller-supplied API argument.\n\nRead [authorization configuration](authorization.md) to configure authentication with the `auth` block and to generate test tokens.\n\n## API Versioning and OpenAPI\n\nTop-level `script.graphql` and `script.operations` configure one `v1` API.\nUse `script.api` to serve multiple versions concurrently; version names are `v` followed by a number.\nA version can configure a schema, operations, or an OpenAPI compatibility baseline; SQRL infers the schema when one is not configured.\nWhen `script.api` is present, it defines the API versions and the top-level API fields do not apply.\n\nSQRL generates OpenAPI from the REST-exposed GraphQL operations for every compiled API version.\nIt is a generated description, not a second API definition.\nConfigure `script.api.<version>.openapi` with a prior generated document only to have compilation reject backwards-incompatible REST changes.\n\n## Testing\n\nUse the `/test-sqrl` skill to implement tests against the API.\n\n## More Information\n\nReview the [reference documentation](interface.md) for additional details:\n\n* how SQRL table/function/relationship definitions map to GraphQL\n* how REST/MCP endpoints are derived from GraphQL\n* how to define explicit operations to explicitly defined REST/MCP API\n* how API requests are executed\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795510972576}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have enough to design the API. Let me also read the design-api authorization.md for token generation. Step 5 and tests need tokens.\n\nLet me read it.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb386576508629c234", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/design-api/authorization.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 7729, "output": 86, "cacheRead": 91520, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 35, "totalTokens": 99335, "cost": {"input": 0.0023187, "output": 0.0001032, "cacheRead": 0.00054912, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0029710199999999996}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510972579, "responseId": "85579b6cdad840f199425c2334d2d469-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2109, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb386576508629c234", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "## Authentication Configuration\n\nConfigure authentication in the `vertx` engine with the `auth` block: the list of trusted token issuers, each with its\nown audiences and signing keys. A token is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\nThe full reference, including the deprecated `jwtAuth` and `oauthConfig` blocks, is in the `/configure-sqrl` skill\n([vertx.md](../configure-sqrl/vertx.md#authentication)).\n\n## Testing Authorization\n\nYou can test record filtering, data masking, and other types of authorization based data access control with DataSQRL's automated test runner via the `test` command (see the `/test-sqrl` skill).\n\n### Generating Tokens\n\nTest tokens are generated with the container's built-in **`jwt`** helper \u2014 a\nsmall, dependency-free CLI available on `PATH` inside the agent. For testing, the\n`HS256` (shared-secret) algorithm is simplest and is what these examples use.\n\nA single secret string is the signing key, and it relates to the config two ways:\n\n- **`--secret`** passed to `jwt encode` is the **raw** secret string.\n- **`k`** of an `oct` key in `auth` is that same secret, **base64url-encoded**\n  without padding. Encode it with `printf` \u2014 not `echo`, which appends a trailing\n  newline that changes the key and makes every token fail to validate \u2014 and strip\n  the line breaks `base64` inserts into long output:\n\n```sh\nprintf '%s' 'mySuperSecretSignerStringThatIsLongEnough' | base64 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=\\n'\n# -> bXlTdXBlclNlY3JldFNpZ25lclN0cmluZ1RoYXRJc0xvbmdFbm91Z2g\n```\n\nSet it as the `k` of an `oct` key, which verifies `HS256` tokens:\n\n```json\n\"auth\": {\n  \"issuers\": [\n    {\n      \"issuer\": \"<jwt-issuer>\",\n      \"audiences\": [\"<jwt-audience>\"],\n      \"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"bXlTdXBlclNlY3JldFNpZ25lclN0cmluZ1RoYXRJc0xvbmdFbm91Z2g\" }]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nA project still on the deprecated `jwtAuth` uses the same secret as `buffer`, which is standard **Base64** with\npadding, in `pubSecKeys`:\n\n```sh\nprintf '%s' 'mySuperSecretSignerStringThatIsLongEnough' | base64 | tr -d '\\n'\n# -> bXlTdXBlclNlY3JldFNpZ25lclN0cmluZ1RoYXRJc0xvbmdFbm91Z2g=\n```\n\nThen mint a token by passing the **raw** secret and the claims (shaped to your\ntest's needs; `iss` / `aud` must match an `auth.issuers` entry, or the deprecated `jwtOptions`):\n\n```sh\njwt encode --secret 'mySuperSecretSignerStringThatIsLongEnough' --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"<jwt-issuer>\", \"aud\": [\"<jwt-audience>\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customerId\": 6, \"roles\": [\"user\"]}'\n```\n\nInspect or verify an existing token with `jwt decode` (pass `--secret` to also\ncheck the signature):\n\n```sh\njwt decode --secret 'mySuperSecretSignerStringThatIsLongEnough' <token>\n```\n\n### Default Test Runner Token\n\nWe can set one token directly to the `test-runner` configuration that the deployed test server will pick up by default.\nAny valid HTTP headers can be defined in `headers` if necessary, but in this context the important one is `Authorization`.\nThese headers will be added to any request that will be executed during the test.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"test-runner\": {\n    \"headers\": {\n      \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJteS10ZXN0LWlzc3VlciIsImF1ZCI6WyJteS10ZXN0LWF1ZGllbmNlIl0sImV4cCI6OTk5OTk5OTk5OSwidmFsIjoxfQ.cvgte5Lfhrsr2OPoRM9ecJbxehBQzwHaghANY6MvhqE\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Test Specific Token\n\nTo be able to test different scenarios, it is mandatory to be able to provide different tokens that simulate them.\nTo achieve this, we can define any new test case under the project's `test-folder`, the test execution will pick them up and also compare it with their respective snapshots.\nA custom JWT test case will require two files, which share the same name that will function as the name of the test case:\n* A `.graphql` file that should define a query, mutation, or subscription.\n* A `.properties` file if the test case requires a different token than the one defined in `test-runner\n\nA sample test case structure with three different test cases looks like the below file tree.\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 tests/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 mutationWithSameToken.graphql\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 subscriptionWithSameToken.graphql\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 differentUserQuery.graphql\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 differentUserQuery.properties\n\u2502   ...\n```\n\nThe content of the `.properties` override the applied `headers` tor the matching `.graphql` requests, making it possible to define different scenarios.\nA simple JWT override header properties file would look like this:\n\n```properties\nAuthorization: Bearer <test-specific-jwt>\n```\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 2, "timestamp": 1795510974691}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now let me write the config files. I'll write the base, test, and prod. Let me design the API too.\n\nDesign of API surface:\n\nRequired:\n- `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`: summary of one loan, 0 or 1 row.\n- `LoanPayments(loan_id)`: payments of one loan, newest due date first, paged (limit, offset).\n- Loans of a customer: all personal loans of the signed-in customer, each with its summary. Call it `CustomerLoans` maybe. The requirement doesn't fix a name. Let me call it `MyLoans` or `CustomerLoans`. Acceptance criterion FR-11: \"the customer query returns the 4 loans PL-006, PL-007, PL-022, PL-033, each with its summary.\" No name fixed. I'll pick `CustomerLoans`.\n\nThe exact table/type names: FR-9/10 require table names `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. The customer query \u2014 I'll define `CustomerLoans`.\n\nNow for the GraphQL schema, we need types `LoanPaymentSummary`, `LoanPayments`, `CustomerLoans` (or similar). The type name must match the SQRL table name that owns relationships. No relationships needed here, but custom schema type names should match table names anyway.\n\nRequirements say the API leaves out `loan_number`, account ids, credit score, DTI. For the customer query, include loan_status, original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents, monthly_payment_cents, next_payment_due_date.\n\nLet me design tables:\n\n`LoanPayments` table: columns per transformations item 3.\n- payment_id STRING\n- loan_id STRING\n- customer_id STRING\n- loan_purpose STRING\n- payment_amount_cents BIGINT\n- principal_cents BIGINT\n- interest_cents BIGINT\n- late_fee_cents BIGINT\n- other_fees_cents BIGINT\n- payment_type STRING\n- payment_status STRING\n- due_date DATE\n- received_date DATE\n- applied_date DATE\n- payment_method STRING\n- principal_balance_after_cents BIGINT\n- days_past_due_at_payment INT\n- confirmation_number STRING\n- return_reason_code STRING\n\nDefault order due_date desc, payment_id.\n\n`LoanPaymentSummary`:\n- loan_id STRING\n- customer_id STRING\n- loan_purpose STRING\n- applied_payment_count BIGINT (COUNT)\n- returned_payment_count BIGINT\n- total_paid_cents BIGINT\n- principal_paid_cents BIGINT\n- interest_paid_cents BIGINT\n- late_fees_paid_cents BIGINT\n- last_payment_date DATE\n\nHmm, but the customer query needs loan details: loan_status, original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents, monthly_payment_cents, next_payment_due_date. These come from latest personal loan, not the summary. So I'll define a `CustomerLoans` table that joins latest personal loans with summary. But the summary itself is anchored on latest personal loans, so I could include the loan detail columns in `LoanPaymentSummary`? No\u2014the loan detail is not part of the summary per FR-6. But the customer query type could be a separate type.\n\nApproach: define `_LatestPersonalLoan`, `_LatestPersonalPayment`, `LoanPayments`, `_AppliedPaymentAgg`, `LoanPaymentSummary`, and a `CustomerLoans` table = latest personal loans join summary.\n\nBut wait: `CustomerLoans` should be filtered by customer_id claim. And `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)` should also filter by claim. Since customer_id isn't a caller argument, we need table functions that read the claim.\n\nDesign functions:\n- `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id STRING NOT NULL, customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT * FROM LoanPaymentSummaryBase WHERE loan_id = :loan_id AND customer_id = :customer_id;`\n\nBut there's a name collision: the table and the function can't both be `LoanPaymentSummary`. We need the base table to have a different name and the function named `LoanPaymentSummary`. GraphQL type must be `LoanPaymentSummary` (or whatever the function result type is). The type name should match the base table that owns relationships. Since no relationships, any type name matching the schema works as long as the compiler maps result type. Actually the custom schema type name must match the result type? Let's see: For a table function, GraphQL field `LoanPaymentSummary` maps to function by name. The result type in schema \u2014 the compiler maps to the function's return type? The custom schema has `type LoanPaymentSummary`. The function returns the base table's row type. I think the compiler matches the GraphQL type name to the function? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at how the lending custom schema works: Their tables are `Mortgage`, `CreditCard`, etc., and functions aren't used. For user_analytics, the schema is inferred (no custom schema). Hmm.\n\nLet me look at another example with custom schema AND auth/table functions, e.g. customer360 or library_loans or support_tickets. The from-api step 2 example:\n\n```sql\n/*+no_query */\nOrderRecord := DISTINCT Orders ON id ORDER BY ts DESC;\nOrderRecord.items := ...;\n\nOrderById(id BIGINT NOT NULL, caller STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.sub') :=\n  SELECT o.* FROM OrderRecord o WHERE o.id = :id AND o.customer = :caller;\n```\n```graphql\ntype Query {\n  OrderById(id: Long!): OrderRecord\n}\ntype OrderRecord {\n  id: Long!\n  items: [Item!]\n}\n```\n\nSo the function result type name maps to the base table name `OrderRecord`. Here the base table is `OrderRecord` and function is `OrderById`. Great, so we can have base table named e.g. `LoanPaymentSummary` and function named `LoanPaymentSummaryById`? But FR-9 says \"Query LoanPaymentSummary by loan_id\". The GraphQL query name isn't strictly fixed as `LoanPaymentSummary`, but the plan step 4 says \"keeping the exact table/type names\". Hmm. The requirement says \"Query LoanPaymentSummary by loan_id\". I think the endpoint name `LoanPaymentSummary` is desirable.\n\nCan we name the base table `LoanPaymentSummary` and the function `LoanPaymentSummary`? Name collision likely not allowed. Options:\n1. Name the base table `_LoanPaymentSummary` (internal, `_` prefix) and the function `LoanPaymentSummary`. Then the GraphQL type returned by the function \u2014 the compiler infers the result type name from the function's name? In custom schema, we declare `type LoanPaymentSummary`. Does the compiler map the function result to type `LoanPaymentSummary`? The from-api example maps function `OrderById` to `OrderRecord` base table type. So the compiler determined the function's result type from the base table `OrderRecord`, and matched the schema `type OrderRecord`.\n\nIf base table is `_LoanPaymentSummary`, its type would be hidden? An underscore-prefixed table isn't in the API/interface. But a function returning its rows needs a result type. Hmm, the example uses `/*+no_query */` on `OrderRecord` (not underscore) precisely so it remains in the interface as a type. So the base table should be `/*+no_query*/ LoanPaymentSummary` and the function named differently.\n\nBut then the GraphQL query field name would be the function name, not `LoanPaymentSummary`. To have the query field named exactly `LoanPaymentSummary` while base table also named `LoanPaymentSummary` \u2192 collision.\n\nHmm. Let's reconsider. Actually, can a table function have the same name as a table? Probably not, since they share the namespace.\n\nAlternative: name the base table `LoanPaymentSummary` with `/*+query_by_all(loan_id)*/` and use a JWT claim filter? But `query_by_all` doesn't read claims. However, we could make customer_id a required query argument? No, requirement says customer_id must come from JWT and not caller-visible. So we need a function.\n\nSo the query field must be the function name. We could name the function `LoanPaymentSummary` and the base type `LoanPaymentSummaryRow`. But then the GraphQL type returned would need to be matched. Let me reconsider the mapping rule: \"Field of type Query \u2192 Table or table function with the field name\". \"Schema field that is a column \u2192 Column with the field name\". For the custom schema, the type name returned should match the base table that owns relationships. Since no relationships, perhaps the type name doesn't need to match the base table exactly? The compiler validates the schema against the SQRL. Let me check how the compiler resolves types for custom schema: It says \"Name each object type that has relationship fields after the SQRL table that owns those relationships.\" If no relationships, any type name matching... Actually the compiler must map each GraphQL type to something. Let me read the design-api interface.md for the mapping. Let me read it.\n\nActually, simpler: Maybe I don't need a separate base table. I can name the function `LoanPaymentSummary` and have it select directly from an internal `_LoanPaymentSummary` table. Then the GraphQL type name could still be `LoanPaymentSummary`? The compiler infers result type from function name? Let's check: In the from-api example, function `OrderById` returns `OrderRecord` because it selects `o.*` from `OrderRecord`. The function's result type is the row type of `OrderRecord`, named `OrderRecord`. If we select from `_LoanPaymentSummary`, the row type name would be `_LoanPaymentSummary`? Or the function name?\n\nHmm, let me just read interface.md. It's important to get this right.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccb4df973e287ea9542", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/design-api/interface.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1344, "output": 2171, "cacheRead": 99328, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 2121, "totalTokens": 102843, "cost": {"input": 0.0004032, "output": 0.0026052, "cacheRead": 0.000595968, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0036043679999999997}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795510974694, "responseId": "679dc85718af40a4bf72ff428f98160e-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 5518, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccb4df973e287ea9542", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "\nBased on the SQRL script, DataSQRL generates the interface for the compiled data pipeline. DataSQRL supports the following interfaces:\n\n* Data Product (Data Lake Views and Database Views)\n* GraphQL (Mutations, Queries, and Subscriptions)\n* MCP (Tools)\n* REST (GET and POST)\n\nFor data products, DataSQRL generates view definitions as deployment assets in `build/deploy/plan` which can be queried directly.\n\nThe last three are APIs that can be invoked programmatically. The `protocols` compiler configuration controls which API protocols are exposed by the server.\n\n## Data Products\n\nFor data products, each visible table defined in the SQRL script is exposed as a view or physical table depending on the pipeline optimization. The mapping between visible tables in the SQRL script and exposed tables in the interface is 1-to-1.\n\nWe recommend generating unique table names for the physical tables by configuring a table-name suffix in the connector configuration, e.g. by configuring the `table-name` for `postgres` or the `catalog-table` for `iceberg` to `${sqrl:table-name}_MY_SUFFIX` . This separates views from physical tables to provide modularity and support updates without impacting downstream consumers.\n\n## APIs\n\nWhen a server engine is configured, the tables, relationships, and functions defined in a SQRL script map to API endpoints exposed by the server.\nDataSQRL builds an object-relationship model from the tables and relationships between them. Tables are mapped to objects with each scalar column as a field. Fields that are nested rows are mapped to child objects with field name as the parent-to-child relationship. Relationships defined between tables are mapped to relationships between the corresponding objects.\n\n### GraphQL\n\nDataSQRL uses the GraphQL data model as the base model for all API access to data because there is a natural 1-to-1 mapping between the object-relationship model of a SQRL project and GraphQL schema:\nEach object maps to a type or input and each relationship maps to a relationship field on the respective types.\n\n#### Model-to-Schema Mapping\n\nSpecifically, tables and functions are exposed as query endpoints of the same name and argument signature (i.e. the argument names and types match).\nTables/functions defined with the `SUBSCRIBE` keyword are exposed as subscriptions.\nInternal table sources are exposed as mutations with the input type identical to the columns in the table excluding computed columns.\n\nIn addition, the result type of the endpoint matches the schema of the table or function. That means, each field of the result type matches a column of the table/function or a relationship by name (see [Schema Customization](#schema-customization) for how relationship fields resolve) and the field type is compatible.\nThe field type is compatible with the column/relationship type iff:\n* For scalar or collection types there is a native mapping from one type system to the other\n* For structured types (i.e. nested or relationship), the mapping applies recursively.\n\n#### Base Tables\n\nTo avoid generating multiple redundant result types in the API interface, the compiler infers the base table for each defined table and function.\n\nThe base table for a defined table or function is the right-most table in the relational tree of the SELECT query from the definition body if and only if that table type is equal to the defined table type. If no such table exists, the base table is the table itself.\n\nThe result type for a table or function is the result type generated for that table's base table.\nHidden columns, i.e. columns where the name starts with an underscore `_`, are not included in the generated result type.\n\n#### Schema Generation\n\nIf no GraphQL schema is configured, the compiler infers one from the SQRL script and writes it to `build/inferred_schema.graphqls`.\n\nConfigure the default `v1` schema with `script.graphql`. To serve multiple API versions, configure each version under `script.api`; each version must provide a GraphQL schema, GraphQL operations, an OpenAPI compatibility specification, or an `mcp-apps` directory. When a version does not provide a schema, the compiler infers one from the SQRL script.\n\nA useful customization workflow is to compile without a configured schema, copy `build/inferred_schema.graphqls` into the project (for example, `api/schema.v1.graphqls`), then configure that file as the API schema.\n\n#### Schema Customization\n\nIf a GraphQL schema is defined, the compiler maps the object-relationship model onto the provided schema. You can write your own GraphQL schema or modify the generated GraphQL schema to control the exposed interface. Any modifications must preserve the mapping to the object-relationship model described above.\n\nYou can customize the GraphQL schema by:\n* Changing field cardinalities (e.g. `[Person]!` to `Person!`)\n* Changing scalar types (e.g. `Long` to `Int`)\n* Changing the argument name for mutations (e.g. `event` to `payload`)\n* Changing the type of fields to compatible types (e.g. `Person` to `SpecificPerson`)\n* Adding enums\n* Adding interfaces and structuring types with interfaces\n\nA custom schema can leave out queries, relationships, and subscriptions, but the compiler still computes them and writes the tables they read to the database or log. If no API version's schema uses an endpoint, the compiler prints a warning that lists it. Endpoints on Iceberg are not listed: an Iceberg table is a data product that other engines read from the catalog, so the API is not its only reader. Postgres tables exist to serve the API, so a Postgres endpoint is listed even when something reads its view directly. The `test` command uses the inferred schema unless `test-runner.use-inferred-schema` is `false`, so by default it does not warn. To stop computing an endpoint that nothing in the schema reads, remove it or start its name with `_`. If the table has to stay, because it is a mutation table or a relationship or table function in the schema returns its rows, hint it `/*+no_query*/` instead: the table stays, without a query endpoint of its own.\n\nWith a provided GraphQL schema, the compiler matches each GraphQL field to an item in the SQRL script:\n* A field of `type Query` matches the queryable SQRL table or SQRL function with the same name, and a field of `type Subscription` matches the `SUBSCRIBE` table or function with the same name. The compiler does not check that a field is in the matching root type.\n* A field of the type that a table, function, or relationship returns matches the first of:\n  1. the SQRL relationship `<GraphQL type name>.<field name>`\n  2. the column with the same name in the returned rows\n  3. the SQRL relationship `<base table>.<field name>`, with the [base table](#base-tables) of the table, function, or relationship\n\n  So the type can have any name, unless a relationship field must resolve by the type name.\n* Only a base table can own a SQRL relationship, not a SQRL function or a table whose base table is another table. A function or table that returns only some columns of a table has no base table, so its type reaches that table's relationships only by the type name, for example `OrderById(id: Long!): OrderRecord` for `OrderRecord.items`. Its rows must include the parent columns that the relationship reads (`this.id`). Otherwise the relationship gets `null` and returns no rows, without a compile error.\n* All tables, functions, and relationships that return the same type must resolve its relationship fields to the same relationships, so a field that resolves on the base table needs the same base table for all of them. Otherwise the compile fails with `Type [<type>] is returned by [...] with base table [...] and by [...] with base table [...], which resolve its relationship fields differently. Use a separate type for each base table.`\n* A nested GraphQL type that the compiler reaches through a relationship can have its own relationship fields, by the same rules.\n* A nested GraphQL type that the compiler reaches through a column with nested data (`ROW<...>` or `ARRAY<ROW<...>>`) can have only column fields. The compiler does not look for relationships inside a column.\n* The compiler checks the column fields of each GraphQL type only once, against the rows of the first field that returns it. If several fields return the same type, the rows of each must contain all its column fields. A missing column compiles and returns `null`. Do not use one type both for a column with nested data and for a table or function result.\n\nMutation input fields must match the column types of the table. A `String` column also accepts enum, `ID`, and `JSON` inputs, where JSON objects and arrays are stored as JSON text. Nullable columns can be omitted from the mutation input. If the mutation result type still includes them, those fields must be nullable and return `null`. In a nested input type, an omitted nullable field must also be omitted from the nested result type.\n\nA GraphQL field that matches no SQRL item stops the compile. The error depends on the type that holds the field:\n* A field of `type Query` or `type Subscription` gives `Could not find table or function for field: <field>`. Compare the field name with the names of the SQRL tables and SQRL functions. A matching name also gives this error if the table or function is not queryable: hidden (`_` prefix), marked `/*+no_query*/`, or not defined in the main script or imported inline (`IMPORT otherscript.*`).\n* A field of `type Mutation` gives `No mutation found for <field>`. Compare the field name with the names of the mutation tables.\n* A field of the input type of a mutation gives `Field '<field>' is not present in the mutation table input`. Compare the field name with the columns of the mutation table.\n* A field of the result type of a mutation without a field of the same name in the input type, and a non-null column of the mutation table that is missing from the input type, both give `Could not find field <field> in type <mutation>`. The result type is checked first. If `<field>` is a non-null column of the mutation table, add it to the input type or make the column nullable. Otherwise, remove it from the result type. Exceptions for the result type:\n  * A metadata column (`event_time TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3) METADATA FROM 'timestamp'`, `'uuid'`, `'auth.<claim>'`) belongs only in the result type, and the server fills it.\n  * A computed column (`total AS price * quantity`) can also stay in the result type, but the server does not compute it and returns `null`. Its result field must be nullable, or the mutation fails at runtime after the event was written.\n  * A nullable top-level column left out of the input. Its result field must be nullable, or the compile fails with `Field '<field>' is omitted from the mutation input, so it must be nullable in the mutation result`.\n* A field of the result type of a query or a subscription gives `Unknown field at location <type>.<field>. No relationship [<type>.<field>] or [<base table>.<field>] exists. Relationships named [<field>] exist on other tables: <Table>.<field>. Possible scalars are [...]`. The scalar list shows only the columns that the compiler searched. The other-tables part appears only if such relationships exist, and a nested type reached through a column has no relationship parts. For a relationship field, name the type after a listed table that has the relationship, or make that table the base table of the query.\n\n:::warning\nThe compiler raises errors when the provided GraphQL schema is not compatible with the object-relationship model.\n:::\n\n#### Authoritative Model\n\nDataSQRL uses the GraphQL schemas the authoritative model for all API protocols. It serves as the foundational model on which operations, endpoints, and access patterns are defined. This simplifies the conceptual model and server execution since any API operation maps to a GraphQL query which is executed by a centralized and optimized GraphQL engine.\n\nThe GraphQL query execution engine sits at the core of the DataSQRL server engine and executes all requests even if the GraphQL API is not exposed. This ensures uniform execution of all requests and a shared authentication and authorization mechanism for security.\n\n`compiler.api.protocols` controls the public API surface. Include `GRAPHQL` to expose the versioned GraphQL HTTP, WebSocket, and (when enabled) GraphiQL routes; include `REST` and/or `MCP` to expose operations through those protocols. Omitting `GRAPHQL` leaves the GraphQL engine internal for REST/MCP requests but does not register GraphQL HTTP, WebSocket, or GraphiQL routes.\n\n`compiler.api.endpoints` controls generated operations and GraphQL flexibility: `FULL` generates operations for every query and mutation (and is the default), `GRAPHQL` keeps public GraphQL flexible but exposes only explicit REST/MCP operations, and `OPS_ONLY` accepts only explicit named operations through a public GraphQL endpoint.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Incoming Request] --> B[HTTP + Authentication]\n    B --> C[Router]\n    \n    C --> D[GraphQL]\n    C --> E[REST]\n    C --> F[MCP]\n    \n    F --> G[Operations]\n    E --> G\n    D --> H[GraphQL Query Engine]\n    G --> H\n    \n    subgraph Server\n        B\n        C\n        D\n        E\n        F\n        G\n        H\n    end\n```\n\n### MCP and REST\n\nDataSQRL exposes an endpoint in MCP or REST for each GraphQL operation.\nDataSQRL generates a list of operations from the GraphQL schema: one for each query and mutation endpoint.\n* Queries are mapped to REST GET endpoints under `rest/queries`; mutations are mapped to REST POST endpoints under `rest/mutations`. For generated result sets, DataSQRL follows relationship fields up to a configured depth `max-result-depth` without loops.\n* When `add-prefix` is enabled (the default), generated query and mutation operations are named with `Get` and `Add` prefixes. MCP exposes generated operations as tools, including those with nested input types, which are passed as nested JSON objects and arrays. Add `@api(mcp: NONE)` to a query or mutation field in the GraphQL schema to omit its generated operation from MCP.\n\nFor complete control over the exposed MCP tools and REST endpoints, define named GraphQL operations in one or more `.graphql` files configured under `script.api.<version>.operations` (or under the deprecated top-level `script.operations`).\n\nThe GraphQL file defining the operations contains named queries or mutations.\nThe name of the operation is the name of the MCP tool and REST endpoint and must be unique.\n\nThe `@api` directive is applied to the directive to control how the operation is exposed:\n* `rest`: `NONE`, `GET`, or `POST` to configure the HTTP method or not expose as REST endpoint.\n* `mcp`: `NONE` or `TOOL` to configure whether the operation is exposed as an MCP tool. `TOOL` is the default.\n* `uri`: An RFC 6570 template to configure the REST path. Any operation arguments that are not defined in the uri template are considered part of the payload for REST (and the method must be POST).\n* `appName`: The name of the [MCP app](#mcp-apps) that renders the tool's result. An empty value (`appName: \"\"`) links no app.\n* `visibility`: `[MODEL]`, `[APP]`, or `[MODEL, APP]` (the default) to configure who an MCP tool is for when [MCP apps](#mcp-apps) are used. Hosts with MCP Apps support hide a tool without `MODEL` from the model, and the extension requires them to reject calls from apps to a tool without `APP`, which not every host does. Hosts without MCP Apps support ignore it and show every tool to the model. It is not an access restriction: the server lists and runs the tool for every MCP client, and the operation keeps its REST endpoint unless you set `rest: NONE`.\n\n```graphql\n# Returns up to 10 people of the given age.\nquery GetPersonByAge($age: Int!) @api(rest: GET, mcp: TOOL, uri: \"/queries/personByAge/{age}\") {\n    Person(age: $age, limit: 10, offset: 0) {\n        name\n        email\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis defines an operation `GetPersonByAge` which is the name of the MCP tool and REST endpoint with the path `/queries/personByAge/{age}` using GET method.\n\nThe `#` comment lines above an operation are its REST (OpenAPI) description and its MCP tool description. A `\"\"\"` description before an operation fails the compile with `Invalid syntax`. In the schema file, write descriptions with `\"...\"` or `\"\"\"...\"\"\"`.\n\nBy default, DataSQRL adds explicit operations to the generated ones. Set `compiler.api.endpoints` to `OPS_ONLY` to omit generated REST/MCP operations and restrict a public GraphQL endpoint to explicitly defined named operations. The server executes its stored operation definition, so clients cannot alter its selection set.\n\nThe MCP server uses the Streamable HTTP transport at `/<version>/mcp` (e.g. `/v1/mcp`) and supports the MCP protocol versions `2024-11-05`, `2025-03-26`, `2025-06-18`, and `2025-11-25`. On `initialize`, it responds with the protocol version the client requests if that version is supported, and with the latest supported version, `2025-11-25`, otherwise.\n\n### MCP Apps\n\nAn MCP app is an interactive HTML UI for an MCP tool. MCP hosts that support the [MCP Apps extension](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps) (`io.modelcontextprotocol/ui`) render the app of a tool in a sandboxed iframe when the tool is called. The app receives the tool's input and result from the host, and it can call the server's tools through the host.\n\nAn app has to initialize with the host before it gets any data: it sends `ui/initialize`, and the host sends the tool's input and result after the app confirms with `ui/notifications/initialized`. The `App` class of `@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps` does this in `connect()`. The apps of the [`mcp-apps` use case](https://github.com/DataSQRL/sqrl/tree/main/sqrl-testing/sqrl-testing-integration/src/test/resources/usecases/mcp-apps/apps) show it without a library.\n\nConfigure the directory with the apps of an API version in `script.api.<version>.mcp-apps` in the `package.json`:\n\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"api\": {\n    \"v1\": {\n      \"schema\": \"api/schema.v1.graphqls\",\n      \"operations\": [\"api/operations-v1/ops.graphql\"],\n      \"mcp-apps\": \"api/apps-v1\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWithout `mcp-apps`, the API version has no MCP Apps support. Apps are only served when `compiler.api.protocols` includes `MCP`.\n\nEach app is a single, self-contained `.html` file directly in the apps directory, with all scripts, styles, and assets inlined. Subdirectories and other files are ignored. The app's name is its file name without `.html`. It must start with a letter, a digit, or `_`, and may only contain letters, digits, `_`, `-`, and `.`. You can build apps with any framework that writes single-file HTML into the apps directory, for example Vite with `vite-plugin-singlefile`. DataSQRL only picks up the finished files.\n\nAn app is a static file that every user gets, and hosts may prefetch and cache it. Don't put secrets or a user's data into an app: data reaches an app only through tool results and the tools it calls.\n\nA tool is linked to an app as follows. All names match regardless of case.\n* By default, a tool is linked to the app with the tool's name. For example, the operation `GetSales` is linked to `api/apps-v1/GetSales.html`.\n* `@api(appName: \"SalesChart\")` links the tool to the app `SalesChart` instead, and `@api(appName: \"\")` links no app. One app can serve several tools.\n* A tool with `visibility: [APP]` doesn't need an app of its own. Typical app-only tools are helpers that an app calls, such as a drill-down query or a mutation that the user submits from a form. Use `visibility: [MODEL]` for a tool that apps must not call.\n\n```graphql\n# Sales by region and product for a time range.\nquery GetSales($from: DateTime!, $to: DateTime!) @api(mcp: TOOL, appName: \"SalesChart\") { ... }\n\n# Daily sales for one region. Called by the SalesChart app for drill-downs.\nquery GetDailySales($region: String!, $from: DateTime!, $to: DateTime!) @api(visibility: [APP]) { ... }\n```\n\nDataSQRL serves each app as the MCP resource `ui://apps/<name>` with the MIME type `text/html;profile=mcp-app`, and lists it in `resources/list`. When the project is compiled, each app is written to the deployment assets as `vertx-<version>-mcp-app-<name>.html`, next to the server model `vertx.json`, which names the file of each app. The server reads these files when it starts, so deploy them together with `vertx.json`. A tool linked to an app has the app's URI in `_meta.ui.resourceUri`. With MCP Apps support, each tool has `_meta.ui.visibility`: its `visibility`, or `[\"model\", \"app\"]` if it sets none. The server advertises the `io.modelcontextprotocol/ui` extension on `initialize`. Tool results don't change: apps parse the JSON in the result's `content`.\n\nWhen you define your own GraphQL schema, declare `appName` and `visibility` in the `@api` directive:\n\n```graphql\nenum _McpMethodType { NONE, TOOL }\nenum _McpVisibility { MODEL, APP }\nenum _RestMethodType { NONE, GET, POST }\ndirective @api(mcp: _McpMethodType, rest: _RestMethodType, uri: String, appName: String, visibility: [_McpVisibility!]) on QUERY | MUTATION | FIELD_DEFINITION\n```\n\nThe compilation fails when the apps directory doesn't exist or has no apps, when an app's file name is not a valid app name, when the names of two apps differ only in case, when an `appName` names an app that doesn't exist, when an operation with `mcp: NONE` has a non-empty `appName` or a `visibility`, and when a `visibility` is empty. It warns about an app that is linked to no tool, about a tool with `visibility: [APP]` in an API version that serves MCP without `mcp-apps`, and about an app whose markup loads external or relative resources with `<script src>`, `<link href>`, or `<img src>` other than `data:` URLs. That last check does not see what scripts and stylesheets load.\n\n### OpenAPI and API Versioning\n\nOpenAPI describes the REST API derived from GraphQL operations; it is not a second API definition. DataSQRL generates an OpenAPI 3 document for every compiled API version from REST-exposed operations at `deploy/plan/vertx-<version>-openapi.json` under the build directory: `build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`.\n\nTop-level `script.graphql` and `script.operations` configure one `v1` API. Use `script.api` for concurrent versions; version names start with `v` followed by a number. When `script.api` is present, it defines the API versions to compile and serve, and top-level API fields are not used. Configure `script.api.<version>.openapi` with a previous generated document only to have compilation fail on backwards-incompatible REST changes.\n\n### Testing\n\nDataSQRL's automated testing via the `test` command executes all GraphQL queries inside the configured `test-folder` and snapshots the returned results. Queries are executed in this order:\n\n1. All subscription queries are registered. Note that subscriptions can only be tested if triggered by mutations.\n2. Mutations are executed sequentially in alphabetical order of filename. The test runner waits the configured `mutation-delay-sec` between mutations. Results are written as snapshots to the snapshot folder.\n3. The test runner waits for the pipeline to process the data (see `delay-sec` and `required-checkpoints` in the test-runner configuration).\n4. Queries are executed and results written as snapshots.\n5. All subscription results are sorted and written as snapshots.\n\nIf a snapshot already exists, results are compared and the test fails if they are unequal. If a snapshot does not exist yet, it is created and, provided no other test failed, verified by re-running the pipeline in the same test invocation.\n\nThe test runner uses the configured `headers` for accessing the API. To test authentication and authorization with different access tokens, create a properties file with the same name as the GraphQL file to configure header properties per query.\n\nFor example, if your test folder contains `myquery.graphql` you can configure custom headers for this query in `myquery.properties`:\n```text\nAuthorization: Bearer XYZ\n```\n\nBy default, tests use the inferred schema even when a custom schema is configured. Set `test-runner.use-inferred-schema` to `false` to test against the configured schema instead.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795510980220}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Key insight: The result type name for a function/table is the base table's generated result type name. For a function whose base table is `LoanPaymentSummary` (a `/*+no_query*/` table), the type name is `LoanPaymentSummary`. So we can have:\n\n- `/*+no_query */ LoanPaymentSummary := ...` (base table, owns type)\n- `LoanPaymentSummaryById(loan_id STRING NOT NULL, customer_id STRING NOT NULL METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id') := SELECT * FROM LoanPaymentSummary WHERE loan_id = :loan_id AND customer_id = :customer_id;`\n\nBut then GraphQL query field must be `LoanPaymentSummaryById`, not `LoanPaymentSummary`. FR-9 just says query by loan_id. Could name the function `LoanSummary`? But the plan step 4 said \"keeping the exact table/type names\". Table names are fixed: `LoanPaymentSummary` and `LoanPayments`. The type names should be those. The query field names can differ. Hmm, but acceptance says `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: \"PL-115\")`. That strongly suggests the query endpoint is named `LoanPaymentSummary`. \n\nCan a table and a function share a name? Let's think: The GraphQL field `LoanPaymentSummary` matches \"the queryable SQRL table or SQRL function with the same name\". If there's both a table `LoanPaymentSummary` with `no_query` and a function `LoanPaymentSummary`, ambiguous. Probably compile error.\n\nAlternative approach: Make `LoanPaymentSummary` itself a **table function** named `LoanPaymentSummary` with the claim argument, and have its base table be an internal `_LoanPaymentSummary` table. Then:\n- The function is `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id..., customer_id claim...)`. GraphQL Query field `LoanPaymentSummary` matches it. \n- The result type: base table of the function is `_LoanPaymentSummary`. Hidden columns (`_` prefix) are not included in result type, but the base table's type name is... hmm the base table `_LoanPaymentSummary` would generate a type named `_LoanPaymentSummary`? Hidden columns are excluded but the type name would be `_LoanPaymentSummary`. The custom schema would then declare `type LoanPaymentSummary` but the function returns type `_LoanPaymentSummary`. The compiler checks field compatibility but not necessarily the type name (the type can have any name unless relationship fields). Since no relationships, the custom schema `type LoanPaymentSummary` would be accepted for the function result? Let me re-read: \"A field of the type that a table, function, or relationship returns matches the first of: 1. the SQL relationship ... 2. the column ... 3. the SQL relationship <base table>.<field>... So the type can have any name, unless a relationship field must resolve by the type name.\" That's about fields *within* the type. But how does the compiler connect `type LoanPaymentSummary` to the function's result? It just validates that all fields in the type are columns. It doesn't require the type name to equal the base table name. So `type LoanPaymentSummary` with the right column fields will work.\n\nHmm, but actually, does the compiler even care about the type name matching? For the query field, the schema `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: String!): [LoanPaymentSummary!]` \u2014 the compiler finds the function