pproach differs from what the skill and compiler accept, rewrite that step to describe what you built, so a post-compaction read of the checklist matches the code on disk.\" So I can edit step text. Let me update step 3 and 12 to mention the actual filenames. Actually, let me do that when marking step 12/13.\n\nNow step 13: README. Invoke write-documentation skill. Let me read it.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tests and snapshots all match the acceptance criteria. Now step 13: documentation. Let me read the write-documentation skill."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccf57bc7003be8179c1", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/write-documentation/SKILL.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccf57e370f1a1d59e76", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"edits": [{"newText": "- [x] 12. Create `run-tests.sh`", "oldText": "- [*] 12. Create `run-tests.sh`"}, {"newText": "- [*] 13. Document the project, its sources", "oldText": "- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources"}], "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 223, "output": 581, "cacheRead": 177920, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 366, "totalTokens": 178724, "cost": {"input": 6.69e-05, "output": 0.0006971999999999999, "cacheRead": 0.00106752, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00183162}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511242298, "responseId": "04aaef284b57404e8f474607df9de514-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2751, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccf57bc7003be8179c1", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: write-documentation\ndescription: Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n---\n\nInitialize the README.md if none exists and make sure it contains a) a high level description of the project, b) the motivation and requirements for the project, c) a concise description of the implementation choices and architecture and d) commands the user can use to compile, test, and run the project. \n\nImportant Rules:\n- Update documentation to reflect changes made. Document changes with motivation or requirements.\n- Keep track of progress and features not yet implemented.\n- Write the commands in the README as described in [Commands](#commands).\n\n## Commands\n\nWrite each command for the user's machine. The user runs Docker and has no `/opt/agent/cmd.sh`.\n\nGive the commands in two forms: the project's test script, and `docker run` commands that show the DataSQRL call.\n\n### The test script\n\n`./run-tests.sh` is the project's own runner, and CI uses it. It mounts the repository, passes `-r`, pins the DataSQRL image and runs any setup steps.\n\nStart the block from the repository root. When the project is in a subfolder, put `cd <project-path>` first. `<project-path>` is the `-r <root>` value that `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations` prints. When `<root>` is `.`, leave out the `cd` line.\n\n```bash\ncd <project-path>                                    # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                                       # test every sub-project\n./run-tests.sh <sub-project>                         # test one sub-project\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env dev <sub-project>     # compile one sub-project with a deployed environment\n```\n\n### The docker run commands\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  compile -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-dev-package.json -b <sub-project>\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  test -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-test-package.json -b <sub-project>\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:<SQRL_VERSION> \\\n  run -r <root> <sub-project>-shared-package.json <sub-project>-dev-package.json\n```\n\nFill in the values from the project:\n\n1. Run `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations <sub-project>` for the test configs, and `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations --compile --env dev <sub-project>` for the compile and run configs. Use `--env local` when the project has a `local` environment and no `dev`. The option prints the invocations, runs nothing and needs no Docker.\n2. Copy `-r <root>` and the config files from those lines, in the same order.\n3. Read `SQRL_VERSION` in `run-tests.sh`.\n4. When the pipeline needs an environment variable, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`, add `-e <NAME>`.\n\nWhen `run-tests.sh` runs a setup step before the suites, state in the README that the `docker run` test command needs that step first.\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `-r <root>` | The project folder, relative to the repository root. `.` when the project is the repository root. |\n| `-b <name>` | Writes the output to `build/<name>`, the same folder that `run-tests.sh` uses. Optional. Keep it when the project has more than one sub-project, so that one build does not overwrite another. |\n| `-it` | Attaches a terminal, so the user can stop `test` or `run` with `Ctrl-C`. Leave it out for `compile`, which stops by itself. |\n| `-p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081` | Opens the API and the Flink WebUI for `run`. Add `-p 9092:9092` to reach Redpanda with Kafka tooling. |\n\nIn the README, explain `-b` in one sentence under the `docker run` commands: `-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nIn Windows PowerShell, use the same mount: `-v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel):/workspace\"`.\n\n### Environments\n\nPair each command with the environment it is made for. `./run-tests.sh --list-envs` prints the environments the project declares.\n\n| Command | Environment | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test` | `test` | The test config adds what the tests need: the snapshot folder, test settings and, in some projects, a test API schema that exposes the `/*+test */` tables. |\n| `compile` | a deployed environment, such as `dev`, `local` or `prod` | A compile builds the pipeline that you deploy. A connector can reach an external system while it compiles, for example S3 through AWS, so a `prod` compile can need production credentials. |\n| `run` | a local environment, such as `dev` or `local` | `run` starts the pipeline on the user's machine. The `prod` config points at production systems, so a local run would read from or write to them. |\n\nWrite every `compile` and every `run` command in the README with `dev`, or with `local` when the project has a `local` environment and no `dev`. The `dev` config needs no production credentials, and a deployed environment works in every project. A compile with the test config (`<sub-project>-test-package.json`) works only when that config leaves the API schema as it is. When the test config swaps in a test API schema, that schema asks for the `/*+test */` tables, which only the `test` command creates. A `compile` or `run` with the test config then fails with `Could not find table or function for field: <Name>Test`.\n\n### Where each command runs\n\n`$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)` mounts the repository root from any folder inside the repository, so `-r <root>` always finds the project. This also makes sibling modules that the project includes (such as `../data-catalog`) available in the container.\n\nA mount of `$PWD` works only from one folder: `-v $PWD` mounts the folder the user starts in, so `-r <root>` finds the project only from the repository root, and a command without `-r` works only from the project folder.\n\nState in the README where each command runs: the `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters. The `docker run` commands work from any folder inside the git repository. From a folder outside it, `git rev-parse` finds no repository and Docker stops with `invalid spec: :/workspace`.\n\nThe `run` command executes the entire pipeline locally and allows the user to interact with it:\n- Flink cluster is accessible through the WebUI at http://localhost:8081/.\n- The Redpanda cluster is accessible on port 9092 (via Kafka command line tooling) when port is mapped.\n- The GraphQL API is accessible at http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/. \n- The Swagger UI for the REST API is accessible at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui\n- The OpenAPI JSON for the REST API is served at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi\n- The MCP API is accessible at `http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795511245055}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccf57e370f1a1d59e76", "toolName": "edit", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md."}], "details": {"diff": "     ...\n  45 - [x] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n  46 - [x] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n  47 - [x] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n  48 - [x] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- 49 - [*] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- 50 - [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n+ 49 - [x] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n+ 50 - [*] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n  51 - [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n  52 \n  53 ## Data Quality & Observability\n  54 - **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md\n@@ -45,10 +45,10 @@\n - [x] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups \u2014 summary by `loan_id`, payments by `loan_id`, and the signed-in customer's loans with their summaries \u2014 reading the `customer_id` claim (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n - [x] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n - [x] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema table-test package covering the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loan ids, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n - [x] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with applied late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria for FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n-- [*] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n-- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n+- [x] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate the snapshots. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n+- [*] 13. Document the project, its sources, its API, the authorization model, and how FR-8 is covered in the README. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n - [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n \n ## Data Quality & Observability\n - **Build** \u2014 none new. The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from personal\n", "firstChangedLine": 49}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 5, "timestamp": 1795511245055}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now write the README. Need to include:\n- high-level description\n- motivation and requirements\n- implementation choices and architecture\n- commands (test script + docker)\n- sources\n- API\n- authorization model\n- how FR-8 is covered\n- Data quality and observability section (per monitor-pipeline skill)\n- Tests description\n\nLet me write it.\n\nFor docker commands, I need the invocations. `./run-tests.sh --list-invocations` prints `-r .` because git missing in this shell. But per the skill, the docker commands use `-r <root>` where root is the project path. The template with git in CI gives ROOT=loan-payments. But in the README we should write commands that work on the user's machine (with git): `cd loan-payments` then `./run-tests.sh`; docker from repo root with `-r loan-payments`. The skill says `<project-path>` is the `-r <root>` value that `--list-invocations` prints. In a real environment with git, it prints `loan-payments`. Since our project is a subfolder of the repo, root=loan-payments.\n\nLet me write the README. SQRL_VERSION=0.12.5.\n\nREADME content:\n\n# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for personal loans. Reads shared data-catalog consumer-loan sources, builds LoanPayments and LoanPaymentSummary, exposes GraphQL/REST/MCP with JWT auth.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n...\n\n## Data Sources\nPersonal_Loan, Loan_Payment from catalog. test: files; prod: Kafka.\n\n## Processing / Architecture\ntables.\n\n## API\nqueries and operations. types.\n\n## Authorization\nJWT claim customer_id. test/prod config, token generation.\n\n## Data Quality and Observability\nno pipeline-local tables; reasoning.\n\n## FR-8 coverage\nby construction.\n\n## Tests\ntwo packages.\n\n## Project structure\n\n## Commands\ntest script and docker.\n\nLet me write.\n\nOne thing: JWT token generation command with the secret and options. Include.\n\nAlso mention no dev/local environment \u2014 only test and prod, per assumptions. The skill says write compile/run with `dev` or `local`. We have no dev/local. Hmm. The compile command should use prod. The skill says \"A compile builds ... a deployed environment\". We have test and prod. For compile, use prod. For run, there's no local environment. Hmm. The requirements only asked for test and prod. The README can show compile prod and note there is no local run environment. Actually the plan assumption says \"only test and prod overlays\". So the README will show compile with prod and no local run. But the write-documentation skill wants a run command. We could show `run` with the test connectors (environment test) which uses filesystem sources \u2014 that works locally! The test package sets environment test and uses test sources. But the test package also sets test-runner and use-inferred-schema. Running with the test package would use the test source config. Hmm, but the skill warns compile/run with the test config may fail if the test schema swaps. Ours doesn't swap the schema; the API test package sets use-inferred-schema false but the schema is the real custom schema. The `run` command with test package would use the real custom schema and filesystem sources. That should work locally. But the test package's auth uses the fixed test secret. That's fine for local demo.\n\nActually, to avoid confusion, I can document:\n- compile prod: `docker run ... compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments`\n- test: `./run-tests.sh`\n- local run: use the shared + api-test configs (environment test, filesystem sources) with `run`. But `run` isn't tested. I'll mention it as an optional local run using the test environment.\n\nHmm, the skill says run with `dev` or `local`; we don't have those. Since the requirements explicitly only ask for test and prod, I'll document compile with prod and note that a local run uses the test environment (filesystem sources). I'll keep it honest.\n\nLet me write the README.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Now I'll write the README."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccf69807a62abe20e41", "name": "write", "arguments": {"content": "# Loan Payments\n\nA customer-facing data application for **personal loans**. It reads the shared finance\n[`data-catalog`](../data-catalog) sources `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment`, builds a payment\nhistory and a per-loan payment summary, and serves them through a JWT-authorized GraphQL, REST and\nMCP API. Each customer sees only the loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\nThe sub-project is `loan-payments`. The table tests run as a second sub-project,\n`loan-payments_table_test`.\n\n## Motivation and Requirements\n\nPersonal-loan customers sign in through the bank SSO and need to look up their own loans:\n\n- `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer and purpose of the\n  loan. It keeps payments in every status, so a customer still sees a returned payment.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts, payment totals and the last\n  payment date. Only `APPLIED` payments contribute to the totals; a loan without payments still\n  appears with zero counts and a null last payment date.\n- Three lookups, all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`: a loan's summary, a loan's payments,\n  and every personal loan of the signed-in customer with its summary.\n\nAuto loans, mortgages and credit cards are out of scope, as are writes: the API has no mutation.\nThe pipeline manages no data of its own \u2014 it reuses the catalog connectors and its test data.\n\n## Data Sources\n\nBoth sources come from the shared `data-catalog` module, declared under `script.include` in\n`loan-payments-shared-package.json` and imported in `loan-payments.sqrl`. The project never copies or\nredeclares them.\n\n| Source | Test (`environment: test`) | Production (`environment: prod`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Personal_Loan` | `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.personal_loan` |\n| `Loan_Payment` | `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl` | Kafka topic `consumer_loans.loan_payment` |\n\nBoth are CDC streams with `ingested_at` watermarks (10 s in test, 30 s in prod). The project uses\nthe catalog watermarks as they are.\n\n## Architecture and Implementation Choices\n\n| Table | What it does | In the API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `_LatestPersonalLoan` | latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key `loan_id`, all statuses | no |\n| `_LatestLoanPayment` \u2192 `_LatestPersonalLoanPayment` | latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` | no |\n| `LoanPaymentsSource` | personal payments inner-joined to their loan record, enriched with `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` | as the `LoanPayments` lookup |\n| `_AppliedPaymentAgg` / `_ReturnedPaymentAgg` | applied and returned payment counts and sums per loan | no |\n| `LoanPaymentSummarySource` | every latest personal loan left-joined to both aggregates | as the `LoanPaymentSummary` lookup |\n| `CustomerLoansSource` | the summary plus the loan details the client shows | as the `CustomerLoans` lookup |\n| `LoanPayments`, `LoanPaymentSummary`, `CustomerLoans` | claim-injected table functions over the sources above | yes |\n\n- **CDC dedup and no windows.** `DISTINCT ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC` keeps the latest\n  version per key, and the summary is an unwindowed, unbounded re-aggregation. A late or changed\n  payment updates the API within the freshness target (about one minute) instead of waiting for a\n  window boundary.\n- **Loans without payments.** `LoanPaymentSummarySource` is anchored on the latest personal loans\n  and left-joins the applied-payment aggregate, so `PL-011` and the other payment-free loans appear\n  with zero counts and sums and a null `last_payment_date`. Grouping only the payments would omit\n  them.\n- **Flink + Postgres + Vert.x, no Kafka engine.** The dedup, enrichment and aggregation run in\n  Flink; the three lookups run in Postgres over indexed tables; Vert.x serves the API. There is no\n  mutation or subscription, so the `kafka` engine is not enabled \u2014 the catalog's Kafka sources are\n  ordinary external Flink connectors.\n- **Custom GraphQL schema.** `loan-payments-api/schema.v1.graphqls` fixes the exposed type and query\n  names, and `loan-payments-api/operations.v1.graphql` adds REST URIs and MCP tools. The claim\n  argument never appears as a caller argument.\n\n## API\n\n| Query | Arguments | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)` | `loan_id` | one loan's summary, or `null` when the loan is not the caller's |\n| `LoanPayments(loan_id, limit, offset)` | `loan_id`, paging | the loan's payments, newest due date first |\n| `CustomerLoans(limit, offset)` | paging | every personal loan of the caller, each with its summary |\n\n`customer_id` is **not** an argument. It is read from the JWT and every query filters on it, so a\nrequest for another customer's loan returns no rows. GraphQL, REST and MCP are all exposed. Explicit\noperations: `LoanSummaryByLoan`, `LoanPaymentsByLoan`, `MyLoans`.\n\nThe API leaves out `loan_number` (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI\nratio. Amounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`).\n\n## Authorization\n\nEvery query needs a valid JWT with the top-level claim `customer_id`; a request without a valid\ntoken is rejected (`JWT auth failed` / `Unauthorized`). The claim is mapped to a required function\nargument with `METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'`.\n\nThe auth provider is configured under `engines.vertx.config.auth` in each test and prod overlay:\n\n- **test**: issuer `loan-payments-test-issuer`, audience `loan-payments-test-audience`, inline HS256\n  key from the fixed secret `dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string` (base64url without padding:\n  `ZHVtbXktc3VwZXItc2VjcmV0LWp3dC1zaWduaW5nLXN0cmluZw`).\n- **prod**: issuer `${AUTH_ISSUER}`, audience `${AUTH_AUDIENCE}`, key `${JWT_SECRET}` (base64url).\n\nMint a test token for any customer id:\n\n```bash\njwt encode --secret 'dummy-super-secret-jwt-signing-string' --alg HS256 \\\n  '{\"iss\": \"loan-payments-test-issuer\", \"aud\": [\"loan-payments-test-audience\"], \"exp\": 9999999999, \"customer_id\": \"CUST-060\"}'\n```\n\n## How FR-8 Is Covered\n\nFR-8 says that when a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary uses\nonly the latest status. It is covered **by construction**, not by a snapshot: `_LatestLoanPayment`\ndeduplicates on `payment_id` by `source_updated_at`, so only one version of each payment reaches\n`LoanPaymentsSource`; the unbounded aggregation then reads that single latest version. A changed\npayment therefore replaces its contribution to the counts and sums. No catalog payment changes\nstatus in the test data, and the scope forbids adding synthetic source data, so there is no\nsnapshot for it.\n\n## Data Quality and Observability\n\nThis project adds **no** pipeline-local `DQ_` or `OB_` tables:\n\n- The one credible silent-loss point is the inner join from a `PERSONAL` payment to\n  `Personal_Loan`: a payment whose loan record never arrives would vanish. The catalog already\n  asserts referential integrity (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in\n  `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`), so no capture is warranted.\n- The two sources are not independent senders, so ingestion counters add no signal; the catalog's\n  root-ontology snapshot queries already surface content changes.\n- The auto-loan exclusion is a required filter, not an anomaly, so it is not monitored.\n\nExisting coverage is reused from the catalog's lending-ontology assertions and root-ontology\nsnapshot queries.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\nsource data. `Category: test` and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\none schema:\n\n| Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `loan-payments` | `loan-payments-api-test-package.json` | custom (`use-inferred-schema: false`) | the API tests in `loan-payments-api/tests/` |\n| `loan-payments_table_test` | `loan-payments-table-test-package.json` | inferred (`use-inferred-schema: true`) | the `/*+test*/` tables in `loan-payments.sqrl` |\n\nAPI tests (each `X.graphql` with an optional `X.properties` token override):\n\n| Test | Token | Checks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payment-returned` | `CUST-439` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` includes `LPMT-046` with status `RETURNED` and reason `R01` |\n| `loan-summary-returned` | `CUST-439` | `PL-115`: `returned_payment_count = 1`, `total_paid_cents = 635360` (the returned 57,760 is excluded) |\n| `loan-late-fees` | `CUST-061` | `PL-035`: `late_fees_paid_cents = 7500` |\n| `zero-payment-loan` | `CUST-066` | `PL-011`: zero counts and sums, null `last_payment_date` |\n| `customer-loans` | `CUST-060` | the four loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary |\n| `wrong-customer-summary` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPaymentSummary(\"PL-115\")` returns `null` |\n| `wrong-customer-payments` | `CUST-060` | `LoanPayments(\"PL-115\")` returns no rows |\n| `missing-token` | none | the request is rejected with an authorization error |\n\nTable tests:\n\n| Test | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `LoanPaymentsCountTest` | 1,010 personal payment rows over 135 loans |\n| `AutoLoanLeakTest` (`no_rows`) | no `AL-` loan id leaked in |\n| `DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `payment_id` |\n| `DuplicateLoanSummaryTest` (`no_rows`) | one row per `loan_id` |\n| `ZeroPaymentSummaryTest` | a payment-free loan has zero counts/sums and a null last payment date |\n\nExpected results are in `snapshots/loan-payments/` and `snapshots/loan-payments_table_test/`.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nloan-payments/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments.sqrl                        # processing, lookups and table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-shared-package.json         # base config: engines, catalog include, API\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api-test-package.json       # custom-schema API tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-table-test-package.json     # inferred-schema table tests\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-prod-package.json           # production auth\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments-api/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 schema.v1.graphqls                    # GraphQL schema\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 operations.v1.graphql                 # REST/MCP operations\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests/                                # API test queries and tokens\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 snapshots/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 loan-payments/                        # API snapshots\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 loan-payments_table_test/             # table snapshots\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 run-tests.sh\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n## Commands\n\nThe `./run-tests.sh` commands run from the project folder, which the `cd` line enters from the root\nof the repository.\n\n```bash\ncd loan-payments                             # the project folder, from the repository root\n./run-tests.sh                               # run the API tests and the table tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments                 # run only the API tests\n./run-tests.sh loan-payments_table_test      # run only the table tests\n./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod loan-payments   # compile the production pipeline\n```\n\nThe `docker run` commands make the same DataSQRL calls and work from any folder inside the git\nrepository.\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json -b loan-payments\ndocker run -it --rm -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  test -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-table-test-package.json -b loan-payments_table_test\n```\n\n`-b <name>` writes the output to `build/<name>`, and it is optional.\n\nThe project has no `dev` environment; the `test` environment reads the catalog's filesystem test data\nand can be used for a local run:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -it --rm -p 8888:8888 -p 8081:8081 -v \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\":/workspace datasqrl/cmd:0.12.5 \\\n  run -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-api-test-package.json\n```\n\nWhile the pipeline runs: the Flink WebUI is at http://localhost:8081/, the GraphQL API and GraphiQL\nat http://localhost:8888/v1/graphiql/, the REST Swagger UI at http://localhost:8888/v1/swagger-ui,\nthe OpenAPI JSON at http://localhost:8888/v1/openapi, and the MCP API at\n`http://localhost:8888/v1/mcp/`. Every request needs a token from the [Authorization](#authorization)\nsection.\n\nA production deployment needs `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE` and `JWT_SECRET`, and the catalog\nrequires `LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS`.\n", "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/README.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1835, "output": 4338, "cacheRead": 178688, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 910, "totalTokens": 184861, "cost": {"input": 0.0005505, "output": 0.0052055999999999995, "cacheRead": 0.001072128, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0068282279999999996}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511245059, "responseId": "f847627e53804158a2d313bd4492630b-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 10488, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccf69807a62abe20e41", "toolName": "write", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully wrote to /workspace/loan-payments/README.md"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 3, "timestamp": 1795511255552}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I referenced `Category: test` accidentally? Let me check: \"The tests read the catalog test data... `Category: test` and table tests live in two packages\". That's a typo. Let me fix: \"API tests and table tests live in two packages\". Let me edit.\n\nAlso I should verify there are no other typos. Let me fix that one.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20ccf8bf8766399730b01", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"edits": [{"newText": "source data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses", "oldText": "source data. `Category: test` and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses"}], "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/README.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 2614, "output": 200, "cacheRead": 182272, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 78, "totalTokens": 185086, "cost": {"input": 0.0007842, "output": 0.00023999999999999998, "cacheRead": 0.001093632, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.002117832}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511255555, "responseId": "123d21d5ba6a4dd1adceff0254119e72-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2870, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20ccf8bf8766399730b01", "toolName": "edit", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /workspace/loan-payments/README.md."}], "details": {"diff": "     ...\n 127 \n 128 ## Tests\n 129 \n 130 The tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\n-131 source data. `Category: test` and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\n+131 source data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\n 132 one schema:\n 133 \n 134 | Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n 135 |---|---|---|---|\n     ...", "patch": "--- /workspace/loan-payments/README.md\n+++ /workspace/loan-payments/README.md\n@@ -127,9 +127,9 @@\n \n ## Tests\n \n The tests read the catalog test data through the catalog test connectors; the project adds no\n-source data. `Category: test` and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\n+source data. API tests and table tests live in two packages because each `sqrl test` run uses\n one schema:\n \n | Sub-project | Test package | Schema | Runs |\n |---|---|---|---|\n", "firstChangedLine": 131}, "isError": false, "durationMs": 4, "timestamp": 1795511258430}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now step 13 done. Step 14 is conditional: tests fail after step 12. They passed, so mark [~] skipped.\n\nNow, before final run, let me also validate the prod compile works (since run-tests.sh covers test but not prod; the requirement says \"concurrency package.json files for the project compile successfully\"). Let me compile prod. Also fix the plan step texts and mark steps 13, 14.\n\nActually, I should also double-check the REST/MCP endpoints and the schema mapping. Let me run a prod compile to ensure prod config compiles (auth with env placeholders). The compile might fail if `${AUTH_ISSUER}` etc. unresolved? The skill says string values can reference env vars resolved at server start. Compile should be fine. But the catalog prod connector requires `${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}` at compile? Let me test.\n\nLet me first update plan, then run prod compile. Then final run-tests.sh (test). \n\nAlso I should reconsider: The `loan-payments_table_test` sub-project has no prod config. The run-tests.sh `--env prod` would fail for it. That's acceptable per template, but a reviewer might run `./run-tests.sh --compile --env prod` and it fails for table_test. Hmm. The library_loans example has the same structure (table_test has no prod) and its suite returns 1. So it's fine.\n\nLet me compile prod. Command with SQRL_PACKAGE_PREFIX? Direct: `/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile -r loan-payments loan-payments-shared-package.json loan-payments-prod-package.json -b loan-payments`. Redirect.\n\nLet me update plan and run prod compile.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Step 13 done