{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:13:56.318297+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd550bc7100af7ac0a4", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "### Testing\n\nA `/*+test */` hint in front of a table definition marks a test case: the table is only executed by the `test` command (ignored otherwise), and its result is snapshotted in the configured `snapshot-folder`. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for what a test table must satisfy (deterministic `ORDER BY`, predictable columns, the `_`-alias for sort-only columns) and for running tests and snapshots.\n\n## NEXT_BATCH\n\n```sql\nNEXT_BATCH;\n```\n\nFor batch pipelines, use `NEXT_BATCH` to split processing into sequential sub-batches; the next batch runs only when the prior one succeeds. Current SQRL code permits `NEXT_BATCH` only when `enabled-engines` is exactly `[\"flink\"]`.\n\nThe batch allocation only applies to `EXPORT .. TO ..` statements. All interfaces are computed in the last batch.\n\nIn the following example, the `NEXT_BATCH` guarantees that the `PreprocessedData` is written completely to the sink before processing continues in the next sub-batch.\n\n```sql\nPreprocessedData := SELECT ...;\nEXPORT PreprocessedData TO PreprocessorSink;\nNEXT_BATCH;\n...continue processing...\n```\n\n:::warning\nSub-batches are executed stand-alone, meaning each sub-batch reads the data from source and not from the intermediate results of the previous sub-batch.\nIf you wish to start with those, you need to explicitly write them out and read them.\n:::\n\n## Pipeline design rules\n- Do not combine event time with processing time. Prefer event time for consistency and reproducible results. (Exception: file-based sources may use an ingestion-time `now()` column as the watermark \u2014 invoke the `/manage-connector` skill and see Event-Time Processing section.)\n- Use Flink 2.3-compatible SQL syntax and table-valued functions (e.g. for time windows)\n- Execute as much processing as possible on append-only `STREAM` tables\n- Use temporal joins (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) and interval joins when possible\n- Move stateful operations (global aggs, inner joins) to end of pipeline\n- Use Flink time window aggregation when requirements specify time intervals (e.g. \"aggregate x by day\")\n\n```sql\nSensorAvg := SELECT sensorid, window_time AS time_hour,\n  AVG(temperature) AS avg_temp\nFROM TUMBLE(TABLE SensorReading, DESCRIPTOR(event_time), INTERVAL '1' HOUR)\nGROUP BY sensorid, window_start, window_end, window_time;\n```\n\n## Comments & Doc-strings\n\n* `--` single-line comment; write it on its own line, never trailing at the end of a statement\n* `/* ... */` multi-line comment\n* `/** ... */` **doc-string**: attached to the next definition. When DataSQRL generates the GraphQL schema, the doc-string becomes the GraphQL description of the query, mutation, subscription or relationship field, and of the type that the definition creates. For a queryable table, a function or a `CREATE TABLE` mutation, it also becomes the description of its generated REST (OpenAPI) operation and MCP tool. With a custom schema, the descriptions in that schema replace the doc-strings (see `/design-api`, API Descriptions).\n\nHow DataSQRL publishes a doc-string:\n\n* A function or relationship that returns the rows of an existing table (`SELECT * FROM T ...`) uses the type of `T`. Its doc-string describes only the field.\n* A definition without a doc-string takes the doc-string of its base table as its type description. This includes a hidden `_` table.\n* A `CREATE TABLE` doc-string describes the type, the generated query and the mutation.\n* A `# Columns:` section describes the columns of the type that the definition creates. A `# Arguments:` section describes the arguments of a function. Each section has one `- name: text` line for each column or argument. A column note becomes the GraphQL field description and the field description in the REST response schema. An argument note becomes the GraphQL argument description and the MCP parameter description.\n* A line indented under a column describes a field nested in it, to any depth: the fields of a `ROW` column, or of the rows of an `ARRAY<ROW<...>>` column (e.g. `- address: ...` and below it `  - street: ...`).\n\n```sql\n/** A registered person.\n# Columns:\n- email: Email address. Null when the person gave none.\n*/\nPerson := SELECT name, email, age FROM sources.Person;\n\n/** The people of one age, sorted by name.\n# Arguments:\n- age: Age in whole years.\n*/\nPersonByAge(age INT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Person WHERE age = :age ORDER BY name;\n```\n\nWrite each doc-string for the API consumer, who sees only the API, and this includes the doc-string of a `_` table. State what the object is and what it returns: its key, its order, and when the result is null or empty. Describe the behavior the consumer sees, such as \"only the latest version of each order\", and leave out how the pipeline computes it. For a `CREATE TABLE`, describe the record it holds, because the same text describes the query and the mutation.\n\nPut requirement IDs and other maintainer notes in `--` or `/* */` comments.\n\n### Tags in doc-strings\n\nWhen the package configuration defines tags under `compiler.tags`, the `@name` and `@name=value` words in a doc-string are tags that the compiler validates (see the [configuration documentation](https://docs.datasqrl.com/docs/configuration#tags-compilertags)):\n\n* Use only the tags and values defined in `compiler.tags`. A boolean tag is written `@name`; an ordered tag needs a value, e.g. `@pii=raw`.\n* A table inherits the tags of every table it reads, including joined and subquery tables. A public table or function must repeat each inherited tag in its doc-string with the same or a higher value, or the compile fails. Copy the tags from the compile error's `inherited from [...]` tables. Hidden tables, relationships and test tables carry their inherited tags without declaring them.\n* Tags in `# Columns:` notes, including nested ones, count toward the whole table.\n* A tag with `required-columns` in `compiler.tags` requires those columns, with a compatible type, in every table that carries the tag, including hidden tables and tables that are only written into. Keep the column in the `SELECT`, or declassify the tag when the requirements allow it.\n* A `CREATE TABLE` that `INSERT INTO` or `EXPORT` writes into must declare the tags of the written data when other tables read it or it is an internal table with an `engine` hint (e.g. an Iceberg or Kafka table in a separate script).\n* To drop or lower an inherited tag, e.g. for an aggregate that no longer contains personal data, add `/*+declassify(tag, ...) */` and declare the lower value or none. Only declassify when the requirements say the data allows it.\n\n## Validation rules\n\nThe following produce compile time errors:\n\n* Reserved SQL keywords used as identifiers without escaping as column names (`value`, `timestamp`, `time`, `date`, `upper`, `lower`, `from`) or as table names (`Order`, `Group`, `Value`, `Key`, `Table`, `Index`, `Row`, `User`, `Transaction`, `Schema`). Use descriptive alternatives instead: `event_value`, `event_timestamp`, `upper_bound`; `ClinicalOrder`, `PatientGroup`, `AccountTransaction`.\n* Duplicate identifiers (tables, functions, relationships).\n* Overloaded functions (same name, different arg list) are **not** allowed.\n* Argument list problems (missing type, unused arg, unknown type).\n* `DISTINCT` must reference existing columns; `ORDER BY` column(s) must be monotonically increasing.\n* An `ORDER BY` column that does not also appear in the `SELECT` list \u2014 in any table definition, including `/*+test*/` tables (`All sort columns must be part of the SELECT clause`. The `/test-sqrl` skill shows how to sort by a column you do not want in the result).\n* Basetable inference failure for relationships.\n* Malformed or invalid hints (wrong delimiter, other text in the hint block that is not a hint, invalid arguments to a known hint such as `ttl`, or more than one `query_by_*` or `no_query` hint on a table).\n* A column name in a hint that matches no column, or that matches several columns whose names differ only in case (see Hints).\n* Tag violations when `compiler.tags` is configured (see Tags in doc-strings above).\n\nAn unknown hint name is **not** an error: the compiler prints the warning `Unrecognized hint: [<name>]` and drops the hint, so a misspelled hint has no effect. Check the compile output for this warning after adding a hint.\n\nA compile reports all errors of the script at once, each with its position, so fix all of them before you compile again. It leaves out the errors that only follow from another error:\n* A table, column or function whose definition failed is not reported again as not found where it is used. A failed column is recognized by its name, so no table is reported as missing a column of that name until you fix its definition.\n* After an `IMPORT package.*` whose package cannot be loaded, no table or function is reported as not found.\n* The tables imported from a script that fails to parse report only the script's parse errors.\n* A statement that adds a column to, exports or inserts into a table whose definition failed is skipped, as is a passthrough definition (`RETURNS`) whose query reads from it.\n* After a `USE` statement fails, the rest of the script is skipped. In an imported script, that is the rest of that script, and of the importing script too if the script is imported inline (`IMPORT package.script.*`).\n\nSo a fix can bring up new errors in the statements that were skipped or used the failed object. Compile again until no errors are left.\n\n## Additional pattern guides\nFor additional patterns, read these guides:\n* [Stream Enrichment](guides/stream-enrichment.md): How to enrich a data stream with dimensional information or state consistently, and how to carry the whole dimension row as one nested object.\n* [Config Templating](guides/templating.md): How to use variables in SQRL scripts for templating that gets substituted at compile time with mustache engine.\n* [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md): How the compiler selects indexes, and how to check and fix the indexes of a slow API query. Read it only in the cases that Indexes for API queries names.\n* [SESSION Windows](guides/session-tvf.md): The `SESSION` TVF for window aggregation has limitations and quirks. Always read this guide before implementing `SESSION` windows.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795511636317}}
{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:13:56.320326+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me work through the issues. Many are duplicates from two verifiers (both referring to `loans.sqrl`, `consumer_loans.sqrl`, `cons_loans.sqrl`, and `pipeline.sql`). Let me categorize the actual fixes to make in our project files (`loan-payments.sqrl`, configs, `run-tests.sh`):\n\n1. **Docstrings on exposed functions LoanPaymentSummary, LoanPayments, CustomerLoans** \u2014 add `/** ... */` with `# Arguments:` for loan_id and customer_id claim.\n2. **Rename intermediate tables** `LoanPaymentsSource` \u2192 `_LoanPaymentsSource`, etc. \u2014 update all references.\n3. **Test tables deterministic ORDER BY** for AutoLoanLeakTest, DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest, DuplicateLoanSummaryTest, ZeroPaymentSummaryTest.\n4. **Intent documentation** \u2014 comments about DISTINCT dedup and why latest status, and ZeroPaymentSummaryTest fixture.\n5. **Prod overlay redundant flink idle-timeout** \u2014 remove from prod.\n6. **@pii / @npi / @confidential tags** \u2014 The catalog uses tags in docstrings. Do we have `compiler.tags` configured? No. Tags are metadata in docstrings; the verifier (pipeline) is checking that sink columns carry tags propagated from sources. Hmm. This requires adding `compiler.tags` config to enable tag validation, and using `declassify`? Wait. Let me think.\n\nActually, the issue says sink columns \"lack required @pii tag propagated from source ... primary_borrower_customer_id\". This is a tag validation feature. The reduced scope: We need to add tags to the SQRL docstrings? But tags in docstrings apply to tables, not columns? Let me re-read the configure-sqrl tags section: \"When `compiler.tags` is present, the compiler validates the `@name` and `@name=value` tags in doc strings and propagates them from the tables a query reads to the table it defines.\"\n\nHmm. And the implement-sqrl \"Tags in doc-strings\" section. Let me read that part. Column-level tags? The source schema column comments have `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential` in the comments (e.g. `* primary_borrower_customer_id: Reference to primary borrower @pii`). These are column-level tags in the catalog's docstrings. When we build derived tables, the compiler checks that the derived columns preserve required tags, and `declassify` hints can remove them.\n\nSo the verifier wants us to enable `compiler.tags` and ensure the derived sink columns carry the tags (or are properly declassified). But wait, the tags are column-level in the catalog. How does the compiler know? It parses the catalog's docstrings for tags. Let me read the implement-sqrl tags section (lines 644+) and maybe a tag reference file.\n\nLet me read the rest of implement-sqrl.md (lines 644-762) which likely covers Comments & Doc-strings and Tags.\n\nAlso note the verifier says \"Location: pipeline.sql\" \u2014 that's `build/.../pipeline.sql`? It's the compiled SQL, so the issue is about tag propagation in the pipeline. Since the pipeline currently has no `compiler.tags` config, no validation happens. But the verifier may be checking the catalog tags and expecting our sink tables to propagate them. Hmm.\n\nActually the task says \"Sink column LoanPayments.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source...\". This implies the project should have tags configured. Let me read the tags documentation.\n\nLet me read implement-sqrl lines 644-762.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I'll work through the fixes. First, let me read the remaining implement-sqrl docs on tags/docstrings and check the current plan/scope."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd550bc7100af7ac0a4", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md", "offset": 644}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 4540, "output": 901, "cacheRead": 190208, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 802, "totalTokens": 195649, "cost": {"input": 0.001362, "output": 0.0010812, "cacheRead": 0.001141248, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.003584448}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511631566, "responseId": "5a0b65fa63b54208a0b0afeb49967c43-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 4740, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd550bc7100af7ac0a4", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "### Testing\n\nA `/*+test */` hint in front of a table definition marks a test case: the table is only executed by the `test` command (ignored otherwise), and its result is snapshotted in the configured `snapshot-folder`. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for what a test table must satisfy (deterministic `ORDER BY`, predictable columns, the `_`-alias for sort-only columns) and for running tests and snapshots.\n\n## NEXT_BATCH\n\n```sql\nNEXT_BATCH;\n```\n\nFor batch pipelines, use `NEXT_BATCH` to split processing into sequential sub-batches; the next batch runs only when the prior one succeeds. Current SQRL code permits `NEXT_BATCH` only when `enabled-engines` is exactly `[\"flink\"]`.\n\nThe batch allocation only applies to `EXPORT .. TO ..` statements. All interfaces are computed in the last batch.\n\nIn the following example, the `NEXT_BATCH` guarantees that the `PreprocessedData` is written completely to the sink before processing continues in the next sub-batch.\n\n```sql\nPreprocessedData := SELECT ...;\nEXPORT PreprocessedData TO PreprocessorSink;\nNEXT_BATCH;\n...continue processing...\n```\n\n:::warning\nSub-batches are executed stand-alone, meaning each sub-batch reads the data from source and not from the intermediate results of the previous sub-batch.\nIf you wish to start with those, you need to explicitly write them out and read them.\n:::\n\n## Pipeline design rules\n- Do not combine event time with processing time. Prefer event time for consistency and reproducible results. (Exception: file-based sources may use an ingestion-time `now()` column as the watermark \u2014 invoke the `/manage-connector` skill and see Event-Time Processing section.)\n- Use Flink 2.3-compatible SQL syntax and table-valued functions (e.g. for time windows)\n- Execute as much processing as possible on append-only `STREAM` tables\n- Use temporal joins (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) and interval joins when possible\n- Move stateful operations (global aggs, inner joins) to end of pipeline\n- Use Flink time window aggregation when requirements specify time intervals (e.g. \"aggregate x by day\")\n\n```sql\nSensorAvg := SELECT sensorid, window_time AS time_hour,\n  AVG(temperature) AS avg_temp\nFROM TUMBLE(TABLE SensorReading, DESCRIPTOR(event_time), INTERVAL '1' HOUR)\nGROUP BY sensorid, window_start, window_end, window_time;\n```\n\n## Comments & Doc-strings\n\n* `--` single-line comment; write it on its own line, never trailing at the end of a statement\n* `/* ... */` multi-line comment\n* `/** ... */` **doc-string**: attached to the next definition. When DataSQRL generates the GraphQL schema, the doc-string becomes the GraphQL description of the query, mutation, subscription or relationship field, and of the type that the definition creates. For a queryable table, a function or a `CREATE TABLE` mutation, it also becomes the description of its generated REST (OpenAPI) operation and MCP tool. With a custom schema, the descriptions in that schema replace the doc-strings (see `/design-api`, API Descriptions).\n\nHow DataSQRL publishes a doc-string:\n\n* A function or relationship that returns the rows of an existing table (`SELECT * FROM T ...`) uses the type of `T`. Its doc-string describes only the field.\n* A definition without a doc-string takes the doc-string of its base table as its type description. This includes a hidden `_` table.\n* A `CREATE TABLE` doc-string describes the type, the generated query and the mutation.\n* A `# Columns:` section describes the columns of the type that the definition creates. A `# Arguments:` section describes the arguments of a function. Each section has one `- name: text` line for each column or argument. A column note becomes the GraphQL field description and the field description in the REST response schema. An argument note becomes the GraphQL argument description and the MCP parameter description.\n* A line indented under a column describes a field nested in it, to any depth: the fields of a `ROW` column, or of the rows of an `ARRAY<ROW<...>>` column (e.g. `- address: ...` and below it `  - street: ...`).\n\n```sql\n/** A registered person.\n# Columns:\n- email: Email address. Null when the person gave none.\n*/\nPerson := SELECT name, email, age FROM sources.Person;\n\n/** The people of one age, sorted by name.\n# Arguments:\n- age: Age in whole years.\n*/\nPersonByAge(age INT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Person WHERE age = :age ORDER BY name;\n```\n\nWrite each doc-string for the API consumer, who sees only the API, and this includes the doc-string of a `_` table. State what the object is and what it returns: its key, its order, and when the result is null or empty. Describe the behavior the consumer sees, such as \"only the latest version of each order\", and leave out how the pipeline computes it. For a `CREATE TABLE`, describe the record it holds, because the same text describes the query and the mutation.\n\nPut requirement IDs and other maintainer notes in `--` or `/* */` comments.\n\n### Tags in doc-strings\n\nWhen the package configuration defines tags under `compiler.tags`, the `@name` and `@name=value` words in a doc-string are tags that the compiler validates (see the [configuration documentation](https://docs.datasqrl.com/docs/configuration#tags-compilertags)):\n\n*