ssing type, unused arg, unknown type).\n* `DISTINCT` must reference existing columns; `ORDER BY` column(s) must be monotonically increasing.\n* An `ORDER BY` column that does not also appear in the `SELECT` list \u2014 in any table definition, including `/*+test*/` tables (`All sort columns must be part of the SELECT clause`. The `/test-sqrl` skill shows how to sort by a column you do not want in the result).\n* Basetable inference failure for relationships.\n* Malformed or invalid hints (wrong delimiter, other text in the hint block that is not a hint, invalid arguments to a known hint such as `ttl`, or more than one `query_by_*` or `no_query` hint on a table).\n* A column name in a hint that matches no column, or that matches several columns whose names differ only in case (see Hints).\n* Tag violations when `compiler.tags` is configured (see Tags in doc-strings above).\n\nAn unknown hint name is **not** an error: the compiler prints the warning `Unrecognized hint: [<name>]` and drops the hint, so a misspelled hint has no effect. Check the compile output for this warning after adding a hint.\n\nA compile reports all errors of the script at once, each with its position, so fix all of them before you compile again. It leaves out the errors that only follow from another error:\n* A table, column or function whose definition failed is not reported again as not found where it is used. A failed column is recognized by its name, so no table is reported as missing a column of that name until you fix its definition.\n* After an `IMPORT package.*` whose package cannot be loaded, no table or function is reported as not found.\n* The tables imported from a script that fails to parse report only the script's parse errors.\n* A statement that adds a column to, exports or inserts into a table whose definition failed is skipped, as is a passthrough definition (`RETURNS`) whose query reads from it.\n* After a `USE` statement fails, the rest of the script is skipped. In an imported