{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:18:34.889792+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_end", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Implementation Improvement Task\n\nImprove the implementation by fixing the following issues in order of priority:\n- [MEDIUM] Exposed tables/functions LoanPaymentSummary, LoanPayments, and CustomerLoans do not have docstrings (/** ... */) in the SQRL source explaining what they compute and, for the table functions, what arguments are expected (loan_id, and the implicitly injected customer_id from auth). Docstrings are the published API documentation and must be present in code. (Location: loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] Intermediate tables LoanPaymentsSource, LoanPaymentSummarySource, and CustomerLoansSource are marked with /*+no_query */ (i.e. not exposed externally) but do not use internal/hidden names starting with an underscore `_`. They should be renamed to _LoanPaymentsSource, _LoanPaymentSummarySource, _CustomerLoansSource. (Location: loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [MEDIUM] Test tables AutoLoanLeakTest, DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest, and DuplicateLoanSummaryTest do not have a well-defined deterministic ORDER BY. If rows are ever returned, the result order is undefined. Add ORDER BY on the grouping/candidate keys. (Location: loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] Insufficient intent documentation. ZeroPaymentSummaryTest filters loan_id = 'PL-011' without explaining why that fixture is interesting (e.g. zero-payment case). The deduplication strategy (_LatestPersonalLoan / _LatestLoanPayment DISTINCT ON ... ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC) and why the LATEST status per loan/payment is needed is not explained with `--` comments. (Location: loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [MEDIUM] The prod overlay repeats `engines.flink.config.table.exec.source.idle-timeout` with the same value ('30 s') as the shared base, so it is redundant. (Location: loan-payments-prod-package.json) (Verifier: config)\n- [HIGH] Sink column LoanPayments.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink columns LoanPayments.payment_amount_cents, LoanPayments.principal_cents, LoanPayments.interest_cents, LoanPayments.principal_balance_after_cents lack required @npi and @confidential tags propagated from Loan_Payment source columns. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink column LoanPaymentSummary.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink columns LoanPaymentSummary.total_paid_cents, LoanPaymentSummary.principal_paid_cents, LoanPaymentSummary.interest_paid_cents lack required @npi tag propagated from aggregated Loan_Payment source amount columns. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink column CustomerLoans.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink columns CustomerLoans.original_amount_cents, CustomerLoans.current_balance_cents, CustomerLoans.monthly_payment_cents lack required @npi and @confidential tags propagated from Personal_Loan source columns. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] Sink columns CustomerLoans.total_paid_cents, CustomerLoans.principal_paid_cents, CustomerLoans.interest_paid_cents lack required @npi tag propagated from aggregated Loan_Payment source amount columns. (Location: pipeline.sql) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] _AppliedPaymentAgg uses a global, non-windowed GROUP BY aggregation with MAX(applied_date). MAX is a non-incremental aggregation function; on a retract stream this can cause unbounded state growth. Consider using an upstream time-window or aggregating in the database if state can be large. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table CustomerLoansSource has no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table LoanPaymentSummarySource has no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table LoanPaymentsSource has no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _AppliedPaymentAgg has no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _ReturnedPaymentAgg has no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] CustomerLoansSource is a Flink state table with a stateful join between _LatestPersonalLoan and LoanPaymentSummarySource on loan_id; this may accumulate state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentSummarySource is a Flink state table with stateful LEFT JOINs between _LatestPersonalLoan and the payment aggregation tables; this may accumulate state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentsSource is a Flink state table with a stateful join between _LatestPersonalLoanPayment and _LatestPersonalLoan on loan_id; this may accumulate state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _AppliedPaymentAgg is a Flink state table with a high-cardinality GROUP BY loan_id aggregation; this may accumulate state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _ReturnedPaymentAgg is a Flink state table with a high-cardinality GROUP BY loan_id aggregation; this may accumulate state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Stateful joins/aggregations in CustomerLoansSource, LoanPaymentSummarySource, LoanPaymentsSource, _AppliedPaymentAgg, and _ReturnedPaymentAgg could potentially be rewritten with time bounds (interval join, temporal join, or time-window aggregation) to reduce state accumulation, if the original requirements and semantics allow it. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Pipeline has no data quality (DQ_) or observability statistics (OB_) tables, nor tables with equivalent purpose by naming/definition/comments. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] `CustomerLoansSource` has no test coverage, and its downstream `CustomerLoans` function also lacks tests. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Parameterized functions `LoanPaymentSummary` and `LoanPayments` have no tests; their source tables are tested, but the function filters and parameter bindings are not covered. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Source tables `Auto_Loan`, `Loan_Application`, and `Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event` are not used by any tested downstream table or function, so they have no coverage. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] The suite table omits `loan-payments_table_test/prod`, so running `./run-tests.sh --env prod` with no sub-project selection errors out instead of running all available prod suites. (Location: run-tests.sh) (Verifier: tests)\n- [MEDIUM] Internal tables LoanPaymentsSource, LoanPaymentSummarySource, and CustomerLoansSource do not start with an underscore and have no /** ... */ docstring. Even though they carry /*+no_query */, they are SQL-level tables consumers of the codebase will see, and they should be documented (or hidden with an underscore) so their purpose is clear. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [MEDIUM] Test tables with the /*+test */ hint lack a deterministic order: AutoLoanLeakTest, DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest, DuplicateLoanSummaryTest, ZeroPaymentSummaryTest. Add ORDER BY on the relevant key (or document explicitly if a single row is guaranteed) so test output is stable. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] LoanPaymentsSource, LoanPaymentSummarySource, and CustomerLoansSource are intermediate tables (marked /*+no_query */) but do not use the internal/hidden underscore prefix convention (e.g. _LoanPaymentsSource). Rename them to start with `_` so they are consistently treated as internal. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] There is insufficient documentation explaining why the intermediate hops (LoanPaymentsSource, LoanPaymentSummarySource, CustomerLoansSource) are declared with the /*+no_query */ hint, why the LEFT JOIN + COALESCE pattern is used in LoanPaymentSummarySource, and what each /*+test */ table verifies. Add -- comments so the intent is understandable without reverse-engineering the pipeline. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [HIGH] LoanPayments sink exposes customer_id derived from @pii primary_borrower_customer_id without @pii tag. (Location: LoanPayments) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] LoanPayments sink exposes payment_amount_cents, principal_cents, interest_cents, and principal_balance_after_cents derived from @npi @confidential sources without @npi and @confidential tags. (Location: LoanPayments) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] LoanPaymentSummary sink exposes customer_id derived from @pii primary_borrower_customer_id without @pii tag. (Location: LoanPaymentSummary) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] LoanPaymentSummary sink exposes total_paid_cents, principal_paid_cents, and interest_paid_cents derived from @npi @confidential sources without @npi and @confidential tags; aggregation by loan_id does not declassify these tags. (Location: LoanPaymentSummary) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] CustomerLoans sink exposes customer_id derived from @pii primary_borrower_customer_id without @pii tag. (Location: CustomerLoans) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] CustomerLoans sink exposes original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents, monthly_payment_cents, total_paid_cents, principal_paid_cents, and interest_paid_cents derived from @npi @confidential sources without @npi and @confidential tags. (Location: CustomerLoans) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] _AppliedPaymentAgg is a stateful, non-windowed GROUP BY aggregation in Flink that uses MAX(applied_date), a non-incremental aggregation function. On a retract stream this causes unbounded state growth because Flink must retain all prior records to correctly recompute the running maximum. Recommend an upstream time-window aggregation or delegating the MAX computation to the database if the state can grow large. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] CustomerLoansSource is a `state` table in Flink with no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). Any table with Type in [state, stream, versioned_state] must expose a valid (event) timestamp. If time-windows were used, users would need to explicitly select and group by window_time. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentSummarySource is a `state` table in Flink with no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentsSource is a `state` table in Flink with no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _AppliedPaymentAgg is a `state` table in Flink with no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _ReturnedPaymentAgg is a `state` table in Flink with no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] CustomerLoansSource is a `state` table computed in Flink and contains a stateful join (JOIN on loan_id between _LatestPersonalLoan and LoanPaymentSummarySource without any temporal or interval constraint). This may accumulate unbounded state over time. No mostRecentDistinct annotation is present. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentSummarySource is a `state` table computed in Flink and contains stateful LEFT JOINs on loan_id against _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg without temporal or interval constraints. This may accumulate unbounded state over time. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] LoanPaymentsSource is a `state` table computed in Flink and contains a stateful join (JOIN _LatestPersonalLoanPayment p JOIN _LatestPersonalLoan l ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id) with no temporal or interval constraint. This may accumulate unbounded state over time. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _AppliedPaymentAgg is a `state` table computed in Flink using a high-cardinality non-windowed GROUP BY on loan_id (~2e6 distinct keys), which may accumulate significant state over time. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _ReturnedPaymentAgg is a `state` table computed in Flink using a high-cardinality non-windowed GROUP BY on loan_id (~2e6 distinct keys), which may accumulate significant state over time. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] CustomerLoansSource's stateful join on loan_id between loan data and payment summary could be rewritten as a temporal join (FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF) if the semantics permit, avoiding unbounded state accumulation. This is a potential performance improvement only if the original requirements and semantics allow it. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] LoanPaymentSummarySource's stateful LEFT JOINs against _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg could be rewritten with time-bounds (interval/temporal joins) if semantics permit, avoiding state accumulation. Only applicable if requirements allow it. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] LoanPaymentsSource's stateful join between payments and loans could be rewritten as a temporal join if the semantics permit, avoiding unbounded state accumulation. Only applicable if requirements allow it. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] _AppliedPaymentAgg's non-windowed GROUP BY aggregation could be rewritten as a time-windowed aggregation (e.g. SESSION/TUMBLE over ingested_at) if the semantics permit, reducing state growth. Only applicable if requirements allow it. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] _ReturnedPaymentAgg's non-windowed GROUP BY aggregation could be rewritten as a time-windowed aggregation if the semantics permit, reducing state growth. Only applicable if requirements allow it. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] The pipeline contains no data-quality tables (DQ_ prefix or equivalent) and no observability/statistics tables (OB_ prefix or equivalent). Adding such tables would improve data quality monitoring and pipeline observability. (Location: cons_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] CustomerLoansSource table definition has no test coverage and no downstream tables or functions that are tested; add a /*+test */ snapshot or no_rows test for CustomerLoansSource. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] CustomerLoans API query (which filters CustomerLoansSource by customer_id) has no test coverage; no GraphQL test query or downstream consumer exercises it. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] LoanPayments API query definition itself is untested; only its underlying LoanPaymentsSource is covered, so filter/order semantics of the query are not verified. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] LoanPaymentSummary API query definition itself is untested; only its underlying LoanPaymentSummarySource is covered, so the loan_id/customer_id filter is not verified. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] The source tables Auto_Loan, Loan_Application and Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event have no dedicated tests and no tested downstream consumers; the AutoLoanLeakTest only asserts absence of auto loans inside LoanPaymentsSource (which already filters loan_type='PERSONAL') and does not exercise Auto_Loan directly. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Runner performs no setup hook to generate or download the fixtures referenced by ${DATA_PATH} (consumer_loans-*.jsonl); the source connectors require these files, so a clean checkout cannot reproduce the test run. Add a guarded setup step (e.g. scripts/generate-testdata.sh under the $LIST guard). (Location: run-tests.sh) (Verifier: tests)\n\nFixing an issue means one of the following:\n1. Updating the implementation to implement a solution that fixes the issue. Use suggested solutions as a starting point.\n2. Adding documentation to the source code or README.md to explain why an identified issue does not apply, because:\n   1. You can deduce from the pipeline constraints or data semantics that the issue cannot arise (e.g. documenting that an aggregation `GROUP BY tenantId` does not result in unbounded state growth because we expect only 100s of tenants).\n   2. It's a known issue that won't be fixed (e.g. mark as `TODO` because it is not a priority right now or the risk is deemed low).\n\n**IMPORTANT**:\nOnly fix issues that are relevant to this implementation and compatible with the user requirements.\nUser requirements always take priority - do not implement fixes that contradict requirements. Add documentation when identified issues conflict with user requirements.\n\n**An issue on a file your plan did not ask you to touch is out of scope \u2014 leave it alone (option 3).**\nRewriting pre-existing, working code that you were not asked to change is unnecessary if it is not critical.\n\n**If you rename a table during refinement**\n- Update every reference to the old name across all `.sqrl` files (definitions, SELECT statements, test tables).\n- Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and run tests. Ensure tests pass.\n\n## Implementation\n\nMake sure to run tests and improve the codebase until tests pass. Run the project's test runner script, `./run-tests.sh`, scoped to the sub-projects your fixes touched: before running it, list every sub-project you edited plus every sub-project affected by an edit to shared code or configuration, and pass exactly those names as arguments. If a fix adds or renames a sub-project, add or update its suite line in `run-tests.sh`. For the details of the tests and the runner script, invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill.\n\nRead AGENTS.md for detailed DataSQRL syntax, patterns, and available tools!\n\n## Response\n\nWhen the fixes are done and the tests you ran pass, your final response must be ONLY the `./run-tests.sh` invocation you ran successfully, on one line, with the sub-project names you scoped it to (no arguments when you ran everything). No markdown, no explanation.\n\nCorrect response example:\n```\n./run-tests.sh [affected-sub-project-names] # only the affected sub-project(s) \n```\nand after a fix to shared code or configuration:\n```\n./run-tests.sh  # everything\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795511631558}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me work through the issues. Many are duplicates from two verifiers (both referring to `loans.sqrl`, `consumer_loans.sqrl`, `cons_loans.sqrl`, and `pipeline.sql`). Let me categorize the actual fixes to make in our project files (`loan-payments.sqrl`, configs, `run-tests.sh`):\n\n1. **Docstrings on exposed functions LoanPaymentSummary, LoanPayments, CustomerLoans** \u2014 add `/** ... */` with `# Arguments:` for loan_id and customer_id claim.\n2. **Rename intermediate tables** `LoanPaymentsSource` \u2192 `_LoanPaymentsSource`, etc. \u2014 update all references.\n3. **Test tables deterministic ORDER BY** for AutoLoanLeakTest, DuplicateLoanPaymentsTest, DuplicateLoanSummaryTest, ZeroPaymentSummaryTest.\n4. **Intent documentation** \u2014 comments about DISTINCT dedup and why latest status, and ZeroPaymentSummaryTest fixture.\n5. **Prod overlay redundant flink idle-timeout** \u2014 remove from prod.\n6. **@pii / @npi / @confidential tags** \u2014 The catalog uses tags in docstrings. Do we have `compiler.tags` configured? No. Tags are metadata in docstrings; the verifier (pipeline) is checking that sink columns carry tags propagated from sources. Hmm. This requires adding `compiler.tags` config to enable tag validation, and using `declassify`? Wait. Let me think.\n\nActually, the issue says sink columns \"lack required @pii tag propagated from source ... primary_borrower_customer_id\". This is a tag validation feature. The reduced scope: We need to add tags to the SQRL docstrings? But tags in docstrings apply to tables, not columns? Let me re-read the configure-sqrl tags section: \"When `compiler.tags` is present, the compiler validates the `@name` and `@name=value` tags in doc strings and propagates them from the tables a query reads to the table it defines.\"\n\nHmm. And the implement-sqrl \"Tags in doc-strings\" section. Let me read that part. Column-level tags? The source schema column comments have `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential` in the comments (e.g. `* primary_borrower_customer_id: Reference to primary borrower @pii`). These are column-level tags in the catalog's docstrings. When we build derived tables, the compiler checks that the derived columns preserve required tags, and `declassify` hints can remove them.\n\nSo the verifier wants us to enable `compiler.tags` and ensure the derived sink columns carry the tags (or are properly declassified). But wait, the tags are column-level in the catalog. How does the compiler know? It parses the catalog's docstrings for tags. Let me read the implement-sqrl tags section (lines 644+) and maybe a tag reference file.\n\nLet me read the rest of implement-sqrl.md (lines 644-762) which likely covers Comments & Doc-strings and Tags.\n\nAlso note the verifier says \"Location: pipeline.sql\" \u2014 that's `build/.../pipeline.sql`? It's the compiled SQL, so the issue is about tag propagation in the pipeline. Since the pipeline currently has no `compiler.tags` config, no validation happens. But the verifier may be checking the catalog tags and expecting our sink tables to propagate them. Hmm.\n\nActually the task says \"Sink column LoanPayments.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source...\". This implies the project should have tags configured. Let me read the tags documentation.\n\nLet me read implement-sqrl lines 644-762.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I'll work through the fixes. First, let me read the remaining implement-sqrl docs on tags/docstrings and check the current plan/scope."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd550bc7100af7ac0a4", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md", "offset": 644}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 4540, "output": 901, "cacheRead": 190208, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 802, "totalTokens": 195649, "cost": {"input": 0.001362, "output": 0.0010812, "cacheRead": 0.001141248, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.003584448}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511631566, "responseId": "5a0b65fa63b54208a0b0afeb49967c43-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 4740, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd550bc7100af7ac0a4", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "### Testing\n\nA `/*+test */` hint in front of a table definition marks a test case: the table is only executed by the `test` command (ignored otherwise), and its result is snapshotted in the configured `snapshot-folder`. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for what a test table must satisfy (deterministic `ORDER BY`, predictable columns, the `_`-alias for sort-only columns) and for running tests and snapshots.\n\n## NEXT_BATCH\n\n```sql\nNEXT_BATCH;\n```\n\nFor batch pipelines, use `NEXT_BATCH` to split processing into sequential sub-batches; the next batch runs only when the prior one succeeds. Current SQRL code permits `NEXT_BATCH` only when `enabled-engines` is exactly `[\"flink\"]`.\n\nThe batch allocation only applies to `EXPORT .. TO ..` statements. All interfaces are computed in the last batch.\n\nIn the following example, the `NEXT_BATCH` guarantees that the `PreprocessedData` is written completely to the sink before processing continues in the next sub-batch.\n\n```sql\nPreprocessedData := SELECT ...;\nEXPORT PreprocessedData TO PreprocessorSink;\nNEXT_BATCH;\n...continue processing...\n```\n\n:::warning\nSub-batches are executed stand-alone, meaning each sub-batch reads the data from source and not from the intermediate results of the previous sub-batch.\nIf you wish to start with those, you need to explicitly write them out and read them.\n:::\n\n## Pipeline design rules\n- Do not combine event time with processing time. Prefer event time for consistency and reproducible results. (Exception: file-based sources may use an ingestion-time `now()` column as the watermark \u2014 invoke the `/manage-connector` skill and see Event-Time Processing section.)\n- Use Flink 2.3-compatible SQL syntax and table-valued functions (e.g. for time windows)\n- Execute as much processing as possible on append-only `STREAM` tables\n- Use temporal joins (`FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF`) and interval joins when possible\n- Move stateful operations (global aggs, inner joins) to end of pipeline\n- Use Flink time window aggregation when requirements specify time intervals (e.g. \"aggregate x by day\")\n\n```sql\nSensorAvg := SELECT sensorid, window_time AS time_hour,\n  AVG(temperature) AS avg_temp\nFROM TUMBLE(TABLE SensorReading, DESCRIPTOR(event_time), INTERVAL '1' HOUR)\nGROUP BY sensorid, window_start, window_end, window_time;\n```\n\n## Comments & Doc-strings\n\n* `--` single-line comment; write it on its own line, never trailing at the end of a statement\n* `/* ... */` multi-line comment\n* `/** ... */` **doc-string**: attached to the next definition. When DataSQRL generates the GraphQL schema, the doc-string becomes the GraphQL description of the query, mutation, subscription or relationship field, and of the type that the definition creates. For a queryable table, a function or a `CREATE TABLE` mutation, it also becomes the description of its generated REST (OpenAPI) operation and MCP tool. With a custom schema, the descriptions in that schema replace the doc-strings (see `/design-api`, API Descriptions).\n\nHow DataSQRL publishes a doc-string:\n\n* A function or relationship that returns the rows of an existing table (`SELECT * FROM T ...`) uses the type of `T`. Its doc-string describes only the field.\n* A definition without a doc-string takes the doc-string of its base table as its type description. This includes a hidden `_` table.\n* A `CREATE TABLE` doc-string describes the type, the generated query and the mutation.\n* A `# Columns:` section describes the columns of the type that the definition creates. A `# Arguments:` section describes the arguments of a function. Each section has one `- name: text` line for each column or argument. A column note becomes the GraphQL field description and the field description in the REST response schema. An argument note becomes the GraphQL argument description and the MCP parameter description.\n* A line indented under a column describes a field nested in it, to any depth: the fields of a `ROW` column, or of the rows of an `ARRAY<ROW<...>>` column (e.g. `- address: ...` and below it `  - street: ...`).\n\n```sql\n/** A registered person.\n# Columns:\n- email: Email address. Null when the person gave none.\n*/\nPerson := SELECT name, email, age FROM sources.Person;\n\n/** The people of one age, sorted by name.\n# Arguments:\n- age: Age in whole years.\n*/\nPersonByAge(age INT NOT NULL) := SELECT * FROM Person WHERE age = :age ORDER BY name;\n```\n\nWrite each doc-string for the API consumer, who sees only the API, and this includes the doc-string of a `_` table. State what the object is and what it returns: its key, its order, and when the result is null or empty. Describe the behavior the consumer sees, such as \"only the latest version of each order\", and leave out how the pipeline computes it. For a `CREATE TABLE`, describe the record it holds, because the same text describes the query and the mutation.\n\nPut requirement IDs and other maintainer notes in `--` or `/* */` comments.\n\n### Tags in doc-strings\n\nWhen the package configuration defines tags under `compiler.tags`, the `@name` and `@name=value` words in a doc-string are tags that the compiler validates (see the [configuration documentation](https://docs.datasqrl.com/docs/configuration#tags-compilertags)):\n\n* Use only the tags and values defined in `compiler.tags`. A boolean tag is written `@name`; an ordered tag needs a value, e.g. `@pii=raw`.\n* A table inherits the tags of every table it reads, including joined and subquery tables. A public table or function must repeat each inherited tag in its doc-string with the same or a higher value, or the compile fails. Copy the tags from the compile error's `inherited from [...]` tables. Hidden tables, relationships and test tables carry their inherited tags without declaring them.\n* Tags in `# Columns:` notes, including nested ones, count toward the whole table.\n* A tag with `required-columns` in `compiler.tags` requires those columns, with a compatible type, in every table that carries the tag, including hidden tables and tables that are only written into. Keep the column in the `SELECT`, or declassify the tag when the requirements allow it.\n* A `CREATE TABLE` that `INSERT INTO` or `EXPORT` writes into must declare the tags of the written data when other tables read it or it is an internal table with an `engine` hint (e.g. an Iceberg or Kafka table in a separate script).\n* To drop or lower an inherited tag, e.g. for an aggregate that no longer contains personal data, add `/*+declassify(tag, ...) */` and declare the lower value or none. Only declassify when the requirements say the data allows it.\n\n## Validation rules\n\nThe following produce compile time errors:\n\n* Reserved SQL keywords used as identifiers without escaping as column names (`value`, `timestamp`, `time`, `date`, `upper`, `lower`, `from`) or as table names (`Order`, `Group`, `Value`, `Key`, `Table`, `Index`, `Row`, `User`, `Transaction`, `Schema`). Use descriptive alternatives instead: `event_value`, `event_timestamp`, `upper_bound`; `ClinicalOrder`, `PatientGroup`, `AccountTransaction`.\n* Duplicate identifiers (tables, functions, relationships).\n* Overloaded functions (same name, different arg list) are **not** allowed.\n* Argument list problems (missing type, unused arg, unknown type).\n* `DISTINCT` must reference existing columns; `ORDER BY` column(s) must be monotonically increasing.\n* An `ORDER BY` column that does not also appear in the `SELECT` list \u2014 in any table definition, including `/*+test*/` tables (`All sort columns must be part of the SELECT clause`. The `/test-sqrl` skill shows how to sort by a column you do not want in the result).\n* Basetable inference failure for relationships.\n* Malformed or invalid hints (wrong delimiter, other text in the hint block that is not a hint, invalid arguments to a known hint such as `ttl`, or more than one `query_by_*` or `no_query` hint on a table).\n* A column name in a hint that matches no column, or that matches several columns whose names differ only in case (see Hints).\n* Tag violations when `compiler.tags` is configured (see Tags in doc-strings above).\n\nAn unknown hint name is **not** an error: the compiler prints the warning `Unrecognized hint: [<name>]` and drops the hint, so a misspelled hint has no effect. Check the compile output for this warning after adding a hint.\n\nA compile reports all errors of the script at once, each with its position, so fix all of them before you compile again. It leaves out the errors that only follow from another error:\n* A table, column or function whose definition failed is not reported again as not found where it is used. A failed column is recognized by its name, so no table is reported as missing a column of that name until you fix its definition.\n* After an `IMPORT package.*` whose package cannot be loaded, no table or function is reported as not found.\n* The tables imported from a script that fails to parse report only the script's parse errors.\n* A statement that adds a column to, exports or inserts into a table whose definition failed is skipped, as is a passthrough definition (`RETURNS`) whose query reads from it.\n* After a `USE` statement fails, the rest of the script is skipped. In an imported script, that is the rest of that script, and of the importing script too if the script is imported inline (`IMPORT package.script.*`).\n\nSo a fix can bring up new errors in the statements that were skipped or used the failed object. Compile again until no errors are left.\n\n## Additional pattern guides\nFor additional patterns, read these guides:\n* [Stream Enrichment](guides/stream-enrichment.md): How to enrich a data stream with dimensional information or state consistently, and how to carry the whole dimension row as one nested object.\n* [Config Templating](guides/templating.md): How to use variables in SQRL scripts for templating that gets substituted at compile time with mustache engine.\n* [Indexes for API Queries](guides/api-query-indexes.md): How the compiler selects indexes, and how to check and fix the indexes of a slow API query. Read it only in the cases that Indexes for API queries names.\n* [SESSION Windows](guides/session-tvf.md): The `SESSION` TVF for window aggregation has limitations and quirks. Always read this guide before implementing `SESSION` windows.\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 7, "timestamp": 1795511636317}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I understand tags: to enable tag validation we must configure `compiler.tags`. The catalog column docstrings have `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential`. If we add `compiler.tags` config, the compiler will validate tags and require public tables to repeat inherited tags. This is a HIGH priority issue (multiple). The verifier explicitly says sink columns lack tags. So I should add `compiler.tags` with these tags and then add the tags to the docstrings of our public sink tables, or declassify where appropriate.\n\nLet me check how the catalog defines tags. Let me look at data-catalog README or ontology.sqrl for a `compiler.tags` config? The catalog package doesn't have compiler.tags. Hmm. But the tags are in docstrings as `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential`. The verifier's check is likely a static/heuristic check of compiled pipeline.sql for tag propagation. It mentions \"Location: pipeline.sql\" and \"must ... propagated from source\". \n\nActually, the DataSQRL compiler tracks column-level tags only when `compiler.tags` is configured. Without it, \"doc strings are plain text\". So to satisfy the verifier's requirement, we probably need to add `compiler.tags` and declare tags with `required-columns`? Hmm, but `required-columns` is a different mechanism.\n\nWait, re-read: \"Tags in `# Columns:` notes, including nested ones, count toward the whole table.\" So column tags attach to the table. When a derived table reads a tagged table, it inherits the tags. Public tables must repeat them.\n\nBut the issue mentions specific sink *columns* lacking tags. This suggests the verifier inspects the docstrings/comments in the compiled pipeline SQL for column-level tags. The `# Columns:` section in our docstrings could carry per-column tags.\n\nLet me think about what the verifier checks. It says \"Sink column LoanPayments.customer_id lacks required @pii tag propagated from source Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id.\" This is a column-level inheritance. In DataSQRL, tags are table-level but can be declared in `# Columns:` notes. The compiler propagates table-level tags? Actually \"A table inherits the tags of every table it reads\" \u2014 table-level. Column tags \"count toward the whole table\", meaning if any column has `@pii`, the table gets `@pii`. Hmm, that's table-level, not column-level.\n\nBut the verifier references specific columns. Perhaps the DataSQRL tag model is column-scoped in the compiled pipeline (`@pii` on the column in pipeline.sql). Let me check the actual docs. Let me look at the configuration docs for tags. There might be more detail in the configure-sqrl skill (already read the tags section): \"Each key is a tag name... `required-columns` maps each column that every table carrying the tag must have to its type.\" This is table-level.\n\nHmm. The verifier likely uses a heuristic. Given the instructions say \"Fixing an issue means one of: updating implementation ... or adding documentation ... explain why it does not apply\". For the tag issues, I think the intended fix is to add `compiler.tags` config and `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential` tags to the docstrings (and possibly declassify). Let me check the catalog README for how tags are defined, and whether there's a shared tag config.\n\nLet me search the repo for `compiler.tags` or `@pii`.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd5637b7800b988a015", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "grep -rn \"compiler\" /workspace/data-catalog/*.json; echo \"=== tags in catalog README ===\"; grep -n -i \"tag\\|pii\\|npi\\|confidential\\|declassify\" /workspace/data-catalog/README.md | head -40; echo \"=== compiler.tags anywhere ===\"; grep -rn \"tags\" /workspace --include=\"*.json\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v build | head; echo \"=== declassify ===\"; grep -rn \"declassify\" /workspace /opt/datasqrl-examples --include=\"*.sqrl\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v build | head"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 2450, "output": 955, "cacheRead": 195584, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 769, "totalTokens": 198989, "cost": {"input": 0.000735, "output": 0.0011459999999999999, "cacheRead": 0.001173504, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0030545039999999996}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511636324, "responseId": "530c0278efbb413e9301d2fedd7fbc74-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 4958, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd5637b7800b988a015", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/workspace/data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json:15:  \"compiler\": {\n=== tags in catalog README ===\n21:| **Confidential** | Disclosure causes material harm or competitive damage |\n22:| **Restricted** | Disclosure causes regulatory, legal, or significant customer harm (PII, NPI, PCI) |\n24:## Sensitivity Tags\n26:| Tag | Category |\n28:| `@pii` | Personally Identifiable Information (name, address, phone, email, DOB) |\n29:| `@spii` | Sensitive PII (SSN, passport, driver's license, biometrics) |\n30:| `@npi` | GLBA Non-public Personal Information (account numbers, balances, transactions) |\n32:| `@confidential` | Internal sensitive but not personal (risk scores, pricing models) |\n34:## Regulatory Scope Tags\n38:## Handling Tags\n40:| Tag | Required Handling |\n53:  declaration order, with its sensitivity tags (`@pii`, `@npi`, `@pci`, `@confidential`, \u2026). The\n=== compiler.tags anywhere ===\n=== declassify ===\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 100, "timestamp": 1795511641384}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Let me read the data-catalog README's tags section and the \"Handling Tags\" to understand the tag semantics and whether a `compiler.tags` config is expected. Also check the catalog's `ontology-shared-package.json` compiler section.\n\nActually, the catalog does not configure `compiler.tags`. The data catalog just documents tags in comments. The verifier's \"pipeline.sql\" location suggests it parsed the catalog source docstrings and the generated pipeline. Let me look at the actual build output to see if tags appear anywhere.\n\nLet me grep the build pipeline for @pii etc. Actually, let me read the data-catalog README tag section fully.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd5706c7291bc270de9", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "sed -n '1,80p' /workspace/data-catalog/README.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd570a37971b0f1a018", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "grep -rn \"@pii\\|@npi\\|@confidential\\|declassify\" /workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"=== catalog tags ===\"; grep -rn \"Tags:\\|@pii\\|@npi\" /workspace/data-catalog/ontology.sqrl | head -20"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 333, "output": 299, "cacheRead": 198912, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 140, "totalTokens": 199544, "cost": {"input": 9.989999999999999e-05, "output": 0.0003588, "cacheRead": 0.001193472, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001652172}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511641386, "responseId": "68affc6c00a64c2aa8f4d7bc6c8263dd-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 3231, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd5706c7291bc270de9", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Retail Bank Data Catalog\n\nA data catalog covering the **bronze** (source) and **silver** (enriched/conformed) layers of a retail bank's data mesh. Tables are documented as SQRL `CREATE TABLE` definitions using Flink SQL syntax, organized by line of business and team.\n\n## Catalog Index\n\n| Line of Business | Team | Folder | Key Datasets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Customer | Customer Data | [customer/customer_data/](customer/customer_data/) | Customer master, KYC/AML, Customer profiles |\n| Deposits & Payments | Accounts | [deposits_payments/accounts/](deposits_payments/accounts/) | Deposit accounts, Balances, Account analytics |\n| Deposits & Payments | Transactions | [deposits_payments/transactions/](deposits_payments/transactions/) | Core transactions, Card/Wire/ACH, Enriched transactions |\n| Lending | Mortgages | [lending/mortgages/](lending/mortgages/) | Originations, Servicing, Performance analytics |\n| Lending | Cards & Consumer Credit | [lending/cards_consumer_credit/](lending/cards_consumer_credit/) | Credit cards, Consumer loans, Credit risk signals |\n\n## Classification Levels\n\n| Level | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Public** | Disclosed publicly or intended for public use |\n| **Internal** | Internal-by-default, low harm if disclosed |\n| **Confidential** | Disclosure causes material harm or competitive damage |\n| **Restricted** | Disclosure causes regulatory, legal, or significant customer harm (PII, NPI, PCI) |\n\n## Sensitivity Tags\n\n| Tag | Category |\n|---|---|\n| `@pii` | Personally Identifiable Information (name, address, phone, email, DOB) |\n| `@spii` | Sensitive PII (SSN, passport, driver's license, biometrics) |\n| `@npi` | GLBA Non-public Personal Information (account numbers, balances, transactions) |\n| `@pci` | Payment Card Industry data (PAN, expiration, cardholder name) |\n| `@confidential` | Internal sensitive but not personal (risk scores, pricing models) |\n\n## Regulatory Scope Tags\n\n`@glba` (Gramm-Leach-Bliley) | `@gdpr` (EU GDPR) | `@ccpa` (California CCPA/CPRA) | `@pci-dss` (PCI DSS) | `@bsa` (Bank Secrecy Act) | `@sox` (Sarbanes-Oxley)\n\n## Handling Tags\n\n| Tag | Required Handling |\n|---|---|\n| `@encrypt` | Encrypted at rest using platform key management |\n| `@tokenize` | Original value replaced by token; raw value in vault only |\n| `@mask` | Display masked outside production and to non-privileged consumers |\n| `@erasable` | Subject to right-to-erasure (GDPR Art. 17, CCPA delete) |\n\n## File Structure\n\nEach dataset is split into a schema file and one connector file per environment:\n- **`{dataset}.sqrl`** \u2014 header comment with layer, classification, and regulatory scope, followed\n  by `_schema`-suffixed `CREATE TABLE` statements. Each table carries a single table-level\n  doc-string that states its grain and a `# Columns:` section documenting every column in\n  declaration order, with its sensitivity tags (`@pii`, `@npi`, `@pci`, `@confidential`, \u2026). The\n  `CREATE TABLE` body itself holds only bare `column TYPE [NOT NULL]` definitions \u2014 no inline\n  column comments.\n- **`{dataset}-test.sqrl`** and **`{dataset}-prod.sqrl`** \u2014 one connector file per environment,\n  each importing the schema file and extending its tables with `LIKE`. The connector table owns\n  the environment-specific pieces the schema table does not: the watermark, the ingestion column\n  (`ingested_at` for bronze sources, `computed_at` for silver), and the `WITH (...)` connector\n  configuration (`filesystem` in test, `kafka-safe`/`iceberg` in prod).\n- **`testdata/`** \u2014 one `.jsonl` file per table, named `{dataset}-{table}.jsonl`, read by the\n  `-test.sqrl` connectors.\n\nBronze tables carry the audit columns `source_system` and `source_updated_at` in their schema, and\n`ingested_at` in their connector table. Silver tables carry `computed_at` in their connector table\ninstead.\n\n**Watermarks are intentionally asymmetric between environments.** Every bronze `-test.sqrl`\nwatermarks its ingestion column 10 seconds back; every bronze `-prod.sqrl` watermarks the same\ncolumn 30 seconds back. This is not a mismatch to reconcile \u2014 test data is small, ordered and\nreplayed once, so a short offset is enough to close out windows quickly, while production Kafka\ndelivery is genuinely out of order and needs the wider tolerance. What must match between the two\n(and does, everywhere in this catalog) is the watermark **column name**, so a table's event-time\nsemantics don't change shape across environments. Likewise, a bronze test connector declares its\n`ingested_at` as a plain physical `TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3)` column while the prod connector sources the\nsame-named column via Kafka `METADATA FROM 'timestamp'` \u2014 the filesystem connector used in test has\nno record metadata to read, so a physical column populated by the test data is the closest\nequivalent. Silver connector tables carry `computed_at` with **no watermark** in either environment,\nbecause they are recomputed upsert snapshots (keyed on the table's grain), not event streams.\n\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 8, "timestamp": 1795511644759}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd570a37971b0f1a018", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:27:    \"description\" : \"Payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:31:    \"description\" : \"Principal portion in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:35:    \"description\" : \"Interest portion in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:71:    \"description\" : \"Principal balance after payment in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:133:    \"description\" : \"Reference to primary borrower @pii\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:137:    \"description\" : \"Loan number displayed to customer @npi @mask\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:149:    \"description\" : \"Original loan amount in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:153:    \"description\" : \"Current principal balance in cents @npi @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:157:    \"description\" : \"Interest rate (APR) @confidential\"\n/workspace/loan-payments/build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json:173:    \"description\" : \"Monthly payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\"\n=== catalog tags ===\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 140, "timestamp": 1795511644759}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The `pipeline_explain.json` carries column `description` fields with the tags from the catalog schema. The verifier inspects the sink columns' descriptions/tags in the compiled pipeline and checks that derived sinks propagate the source tags. This is a heuristic independent of `compiler.tags`.\n\nSo the fix: add `# Columns:` sections with `@pii`, `@npi`, `@confidential` tags to the docstrings of our public sink tables/functions so the sink column descriptions carry the tags. But wait\u2014the compiler's tag validation only activates with `compiler.tags`. Without it, the docstring text is just a description. The verifier apparently wants the tags present in descriptions. To be safe, I should:\n1. Add `compiler.tags` config defining these tags. This enables validation and propagation.\n2. Add the tags in the `# Columns:` docstring sections of our public tables/functions.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: a public table must repeat each inherited tag. If we configure `compiler.tags`, the compiler will enforce. Tags may be column-level. Also `required-columns` isn't needed.\n\nActually, the tags are on the *source schema* tables (Personal_Loan_schema, Loan_Payment_schema). Those are hidden? They're `CREATE TABLE` schemas. When we read `cons_loans.Personal_Loan` (the connector table with LIKE), does it inherit the schema's column tags? The pipeline_explain.json shows the connector columns have descriptions with tags. So yes, the column descriptions propagate.\n\nNow, if I configure `compiler.tags`, the compiler will propagate tags from `Personal_Loan`/`Loan_Payment` to our derived tables. Since our derived tables are public, we must declare the tags. Let me look at what tags are inherited:\n- customer_id \u2190 primary_borrower_customer_id @pii\n- payment_amount_cents @npi @confidential\n- principal_cents @npi @confidential\n- interest_cents @npi @confidential\n- principal_balance_after_cents @npi @confidential\n- loan_purpose? The source column `loan_purpose` has no tag. \n- late_fee_cents: \"Late fee amount in cents\" no tags.\n- other_fees_cents: no tags.\n- original_amount_cents @npi @confidential\n- current_balance_cents @npi @confidential\n- monthly_payment_cents @npi @confidential\n- interest_rate @confidential (not exposed)\n\nNow the tags are attached to the *table* when declared in `# Columns:` notes: \"Tags in `# Columns:` notes, including nested ones, count toward the whole table.\" Hmm, that says column tags count toward the whole table, i.e., they become table-level tags. Then a public table needs to repeat the table-level tags. But the issue talks about sink *columns*. \n\nMaybe the DataSQRL implementation is more granular. Let me check how the compiler handles tags. Let me look at the config docs online? Not available. Let me search the jar? Hard.\n\nAlternative: The verifier likely parses the `pipeline_explain.json`/`pipeline.sql` generated by the compiler and checks per-column `description` for tags matching the source. If I add `# Columns:` sections to the docstrings of our public definitions with the same tags on the corresponding columns, the generated descriptions would carry them. Let me verify by adding and compiling.\n\nBut do tags propagate through aggregation? The issue says \"aggregation by loan_id does not declassify these tags\", meaning the aggregation output columns derived from @npi sources must also carry @npi. If I explicitly add `@npi` to `total_paid_cents` in the summary docstring, the verifier should see it.\n\nLet me think about whether configuring `compiler.tags` is necessary or risky. If I add `compiler.tags` and don't declassify correctly, the compile may fail. But the instructions want the fix. Let me first check the exact tag semantics by reading the configuration docs in the jar? Or maybe there's a docs folder. Let me search for \"declassify\" in the docs directory of pi or the DataSQRL docs.\n\nActually, let me look for tag config examples in /opt/datasqrl-examples. None found. Let me search the sqrl jar's resources? That's a 700MB jar; grep might be slow but possible with unzip -l? Let me search for a tag test in the examples/docs.\n\nHmm. Let me instead try adding `compiler.tags` and tags to our public tables and compile. If errors, I'll learn the exact semantics.\n\nBut wait\u2014the verifier's `pipeline.sql` location suggests it's checking generated SQL comments/descriptions. The pipeline_explain.json is where descriptions live. Let me look at our current generated pipeline_explain.json for our sink tables to see what descriptions currently show.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd5827d7972be92349a", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json'))\n# print structure\nprint(type(d), list(d.keys())[:10] if isinstance(d,dict) else len(d))\n\" 2>/dev/null; echo \"---- grep our sink tables ----\"; grep -n \"customer_id\\|total_paid_cents\\|original_amount_cents\\|customerType\\|description\" build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json | head -5"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd5833f74329b546c94", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && grep -n \"_AppliedPaymentAgg\\|LoanPaymentSummarySource\\|CustomerLoansSource\\|LoanPaymentsSource\" build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.txt | head; echo \"=== timestamps ===\"; grep -n \"Timestamp\\|Type:\" build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.txt | head -60"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 1766, "output": 1313, "cacheRead": 199424, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 1061, "totalTokens": 202503, "cost": {"input": 0.0005298, "output": 0.0015756, "cacheRead": 0.001196544, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.003301944}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511644762, "responseId": "79306d0afde143d28dbcbeececb139cc-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 4521, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd5827d7972be92349a", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "<class 'list'> 15\n---- grep our sink tables ----\n15:    \"description\" : \"Unique identifier for the payment\"\n19:    \"description\" : \"Reference to loan\"\n23:    \"description\" : \"Loan type: PERSONAL, AUTO\"\n27:    \"description\" : \"Payment amount in cents @npi @confidential\"\n31:    \"description\" : \"Principal portion in cents @npi @confidential\"\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 34, "timestamp": 1795511649319}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd5833f74329b546c94", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "80: - default_catalog.default_database.CustomerLoansSource\n83: - base-table: CustomerLoansSource\n85:=== CustomerLoansSource\n86:ID:          default_catalog.default_database.CustomerLoansSource\n110: - default_catalog.default_database.LoanPaymentSummarySource\n113:Table CustomerLoansSource (postgres):\n115: - index CustomerLoansSource_hash_c1 (hash): customer_id\n123: - default_catalog.default_database.LoanPaymentSummarySource\n126: - base-table: LoanPaymentSummarySource\n128:=== LoanPaymentSummarySource\n=== timestamps ===\n3:Type:        stream\n6:Timestamp:   ingested_at\n36:Type:        stream\n39:Timestamp:   ingested_at\n76:Type:        query\n87:Type:        state\n90:Timestamp:   -\n119:Type:        query\n130:Type:        state\n133:Timestamp:   -\n157:Type:        query\n168:Type:        state\n171:Timestamp:   -\n204:Type:        state\n207:Timestamp:   -\n223:Type:        state\n226:Timestamp:   ingested_at\n256:Type:        state\n259:Timestamp:   ingested_at\n296:Type:        state\n299:Timestamp:   ingested_at\n329:Type:        state\n332:Timestamp:   -\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 9, "timestamp": 1795511649319}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The pipeline_explain.json is a list of 15 tables with columns and descriptions. Let me inspect the entries for our sink tables to see the current descriptions. Let me print table names and their column descriptions.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd588127ee18c426bcd", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json'))\nfor t in d:\n    name=t.get('name') or t.get('tableName') or t.get('id')\n    cols=t.get('columns') or t.get('columnDefinitions') or []\n    print('===',name, t.get('type'))\n    for c in (cols if isinstance(cols,list) else []):\n        print('  ', c.get('name'), '|', (c.get('description') or '')[:70])\n\""}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 414, "output": 209, "cacheRead": 202496, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 42, "totalTokens": 203119, "cost": {"input": 0.00012419999999999998, "output": 0.00025079999999999997, "cacheRead": 0.001214976, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0015899759999999999}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511649324, "responseId": "49bf323f40ec439fa009f023e6fe61e8-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 1297, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd588127ee18c426bcd", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "=== Loan_Payment stream\n=== Loan_Payment import\n=== Personal_Loan stream\n=== Personal_Loan import\n=== CustomerLoans query\n=== CustomerLoansSource state\n=== LoanPaymentSummary query\n=== LoanPaymentSummarySource state\n=== LoanPayments query\n=== LoanPaymentsSource state\n=== _AppliedPaymentAgg state\n=== _LatestLoanPayment state\n=== _LatestPersonalLoan state\n=== _LatestPersonalLoanPayment state\n=== _ReturnedPaymentAgg state\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 25, "timestamp": 1795511650647}, {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The columns are nested differently. Let me inspect the raw JSON structure for one entry.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd5914d7740b7e33af9", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/loan-payments && python3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json'))\nfor t in d:\n    if t.get('name')=='LoanPaymentsSource':\n        print(json.dumps(t, indent=2)[:3000]); break\n\""}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 222, "output": 125, "cacheRead": 203008, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 17, "totalTokens": 203355, "cost": {"input": 6.659999999999999e-05, "output": 0.00015, "cacheRead": 0.001218048, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.001434648}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795511650651, "responseId": "2031b8b76781448598b4744bf4b9c8cf-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2231, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cd5914d7740b7e33af9", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"id\": \"default_catalog.default_database.LoanPaymentsSource\",\n  \"name\": \"LoanPaymentsSource\",\n  \"type\": \"state\",\n  \"stage\": \"flink\",\n  \"inputs\": [\n    \"default_catalog.default_database._LatestPersonalLoan\",\n    \"default_catalog.default_database._LatestPersonalLoanPayment\"\n  ],\n  \"annotations\": [],\n  \"plan\": \"LogicalProject(payment_id=[$0], loan_id=[$1], customer_id=[$23], loan_purpose=[$26], payment_amount_cents=[$3], principal_cents=[$4], interest_cents=[$5], late_fee_cents=[$6], other_fees_cents=[$7], payment_type=[$8], payment_status=[$9], due_date=[$10], received_date=[$11], applied_date=[$12], payment_method=[$13], principal_balance_after_cents=[$14], days_past_due_at_payment=[$15], confirmation_number=[$16], return_reason_code=[$17])\\n  LogicalJoin(condition=[=($1, $21)], joinType=[inner])\\n    LogicalTableScan(table=[[default_catalog, default_database, _LatestPersonalLoanPayment]])\\n    LogicalTableScan(table=[[default_catalog, default_database, _LatestPersonalLoan]])\\n\",\n  \"sql\": \"CREATE VIEW `LoanPaymentsSource` AS \\n  SELECT p.payment_id,\\n         p.loan_id,\\n         l.primary_borrower_customer_id AS customer_id,\\n         l.loan_purpose,\\n         p.payment_amount_cents,\\n         p.principal_cents,\\n         p.interest_cents,\\n         p.late_fee_cents,\\n         p.other_fees_cents,\\n         p.payment_type,\\n         p.payment_status,\\n         p.due_date,\\n         p.received_date,\\n         p.applied_date,\\n         p.payment_method,\\n         p.principal_balance_after_cents,\\n         p.days_past_due_at_payment,\\n         p.confirmation_number,\\n         p.return_reason_code\\n  FROM _LatestPersonalLoanPayment p\\n  JOIN _LatestPersonalLoan l ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id;\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"-\",\n  \"schema\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"payment_id\",\n      \"type\": \"VARCHAR(2147483647) CHARACTER SET \\\"UTF-16LE\\\" NOT NULL\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"loan_id\",\n      \"type\": \"VARCHAR(2147483647) CHARACTER SET \\\"UTF-16LE\\\" NOT NULL\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"customer_id\",\n      \"type\": \"VARCHAR(2147483647) CHARACTER SET \\\"UTF-16LE\\\" NOT NULL\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"loan_purpose\",\n      \"type\": \"VARCHAR(2147483647) CHARACTER SET \\\"UTF-16LE\\\"\"\n    },\n    {