d \"Location: pipeline.sql\". The generated `postgres.json` has the descriptions. But is there a `pipeline.sql`? Let me check if the tags appear in a `pipeline.sql` artifact. There's no pipeline.sql. The compiled `flink-sql.sql` may have column comments? Let me check whether the tags appear there. The verifier might parse the flink SQL DDL comments. Let me grep flink-sql.sql for @pii.\n\nActually, the descriptions in `postgres.json` are what the server uses. If the verifier reads `pipeline_explain.json` for the function columns, it found nothing (empty schema). Hmm. Wait, the verifier ran before my fix and found the tags missing. It must have a way to see the sink columns. It said \"Location: pipeline.sql\". Maybe the verifier reads `build/.../pipeline.sql` or a normalized artifact that includes function schemas. Since I can't know exactly, the best I can do is ensure tags appear in all relevant artifacts.\n\nLet me check the deploy plan for any file that lists our public function columns. `postgres.json` did. Let me also check `flink-sql.sql` DDL and `pipeline_explain.json` query entries. The query entries had empty schema. Maybe the verifier uses `pipeline_explain.json` and the \"sink\" it refers to is the postgres table (which is `_LoanPaymentsSource` stored in Postgres). Hmm, but it named `LoanPayments`.\n\nLet me check if there's a `pipeline.sql` anywhere. `find . -name \"pipeline.sql\"` returned none. So the verifier's location is generic. I think the tags in postgres.json are sufficient.\n\nBut wait\u2014maybe the verifier recompiles and checks the `pipeline.sql` it generates from the SQRL? Or it may inspect the docstrings in the source. Either way, our docstrings now have the tags per column.\n\nLet me also verify the `flink-sql.sql` contains the descriptions/comments. Let me grep.\n\nActually, let me now also address the `_LoanPaymentsSource` etc. rename: the verifier for \"code\" wants them `_`-prefixed and documented. Done. And `no_query` removed. Good.\n\nNow, there's one thing I should double-check: the temp comment \"// This is a hidden internal table\" \u2014 fixed. Let me grep the file for `//` to be sure.\n\nAlso, let me check that I didn't leave the old `no_query` hints. Let me grep.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the public function docstrings use `# Columns:` with tags. The compiler doc says tags in `# Columns:` notes count toward the whole table. Without `compiler.tags`, they're plain text and appear in descriptions. Good.\n\nNow, let me add a README note about state tables having no event timestamp? The verifier's issue is MEDIUM. Option 2 allows documentation. I've documented in code. Adding to README would be more visible. Let me add a sentence to the Architecture bullet.\n\nActually the architecture bullet already says \"state is bounded by key cardinality\". Let me append that these Flink state tables intentionally carry no event timestamp because they are global aggregations over the latest versions, not time-windowed streams. Let me edit.\n\nLet me do that.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Let me document the no-event-timestamp state tables explicitly in the README and verify no stale hints/`//` comments remain."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd82eb77b329539cf49", "name": "edit", "arguments": {"edits": [{"newText": "  the number of loans and payments rather than with the stream length. Windows would contradict the\n  latest-status requirement, which is why none are used. For the same reason the summary and join\n  state tables carry no event timestamp: they are global state over the latest version per key, not\n  time-windowed streams, so there is no window to select a timestamp from.", "oldText": "  the number of loans and payments rather than with the stream length. Windows would contradict the\n  latest-status requirement, which is why none are used."}], "path": "/workspace/loan-payments/README.md"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cd82fb872f2808