{"request_uuid": "a5a0a341-67dd-4fe9-88b6-c2c5702db363", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T09:24:19.721124+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "implementation", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Implementation Improvement Task\n\nImprove the implementation by fixing the following issues in order of priority:\n- [MEDIUM] Docstrings of the exposed table functions (LoanPaymentSummary, LoanPayments, CustomerLoans) document pipeline plumbing instead of the consumer-visible contract, e.g. 'customer_id: Injected from the JWT claim `customer_id`; never supplied by the caller.' The customer_id argument is not exposed in the GraphQL schema (it is injected via auth), so this internal wiring detail should not appear in doc-strings surfaced to API/REST/MCP consumers. (Location: loan_api.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] Internal aggregation tables _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg (and most other non-obvious choices such as the 30-second watermark delay, earliest-offset startup mode, and DISTINCT ON source_updated_at deduplication) lack -- or /* */ comments explaining why they are defined this way, making the intent of the pipeline hard to reconstruct from the codebase alone. (Location: loan_api.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [HIGH] Table _AppliedPaymentAgg uses a global, non-windowed GROUP BY aggregation with MAX(applied_date), a non-incremental aggregation function. On a retract stream this can cause unbounded state growth. Recommend using an upstream time-window, aggregating in the database, or another alternative if the state can become large. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State tables _AppliedPaymentAgg, _ReturnedPaymentAgg, _LoanPaymentSummarySource, _CustomerLoansSource, and _LoanPaymentsSource have no valid event timestamp (Timestamp: -). If these tables use time-windows, the timestamp must be explicitly selected and grouped by window_time. Otherwise, they lack event-time semantics for downstream operations. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] Possible state accumulation in Flink state tables: _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg use high-cardinality global aggregations over unbounded streams; _LoanPaymentsSource, _LoanPaymentSummarySource, and _CustomerLoansSource use stateful regular joins. These operators can retain unbounded state over time. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential time-bound optimization (only if the original requirements and semantics allow it): _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg global aggregations could be rewritten as time-window aggregations; _LoanPaymentsSource regular join could be rewritten as an interval or temporal join; similarly, the joins in _LoanPaymentSummarySource and _CustomerLoansSource could potentially be time-bounded. (Location: Pipeline Definition) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] The pipeline has no data quality tables (DQ_ prefix) or observability statistics tables (OB_ prefix), and no equivalent named tables or comments describing that purpose. Consider adding DQ_ and OB_ tables for data quality and observability. (Location: SQRL Code) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Auto_Loan table has no test coverage and no downstream tables or functions that are tested. It is a source table that is not exercised by any test. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Loan_Application table has no test coverage and no downstream tables or functions that are tested. It is a source table that is not exercised by any test. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event table has no test coverage and no downstream tables or functions that are tested. It is a source table that is not exercised by any test. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] LoanPaymentSummary function has no test coverage. Tests only target the internal _LoanPaymentSummarySource table, not the exported function's loan_id and customer_id filtering logic. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] LoanPayments function has no test coverage. Tests only target the internal _LoanPaymentsSource table, not the exported function's loan_id and customer_id filtering and ordering. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] CustomerLoans function has no test coverage. Tests only target the internal _CustomerLoansSource table, not the exported function's customer_id filtering and ordering. (Location: SQRL code) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] run-tests.sh does not fetch or initialize the ../data-catalog submodule that holds the test fixtures, so a clean checkout will lack test data and the suite is not reproducible. (Location: run-tests.sh) (Verifier: tests)\n- [HIGH] Watermark on file-based sources (Personal_Loan, Auto_Loan, Loan_Payment, Loan_Application, Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event) is placed on a plain `ingested_at` column that is neither declared as a computed `AS now()` column nor guaranteed quasi-monotonic. Per the file-source exception, the correct pattern is a computed ingestion-time column (`ingested_at AS now()`) used as the watermark; a bare data column that is not monotonically increasing risks dropping late files. Additionally, the 10-minute `source.monitor-interval` combined with a 10-second watermark delay means data can advance past the watermark between polls and get dropped. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [MEDIUM] The build is the `prod` configuration but the source connectors are `filesystem` with a 10-minute `source.monitor-interval`. This is a development/test-oriented ingestion pattern and is not production-grade (no durability guarantees, no back-pressure, no high-availability source of truth). A production build should use a production-grade connector (e.g. Kafka/Kinesis/Pulsar/etc.) or explicitly document why filesystem is the production source of truth. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [LOW] The pipeline relies almost entirely on `/** ... */` doc-strings for explanation and has very few `--` / `/* */` comments justifying design decisions (e.g. why `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` starts from `_LatestPersonalLoan` and LEFT JOINs the two aggregates, why returned payments are aggregated separately, why `_CustomerLoansSource` re-joins a summary that is already derived from `_LatestPersonalLoan`). Reviewers/maintainers cannot easily reconstruct intent from the code alone. (Location: consumer_loans.sqrl) (Verifier: code)\n- [HIGH] _AppliedPaymentAgg is a Flink state table that performs a global (non-windowed) GROUP BY over _LoanPaymentsSource and includes the non-incremental aggregation function MAX(applied_date) (alongside SUM/COUNT). Non-incremental aggregations over a retract stream require the operator to retain all input rows (or per-key state) indefinitely, causing unbounded state growth. Recommend replacing with an upstream time-window aggregation or pushing the computation into the database if the retained state can be large. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._AppliedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] _AppliedPaymentAgg performs a global (non-windowed) GROUP BY loan_id over a filesystem source (connector = 'filesystem', source.monitor-interval = '10 min') that periodically re-lists and ingests the complete JSONL snapshot each cycle. Without an ingestion-burst-aware window the aggregation will double-count rows across snapshots (each row counted once per snapshot), inflating totals every cycle. Recommend wrapping the aggregation in a SESSION window over an ingestion-time now() column partitioned by loan_id (each snapshot's ingestion burst becomes one session). Note that a PRIMARY KEY on an append-only file source does not deduplicate. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._AppliedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [HIGH] _ReturnedPaymentAgg performs a global (non-windowed) GROUP BY loan_id over a filesystem source (connector = 'filesystem', source.monitor-interval = '10 min') that periodically re-lists and ingests the complete JSONL snapshot each cycle. Rows will be double-counted across snapshot loads, inflating returned_payment_count over time. Recommend aggregating per snapshot with a SESSION window over an ingestion-time now() column partitioned by loan_id. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._ReturnedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _AppliedPaymentAgg has no valid (event) timestamp column. Flink state tables should declare a valid rowtime/event-time attribute (e.g. from an ingestion-time column) to enable time-based operations and proper watermarking. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._AppliedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _CustomerLoansSource has no valid (event) timestamp column. Since this is a Flink state table that joins upstream streams, a timestamp is required for correct time-based processing; if time-windows are used it must explicitly select and group by window_time. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._CustomerLoansSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _LoanPaymentSummarySource has no valid (event) timestamp column. Flink state tables that derive from joins and aggregations need a valid rowtime to enable time-based processing and any windowed variants. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentSummarySource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _LoanPaymentsSource has no valid (event) timestamp column. As a Flink state table that joins two upstream streams this requires a rowtime/event-time attribute. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentsSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] State table _ReturnedPaymentAgg has no valid (event) timestamp column. Flink state tables performing aggregations should declare a valid rowtime/event time. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._ReturnedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _AppliedPaymentAgg performs a high-cardinality aggregation over ~2e6 loan groups on the Flink state engine. Group state is retained per loan_id indefinitely, which can accumulate significant state over time. Consider partitioning/limiting the retained keys or using a windowed aggregation where semantics permit. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._AppliedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _CustomerLoansSource performs plain equi-joins between _LatestPersonalLoan and _LoanPaymentSummarySource on loan_id without any time constraints. Regular Flink stream joins must retain all input history for both sides and therefore may accumulate substantial state over time. Only flag-free if the join were a temporal join (FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF) or an interval join with timestamp bounds. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._CustomerLoansSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _LoanPaymentSummarySource performs plain equi-joins on loan_id between _LatestPersonalLoan, _AppliedPaymentAgg and _ReturnedPaymentAgg. These stateful joins without time bounds retain all upstream records and can accumulate unbounded state. Temporal or interval join variants would avoid this. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentSummarySource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _LoanPaymentsSource performs a plain equi-join between _LatestPersonalLoanPayment and _LatestPersonalLoan on loan_id without any time constraint. This stateful join stores both sides' history in Flink state, which can grow with the number of loan/payment records (~1e8) over time. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentsSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [MEDIUM] _ReturnedPaymentAgg performs a high-cardinality aggregation grouped by loan_id over _LoanPaymentsSource and retains per-key accumulators for potentially all loans, which can accumulate substantial state over time on the Flink state engine. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._ReturnedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential performance improvement: the global GROUP BY aggregation in _AppliedPaymentAgg could be rewritten using time-bounded aggregation (e.g. time-window aggregation over a suitable rowtime column) to avoid unbounded state growth. This only applies if the original semantics allow the aggregation to be constrained to a window. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._AppliedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential performance improvement: the equi-join in _CustomerLoansSource between loan state and payment summary could be rewritten as a temporal join (FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF) or an interval join constrained on the timestamp columns to bound state. Only applicable if the original job semantics permit it. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._CustomerLoansSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential performance improvement: the equi-join in _LoanPaymentsSource between _LatestPersonalLoanPayment and _LatestPersonalLoan could be rewritten as a temporal or interval join with time bounds to avoid unbounded state, provided the original requirements and semantics allow it. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentsSource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential performance improvement: the equi-joins in _LoanPaymentSummarySource (loan with applied and returned payment aggregates) could be rewritten with time-bounded joins (interval/temporal) to limit retracted-state accumulation, if the semantics of the job permit it. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._LoanPaymentSummarySource) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Potential performance improvement: the aggregation in _ReturnedPaymentAgg could be replaced by a time-window aggregation over the source rowtime to bound state, only if the original semantics allow windowing rather than a global per-loan count. (Location: default_catalog.default_database._ReturnedPaymentAgg) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] The pipeline contains no data quality tables (DQ_ prefix or equivalent) and no observability statistics tables (OB_ prefix or equivalent). Adding DQ_ tables (null/duplicate/range checks on the loan and payment streams) and OB_ tables (record counts, lag, freshness) would greatly improve observability of this pipeline. (Location: cons_loans pipeline (all tables)) (Verifier: pipeline)\n- [LOW] Auto_Loan (and Auto_Loan_schema) has no test coverage. The source table is imported but is not consumed by any downstream table or function, and no /*+test */ query references it (AutoLoanLeakTest only filters the personal-loan payment source and does not exercise the Auto_Loan table). (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Loan_Application (and Loan_Application_schema) has no test coverage. The source table is imported but never used by any downstream table or function, and no test queries it. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event (and Consumer_Loan_Delinquency_Event_schema) has no test coverage. The source table is imported but never used by any downstream table or function, and no test queries it. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] The user-facing function LoanPaymentSummary has no test. Only its underlying internal table _LoanPaymentSummarySource is snapshot-tested; the function's metadata-injected customer_id/loan_id filter and column projection are not exercised by any test. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] The user-facing function LoanPayments has no test. Only its underlying internal table _LoanPaymentsSource is snapshot-tested; the function's metadata-injected customer_id/loan_id filter, ordering, and projection are not exercised by any test. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] The user-facing function CustomerLoans has no test. Only its underlying internal table _CustomerLoansSource is snapshot-tested; the function's metadata-injected customer_id filter and ordering are not exercised by any test. (Location: loan-payments.sqrl) (Verifier: tests)\n- [LOW] run-tests.sh depends on the ../data-catalog submodule's lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/*.jsonl fixtures (the setup block is commented out on the assumption that ${DATA_PATH} points at that folder), but the script never initializes or fetches that submodule. From a clean checkout the fixture data may be absent, making the run non-reproducible. (Location: run-tests.sh) (Verifier: tests)\n\nFixing an issue means one of the following:\n1. Updating the implementation to implement a solution that fixes the issue. Use suggested solutions as a starting point.\n2. Adding documentation to the source code or README.md to explain why an identified issue does not apply, because:\n   1. You can deduce from the pipeline constraints or data semantics that the issue cannot arise (e.g. documenting that an aggregation `GROUP BY tenantId` does not result in unbounded state growth because we expect only 100s of tenants).\n   2. It's a known issue that won't be fixed (e.g. mark as `TODO` because it is not a priority right now or the risk is deemed low).\n\n**IMPORTANT**:\nOnly fix issues that are relevant to this implementation and compatible with the user requirements.\nUser requirements always take priority - do not implement fixes that contradict requirements. Add documentation when identified issues conflict with user requirements.\n\n**An issue on a file your plan did not ask you to touch is out of scope \u2014 leave it alone (option 3).**\nRewriting pre-existing, working code that you were not asked to change is unnecessary if it is not critical.\n\n**If you rename a table during refinement**\n- Update every reference to the old name across all `.sqrl` files (definitions, SELECT statements, test tables).\n- Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill and run tests. Ensure tests pass.\n\n## Implementation\n\nMake sure to run tests and improve the codebase until tests pass. Run the project's test runner script, `./run-tests.sh`, scoped to the sub-projects your fixes touched: before running it, list every sub-project you edited plus every sub-project affected by an edit to shared code or configuration, and pass exactly those names as arguments. If a fix adds or renames a sub-project, add or update its suite line in `run-tests.sh`. For the details of the tests and the runner script, invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill.\n\nRead AGENTS.md for detailed DataSQRL syntax, patterns, and available tools!\n\n## Response\n\nWhen the fixes are done and the tests you ran pass, your final response must be ONLY the `./run-tests.sh` invocation you ran successfully, on one line, with the sub-project names you scoped it to (no arguments when you ran everything). No markdown, no explanation.\n\nCorrect response example:\n```\n./run-tests.sh [affected-sub-project-names] # only the affected sub-project(s) \n```\nand after a fix to shared code or configuration:\n```\n./run-tests.sh  # everything\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795512259717}}
