 (filesystem), even though our main script imports the PROD connectors (Kafka). So the build contains both the test filesystem tables and the prod Kafka tables? But our main script only reads `cons_loans.Personal_Loan` from the prod import. The catalog's own ontology tables (file-based) are loaded but not used by our pipeline. So the prod build should not include the filesystem sources in the actual pipeline.\n\nWait, the verifier says \"_AppliedPaymentAgg performs a global GROUP BY over a filesystem source (connector='filesystem', source.monitor-interval='10 min')\". That suggests the actual pipeline used the filesystem source in the prod build. Hmm. Let me check the compiled prod pipeline. Earlier when I compiled prod, the pipeline_explain.json showed `Loan_Payment` with topic `consumer_loans.loan_payment` (Kafka). So prod uses Kafka. But wait, that was after I compiled with prod. Let me re-verify: In the very first compile (api-test), the log showed `Loan_Payment` with `value.format` etc.? Actually the first grep of pipeline_explain.json showed Loan_Payment with `ingested_at` and kafka topic. Let me check which compile produced that. The `/tmp/loanpay_compile.log` was the api-test compile, and the pipeline_explain.json showed the Kafka connector? Let me re-read: earlier I ran `python3` on `build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json` and it showed `Loan_Payment` with `\"connector\"`? Actually the grep for @pii showed `pipeline_explain.json` lines with descriptions. And the Loan_Payment entry earlier (from the prod compile? no, the api-test compile) showed:\n```\n'connector' = 'kafka-safe', topic consumer_loans.loan_payment\n```\nWait that was from `build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json` after the api-test compile? Let me scroll: The output at \"=== raw grep context ===\" showed `Loan_Payment` with Kafka topic. That was after the api-test compile? Actually I ran that after the first compile (api-test). But the test environment should use filesystem. Hmm, that's confusing.\n\nWait, no. The first compile was with `loan-payments-api-test-package.json`, but the base include uses `ontology-test-package.json` and `is_batch:false`. Our main script imports `consumer_loans-{{environment}}` with environment=test \u2192 filesystem. So the test build should haves filesystem. But the pipeline_explain.json showed Kafka. That means... the build/loan-payments/pipeline_explain.json I inspected was from the PROD compile (I ran prod compile before that inspection). Let me recheck the sequence:\n1. api-test compile \u2192 /tmp/loanpay_compile.log. Then I ran test (which recompiled test).\n2. Then prod compile \u2192 /tmp/loanpay_prodcompile.log.\n3. Then I inspected pipeline_explain.json \u2014 that was the prod build! Yes, because the prod compile was the last compile. So the pipeline_explain.json I looked at was prod (Kafka). Good.\n\nBut the verifier now says the pipeline uses filesystem sources in the prod build. Hmm, maybe the verifier compiled with the test config? Or maybe the verifier's \"prod configuration\" claim is wrong. Let me verify by compiling prod again and checking the connector. Actually, the verifier's issue says \"The build is the prod configuration but the source connectors are filesystem\". That contradicts my finding. Let me re-check carefully.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe the issue is that the catalog's own ontology files are loaded and compiled into the pipeline even though not used? No, unused tables aren't in the pipeline.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider the include: `\"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json\"` with `config: {is_batch:false}`. The package's `script.config.environment` is `test`. Our main script's import uses OUR `{{environment}}` = prod. So main reads consumer_loans-prod (Kafka). The catalog's own `lending-ontology.sqrl` imports consumer_loans-test (filesystem) because the include's config environment is test. But is `lending-ontology.sqrl` part of our pipeline? It's in the include namespace but not imported by our main script. DataSQRL loads it into build/data_catalog but doesn't include its tables in our pipeline unless imported. So prod pipeline uses Kafka.\n\nUnless... the compiler, when `is_batch:false` and the include root has `ontology.sqrl` as main? The include package `ontology-test-package.json` has no `script.main` (it's an overlay). So no main script. Only files loaded.\n\nHmm, but the verifier explicitly says filesystem in the prod build. Maybe the verifier compiled with the `test` config but labeled it prod? Or maybe the verifier is inspecting `pipeline_source.sqrl`? Let me check `build/loan-payments/pipeline_source.sqrl` \u2014 that might show the imported source. Actually, let me just compile prod and grep the connector.\n\nRegardless, the double-counting issue only matters for filesystem sources. In test, the filesystem source `source.monitor-interval='10 min'`. The test runner runs once, so no repeated snapshots. In prod, Kafka. So the double-counting concern doesn't apply to prod. For test, the test runner drains once, so no double-count. So it's a non-issue. But I should document.\n\nActually, more seriously: if the verifier believes the prod build uses filesystem, maybe there's a real problem with my include config: the catalog's own ontology (with environment=test) might be pulled into the pipeline because... no.\n\nLet me actually verify by compiling prod and checking the `_AppliedPaymentAgg` inputs' connectors. Let me do that.\n\nAlso, the watermark issue is in the read-only catalog; document as out-of-scope.\n\nNow the concrete actionable items for this round:\n\n1. **Docstrings leak internal plumbing**: Remove \"customer_id: Injected from the JWT claim...; never supplied by the caller\" from `# Arguments:` of the three functions. Actually, the customer_id argument is not part of the GraphQL schema, so it shouldn't be in the docstring's `# Arguments:`. But the docstring's `# Arguments:` describes function arguments including metadata ones? The compiler doc says \"An argument note becomes the GraphQL argument description\". Since customer_id isn't exposed, the note is ignored or leaks. The verifier wants it removed. I'll remove the customer_id argument line from `# Arguments:` and keep only `loan_id`. For `CustomerLoans`, remove the `# Arguments:` section entirely (only customer_id, not exposed). But the function still needs documentation that it's the signed-in customer. I can say that in the main description.\n\nActually, the function docstring should describe the consumer-visible contract. For `LoanPaymentSummary`, `# Arguments: - loan_id: ...`. For `LoanPayments`, `# Arguments: - loan_id: ...`. For `CustomerLoans`, no arguments. The customer_id injection can be mentioned in the description as behavior? The verifier says internal wiring shouldn't appear. I'll phrase the description as \"for the signed-in customer\" without mentioning the claim.\n\n2. **More `--` comments** for `_AppliedPaymentAgg`, `_ReturnedPaymentAgg`, watermark delay, earliest-offset, DISTINCT. Some already exist. Add comments for the two aggregates (why separate, why returned separately). Add a comment about the watermark delay? The watermark is set by the catalog connectors (test 10s, prod 30s) \u2014 we don't define it. I can document in README/comment that we use the catalog watermarks. Already in README. Add a code comment.\n\n3. **HIGH: _AppliedPaymentAgg MAX non-incremental / unbounded** \u2014 already documented. But the verifier keeps flagging. Maybe I should actually change the implementation to avoid MAX. Options:\n   - Compute `last_payment_date` without MAX? \n   - Move the aggregation to the database.\n   - Add `table.exec.state.ttl` config to bound state.\n   \n   Actually, a good mitigation is to add `table.exec.state.ttl` to the Flink config. This bounds all state. The plan didn't mention it, but it's a safe production setting. However, it could drop old state and affect correctness (e.g., if a loan's last payment is older than TTL, the summary could lose data). With TTL, Flink state expires after the duration; the aggregation would then emit incorrect results for keys not touched within TTL. Since the API must keep all payments with no expiry (retention requirement), TTL could break correctness. So not advisable.\n\n   Better: Move the aggregation to Postgres. If `_AppliedPaymentAgg` is `/*+engine(postgres)*/`, Postgres computes it as a view/query over `_LoanPaymentsSource`. But `_LoanPaymentsSource` is a Flink table stored in Postgres. Then `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` would join the Postgres view with the Flink table? A Postgres table can't feed a Flink table. `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` currently runs in Flink. If we move `_AppliedPaymentAgg` to Postgres, then `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` (Flink) can't read it. So we'd need to move `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` to Postgres too, and it reads `_LatestPersonalLoan` which is Flink. A Postgres table cannot read a Flink table either. Hmm.\n\n   Actually, in DataSQRL, the function runs in Postgres; the base table `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` is computed in Flink and materialized in Postgres. If we want the aggregation in Postgres, we'd define the summary as a view over the Flink-materialized `_LatestPersonalLoan` and `_LoanPaymentsSource`. But `_LatestPersonalLoan` and `_LoanPaymentsSource` are Flink tables materialized into Postgres. Are they available as Postgres tables? Yes, Flink writes them to Postgres. But DataSQRL's engine assignment: if `_LoanPaymentSummarySource` is postgres, it can read Flink-materialized tables? The rule says \"A table in Postgres cannot feed a table in Flink. Pinning such a table to postgres fails...\" Actually the reverse: a Flink table materialized to Postgres is available. Let me recall: \"A table in Postgres stores no rows for the table... When the API reads the table directly, the compiler creates a view with the query of the table.\" If the query reads Flink tables, the view would reference the Postgres materialized tables. But the compiler determines this. Actually, a Postgres-stage table that reads a Flink table: the skill says \"A table in Postgres cannot feed a table in Flink.\" and \"A table in Flink can read only from Flink or Kafka.\" It doesn't say a Postgres table can't read Flink tables. Hmm. Actually, the compiler materializes Flink tables into Postgres, so a Postgres view can read them. But the optimizer might complain.\n\n   This is getting risky. Given the requirements and time, I'll document and add a clear rationale. But the verifier keeps flagging HIGH. The task says \"Use suggested solutions as a starting point\" but also allows documentation. However, three consecutive rounds flagged this; maybe I should actually make a change.\n\n   Let me think about a robust fix: The double-counting/state issue stems from the aggregation. What if I compute the summary in Flink but use `/*+engine(flink)*/` and add a `table.exec.state.ttl`? No.\n\n   Alternative: Since the aggregation is grouped by loan_id and the source is deduplicated (one row per payment), the state is exactly one entry per loan (for the aggregate) plus one per payment (dedup). That's not \"unbounded over time\"; it's bounded by the number of distinct keys (~1M loans + payments). The plan's workload assumption is 1M loans, 50k payments/day, retention all payments. Over years, payments accumulate (e.g., 50k/day * 365 * several years = tens of millions), so state grows. But that's inherent to serving all payment history. The requirement says \"the API keeps all payments of all personal loans, with no expiry.\" So the state must grow with the data. This is a documented, requirement-driven design.\n\n   I'll strengthen the documentation and also add a `/*+engine(flink)*/`? No.\n\n   Actually, wait: maybe I can avoid the non-incremental MAX by restructuring: instead of `MAX(applied_date)`, I could use a \"latest applied payment date\" derived from a separate dedup: `DISTINCT _LoanPaymentsSource ON loan_id ...`? No, that dedups on loan_id, not what we want.\n\n   Or use `last_value`? Not simpler.\n\n   I think the correct response is documentation: state is bounded by key cardinality, and the requirement mandates retaining all payments, so growth is proportional to the dataset, not the stream rate. This is option 2. I've documented it. Let me make it even more explicit and reference the workload.\n\n   Also the verifier's \"double-count across filesystem snapshots\" \u2014 I'll document that the filesystem source is test-only; prod is Kafka. And the catalog owns that connector. If the verifier really sees filesystem in prod, there's a config issue. Let me verify.\n\n4. **State tables no event timestamp** \u2014 document (already partly). The verifier wants a timestamp. Can I add one cheaply? For `_LoanPaymentsSource`, I could select a timestamp column, e.g., `GREATEST(p.source_updated_at, l.source_updated_at) AS ...`? But source_updated_at is a business timestamp, not event time. The event time is `ingested_at`. The join could carry `GREATEST(p.ingested_at, l.ingested_at) AS _rowtime`. But whether SQRL uses it as the table timestamp depends on naming/type. Actually, DataSQRL determines the timestamp from the input tables' watermarks when the query is a stream operation. For a regular join, the rule says the result timestamp should be `GREATEST(l.ts, r.ts)`. Maybe if I explicitly write it, the state table gets a timestamp. Let me try adding a timestamp to the join tables and aggregations.\n\n   For `_LoanPaymentsSource`, add `GREATEST(p.ingested_at, l.ingested_at) AS _rowtime`? But `ingested_at` is the watermark column in the connector tables. The DISTINCT tables `_LatestPersonalLoan`/`_LatestLoanPayment` \u2014 do they retain `ingested_at`? Yes, SELECT *. So I can add. But adding a `_`-prefixed column is hidden from the API. The verifier's \"Timestamp: -\" might be fixed if SQRL picks `_rowtime`. But actually, SQRL's timestamp assignment uses the `rowtime` marker, not arbitrary columns. Hmm.\n\n   Alternatively, the `WATERMARK` on the source gives event time to `Personal_Loan`/`Loan_Payment`. The DISTINCT tables should carry that timestamp? Let me check pipeline_explain.txt: `_LatestPersonalLoan` had `Timestamp: ingested_at` (lines 223/226). `_LatestPersonalLoanPayment` had `Timestamp: ingested_at`. But `_LoanPaymentsSource` (join) has `Timestamp: -`. So the regular join loses the timestamp. The skill says to set the timestamp with `GREATEST`. But how is that expressed so SQRL recognizes it? Perhaps SQRL automatically assigns `GREATEST` if written as a column? The skill example: \"Give `GREATEST` one argument for each joined table that has a timestamp.\" But it doesn't say to alias it. Hmm.\n\n   Let me read the stream-enrichment guide for regular join timestamps. Actually, maybe DataSQRL automatically infers the join timestamp if both inputs have timestamps. But it shows \"-\". Maybe because the join is a regular inner join and the compiler didn't infer. Let me test adding `GREATEST(p.ingested_at, l.ingested_at) AS _rowtime` and see if the pipeline_explain timestamp becomes `_rowtime`.\n\n   Hmm, but this is getting deep. The state timestamp is a MEDIUM issue; documentation is acceptable. But the verifier has flagged it 3 times. Let me attempt a fix for at least the join/aggregate tables by selecting the max/ingested timestamp. But it might break the API if the column is exposed. Using a `_`-prefixed alias hides it.\n\n   Actually, for aggregations, the skill says state tables from group-by have no timestamp unless windowed. So we can't easily give them a timestamp without windows. So document.\n\n   For joins, maybe I can add the timestamp. Let me experiment after the docstring fixes. If it works, great; if not, document.\n\n5. **DQ/OB tables** \u2014 document. Already done. The verifier suggests adding them. The plan decided not to. Keep documentation.\n\n6. **Source tables Auto_Loan etc. untested** \u2014 document (out of scope). Already in script. Maybe add a `/*+test(no_rows)*/`? No, we can't meaningfully test unused tables. Document.\n\n7. **Functions untested** \u2014 they ARE covered by API tests. The verifier's static test-coverage check doesn't count API tests. I could add table tests that call the functions? Table tests can't call functions with metadata claims... Actually, the test runner executes `/*+test*/` tables via the inferred schema. Could I define a `/*+test*/` table that selects from the function? Like `SELECT * FROM LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id => 'PL-115')`? Functions with metadata args: when called in SQL, the metadata arg is... not available. So no.\n\n   But the API tests DO cover the functions. I'll document in README (already lists them). Maybe add a comment in the script. Already added.\n\n8. **run-tests.sh submodule init** \u2014 Add a guarded setup step to initialize the submodule. But `git` isn't available in the container, and the repo's data-catalog is a submodule. The verifier wants `git submodule update --init` in run-tests.sh. I can add a guarded setup that runs `git submodule update --init --recursive` if `data-catalog` is missing. But in the agent container, the submodule is already mounted; git is missing. Adding a guarded step that checks existence and skips if present is safe. Let me add:\n   ```bash\n   [ -n \"$LIST\" ] || { [ -d ../data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata ] || git submodule update --init --recursive; }\n   ```\n   But run-tests.sh's cwd is the project dir (/workspace/loan-payments). `../data-catalog`. If git missing, the command fails; but guarded by the dir existence check, it would skip. Good. Actually, in a clean checkout CI with git, it would init. Let me add this.\n\n   But careful: the setup block runs before the suite table. It's guarded by `[ -n \"$LIST\" ]`. Good.\n\nNow, the big question: is the prod build actually using filesystem? Let me verify before deciding. Let me compile prod and check `_AppliedPaymentAgg`'s source connector.\n\nLet me run a prod compile and inspect. Actually, I'll do it after edits to avoid extra compiles. But the connector question affects documentation. Let me quickly check the existing `build/loan-payments` (last compile was the final test = test env). Hmm, the final test compile overwrote build with test env. So pipeline_explain.json now is test (filesystem). Let me check. That's likely why the verifier (which may inspect the last build) sees filesystem. The verifier ran after my final test run, which compiled the test config! So the build folder had test config \u2192 filesystem. That's why it says \"prod configuration but filesystem\". Wait, the verifier says \"build is the prod configuration\" \u2014 maybe it inspects `build/loan-payments` after the final run which was test. Confusing.\n\nThe key point: the double-counting across filesystem snapshot reloads only matters if the filesystem source is polled repeatedly in a long-running job. In test, the job runs once and drains. In prod, Kafka. So no double-count in the required scenarios. I'll document.\n\nBut wait, could the test runner poll the filesystem multiple times within a single test run? `source.monitor-interval='10 min'`, and the test run is ~1 min. So no repeated poll. Good.\n\nNow, the watermark issue: the catalog's test connector declares `ingested_at` as a plain column. The verifier says it should be `AS now()`. But the catalog is read-only. Document as out-of-scope (catalog owns connectors).\n\nLet me now also address the HIGH double-count by considering: is it possible that the filesystem source's monitor re-lists? With `is_batch:false`, the test connector includes `source.monitor-interval='10 min'`. If the job runs longer than 10 min, it would re-list and re-ingest, double-counting. But the test job doesn't. Again, document.\n\nOK. Let me also reconsider whether I should set `is_batch: true` for the test config to avoid monitor re-listing? No, that would change behavior. Actually, the test runner needs the streaming pipeline.\n\nLet me now make the edits:\n\n### Edit 1: loan-payments.sqrl docstrings + comments\n\nRemove customer_id from `# Arguments:`. Add more comments.\n\nLet me view the current file sections to edit precisely. Let me read the file.\n\nActually, I wrote it, so I know the content. Let me do targeted edits.\n\nFor `LoanPaymentSummary` docstring:\nCurrent:\n```\n/** The payment counts, totals and last applied payment date of one personal loan, for its owner.\n    Totals and the last payment date cover only APPLIED payments. The loan is looked up under the\n    loan_id and the signed-in customer, so it returns null when the loan does not exist or does not\n    belong to the caller.\n# Arguments:\n- loan_id: The loan whose summary to return.\n- customer_id: Injected from the JWT claim `customer_id`; never supplied by the caller.\n# Columns:\n...\n```\nNew: remove the customer_id argument line. Keep loan_id.\n\nFor `LoanPayments`, remove customer_id line.\n\nFor `CustomerLoans`, there's a `# Arguments:` with only customer_id. Remove the entire `# Arguments:` section. The description already says \"of the signed-in customer\".\n\n### Edit 2: comments for aggregates\n\nAdd `--` comments before `_ReturnedPaymentAgg` and strengthen `_AppliedPaymentAgg`. Already have some. Let me add more about why returned is separate.\n\n### Edit 3: run-tests.sh submodule setup\n\nReplace the commented setup with an active guarded line.\n\nLet me also add a comment about the watermark/earliest-offset (catalog-owned).\n\nLet me do the edits. Let me first read the relevant portion of the file to get exact text."}}
